    "history": "Originating in the United States, Abyssinia was once a staple of 'Dealer's Choice' home games. It was extensively documented in Scarne on Cards (1949). - 1940s: Emerged as a competitive stud variant in high-stakes home games. - 1950s: Standard tier-pricing (2u/1u/4u) for ranks became the house standard. - Present: Remained a niche favorite in professional poker circles. Replaced the random deal of Five-Card Stud with a tiered-cost economy for specific card ranks. Reflects the American post-war home-game culture of introducing complex rules to standard gambling games.",
    "setup": "Players: 3-7 players (Optimal with 5). Deck: Standard 52-card deck. No jokers. Equipment: Standard deck, poker chips, and a dealer button. Dealing: 1. All players ante. 2. Dealer turns up two cards and places them beside the face-down deck (the Market). 3. No initial cards to players; each builds a 5-card hand over 5 buy rounds.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the pot by having the highest or lowest hand (or both) at the showdown. Gameplay: 1. **Ante**: All players post the ante. 2. **Market Setup**: Dealer turns up two cards and places them beside the deck. 3. **Buy Phase**: Starting left of dealer, each player buys one card from the market (face-up) or stock (top card, revealed on purchase). 4. **Refill**: Purchased market cards are replaced immediately from the stock. 5. **Bet Phase**: After each buy round, a standard betting round. 6. **Repetition**: Buy/bet sequence repeats 5 times until each player has 5 cards. 7. **Declaration**: Active players secret-declare High, Low, or Both using chips in hand. 8. **Showdown**: Cards revealed; pot split per declarations. Scoring: High-Low Split pot. 50% to the best poker hand, 50% to the best low hand (8-or-better qualifier). - Market cards cost based on rank: 2-5 (2u), 6-K (1u),...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep your 'down' cards a secret while tracking what others buy face-up. Cheap 6-K cards are great for bluffing a high hand. Intermediate: Aces are versatile but expensive. Only buy them if they complete a flush/straight or a top-tier low. Advanced: Defensive rank-blocking: If the refill shows a 2 and an opponent is building a low, buy that 2 even if you have a high hand. - Tip: Being on the dealer's left is high value; you get the first choice of the fresh market refill. - Tip: Check the threshold: Ensure you actually have an 8-low or better before declaring Low. - Tip: Track opponent face-up purchases to estimate their declaration direction before chips-in-fist.",
    "variations": "- Fixed Market Price: All cards cost 2 units regardless of rank. Removes the tiered economy. - Hidden Stock: Cards bought from the stock remain face-down (private), instead of being revealed to the table. - 7-Card Abyssinia: Hand size is 7; best 5 of 7 play. Buy phases extend to 5 rounds."
