    "overview": "Accordion is a single-player patience game where all cards are laid into one row and compressed into piles by matching suit or rank. A card can move onto the pile immediately to its left or three positions to its left. The goal is to finish with one pile. Origin: Europe (19th century, likely England or France) Players: 1 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Expert Duration: 5 min",
    "history": "Rules for The Idle Year published by William Brisbane Dick (1883) and Tarbart (1905). Wood & Goddard (1940) recorded it as Tower of Babel. The name Accordion appears in the 1950s (Culbertson, Goren). Parlett equates Accordion with Idle Year, Methuselah and Tower of Babel. - 1883: Dick publishes strict rules (packet must move if possible). - 1905: Tarbart publishes lax rules (judgment on whether to play). - 1940: Wood & Goddard record as Tower of Babel. - 1950s: Accordion name appears; Culbertson and Goren allow further deal before deciding moves. Originally cards were laid one at a time with moves only when no deal possible; modern rules adopt deal-then-play or play-as-you-go for better win odds. Known as The Idle Year because a well-shuffled pack may require about a year to accomplish. Odds of winning ~1 in 100.",
    "setup": "Players: One player. Accordion is a patience (solitaire) game played by a single player; there are no opponents or partners. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Equipment: One shuffled deck and table space for a long row of piles. Dealing: Deal cards one by one from stock into a single row. After each deal, resolve any legal compression moves before dealing again if playing the incremental style.",
    "rules": "Objective: Compress the entire deck into a single pile. The game is won when all 52 cards are in one pile; the layout is so named because compressing the row resembles closing accordion pleats. Gameplay: 1. Deal one card at a time into a row of face-up piles. 2. A top card may move onto the pile immediately to its left or three piles to its left if the destination top card matches suit or rank. 3. After a move, close gaps so piles stay contiguous. 4. Continue moving whenever legal moves exist. 5. Hand ends when no legal moves remain. Scoring: Score by final number of piles; fewer is better. - Moves are always to the left. - A move is legal only when suit or rank matches the destination top card. - The allowed jump distances are exactly 1-left and 3-left. Win condition: Finish with exactly one pile.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Identify four cards of the same rank close together near the end of the layout; move these sweeper cards as a group toward the front without covering them until the end. Intermediate: Prefer 3-left moves when they do not block future sweeper sequences. Preserve connector piles that enable multiple 3-left paths. Advanced: Treat the row as a dependency graph. Odds of winning are around one in a hundred. Alfred Sheinwold suggests considering five piles or fewer a win. - Tip: Deal-all and play-as-you-go require different planning horizons. - Tip: Track sweeper ranks (four of a kind) near the right side.",
    "variations": "- Deal-All First: Deal all cards to the row first, then start moving. - Play-As-You-Go: Deal one card at a time; resolve moves before dealing next. Original style, exceedingly difficult. - Queen and Her Lad: Jones 1892. Q♥ as commencing card, J♥ at bottom of stock. Discard cards between two of same suit/rank. - Royal Marriage: Reduce deck to King and Queen of same suit, placed at start and end of layout. - Nidgi Novgorod: 1904 Standard Hoyle. Two equal-rank cards at top/bottom of pack; deal 4 in row; discard inner cards when outer match."
