    "overview": "Ace-To-Five Lowball is a draw poker variant in which the lowest hand wins. Aces are low, and straights and flushes do not count against a low hand. Origin: United States Players: 2-8 Deck: Standard 52-card deck (some tables allow one joker as a bug) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-20 minutes per hand",
    "history": "Ace-to-five lowball developed in U.S. card rooms and became a core lowball form, especially in California. - 20th century: California draw lowball rooms popularize ace-to-five ranking. - Modern mixed games: Ace-to-five appears in single-draw and triple-draw formats. Compared with ace-to-six and deuce-to-seven, this form removes straight/flush penalties, making wheel-building central. A foundational lowball game in dealer's choice and mixed poker lineups.",
    "setup": "Players: Two to eight players; the game is similar to Five Card Draw with one draw round. Typically played with blinds (dealer, small blind, big blind) or an ante; best with about six at the table. Deck: Standard 52-card deck; optional joker bug in some house rules. Equipment: Deck, chips, dealer button, and blind markers if blind structure is used. Dealing: Deal 5 cards face-down to each player.",
    "rules": "Objective: Make the lowest five-card hand by ace-to-five ranking. Gameplay: Players contribute forced bets (ante or blinds), receive five down cards, complete first betting, perform a single draw (discard 0-5 then draw equal replacements, or stand pat), complete final betting, and go to showdown. Scoring: Pot game; best low hand wins the pot at showdown. - Aces are always low. - Straights and flushes are ignored for low ranking. - Best hand is A-2-3-4-5 (wheel). - Hands are compared from highest card downward among low hands. - In some California rules, made sevens may be forced to bet. Win condition: Lowest valid ace-to-five hand at showdown wins.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prefer smooth lows and avoid drawing to rough one-card gutters. Intermediate: Use opponent draw counts to estimate made hands versus drawing hands. Advanced: Apply pressure when pat, and represent made lows in heads-up final betting spots. - Tip: A-2-3 starts are strong; rough 9-low draws are often traps. - Tip: A pat hand can be value or bluff depending on prior draw pattern.",
    "variations": "- Single Draw: One draw round and two main betting rounds. - Triple Draw: Three draw rounds with additional betting rounds."
