    "history": "Believed to have been introduced to East Africa by Portuguese sailors or traders, Albastini (corruption of 'Al-Bisca' or similar) evolved into a distinct Tanzanian game with localized terminology like 'Mrithi' and 'Ndoa'. - 16th-18th Century: Portuguese Bisca enters East African ports (Zanzibar, Dar es Salaam). - Late 20th Century: Formalized local rules documented in Tanzania. - 2010s: Digital versions by local developers (like Augustino Mponzi) popularize it in the app stores. While Bisca required following suit or trumping in its Italian/Portuguese origins, Albastini dropped this constraint, allowing for deeper 'sacrifice' plays. Commonly played in 'vijiwe' (social meeting points) in Tanzania. Winning a trick is called 'Kula' (eating).",
    "setup": "Players: 2, 3, or teams of 2 (4 players) or 3 (6 players). Deck: 36-card standard (Remove 2s, 8s, 9s, 10s). Equipment: Standard modified deck, score sheet. Dealing: 5 cards dealt to each player. Remaining cards form the stock. Dealer is chosen by agreement.",
    "rules": "Objective: Capture tricks containing high-value cards to surpass 60 points (out of 120 total). Gameplay: 1. **Bidding (Otea)**: Dealer's opponents place one card face-up. This signifies their desire for that suit to be trumps. 2. **Trump Flip**: Dealer turns up the top card of the stock. This suit becomes trump. If an opponent bid that suit, they swap their bid card for the trump card. 3. **The Play**: Lead any card. Others play any card (no follow-suit requirement). 4. **Winning**: Highest trump wins. If no trump, highest card of the led suit wins. 5. **Drawing**: Winner 'eats' the trick, draws a new card from the stock first, then others draw. Lead next. 6. **Endings**: Play continues until stock and hands are empty. Scoring: Scoring is based on the total 120 card points and 'Victory Points' (VP). - Card Values: Ace=11, 7=10, King=4, Jack=3, Queen=2. Total in deck = 120. - Ndoa...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Don't waste your trumps early. Use low-value cards (6, 5, 4, 3) to probe your opponents and safe-guard your points. Intermediate: Strategic Passing: Since you don't have to follow suit, use your point-cards (Ace/7) to win tricks that contain high-value discards from your partner. Advanced: Card Counting: There are only 36 cards. If you track the fallen 10s and face cards, you can calculate precisely when it's safe to drop your 7s. - Tip: The '7' is the second highest card in Albastini—it is extremely valuable and should be protected/hunted. - Tip: Always try to win the second-to-last trick to draw the face-up trump card.",
