    "history": "Rooted in the Spanish Brisca tradition, Alcalde evolved in Puerto Rico as a social game where the leader is challenged by a temporary alliance. Its name, 'Alcalde', reflects the local governance structure where the 'Mayor' must satisfy the points to retain their office. - 1800s: Brisca becomes the dominant trick-taking game in Puerto Rico. - Early 1900s: Asymmetrical variants for odd player counts (3 or 5) emerge. - 1990s: Formal rules documented by José M. Carrillo-Muniz. Transitioned from a 4-player partnership game to a '1 vs 2' format where the single player is compensated with larger hand size and draw priority. Commonly played in plazas and community centers in Puerto Rico. The 'Alcalde' role is often rotated, symbolizing the fleeting nature of political power.",
