    "history": "Part of the oldest surviving family of European card games, Alkort was preserved in the isolation of Iceland for centuries. It shares DNA with the German Karnöffel and Swiss Kaiserspiel. - 1726-1768: Referenced by Eggert Ólafsson as a native Icelandic pastime. - 1880s: Detailed rules published by Ólafur Davíðsson. - 1989: Modern Icelandic standard finalized in 'Spilabók AB'. Has changed little since the 18th century, maintaining its complex 'Stock Exchange' rule and the 'Stroke' scoring system. Regarded as a test of memory and partnership intuition. The term 'Bísefarnir' (Sevens) is a unique Icelandic card-game jargon.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two fixed partnerships (North-South vs East-West). Deck: 44 cards (52 minus the 10s and 5s). Ace is high, but rankings are non-linear. Equipment: 44-card deck, scoreboard. Dealing: Dealer gives 9 cards to each player in batches of 3. The remaining 8 cards form a face-down stock (hidden).",
    "rules": "Objective: Win at least 5 tricks (out of 9) to score. Bonus points for 'Múk' (first 5 tricks) or a 'Stroke' (uninterrupted run of tricks). Gameplay: 1. **Showing**: Partners show each other their highest-ranking card before play starts. 2. **Friðufær Check**: If a player has no cards high enough to beat an 8, they can show their hand and swap it for the 8-card stock. 3. **The Trick**: Play is clockwise. There is NO requirement to follow suit. 4. **Ties**: If two cards of equal rank are played (e.g., two Jacks), the first one played wins the trick. 5. **Leading Sevens**: You cannot lead a Seven until you have won at least one trick previously. Scoring: Points awarded at the end of each deal. - Hierarchy: K♦ is top. Then 'The Honors' (2♥, 4♣, 8♠, 9♥, 9♦). Then Aces, then Jacks, then Sixes (Póstarnir), then Eights (Friðurnar). - Rubbish: Kings (except K♦), Queens, and low numbers (2, 3,...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Save your 'Honors' (2♥, 4♣, etc.) to capture opponent High Cards. Don't waste your Sevens; they are your absolute weapons for leading when you are sure to win. Intermediate: If you have a terrible hand, use the 'Friðufær' rule to swap with the stock—it's often better than playing dead cards. Advanced: Coordinate with your partner's high card (shown at start). If your partner has the K♦, play defensively to let them win the lead, then protect their leads with rubbish. - Tip: A Jack is often stronger than an Ace in this game because of the specific Karnöffel ordering. - Tip: Count the Eights. If they are gone, your Sixes (Póstarnir) become the masters of the board.",
