    "history": "Developed as a point-heavy variant of All Fours in the 19th century. Is often confused with the domino game of the same name, though they are entirely unrelated in mechanics. - 1800s: All Fives diverges from Seven-Up/All Fours in the United States. - 1900s: Becomes a popular gambling variant where point-capture requires tighter trump management. - Present: Still played as a retro variant of the All Fours family. Replaces the 'High, Low, Jack, Game' points of All Fours with a fixed point schedule for specific cards (A, K, Q, J, 10, 5 of trumps). Often played in American south and rural England as a faster, more score-dense version of traditional trick games.",
    "setup": "Players: 2 or 3 (individual play). Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Equipment: Standard deck, score tracking to 61. Dealing: Dealer gives 6 cards to each player. The next card is turned up for trumps.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score 61 points by winning tricks containing the Ace, King, Queen, Jack, 10, or 5 of the trump suit. Gameplay: 1. **Trump Determination**: Eldest hand can 'Stand' or 'Beg'. If they beg, the dealer either gifts 1 point to the opponent or deals 3 more cards to each player to find a new trump suit. 2. **Trick Play**: Winner of the lead (or eldest) leads the first trick. Players must follow suit if they can, or they may trump. 3. **Winning**: Highest trump wins. If no trumps, the highest card of the led suit wins. 4. **Point Capture**: Points are ONLY awarded for the following cards IN THE TRUMP SUIT: - **Ten**: 10 points - **Five**: 5 points - **Ace**: 4 points - **King**: 3 points - **Queen**: 2 points - **Jack**: 1 point 5. **Total**: There are 25 total points available in each hand. The first to reach 61 wins. Scoring: Points are scored immediately upon winning a trick...",
    "strategy": "Basic: If you hold the 10 or 5 of trumps, do not lead them! Wait until a higher trump (Ace or King) has been played so you can safely drop them on a winning trick. Intermediate: Begging is more common in All Fives than All Fours because you need a high-point trump suit to score effectively. If the turned-up trump is a suit you are short in, always beg. Advanced: Defensive Trumping: If an opponent leads a high non-trump suit (e.g., Ace of Spades) and you suspect they have the point-trumps, trump early to 'steal' the lead and force them to play their trumps out of order. - Tip: The 5 of trumps is a huge prize. It often gets lost in early tricks; keep track of it. - Tip: If you have the 10 and the Ace of trumps, you have a guaranteed 14 points if you lead correctly.",
