    "history": "All Fours emerged in England in the 17th century and later spread to North America and the Caribbean. - 1674: Charles Cotton records an early two-player form. - 19th century: North American Old Sledge / Seven Up forms become common. - 20th-21st century: Trinidad partnership form becomes the best-known living version, while Yorkshire and Lancashire forms continue in England. Many descendants branch from the same High-Low-Jack-Game core: Pitch, Pedro, and regional All Fours family games. All Fours is considered a heritage game in Trinidad and Tobago and remains a pub/league game in parts of northern England.",
