    "rules": "Objective: Reach the target score by collecting High, Low, Jack, and Game points each hand. Gameplay: After six cards each and a turned trump indicator, eldest hand may stand or beg. If begging is accepted, dealer runs three more cards each and turns again until trump suit changes. In play, highest trump wins a trick; if no trump is played, highest card of the led suit wins. Players with the led suit may either follow suit or play trump, but cannot throw a non-trump card of another suit when they could follow. Score hand points in order High, Low, Jack, Game; first side to target wins the game. Scoring: Each hand can award up to four core points: High, Low, Jack, and Game. - High: point to side originally holding highest trump dealt. - Low: point to side originally holding lowest trump dealt. - Jack: point if your side wins trick containing jack of trumps (hang-jack variants award...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Secure High and Low early by controlling trump suit length and timing. Intermediate: Protect or attack the Jack of trumps; that point often decides hands near the finish. Advanced: Track tens and honors in captured tricks to manage the Game point race even when trump control is split. - Tip: Do not spend top trump too early if Jack is still unseen. - Tip: Game point math is ten-heavy; saving tens in winning tricks is critical.",
    "variations": "- Trinidad All Fours: Anticlockwise play, beg/take-one flow, target commonly 14, with hang-jack convention in many tables. - West Yorkshire All Fours: Pitch-led trump (no turn-up), scoring target 11, and regional partnership signaling/throw-in conventions. - Lancashire All Fours: Related to Yorkshire form, but Low can be awarded to trick winner of the low trump. - North American Seven Up / Old Sledge: Clockwise forms with gift/run options, often to 7 or 10, and tie conventions for Game point. - 17th-Century All Fours: Two-player historical form from Cotton with related run-card and scoring details."
