    "history": "Part of the North of England's deep-rooted pub game culture. While Yorkshire developed its own league standards, Lancashire refined the 'Low' rule to make the game more aggressive and trick-focused. - 1800s: All Fours becomes a staple in Northern English pubs. - 1970s: Documented by Arthur Taylor in 'Pub Games' as a living tradition in Blackburn. - Present: Still played in localized pub leagues and social clubs. The removal of 'The Beg' and the shift of the 'Low' point to a 'captured' status are the defining evolutionary steps that distinguish it from the 17th-century progenitor. In Blackburn, the first lead is often decided by a 'Red or Black' guess after the cut. It's a game of heavy social interaction and 'pegging' on boards.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two fixed partnerships (North-South vs East-West). Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Ace is high. Equipment: Standard deck, All Fours pegging board (traditionally to 11 points). Dealing: Each player receives 6 cards in batches of 2 or 3. No card is turned up for trumps.",
    "rules": "Objective: First partnership to 11 points by scoring High, Low (taken), Jack, and Game. Gameplay: 1. **The Pitch**: The player to the dealer's left (the Pitcher) leads any card. The suit of this card immediately becomes trumps for the hand. 2. **Trick Play**: Follow suit if possible. You may trump instead of following suit, even if you hold a card of the led suit. 3. **The 'Low' Hunt**: Unlike other variants, the 'Low' point is awarded to the team that WINS the trick containing the lowest trump card that was dealt in that hand. 4. **Scoring Order**: Points are counted and pegged in the following order: High, Low, Jack, Game. Scoring: Pegging points immediately at the end of the hand. - High (1 pt): Awarded to the team of the player dealt the highest trump. - Low (1 pt): Awarded to the team that WINS the trick containing the lowest trump card dealt. - Jack (1 pt): Awarded to the team...",
    "strategy": "Basic: If you hold a Ten, try to throw it on your partner's winning trick. If you hold the Jack of trumps, hide it until you are sure your partner can protect it. Intermediate: The Low Strategy: Since 'Low' is taken, not dealt, you must win the trick it's in. If you have the 2 of trumps, don't lead it; hold it as an escape card or wait until you can win a trick with a higher trump to secure it. Advanced: Pitch Manipulation: If you are the Pitcher and hold the Ace and Ten of a suit, lead the Ace. You've fixed the trumps and secured 4 card-points toward 'Game' in one move. - Tip: Always count the trumps played. With only 24 cards dealt, knowing the remaining trumps is easier than in other games. - Tip: In Lancashire, winning the Low point (the 'Taken Low') is often the difference between winning and losing a match.",
