    "history": "Brought to the Caribbean by English colonial administrators, the game took deep root in Trinidadian culture, evolving from a simple tavern game into a sophisticated sport with national tournaments. - 1800s: Traditional English All Fours is introduced to Trinidad. - Early 1900s: Rules shift to an anti-clockwise direction and a 14-point target. - Present: A central part of social life, played in community centers and sanctioned leagues across the island. The most significant Caribbean innovations were the 'Hang Jack' bonus (3 points) and the immediate scoring of turn-up cards (Ace, Six, Jack). All Fours in Trinidad is noted for its high-tension atmosphere and loud, physical card-playing style (often 'slamming' cards for emphasis).",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two partnerships (sitting opposite). Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Ace is high. Equipment: Standard deck, 14-point pegboard. Dealing: Deal and play are STRICTLY anti-clockwise. Each player receives 6 cards (usually dealt 3 at a time). The 25th card is turned up for trumps.",
