    "history": "Rooted in the industrial heartlands of Yorkshire, the game evolved alongside the textile mills. It became a formalized league sport in the mid-20th century. - 1800s: Traditional All Fours is adopted in Yorkshire taverns. - 1950s: Formal leagues (like the Heckmondwike league) establish standardized regional rules. - 2003: Documented extensively by card historians John and Anita Dunning. Yorkshire play stabilized around an 11-point target and the 'Low-to-Holder' rule, specifically rejecting the Lancashire 'Low-is-Taken' innovation. Matches are traditionally held in 'Tap Rooms' of pubs. The game is known for its steady, methodical pace and strict observance of 'The Pitch'.",
