    "history": "Alsos emerged in Budapest in the late 19th century and remained popular through the first half of the 20th century. - 1880s: Developed as a competitive alternative to simpler Jass games. - 1930s: Standardized in G.J. Potter's card game manuals. - Mid-20th Century: Reached peak popularity in cafes and clubs across Hungary. It evolved from Kalaber-style Ace-Ten play by adopting announced figures from Tarokk, adding heavy bidding and kontra depth. Regarded as one of the most intellectually stimulating games of central Europe, often compared to Bridge in its depth.",
    "setup": "Players: 3 active players. With 4 players, dealer sits out each hand. Deck: 32 cards (A, K, Over, Under, 10, 9, 8, 7). Usually Hungarian suits (Acorns, Hearts, Leaves, Bells). Equipment: Hungarian deck, complex scoring sheet. Dealing: Each player receives 9 cards (dealt in batches of 3-3-3). 5 cards remain in the 'Talon' (one face up to propose trump).",
    "rules": "Objective: As declarer, score more points through tricks, melds, and 'figures' than the highest-scoring opponent. Gameplay: 1. **Bidding**: Players bid to accept the turned-up trump or name a new suit/game (Betli, Klopitzky). 2. **Melding**: Before the first trick, players announce 'sequences' and 'Vannak' (four of a kind). 3. **The Play**: Declarer competes against the two opponents (who cooperate). 4. **Figures**: During play, players may announce 'Ultimo' (winning last trick with trump 7) or 'Volat' (winning all tricks). Scoring: Total points come from card values, melds, and figures. - Trump Rank: Under (Alsos) highest, then 9, A, 10, K, Over, 8, 7. - No-Trump Rank: A, 10, K, Over, Under, 9, 8, 7. - Kontra: Opponents can double the score if they feel the declarer will lose. Declarer can 'Rekontra' to quadruple. - Last Trick: Worth 10 bonus points. Win condition: The declarer wins...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Track the trump Under and 9 closely; those two cards control many contracts. Intermediate: When legal, use talon exchange carefully to convert weak cards into suit control before trick play. Advanced: Ultimo timing is critical: if opponents can force out your trumps early, last-trick plans collapse. - Tip: Cooperation is key for opponents: if one partner has a weak hand, they should sacrifice high cards to their partner's tricks to ensure the declarer is outscored. - Tip: Betli (losing all tricks) is a high-reward defensive bid if you are dealt a hand with many 7s and 8s.",
