    "overview": "Anrufen (meaning 'to call') is an elegant Austrian trick-taking game belonging to the Marriage family. It is distinguished by its 'Call-a-Card' partner selection and a simplified scoring system where only 'Gwisse' (Aces, Tens, and the Last Trick) carry numerical value. It combines the secret partnerships of bridge with the point-collecting mechanics of MariÃ¡Å¡. Origin: Austria Players: 4 Deck: 24 cards (A-10-K-O-U-9) or (A-10-K-Q-J-9) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 15-30 minutes",
    "history": "A descendant of the 18th-century French game 'Mariage', which spread across the Austro-Hungarian Empire. The Austrian version settled into a 24-card format common in the Salzburg and Alpine regions. - 1700s: Mariage becomes a court favorite in Europe. - 1850s: Austrian regional versions (Anrufen, KÃ¶nigrufen) diverge from the Czech MariÃ¡Å¡. - 1930s: Documented as a staple of Alpine tavern play. The game simplified the complex point-counting of 32-card games to prioritize 'Marriages' and the pivotal 'Last Trick'. Commonly played for small stakes or tokens in Austrian 'GasthÃ¤usern'. The 'Call' is often made with great ceremony to reveal the secret partner.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two secret partnerships. Deck: 24 cards. Using French suits (A-10-K-Q-J-9) or German suits (A-10-K-O-U-9). Equipment: Standard 32-card deck (remove 7s and 8s), score sheet or tokens. Dealing: Each player is dealt 6 cards in batches of 3-3. Bidding rotates clockwise starting from the dealer's left.",
    "rules": "Objective: As a member of the bidding side, capture 'Gwisse' points and declare 'Marriages' to reach the target of 50 points (or more for higher bids). Gameplay: 1. **Bidding**: Players bid in levels: Simple (50+), 100 Points, All Tricks, or No Tricks. 2. **Calling**: The high bidder names a card (e.g., 'Ace of Hearts'). The holder is their partner. The suit of that card is TRUMP. 3. **The Play**: Bidder leads the first trick. Players MUST follow suit. If void, they MUST trump. If possible, they must head the trick (win it). 4. **Marriages**: A player holding the King and Queen (or Over) of a suit can declare a 'Marriage' when leading the first card of that hand. 40 points for trump marriage, 20 for others. 5. **Last Trick**: Worth 10 bonus points. Scoring: Total points are 90 (8 Aces/Tens + Last Trick) plus any marriage bonuses. - Follow-Suit & Must-Trump: Strict trick-taking rules...",
    "strategy": "Basic: If you hold many trumps but lack the Ace, call your own Ace's suit to ensure your partner has a powerful entry card. Intermediate: Last Trick Management: Save your highest trump (usually the Ace) for the final trick, as capturing it is worth the same value as an entire Ace or Ten. Advanced: Defensive Marriage Spoiling: If you suspect an opponent has a Marriage, lead that suit early to force them to play one of the marriage cards defensively before they can declare it. - Tip: Always count the 'Gwisse'. There are only 8 counting cards in the deck; knowing who has taken three of them is key. - Tip: In 'Simple' games, your priority is the 50-point threshold. Don't take unnecessary risks for extra tricks.",
    "variations": "- Graden Style: No 'Simple' bid allowed. If all pass, the hand is played as 'Schurln' (lowest score loses). - 3-Player Anrufen: 7 cards each and a 3-card talon. High bidder plays alone against two."
