    "overview": "Arizona 29-card Pitch is a specialized regional evolution of the classic American game Pitch (Setback). It uses a condensed 29-card deck and incorporates an unbeatable Joker into the trump hierarchy. Played in partnerships, it rewards both the capture of legendary cards (High, Low, Jack) and the strategic management of a 5-card kitty. Origin: USA (Arizona) Players: 4 (Partnerships) Deck: 29 cards (A,K,Q,J,10,9,6 of all suits + 1 Joker) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 15-30 minutes",
    "history": "Developed in the Southwestern United States as a faster, more intense version of the standard 52-card Partnership Pitch. The use of the '6' as the low card instead of the '2' is a regional hallmark. - 1800s: Pitch (Setback) becomes popular in back-woods America. - mid-20th C.: Arizona players standardize the 29-card 'short deck' format. - Present: Remains a popular 'Social Club' game in Arizona and neighboring states. The transition to a 29-card deck was likely an adaptation to make the Joker (a common 53rd card) more central to the gameplay, as it acts as a 'Middle-Trump'. In Arizona, this game is often preferred over standard 'Seven Up' for its predictive bidding and the power of the 'Kitty' exchange.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two partnerships (North-South vs East-West). Deck: 29 cards: Ace, King, Queen, Jack, Ten, Nine, Six of each suit, plus one Joker. Remove 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, and 8 from a standard deck. Equipment: Standard pack (modified), score sheet. Dealing: Each player is dealt 6 cards in batches of 3. The 5 remaining cards are placed face down as the 'Kitty'.",
    "rules": "Objective: The bidding team must earn at least as many points as they bid. Points are scored for High, Low, Jack, Joker, and Game. Gameplay: 1. **Bidding**: Starting left of the dealer, players bid for the right to 'Pitch' (name trump). Bids range from 1 to 5. 2. **The Kitty**: The high bidder takes the 5-card kitty and discards 5 cards to return to a 6-card hand. 3. **The Pitch**: The high bidder leads the first card; the suit of this card is trumps. 4. **Trump Hierarchy**: A (High), K, Q, J, Joker, 10, 9, 6 (Low). 5. **Trick Play**: Must follow suit if possible; else any card. Highest trump wins the trick; if no trump, highest card of led suit wins. 6. **Scoring**: At hand's end, count points. Scoring: Pitch uses a 'Setback' scoring system where failing a bid subtracts from your total. - High (1 pt): Winning or holding the highest trump in play (Ace). - Low (1 pt): Winning or holding...",
    "strategy": "Basic: If you win the bid, use the kitty to 'flush' your hand of non-trump suits. Keeping only trumps and high cards (Aces/Tens) is the path to winning the 'Game' point. Intermediate: Trump Pulling: If you have the Ace of trumps, lead it early to draw out the opponents' Joker and Jack. This 'purifies' the remaining trumps for your partner. Advanced: Defense: If the opponents bid 5 (the maximum), your only goal is to 'Set' them by stealing just one of the 5 points. Focus your play on capturing the Joker or the Jack specifically. - Tip: The Nine and Six are often overlooked, but they contribute to the 'Game' point count if they capture a Ten. - Tip: Joker Management: The Joker is a 'wild' trump that ranks between the Jack and the Ten. Use it to kill a King or Queen lead if the Jack is already gone.",
    "variations": "- Standard Partnership Pitch: Played with the full 52-card deck (minus Joker). High to Low is A to 2. - Flat Pitch: No kitty is dealt; players play only with the 6 cards originally dealt."
