    "history": "Developed in Italy as a simplified or faster variation of Scopa. The name explicitly translates to 'Ace Takes All' (Pigliatutto). It is often taught to children as a gateway to standard Scopa because the Ace rule provides exciting, easy captures. - 1800s: Scopa evolves from earlier fishing games like Primiera. - 1900s: Asso Pigliatutto emerges as a popular regional variant, particularly in Southern Italy. - Present: Widely played in Italian cafes and digital apps as 'Scopa d'Assi'. The introduction of the 'Ace Sweep' mechanic reduced the mental math required for sweeping the table, making the game faster and more aggressive. A staple of Italian social life. 'Scaracoccia' is a regional name used in some dialects.",
