    "history": "A derivative of the standard 'Draw' domino game, adding elements of poker-style betting and auction theory. Likely developed in tavern settings where gambling on dominoes was a common pastime, and documented in English domino circles. - Unknown: Traditional Draw dominoes establishes the core matching mechanic. - 1900s: Gambling variants like Auction emerge, adding chip pools to social games. - Present: Popular in regions where dominoes remains a primary social competition. The transition from score-based winning to pool-based winning allows for shorter, more intense rounds. Used as a 'session game' where players can drop in and out of the pool between rounds.",
    "setup": "Players: 2-4 players. Each starts with an equal stack of chips. Deck: Standard Double-Six set (28 tiles, 0-0 to 6-6). Equipment: Domino set, poker chips, table. Dealing: 2-3 players: 7 tiles each. 4 players: 6 tiles each. Remaining tiles form the 'Boneyard'.",
