    "releaseStatus": "WorkInProgress",
    "overview": "Australian Patience is a strategic solitaire game that blends the layout of Klondike with the move-logic of Scorpion. It is characterized by having all tableau cards face-up from the start, requiring the player to build down in suit. Unlike Klondike, any cards sitting on top of a card being moved are carried along, making it a game of foresight and column management. Origin: Australia Players: 1 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-20 minutes",
    "history": "Originating in Australia as a more skill-based alternative to Klondike. It gained international popularity in the digital era as part of high-difficulty solitaire collections. - Early 20th Century: Variations of open-layout solitaire emerge in the UK and Commonwealth. - 1990s: Inclusion in early computer solitaire packages alongside Scorpion and Yukon. - Present: Recognized as one of the 'top-tier' strategic solitaires due to its low luck factor compared to Klondike. The game simplifies the 'hidden card' mechanic of Klondike by making everything visible, which shifts the challenge from discovery to optimization. Favored by competitive solitaire players who enjoy 'solvable' puzzles where defeat is usually the result of a misplay rather than a bad shuffle.",
    "setup": "Players: One player for the standard solitaire game. The player shuffles and deals their own tableau; there is no dealer or opponent. Australian Patience can also be played competitively by two players (each with their own 52-card pack and tableau), but the primary form documented here is single-player. Deck: Standard 52 cards (Aces low). Equipment: Standard pack, ample table surface. Dealing: 7 columns of 4 cards are dealt face-up, slightly overlapped. This uses 28 cards. The remaining 24 cards form the draw pile (stock).",
    "rules": "Objective: Move all 52 cards to the four foundation piles, organized by suit from Ace to King. Gameplay: 1. Deal: 7 columns of 4 cards each, face-up (28 cards). Stock = 24 cards. 2. Play: Build tableau down in suit only. Move cards (and any cards on top) to other columns or to foundations. Flip stock one at a time to waste; top of waste is playable. 3. Foundations: Aces up in suit (A-2-3...K). 4. Win when all 52 cards are on foundations. Scoring: Standard solitaire scoring (points per card moved to foundation) or simple win/loss. - Suit Building: You MUST follow suit in the tableau (unlike Klondike's alternating colors). - Visibility: All 28 starting cards are visible; use this to plan 3-4 moves ahead. - No Redeal: If the stock is empty and no moves remain, the game is lost. Win condition: Clearing the tableau and stock into the foundations.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize exposing and moving Aces to the foundations immediately. Without your Aces, you cannot clear any cards. Intermediate: Column Management: Avoid creating empty columns unless you have a King ready to move into it. An empty column with no King is a wasted space that could have held card sequences. Advanced: Look-Ahead Analysis: Since all cards are face-up, identify 'Blocks'. For example, if the 5 of Hearts is trapped under the 7 of Hearts, you must find a way to move the 7 before you can ever play that 5. - Tip: The 'No Redeal' rule makes every card in the waste pile precious. Don't flip a stock card if you have a viable move on the tableau. - Tip: Try to keep your tableau sequences 'pure' (uninterrupted suit order) to make them easier to move later.",
    "variations": "- Competitive Australian: Two players play with their own decks. The first to clear their foundations wins."
