    "overview": "Austrian Dominoes is a unique European variant traditionally played with a Double-Eight set. Its most striking features are the complete removal of all double tiles and a 'brick-layer' tableau formation where tiles are matched side-by-side rather than end-to-end. It is a strategic draw game where players value 'heavier' tiles early in the round. Origin: Austria Players: 2-4 Deck: 36 tiles (Double-8 set minus all doubles) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 15-30 minutes",
    "history": "Documented in Vienna as early as 1830, Austrian Dominoes reflects the Central European preference for Double-Eight sets over the more common Double-Six set used in the West. - 1830: F. Tendler publishes 'Verstand und GlÃ¼ck im Bunde', describing the game with 45 tiles. - 1960: Fritz Beck standardizes the 36-tile 'No-Double' variant in 'Domino in vielen Spielarten'. - Present: Remains a traditional past-time in Austrian card clubs and cafes. The removal of doubles removes the 'spinners' and the high-point anchors typical of Caribbean or American games, shifting focus to pure numerical matching. Often played in a 'Buki' format (4 players) in rural Austria.",
    "setup": "Players: 2-3 players (Draw game) or 4 players (Block game). Deck: 36 tiles. To prepare, take a Double-Eight set (45 tiles) and discard all 9 doubles (0-0, 1-1... up to 8-8). Equipment: Domino set, score sheet. Dealing: 2 players: 8-9 tiles each. 3 players: 7 tiles each. 4 players: 8 tiles each. Remaining tiles form the 'Stamm' (Boneyard).",
    "rules": "Objective: To be the first player to reach 100 points across multiple rounds. Gameplay: 1. **Leading**: The player with the heaviest tile (8-7 being the heaviest in this set) leads first. 2. **Matching**: On your turn, you must place a tile where one of its ends matches an available end on the board. 3. **The Draw**: If you cannot play, you must draw from the boneyard until you find a playable tile. (Common house rule: maximum of 3 draws). 4. **Passing**: If the boneyard is empty and you cannot play, you pass. 5. **Ending**: A round ends when one player 'Dominoes' (empties their hand) or when the game is blocked. Scoring: Round-based point accumulation. - No Doubles: The absence of doubles means every tile is distinct and has two different numbers. - The 8-7 Lead: The highest tile in play (8-7) always starts the first hand. - Brick Formation: The tableau grows in two directions from...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep a variety of numbers in your hand. Since there are no doubles, you don't have to worry about 'blocking' your own suits in the same way. Intermediate: High-Pip Management: Early in the game, try to play your 8s and 7s. If someone else dominoes, you don't want to be caught with high-counting tiles in your hand. Advanced: Boneyard Counting: Since there are only 36 tiles and you know exactly which ones are missing (the doubles), you can more accurately track which tiles are left in the boneyard vs in opponents' hands. - Tip: If you are playing the 4-player block variant, remember that there is no boneyard. Information is perfect if you can track all 36 tiles. - Tip: The 'Brick' layout can sometimes be confusing; visualize the ends as 'slots' that must match.",
    "variations": "- Buki: A 4-player block version where all titles are dealt out and no drawing is allowed."
