    "releaseStatus": "WorkInProgress",
    "overview": "Authors is a classic memory-based card game where players compete to collect complete sets (books) of four cards of the same rank. Unlike 'Go Fish,' where players ask for a whole rank, Authors requires the precision of asking for a specific card by both rank and suit. It rewards keen observation and a sharp memory of which cards have been requested and by whom. Origin: Salem, Massachusetts, United States Players: 3-6 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Beginner Duration: 15-20 minutes",
    "history": "Originally published in 1861 by G.M. Whipple and A.A. Smith in Salem, Massachusetts. The original set featured cards with names of famous authors and their book titles (e.g., Charles Dickens and 'Oliver Twist'). - 1861: The game 'Authors' is first published in the USA. - Late 1800s: Becomes a standard 'improving' educational game for children. - 1900s: Adapted to the standard 52-card deck, where 'Books' became card ranks instead of literary works. - Present: Still popular in Europe as 'Quartets' or 'Happy Families'. The transition from specifically printed 'Author' sets to the standard deck allowed it to become a universal parlor game. Used historically to teach literature and history; now primarily a foundational game for building children's logic and memory skills.",
    "setup": "Players: 3-6 players (best with 4-5). Deck: Standard 52 cards. Equipment: Standard pack, score sheet for counting books. Dealing: The entire deck is dealt out as evenly as possible. Some players may have one more card than others.",
    "rules": "Objective: Capture the most 'Books' (sets of all four cards of a single rank). Gameplay: 1. **Inquiry**: On your turn, you ask a specific player for a specific card (rank and suit), for example: 'Sarah, do you have the Nine of Diamonds?'. 2. **Prerequisite**: You can only ask for a card if you already hold at least one card of that same rank. 3. **The Exchange**: If the player has the card, they MUST hand it over. You then get another turn to ask anyone for another card. 4. **The End of Turn**: If the player does not have the card, your turn ends immediately and passes to the left. 5. **Completing a Book**: As soon as you hold all four suits of a rank, you must lay them face down on the table as a 'Book'. Scoring: Each book is worth 1 point. - No Lying: If asked for a card you hold, you must give it up immediately. - Rank Constraint: You cannot ask for a card of a rank you do not have....",
    "strategy": "Basic: Remember what other people ask for. If Tom asks Sarah for the Jack of Spades and Sarah doesn't have it, you now know Tom has a Jack and Sarah does not have the Jack of Spades. Intermediate: The Bluff: If you hold three Kings and need the fourth, ask someone who you know DOESN'T have it to end your turn, making others think you are weak on Kings. (Risky, as someone might target you next). Advanced: Elimination Mapping: Mentally track every revealed suit of a rank. If you see the Jack of Spades and Jack of Hearts go to one player, and you hold the Jack of Clubs, you only need to find the Jack of Diamonds to swoop the whole set. - Tip: Pay attention even when it's not your turn. The information revealed during other people's turns is essential for winning. - Tip: Collect 'quietly'. Don't ask for the same rank three times in a row unless you are sure you can complete the book on...",
    "variations": "- Go Fish Style: Played with a stock (boneyard). If the player doesn't have the card, you 'fish' from the deck. - Literature: A much more complex version played in teams where you ask for ranks from teammates and opponents."
