    "overview": "Badugi is a unique and fascinating poker variant characterized by its triple-draw mechanic and four-card hand structure. Unlike traditional poker, where flushes and straights are valued, Badugi is a 'lowball' game that penalizes matching suits and ranks. The ultimate goal is to hold a 'Badugi'â€”four cards of distinct suits and distinct ranks, with the lowest possible values. It's a game of deception, probability, and ruthless discarded-card tracking. Origin: South Korea / Canada Players: 2-8 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 15-30 minutes",
    "history": "The exact origins are debated, with strong claims from both South Korea (where the name 'Badugi' refers to a black-and-white spotted dog) and Canada (where it was played as 'Off Suit Lowball' in the 1980s). - 1980s: Variations appear in Canadian pool halls and South Korean social clubs. - 2000s: Introduced to online poker platforms, gaining a cult following. - 2008: Added to the World Series of Poker (WSOP) as part of mixed games. - Present: A staple in high-stakes '8-Game' and 'H.O.R.S.E.' mixes. Badugi evolved from 5-card triple draw by stripping away the fifth card to make achieving a 'four-suit' hand statistically challenging and rewarding. In Korea, the game is a popular television and online gambling pastime.",
    "setup": "Players: 2-8 players. Typically played with a dealer button and small/big blinds. Deck: Standard 52 cards. Aces are always LOW. Equipment: Standard pack, poker chips, dealer button. Dealing: Each player is dealt 4 cards face down.",
    "rules": "Objective: Capture the pot by holding the lowest qualifying hand at showdown. A 4-card 'Badugi' (all different suits/ranks) is the highest tier of hand. Gameplay: 1. **First Betting Round**: Following the blinds. 2. **First Draw**: Players discard any number of cards (0-4) and receive replacements. 3. **Second Betting Round**. 4. **Second Draw**. 5. **Third Betting Round**. 6. **Third Draw**. 7. **Final Betting Round**. 8. **Showdown**. Scoring: Pot-based betting. No point-scoring. - No Suits Rank: All suits are of equal value for winning purposes. - Aces Low: The Ace is the best (lowest) card. - The 'Nuts': The best hand is A-2-3-4 of four different suits. Win condition: Lowest hand at showdown or all other players fold.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize 'Badugi' potential. If you are dealt 3 cards of the same suit, you MUST discard two of them, leaving you with a very weak 2-card hand. Intermediate: Snowing (Bluffing): In a triple draw game, you can 'Pat' (discard 0 cards) to make opponents think you already have a powerful Badugi, forcing them to fold or play defensively. Advanced: Range Analysis: If an opponent draws only 1 card on the first draw and then stands pat on the second, they almost certainly have a completed Badugi. If that Badugi is an 8-high or better, you should fold unless you are drawing to a 7-high or better. - Tip: Don't fall in love with a King-high Badugi. It might be a 4-card hand, but it is easily beaten by a 3-card hand that happens to be A-2-3. - Tip: Pay attention to 'Board Cards' (what you have discarded). If you've discarded three Aces, your chances of drawing an Ace-high Badugi are...",
