    "releaseStatus": "WorkInProgress",
    "overview": "Bai Fen (literally 'Hundred Points') is a foundational Chinese trick-taking game that serves as the progenitor to more complex multi-deck games like Tractor (Sheng Ji). Played with a single 54-card deck, it is a game of partnership survival where the Attacking team tries to capture specific point cards (5s, 10s, and Kings) while the Defending team protects their lead. It features a trump system where a trump rank is rotated and combined with jokers to create a powerful hierarchy. Origin: China Players: 4 (Partnerships) Deck: 54 cards (Standard 52 + 2 Jokers) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-45 minutes",
    "history": "Originating in Northern China as a simplified version of the older 'Zheng Fen' (Compete for Points) games. It standardized the '100 Point' economy of a single deck, making it accessible to a wider audience before the 1980s explosion of multi-deck 'Tractor' variants. - Early 20th Century: Single-deck point games emerge in family social circles. - 1950s: Rules standardized across urban centers like Beijing and Shanghai. - 1980s: The game evolves into 'Ba Shi Fen' (80 Points) and 'Tractor' using two decks. - Present: Still played in households that prefer the tighter, more mathematical constraints of a single deck. The transition from 'Bai Fen' to 'Tractor' marked a shift from individual card tracking to pattern-based 'sequential' play. Frequently played at weddings and festivals; it is seen as a game of cooperation and shared fortune.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two fixed partnerships. Deck: One standard 52-card deck plus both Jokers (Red and Black). Equipment: Standard pack, score sheet. Dealing: The deck is shuffled and players draw one card at a time until the deck is exhausted. The dealer role rotates clockwise.",
    "rules": "Objective: The 'Attacking' team must collect at least 40 points in their tricks to 'win' the round. Collecting 100 points (all point cards) is a 'cleaning' victory. Gameplay: Standard trick-taking rules apply. Players must follow suit if possible. The highest trump wins the trick; if no trumps, the highest card of the led suit wins. Scoring: Points are tallied at the end of each hand. Only the Attacking team's points are counted towards the 'Win/Loss' threshold. - The 'Kicker': On the final trick, if the Attacker wins, the points in the final trick are multiplied (doubled or tripled depending on the house rules). - No Trump: In some variants, if no trump suit is declared, only Rank cards and Jokers are trumps. Win condition: 40 points = Attacker wins. 80 points = Attacker wins big (+2 levels in Sheng Ji variants).",
    "strategy": "Basic: Bankers should always lead their strongest non-trump suits early to force the Attackers to spend their trumps on low-value tricks. Intermediate: Point Control: If you are the Attacker and know you can't win the trick, throw your point cards (5s, 10s) to your PARTNER if they are likely to win it. Advanced: The Void Tactic: Try to empty your hand of a low-value suit early so you can 'Trump In' (ruff) when that suit is led, stealing the points from the Bankers. - Tip: Count the 5s and 10s. There are only four of each rank. Knowing how many are left in play is the difference between a master and a novice. - Tip: Never lead a King unless you are certain it is the highest card remaining; losing a King is a 10-point swing to the opponent.",
    "variations": "- Da Bai Fen (Great 100): Uses a 'Leveling' system where winning a hand allows you to promote your Trump Rank (e.g., from 2 to 3)."
