    "history": "Bao Huang emerged from the coastal cities of Shandong (specifically Qingdao and Weifang) in the mid-20th century. It traces its ancestry to simpler climbing games like 'Zheng Fen' but introduced the imperial theme and the unusually large deck to accommodate longer, more strategic social sessions. - 1960s: Developed in Shandong as a recreational game among workers. - 1990s: Standardized for regional tournaments with dedicated 168-card packs. - 2005: Online platforms (like QQ Games) bring Bao Huang to a national audience in China. - Present: One of the 'Big Three' Chinese climbing games alongside Zheng Fen and Sheng Ji. The game's deck size has fluctuated between 168 and 216 cards across different cities, with 168 becoming the professional standard. Symbolizes the complex loyalty structures of the old Chinese courts. Playing Bao Huang is often considered a 'diplomatic' skill in northern...",
    "setup": "Players: 5 players. Team A: Emperor & Eunuch. Team B: Three Commoners. Deck: 168 cards. Take four standard 52-card decks, remove all 3s, 4s, and 5s. Use 4 Red Jokers and 4 Black Jokers. One specific Red Joker is marked 'Emperor' and one specific Black Joker is marked 'Eunuch'. Equipment: Special 168-card deck, score pad. Dealing: Players draw cards one by one from a shuffled wall. Everyone receives 33 or 34 cards.",
    "rules": "Objective: Escape the hand. Points are awarded based on the order in which players finish. The Emperor's team wins if they finish in the top positions. Gameplay: Standard climbing rules: You must play the same number of cards as the leader, but of higher rank. If someone plays four 7s, you must play four 8s or higher. If you can't or won't play, you pass. Scoring: Points are based on finishing order (1st place through 5th place). - Uprising (Zaofan): Before play, Commoners can declare an uprising if they have strong hands, doubling the stakes. - Tribute (Jin Gong): Losers of the previous round must give their highest cards to the winners at the start of the next round. - Emperor alone: If the Emperor holds both marked cards, they play against the other four alone for quadruple points. Win condition: Calculated per hand. Typically: 1st/2nd finish by Royals = +6 points. 1st/3rd = +4...",
    "strategy": "Basic: As a Commoner, never trust a 'helpful' pass. If someone passes on the Emperor's 7, they might be the Eunuch saving their high cards for later. Intermediate: The Lead Tactic: If you are the Emperor with many 6s (the lowest card), lead them in big sets (e.g., 'Six 6s') early. This forces the Commoners to break up their strong sets to stop you. Advanced: Eunuch Camouflage: The Eunuch should intentionally play a moderately high card against the Emperor early to verify their 'loyalty' to the Commoners, then switch sides at the most damaging moment. - Tip: Count the Jokers. There are 8 in the deck. If 6 have been played, the last 2 are the most powerful cards in existence. - Tip: Don't waste 2s on small tricks. Save them to regain the lead when your team is ready to dump a long sequence of low cards.",
