    "history": "Originating in Australia in the mid-20th century as a social variant of 'Eights' or 'Uno'. It was designed to maintain interest over long sessions by allowing the game to evolve, becoming more difficult as the night progressed. - 1950s: First recorded play in Sydney and Melbourne university circles. - 1970s: Becomes a popular choice in international hostels as 'Warthog'. - 2004: Widely published rules appear on online card game archives. - Present: A staple of the 'Meta-Rule' genre, where the game design is part of the play. The game moved from oral tradition to a standardized format where the 'Rule List' is often transcribed on a piece of paper (or mentally tracked in 'Hardcore' modes). Regarded as a test of mental endurance and creative trap-making.",
    "setup": "Players: 2-12 players. Best with 4-6. Deck: One standard 52-card deck for 2-5 players. Two decks for 6 or more. Equipment: Standard pack, score sheet, and a pen/paper to record the new rules. Dealing: Dealer (rotating) deals 7 cards to each player. The next card is turned face up to start the discard pile; the remainder is the draw pile.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first player to shed all cards from your hand in a round. The ultimate goal is to win the round and implement a rule that benefits you or hinders your rivals. Gameplay: On your turn, you must play a card that matches either the **Suit** or the **Rank** of the top card on the discard pile. If you cannot play, you must draw one card from the deck. If that card is playable, you may play it immediately. Scoring: Bartok is usually played as a series of independent rounds with no shared score track; players simply note who wins each round. - Match Suit or Rank. - Call 'Bartok' on the penultimate card. - Winner creates a new rule; rules stack session-wide. Win condition: Each round is won by the first player to empty their hand; tables may track number of rounds won, but there is no fixed overall score.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep track of the rules! The most common way to lose is forgetting a rule that was made four rounds ago. In the early game, get rid of your high-point cards (Face cards). Intermediate: Rule Synergies: If there's a rule that says 'The next player draws 2 on a 7', and you hold two 7s, try to hold them until the player to your left is low on cards. Advanced: Rule Manipulation: When you win, create a rule that targets a specific card you know is gone from the deck or one that you personally have many of in the next hand (if you can predict the shuffle). Or, create a rule that is linguistically difficult ('Must speak only in rhymes on Clubs'). - Tip: Don't build too many 'Draw 2' rules, or the deck will run out before anyone can win. - Tip: Assign one player as the 'Rule Keeper' to prevent arguments about the wording of old rules.",
