    "overview": "Bauernschnapsen (Viererschnapsen) is the premier four-player partnership variant of the Austrian national game Schnapsen. Played with a stripped 20-card deck, it combines the intense card-point counting of the original game with complex partnership bidding and higher stakes. Each round is a battle to reach 66 points through tricks and marriages, while the overall session is a race to reach 24 game points. Famous for its 'Stechzwang' (trump compulsion) and various special contracts like 'Gang' and 'Bauern', it is a cornerstone of Central European tavern culture. Origin: Austria Players: 4 (Fixed Partnerships) Deck: 20 cards (Stripped Pack) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 15-30 minutes",
    "history": "Originating in Austria as an evolution of 2-player Schnapsen (and the earlier 66), Bauernschnapsen adapted the 'short deck' mechanics to a four-player format. It emerged as a distinct social tradition in Austrian rural communities ('Bauern' meaning farmers) where partnership play was preferred for social gatherings. - 18th Century: Early 'Sixty-Six' games spread across the Habsburg Empire. - 19th Century: Schnapsen stabilizes as the primary Austrian variant. - Early 20th Century: Bauernschnapsen (Viererschnapsen) becomes the standardized partnership format in Alpine regions. - Present: One of Austria's most popular card games, with dedicated tournaments and regional clubs. The game evolved from a simple point-race into a complex bidding game where the value of game points depends on the margin of victory (Schneider/Schwarz). Regarded as a test of mental arithmetic and partnership...",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two fixed partnerships; partners sit opposite each other. Deck: 20 cards. Ranks: Ace (11), 10 (10), King (4), Queen (Ober, 3), Jack (Unter, 2) in four suits. Equipment: Standard 20-card deck (French or German suited), score sheet. Dealing: Dealer deals 3 cards to each player. Forehand (left of dealer) calls trump or knocks. Then 2 more cards are dealt to each player.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score 66 points in a deal to win the round, or satisfy a higher bid contract. First team to reach 24 game points (counting down) wins the session. Gameplay: 1. **Bidding**: Forehand determines trump or everyone bids for special contracts. 2. **Marriages**: King and Queen of the same suit score bonus points (40 for trump, 20 for others) when one is led. 3. **Tricks**: Players must follow suit. If unable to follow suit, players MUST play a trump (Stechzwang). 4. **Counting**: Ace=11, 10=10, K=4, Q=3, J=2. Total in deck is 120 points. Scoring: Points are tallied from tricks and marriages. The team reaching 66 points first wins the hand. - Must follow suit if possible. - Must trump if void in the led suit (Stechzwang). - Winning the last trick scores no extra points, but provides the final tally. - If a team reaches 66 points, they stop the game immediately. Win condition: The...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Count your points! Every card matters in a 20-card deck. Know when you have reached 66 so you can stop the hand before your opponents gain more points. Intermediate: The 'Volle' Strategy: Focus on capturing the Tens and Aces (the Volle). In a stripped deck, these 10/11 point cards are the difference between victory and defeat. Advanced: Bidding Precision: Understanding when to bid 'Gang' (winning all 5 tricks) vs 'Normal' requires perfect reading of the distribution based on the 3-card initial deal. - Tip: A marriage declaration is often more valuable than a high-point capture. - Tip: Lead trumps early if you have a marriage to prevent opponents from trumping your King/Queen lead.",
