    "history": "Believed to be derived from the Dutch game 'Klaverjas', brought to France by diamond merchants or Jewish communities in the early 20th century. - 1914: Becomes popular among French soldiers (Poilus) in WWI trenches. - 1920s: Establishes itself as the dominant café game in Paris, overtaking Manille. - Present: The most played card game in France with a codified federation (FFB). The game evolved into 'Belote Contrée' and 'Coinche' where bidding is more strategic, but classic Belote remains the foundation. In France, 'Une petite Belote?' is a standard social invitation. The nuances of signalling and the 'Dix de Der' (10 points for the last trick) are ingrained in French culture.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in fixed partnerships (North-South vs East-West). Deck: 32 cards. Ranks: Ace (11), 10 (10), King (4), Queen (3), Jack (20/2), 9 (14/0), 8 (0), 7 (0). Equipment: Standard 32-card French pack (Piquet deck), score pad. Dealing: Deal 5 cards to each player (3-2). One card is turned face up (The Proposal). Players bid to take it as trump. If all pass, a second round of bidding allows naming any other suit. Remaining cards are dealt (3 each) for a total of 8.",
    "rules": "Objective: The contracting team must score more points than the defenders (usually > 81 points out of 162 total). First team to 501 or 1000 wins the match. Gameplay: 1. **Bidding**: Accept turned trump or call another suit. 2. **Declarations**: Announced during the first trick. Only the team with the highest declaration scores theirs. 3. **The Play**: Forehand leads. Must follow suit. If void, must trump (unless partner winning). Must over-trump if trumps are led. 4. **Scoring**: Sum card points + declarations + 'Dix de Der' (10 bonus). Scoring: Points accumulate over deals. - The Jack of Trumps is the boss card (20 pts). The 9 of Trumps is the second boss (14 pts). - Strict trumping rules: You cannot undercut/under-trump unless forced. - Melds (Declarations) are only valid if they are better than the opponents' best meld. Win condition: First team to 501 (short game) or 1000 (long...",
