    "history": "Originating in the United States in the late 19th or early 20th century, Bid Whist grew out of the standard Whist brought by European settlers but was heavily adapted by African-American communities into a complex tactical game. - 1800s: Standard Whist is the dominant trick-taking game. - Early 1900s: Auction-style bidding elements are added, creating Bid Whist. - 1940s-1960s: Becomes a foundational social game in African-American neighborhoods and social clubs. - Present: Remains highly popular, with national tournaments and a deep cultural legacy. The introduction of the 'Kitty' (widow) and the 'Downtown' ranking system were key innovations that added strategic layers unknown in traditional Whist. Regarded as a game of 'smack talk' and mental dominance. Mastery of Bid Whist is often considered a rite of passage in many communities.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two fixed partnerships. Deck: 54 cards: Standard 52-card pack plus two distinct Jokers (Big Joker and Little Joker). Equipment: Standard pack with jokers, score pad. Dealing: Deal 12 cards to each player. 6 cards are dealt face down to the center to form the 'Kitty'.",
