    "history": "Rooted deeply in the Austrian Tyrol and Bavarian card-game tradition. The WELI card is a hallmark of Tyrolean card games. Bieten draws on the same tradition as Watten and Schnapsen. - 1800s: Documented in Austrian card parlors. - Present: Still played actively in Tyrol, Bavaria, and South Tyrol, especially in mountain communities. The game's core innovation is its multi-point structure. Unlike most games where ONE thing wins, Bieten has FIVE simultaneous point categories, each bettable independently. The WELI card is unique to this region. In Bieten, it acts as a wild card and can represent any card.",
    "setup": "Players: 3 to 5 players. Deck: 33-card German-suited pack: A, K, Over (O), Under (U), 10, 9, 8, 7 in 4 suits (Hearts/Rot, Leaves/GrÃ¼n, Bells/Schellen, Acorns/Eichel) + WELI (special 6 of Bells wild card). Equipment: Chips or coins for betting. Dealing: Deal 3 cards to each player. No trump is established. No kitty.",
