    "history": "Originating in the Netherlands, Billitonnen takes its name from Billiton (Belitung), an Indonesian island formerly part of the Dutch East Indies. It is a lighthearted variant of European line games, prized for its chaotic and social nature. - 19th Century: Blind domino games become popular in European cafes. - Early 20th Century: Billitonnen establishes itself as the standard Dutch 'wind-down' game. - Present: Still played in traditional Dutch gaming circles and recorded in national heritage archives. The game follows the 'Blind Hughie' template but introduces the strict 'Leftmost First' rule, which increases the frustration and humor of the game. A classic 'after-match' game, meant to be played with drinks and conversation rather than intense competitive focus.",
    "setup": "Players: 2 to 4 players. Deck: Standard double-six domino set (28 tiles). Equipment: Standard pack. Dealing: Players draw tiles based on count (2p=7, 3p=7, 4p=6). Tiles are placed in a face-down row in front of each player. No one may look at their tiles.",
