    "history": "Developed in Britain in the late 19th century. The game is named after Black Maria, slang for a police paddy wagonâ€”suggesting the Queen of Spades is as much trouble as getting arrested. - 1880s: Emerges in Britain as a derivative of Hearts-type games. - 1900s: Widely played as a family game across Britain and the Commonwealth. - Present: Superseded in global popularity by the American Hearts rules, but still distinctly played in the UK. The key differences from standard Hearts: Ace and King of Spades also carry penalties (10 and 7 pts respectively in some variants), and the passing direction differs. A quintessentially British parlor game, often played at Christmas and family gatherings.",
