    "history": "Descended from the 17th-century French game 'Vingt-et-Un' (Twenty-One). The name 'Blackjack' originated in American gambling halls that offered a special 10-to-1 payout for an Ace of Spades and a black Jack (Spades or Clubs). - 1700s: Vingt-et-Un is played in French courts and casinos. - 1800s: Brought to the United States; rules evolve into the modern banking format. - 1962: Edward O. Thorp publishes 'Beat the Dealer', proving that card counting can overcome the house edge. - Present: The standard for casino table games worldwide and a massive online gaming staple. The game moved from single-deck play to multi-deck 'shoes' and automated shuffling machines to counter strategic card counting. A symbol of the high-stakes casino environment, famously featured in countless films and literature as the ultimate game of skill vs. house.",
    "setup": "Players: Typically 1 to 7 players seated at a semi-circular table, all playing against a central Dealer. Deck: Standard 52-card French decks (usually 6 or 8 decks in a shoe). Equipment: Casino table, chips, card shoe, discard tray. Dealing: After bets are placed, players and the dealer receive two cards each. Usually, one of the dealer's cards is face up (upcard) and one is face down (hole card).",
    "rules": "Objective: Achieve a hand value closer to 21 than the dealer's hand, without exceeding 21. Gameplay: 1. **Wagering**: Players place bets. 2. **Deal**: Players and dealer receive two cards. 3. **Player Turn**: Players choose to 'Hit' (take card), 'Stand' (keep hand), 'Double Down' (double bet for one card), or 'Split' (split pairs). 4. **Dealer Turn**: Dealer reveals hole card and must hit until reaching 17+. 5. **Settlement**: Higher hand wins; dealer bust pays all remaining players. Scoring: Payouts based on hand outcome. - Aces count as 1 or 11. - Face cards (K, Q, J) count as 10. - Blackjack (Natural 21) typically pays 3-to-2. - Insurance is offered if the dealer's upcard is an Ace. Win condition: Higher total than dealer or dealer bust.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Follow the 'Basic Strategy' chart: always hit on 11 or less, always stand on hard 17 or more. Stand on 12-16 if the dealer shows a 2-6 (bust cards). Intermediate: Double Down Timing: Always double down on a total of 11 unless the dealer shows an Ace. Double on 10 against a dealer 2-9. Advanced: Card Counting: Use the Hi-Lo system to track the ratio of high cards (10s, Aces) to low cards. Increase your bet when the 'True Count' is positive, as this increases the probability of a player blackjack. - Tip: Never take insurance; it is a 'sucker bet' with a high house edge. - Tip: Avoid 6-to-5 payout tables; always look for traditional 3-to-2 blackjack payouts.",
