    "history": "Descended from the 17th-century game All Fours via the Irish game Dom Pedro. 'Don' emerged as a distinct British regional variant, with Blind Don being the adaptation for two players to maintain the competitive tension of the partnership version. - 1800s: All Fours variants like Dom Pedro spread through the UK and Ireland. - 1900s: Nine-Card Don becomes a staple pub game in Lancashire. - Present: Survival as a niche traditional game in British regional card clubs. The game's 'two-part' deal structure was developed to allow two players to use the full deck while maintaining small, manageable hand sizes. Reflects the 'Old School' British pub gaming culture where memory and card-counting are highly respected skills.",
    "setup": "Players: Exactly 2 players. Deck: Standard 52-card French pack. Ranks: A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2. Equipment: Standard pack, score pad. Dealing: Deal 13 cards to each player. The remaining 26 cards are set aside face-down for the second half of the game. The first card played (the 'pitch') by the non-dealer sets the trump suit for the entire hand.",
