    "history": "Originating as a casual variation of the standard Block domino game, Blind Hughie was developed to simplify the strategy and increase the element of surprise. It is a traditional Western folk game with long-standing popularity in English-speaking card rooms and Dutch cafes (where it is known as Billitonnen). - 19th Century: Variations of 'Blind' dominoes appear in European gaming records. - 1950s: Standardized as Blind Hughie in popular American and British domino books. - Present: Remains a favorite introductory game for children and a casual 'wind-down' game for experienced players. The game essentially removes hand-management from the Block domino engine, replacing it with a sequential memory mechanic. Valued for its ability to level the playing field between experienced players and novices, emphasizing fun and memory over complex calculation.",
    "setup": "Players: 2 to 4 players playing individually. Deck: Standard double-six domino set (28 tiles). Equipment: Standard pack, score sheet. Dealing: Players draw tiles based on player count (2p=7, 3p=5, 4p=5). Tiles are placed in a face-down row in front of each player. No one may look at their cards.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first player to play all tiles from your face-down row. Gameplay: 1. **Start**: A tile is turned from the boneyard to start the central line. 2. **Turn**: Flip over the RIGHTMOST face-down tile in your row. 3. **Match**: If the tile matches either open end of the line, you must play it. 4. **Cycle**: If the tile does not match, return it face-down to the LEFT end of your row. 5. **Boneyard**: Tiles in the boneyard are not used after the start. Scoring: Winner scores sum of losers' pip totals minus their own remaining pips. Game to 100 (2-player) or 61 (3-4 player). - You must always flip the rightmost tile first. - Returned tiles must be placed at the opposite end of the row to preserve their cycle order. - The game ends when a player goes domino or the line is blocked. Win condition: Emptying the player's row or having the fewest pips at a block.",
