    "history": "Derived from the British game 'Nomination Whist' which gained popularity in the 1930s. The Dutch variant, Boerenbridge, introduced a structured trump rotation and specific scoring rules that became standardized in Dutch social clubs. - 1930s: Oh Hell! emerges in the UK and USA. - 1950s: Boerenbridge becomes a common family game in the Netherlands. - Present: Remains one of the most played trick-taking games in Dutch schools and pubs. The game shifted from a simple 'Up the River' format to a symmetric 'Up and Down' cycle, ensuring that the highest skill testing occurs at the 1-card and maximum-card rounds. A foundational game in Dutch society, often used to teach children the principles of suit following and trick valuation.",
    "setup": "Players: 3 to 7 players playing individually. Deck: Standard 52-card French pack (No Jokers). Equipment: Standard pack, score pad. Dealing: The round number (N) determines the hand size. Start at 1 card, increase by 1 each deal to a maximum (e.g., 10), then decrease back to 1. A trump card is turned after each deal.",
