    "history": "Originating in rural Catalonia in the 19th century, Botifarra evolved from the older game of Manille. Its name literally refers to a type of Catalan sausage, though in the game it denotes the 'Botifarra' (No Trump) contract. - 1800s: Manille variants become popular in North-Eastern Spain. - Early 1900s: Distinct Botifarra rules (delegating trump, doubling) stabilize. - 1980s: Codification of regional tournament rules by Catalan associations. - Present: A symbol of Catalan national identity, played in every village and social club. The game's complexity grew with the addition of 'Contrar' (doubling) and 'Recontrar' (redoubling) mechanics, which add a gambling layer to the strategic play. Played with extreme seriousness; many clubs forbid talking or signaling during play. It is an integral part of Catalan social life.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two fixed partnerships. Deck: 48 cards (1-12 in Coins, Cups, Swords, Batons). Ranking: 9 (Manille, 5 pts), Ace (4 pts), King (3 pts), Knight (2 pts), Jack (1 pt), then 12, 11, 10, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2 (0 pts). Equipment: Spanish-suited deck, score pad. Dealing: Deal all 48 cards clockwise (12 to each player).",
    "rules": "Objective: Score more than 36 points (half of 72 total card points) in a deal. First team to reach a target (usually 101) wins. Gameplay: 1. **Trump Selection**: Dealer names a suit, No Trump ('Botifarra'), or delegates ('Delegar') to partner. 2. **Doubling**: Opponents can 'Contrar' to double the points. 3. **The Play**: Forehand leads. **Must Follow Suit**. If void, **Must Trump** if the opponent is winning the trick. 4. **Tricks**: Highest trump wins; else highest of led suit. Scoring: 72 points in the pack. Winners score the difference over 36, multiplied by any doubles. - Ranking is A-7-K-J-Q? No, in Botifarra: 9-A-K-Knight-Jack-others. - Jack of Trumps is NOT the boss; the 9 is. - The 'Botifarra' (No Trump) contract doubles the points of the deal automatically. Win condition: First team to reach 101 total match points.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Protect your 9s and Aces. Since the 9 is worth 5 points, capturing even one is a huge boost. Avoid leading trumps if you are weak. Intermediate: The 'Delegar' Logic: Only delegate to your partner if your hand is perfectly balanced with no strong suits. If you have five cards of one suit, always name it trumps yourself. Advanced: Managing the 'Contrar': Doubling is a psychological tool. Use it not just when you have trumps, but when you suspect the dealer delegated because they have a terrible hand. - Tip: Track the 'Manilles' (the four 9s). If all four are out, the Aces become the bosses. - Tip: Always try to win the last trick; while it scores no bonus points, it often contains the final 'point' cards.",
    "variations": "- Strict Trumping: Standard tournament rules: you must always trump if void, even if your partner is currently winning the trick."
