    "setup": "Players: 3 to 8 players playing individually (5 is optimal for suit distribution). Deck: Standard 52-card French pack (No Jokers). Equipment: Standard pack, large central pot for chips or currency. Dealing: Each player contributes an ante. Deal 5 cards each clockwise. Turn the next card face-up to set the trump suit.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the most tricks to claim the pot, while ensuring you win at least one trick to avoid the penalty. Gameplay: 1. **Ante**: Mandatory contribution. 2. **Deal**: 5 cards each + trump reveal. 3. **Participation**: Players choose to 'Play' or 'Fold'. 4. **Exchange**: Active players may discard and draw new cards from the stock. 5. **Play**: Must follow suit; if void, MUST trump; if trumps are led, MUST over-trump. 6. **Settlement**: Winner takes pot. Non-winners with 0 tricks match the pot. Scoring: Zero-sum gambling chips. Pot winner takes all; Bourréd players match the pot for the next deal. - If you choose to Play and win 0 tricks, you are 'Bourréd' and pay the whole pot. - Dealer must play if all other players fold. - Aces are high; 2s are low. Win condition: Win the most tricks in the hand.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Only stay in the hand if you have at least one high trump or a void in a side suit that allows you to trump early and secure your safety trick. Intermediate: The 'Exchange' Tell: Watch how many cards opponents draw. A player drawing 1 card likely has a strong four-flush or triple; a player drawing 3 is gambling on the draw. Advanced: Defensive Over-trumping: If you hold the second-best trump, wait for the leader to play trumps so you can over-trump and seize control, potentially 'Beasting' (Bourréing) the opponents. - Tip: Don't be greedy—avoiding the penalty is more important than winning a small pot. - Tip: In a 5-player game, 25 cards are out; use this to estimate if your King-high suit is safe.",
