    "overview": "3-Card Brag is Britain's premier vying (betting) card game and a direct ancestor of modern Poker. Each player receives three cards and competes for a central pot. Its most distinctive feature is the 'Blind' betting system, where a player can bet without looking at their cards for half the cost, maintaining a psychological and mechanical advantage over 'Seeing' players. It is fast, aggressive, and deeply rooted in British pub and gambling traditions. Origin: Great Britain (16th Century) Players: 2-8 (Best with 4-6) Deck: 52 cards (Standard) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: Indefinite (ante-based rounds)",
    "history": "Brag emerged in 16th-century England as a three-card vying game. It evolved into the parent of Indian Teen Patti and influenced the betting structures of early American Poker variants like Three-Card Poker. - 1500s: Early versions of 'Post and Pair' and Brag documented in England. - 1700s: The 'Bragger' (wild card) mechanic is introduced, usually the Jack of Clubs. - 1900s: Remains the standard gambling game in British military and social clubs. - Present: A cultural staple of British pub life and high-stakes private games. The game's hand rankings (Prial > Running Flush > Run) and the half-price blind betting have remained largely consistent for centuries, despite the global dominance of Poker. Symbolizes 'working class' poker in the UK; featured in numerous British films like 'Lock, Stock and Two Smoking Barrels'.",
    "setup": "Players: Typically 3 to 8 players playing individually. Deck: Standard 52-card French pack (No Jokers). Equipment: Standard pack, betting chips or currency. Dealing: Every player contributes an ante. Dealer deals three cards face-down to each player.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the pot by holding the best three-card combination or by being the last player remaining after others fold. Gameplay: 1. **Blind/See**: Players decide whether to bet 'Blind' (unseen) or 'See' their cards. 2. **Betting**: Blind players bet N; Seeing players must bet at least 2N. 3. **Folding**: A player can fold at any time, losing all previous contributions. 4. **Showdown**: If only two players remain, one can 'See' the other by paying double the current bet to compare hands. 5. **Hand Ranks**: Prial (Three of a Kind), Running Flush (Straight Flush), Run (Straight), Flush, Pair, High Card. Scoring: Standard pot-based resolution. - Prial of 3s is the highest possible hand (3-3-3 beats A-A-A). - A Blind player's hand beats a Seeing player's identical hand (Blind advantage). - A Seeing player can only 'See' a player when only two people remain in the hand. Win condition:...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Stay Blind as long as possible. Betting half-price allows you to build the pot while making it expensive for Seeing players to stay in with marginal hands. Intermediate: The 'Prial' Hunt: If you hold a pair of 3s, you are statistically likely to have the best hand. If you hold a 3 and a wild card ('Bragger'), your hand is incredibly strong. Advanced: Range Inference: Watch the Betting Speed. A player who sees their cards and immediately raises is often representing a Run or better. A player who stays Blind for many rounds is either bluffing or has complete confidence in the shuffle. - Tip: Never 'See' your cards too early; the psychological pressure of a Blind player is your best weapon. - Tip: If you are a Seeing player, don't chase a Blind bet unless you have at least a Flush.",
