    "history": "Part of the traditional British multi-card Brag family, 5-Card Brag emerged as a way to provide more strategic depth than the original 3-card game. It shares roots with partition games like Crash and 9-Card Brag. - Mid-20th Century: Multi-card Brag variants gain popularity in British card clubs. - 1980s: Becomes a standard Dealer's Choice option in social gambling. - Present: Popular in both physical card rooms and online implementations. The game retains the core vying and blind-betting rules but introduces a pre-play hand optimization phase. Reflects the British preference for 'choice' based gambling games that allow for more tactical discarding.",
    "setup": "Players: Typically 3 to 8 players playing individually. Deck: Standard 52-card French pack (No Jokers). Equipment: Standard pack, betting chips or currency. Dealing: Every player contributes an ante. Dealer deals five cards face-down to each player. Players choose three to keep and place the other two face-down in a shared muck pile.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the pot by forming the best three-card hand from the five dealt, or by forcing others to fold. Gameplay: 1. **Selection**: Players receive 5 cards and discard 2. 2. **Vying**: Standard Brag betting follows. Players choose to play 'Blind' (without looking at their chosen 3) or 'See'. 3. **Ranks**: Prial > Running Flush > Run > Flush > Pair > High Card. Scoring: Standard pot-based resolution. - The choice of which three cards to keep is the primary skill element. - Blind players bet half the price of Seeing players. - Equal hands favor the player who remained Blind longer. Win condition: Last player standing or best three-card hand at showdown.",
    "strategy": "Basic: With 5 cards to choose from, you should rarely play a hand that isn't at least a Flush. Look for high pairs or consecutive suit sequences. Intermediate: The 'Hidden' Prial: If you are dealt a pair and two cards of another suit, evaluate if it's better to keep the pair for a safe 'Seeing' bet or gamble on a 'Blind' bet with potentially higher hidden value. Advanced: Discard Tracking: Since every player mucks two cards, 8-16 cards are taken out of play in a 4-8 player game. Track which ranks are most common in your 5 cards to deduce which suits/ranks are depleted in opponents' hands. - Tip: Don't fall in love with a low pair; in 5-card Brag, a low pair is a very weak hand. - Tip: If you hold a 3 and two wild cards ('Braggers'), you have the top hand (Prial of 3s).",
    "variations": "- Brag with Braggers: Standard variant where J♣, 9♦, and A♦ are wild cards (Braggers)."
