    "releaseStatus": "WorkInProgress",
    "overview": "6-Card Brag is a complex partition and vying game that bridges the gap between standard Brag and 9-Card Brag. Every player is dealt six cards and must divide them into two independent three-card hands. These hands are typically played against opponents in sequence (front and back) or used to decide a single highest-rank winner. It rewards both the skill of hand optimization and the traditional bluffing and blind-betting mechanics of the Brag family. Origin: Great Britain Players: 2-6 Deck: 52 cards (Standard) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-40 minutes",
    "history": "Developed as a strategic expansion of British pub Brag, 6-Card Brag introduces the 'partition' mechanic found in Asian games like Chinese Poker but retains the core British vying structure. - Mid-20th Century: Partition variants of Brag appear in Northern English card rooms. - 1980s: Standardized as a specialty 'big hand' variant. - Present: Played in competitive Brag circles and high-stakes social games. The transition from a single three-card hand to two simultaneous hands forced a total recalibration of betting strategies, as a player might have one unbeatable hand and one complete bluff. Regarded as a 'thinking man's' Brag, where the math of partitioning cards is as vital as the bluffing.",
    "setup": "Players: Typically 3 to 6 players playing individually. Deck: Standard 52-card French pack (No Jokers). Equipment: Standard pack, betting chips or currency. Dealing: Ante into pot. Deal six cards face-down to each player. Players divide their six cards into two separate three-card hands.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the pot by having the highest-ranked hands across the partition or by forcing others to fold. Gameplay: 1. **Partition**: Players divide 6 cards into Hand A and Hand B. 2. **Vying**: Betting rounds proceed as in standard Brag. 3. **Comparison**: Showdown involves comparing corresponding hands. 4. **Ranks**: Prial > Running Flush > Run > Flush > Pair > High Card. Scoring: Standard pot-based resolution across dual-hand comparisons. - Both three-card hands must be legal Brag combinations. - In many variants, you must win both hand comparisons to take the entire pot; otherwise, it carries over. - Blind betting applies to the entire 6-card set before viewing and partitioning. Win condition: Win both hand comparisons OR be the last player not to fold.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Avoid making one 'Super Hand' and one 'Trash Hand'. A balanced partition (e.g., two Flushes) is often more reliable than a single Prial and a High-Card hand. Intermediate: The 'Front' Signal: If playing in rounds, winning the first hand comparison puts immense pressure on the opponent. Invest your best cards in Hand A if you intend to bluff Hand B. Advanced: Range Splitting: Evaluate the total number of trumps and high cards in the 6-card deal. If the deck is 'heavy' with Spades, splitting your Spades across two hands might be a safer defensive move than one strong Flush. - Tip: Don't ignore the High Card tie-breakers; kickers are vital in partition games. - Tip: If you are dealt four of a kind, you have two pairs or a Prial—choose the split that maximizes your win probability across both comparisons.",
    "variations": "- 9-Card Brag: Partitioning nine cards into three hands; the final evolution of the multi-card Brag series."
