    "history": "Developed as a 'Honeymoon' Bridge variant to allow two players to enjoy the strategic complexity of Bridge without a full table. It borrows the 'uncovering' mechanic from games like German Whist. - Early 20th Century: Honeymoon Bridge variants proliferate. - 1950s: The semi-exposed dummy format becomes a standard recommendation in Bridge primers for two players. - Present: Popular as a training tool for Bridge players to practice card-reading and deduction. The game shifted from simple 'Draw Whist' to this format to better simulate the presence of four hands and the partnership dynamics of standard Bridge. A staple of the 'Honeymoon' card game sub-genre, emphasizing intimacy and mental rivalry.",
