    "history": "Brisca is the Spanish adaptation of the Italian Briscola, sharing the same unusual card ranking (Ace > Three > picture cards). It spread throughout the Iberian Peninsula and Latin America. - 1600s-1700s: Briscola-type games cross from Italy into Spain. - 1800s: Brisca standardized as a household and tavern game in Spain. - Present: A Spanish national game alongside Mus and Tute; widely played in Spain and Spanish-speaking Latin America. Brisca is virtually identical to Italian Briscola. The main differences: Spanish use of the 40-card baraja espaÃ±ola deck; the 7 of trump (called the 'Siete de Velo' or 'Manilla' in some regions) has elevated importance in some regional variants. Portuguese Bisca uses the 7 of trump as the second-highest card. A deep part of Spanish cafÃ© and park culture. Spain's 'big three' card games are traditionally Mus, Tute, and Brisca.",
    "setup": "Players: 2 or 4 (4 in 2v2 partnerships). Deck: 40-card Spanish deck (baraja espaÃ±ola). OR: French 52-card deck stripped of 8s, 9s, 10s. Equipment: Cards. Dealing: Deal 3 cards each. Turn one card face-up beside stock to indicate trump (Brisca) suit.",
    "rules": "Objective: To win, a player or team must earn more card points than the opponent(s). The deck has 120 points in total; the first side to exceed 60 points wins the deal. If both sides tie at 60, the side with more cards wins. Gameplay: Lead any card (no follow-suit obligation). Opponent plays any card. Highest Brisca (trump) wins. If no trump played, highest of led suit wins. Winner draws first from stock; loser draws second. Winner leads next trick. Scoring: Card points from taken tricks. - No follow-suit obligationâ€”ever. - Trump beats all off-suit cards regardless of rank. - Last stock card taken is the trump indicator card. - Target: 61 of 120 total points. Win condition: First to 61 card points wins.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Protect the Ace and Three of trumpâ€”they alone are worth 21 of the 120 points. Intermediate: Lead low worthless cards (7-2) to bait opponents into wasting Aces. Trump an opponent's Ace only if you're sure to also score significant points. Advanced: Once the stock empties, you know both hands. Calculate exactly what remains and play to prevent the opponent's Ace of trump from taking your Three of trump. - Tip: The Three of Brisca (10pts) is the most underestimated cardâ€”treat it like an Ace. - Tip: Never trump a 0-point trick unless you're holding excess trump.",
    "variations": "- Brisca 4 Players (Partnership): 2v2 with partners sitting opposite. Play all 40 cards across 10 tricks (no draw). Silent communication through plays."
