    "history": "Buck Euchre is an American variant of standard 4-player Euchre, adapted for larger groups where partnership play is impractical. It is particularly popular in the American Midwest. - 1800s: Standard Euchre firmly established in the American Midwest. - Late 1800s-1900s: Buck Euchre variants emerge for four players in Ohio and for three players in Indiana. - Present: Regional variant; popular in rural Midwest and other North American card nights. By removing partnerships and making each player responsible for their own trump bid and tricks, Buck Euchre shifts the game from coordinated partnership play to individual risk management, closer to Rams-style penalty games. A staple of American Midwest and broader North American card culture, often played at home games, community halls, and social gatherings where everyone plays for themselves.",
    "setup": "Players: Four players, each for themselves; some groups extend to 5-6 players by adding extra low cards as described in Dirty Clubs variants. Deck: 24 cards (A, K, Q, J, 10, 9 of each French suit) for the core four-player Buck Euchre game; groups that add players can extend the pack with extra low cards such as eights and sevens. Equipment: Cards. Score tokens or paper. Dealing: 5 cards each. Remaining cards form a kitty; top card turned face-up to propose trump.",
