    "history": "A traditional Russian 2-player card game of the point-trick family. Bura is closely related to other Eastern European draw-trump games. Documented in Russian card game literature through the 20th century. - Pre-1900: Traditional Russian card game. - 1900s: Standard across Russia and Soviet Union households. - Present: Still played in Russia; digital versions exist. The 'Bura!' declaration mechanic gives the game an exciting tensionâ€”players race to accumulate 31 points while managing the declaration risk. A beloved Russian domestic game, often played by two people without any additional equipment.",
    "setup": "Players: Two or more players. Deck: 36 cards: 6-7-8-9-10-J-Q-K-A in each of 4 suits. Equipment: Cards. Dealing: Deal 3 cards each. Turn the last card face-up as trump indicator; place it under the stock pile with the suit visible.",
    "rules": "Objective: Accumulate 31 or more card points in your trick pile. Gameplay: Lead any card. No follow-suit obligation. Highest trump wins; else highest led-suit card. Winner draws first, loser draws second. Winner leads next. Continue until stock exhausted. Scoring: 31 card points to win. - No follow-suit obligation. - Trump (set by last dealt card) beats any off-suit. - After stock exhausted, must follow suit. - 'Bura!' can be declared only after stock is empty. Win condition: First to accumulate 31+ card points in tricks.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Protect Aces and Tensâ€”they are your primary point sources (11 and 10 pts each). Intermediate: Track your running point total. Know when to push for the final trick vs. let the opponent win junk. The Bura declaration is a commitmentâ€”miscounting is fatal. Advanced: Trump Management: Save trump for high-point captures (opponent's Ace or Ten). No follow-suit makes it dangerous to lead Aces into potential enemy trump traps. - Tip: An Ace + Ten = 21 points. Win both and you're 2/3 of the way to Bura. - Tip: Once stock is empty, follow-suit is mandatoryâ€”plan your endgame carefully.",
