    "history": "Busca is a variation of CiapanÃ² (also known as Rovescino, Traversone, or Tressette a non Prendere), a reverse trick-taking game from Lombardy, Italy. CiapanÃ² was very popular in Lombardy until the 1980s and is still played there. - Traditional origins: CiapanÃ² played for generations in Lombardy - 1980s: Peak popularity of CiapanÃ² in Lombardy - Modern era: Busca variation documented by Davide Gullo - Contemporary: Still played in Italian regions Busca modifies CiapanÃ² scoring by multiplying all points by three and introducing the special Pita card (Ace of Coins worth 8 points). The objective shifts from simply avoiding points to taking at least one but not the most. Busca and related games like CiapanÃ² are part of Italian regional card game traditions. The Milanese dialect name 'CiapanÃ²' translates roughly to 'Don't catch it!' reflecting the reverse nature of the game.",
