    "overview": "Call Bridge (also known as Call Break in South Asia) is a 4-player trick-taking card game enormously popular in Nepal, India, and Bangladesh. It resembles Spades but with individual bidding â€” each player bids the number of tricks they will win, and Spades are permanent trump. Unlike exact-bid games, players must win AT LEAST their bid (over-tricks are allowed but under-tricks are penalised). It is one of the most widely played card games in Nepal. Origin: South Asia (Nepal, India, Bangladesh) Players: 4 Deck: 52 cards Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-60 minutes",
    "history": "Call Bridge/Call Break is deeply embedded in Nepali card culture and is widely played throughout South Asia. It likely developed from Whist-family games introduced during the British colonial period, merged with local bidding traditions. - Early-mid 1900s: Spreads across Nepal, India, Bangladesh as a social card game. - Present: The dominant card game in Nepal; played digitally on multiple apps with millions of users. The distinctive 'at least your bid' scoring system (rather than exact-bid) differentiates it from Spades and makes overbidding a viable strategy. A staple of Nepali social life. Digital implementations have made it one of the most popular mobile card games in South Asia.",
    "setup": "Players: 4. Individual play (no fixed partnerships). Deck: 52 standard cards. Spades are permanent trump. Equipment: 52-card deck. Score sheet. Dealing: 13 cards each. Entire deck dealt.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score the most points over agreed rounds (usually 5) by winning at least your bid each hand. Gameplay: 1. Deal 13 cards each. 2. Each player bids number of tricks they will win (min 1). 3. Player left of dealer leads. 4. Follow suit; must trump if void and holding spades. 5. Spades are permanent trump. 6. Highest card of led suit or trump wins trick. 7. Win at least your bid: +bid; fail: -bid; over-tricks +0.1 each. Scoring: Decimal scoring: met bid = bid value + 0.1 per over-trick; failed = -bid. - Spades are ALWAYS trump â€” no other suit can be called. - You MUST trump if you cannot follow suit and hold spades. - Over-tricks count as 0.1 points each (minor bonus). - Under-bidding (failing to make your bid) results in a penalty of -bid. Win condition: Highest score after agreed number of rounds (typically 5).",
    "strategy": "Basic: Bid conservatively based on your spades (trump) â€” each spade is likely to win a trick. Bid 1 for a weak hand. Intermediate: Count your high cards carefully: Ace, King, Queen of any suit + all spades. Bid your confident tricks only. Advanced: Position Awareness: In later bidding positions, you know (partially) what others have bid. If three players bid 8 tricks total, the remaining distribution gives you endgame information. Trump Forcing: Lead low spades to exhaust opponents' trump supply. - Tip: Never bid 0 â€” minimum bid is 1. - Tip: Over-tricks only earn 0.1 pts each â€” don't chase them at the cost of failing your bid.",
    "variations": "- Call Break (Nepal variant): Most common Nepali form. Identical mechanics. Often played to exactly 5 rounds."
