    "history": "Call Partner Rook is a four-player Rook variant from the United States. Partner is determined each hand by the bidder naming a card; whoever holds it plays with the bidder for that hand. - 1906: Rook deck introduced by Parker Brothers; games such as Call Partner developed as local variants using the full 57-card deck and partner-calling. Rook was introduced by Parker Brothers in 1906. Call Partner evolved as one of many local Rook variants, using the full 57-card deck and the call-partner mechanism; it is distinct from Kentucky Rook and from the booklet games supplied with the deck. Played in North American communities where Rook is popular, including Eastern Kentucky and Mennonite communities in Southern Ontario, Manitoba, Pennsylvania, and Ohio. The Rook deck was originally marketed to groups that avoided standard playing cards.",
    "setup": "Players: Four players; two teams per hand (bidder and called partner vs others). Deck: Rook 57-card deck (4 colors 1–14 plus Rook). Equipment: Rook deck Dealing: One card at a time to each player until each has 13; first card goes to the player to dealer's left. After each of the first five rounds of the deal, one card is placed face down in the center to form the five-card nest.",
    "rules": "Objective: As the bidding team, take at least the number of card points you bid; as the opposing team, score the points you take. First player to reach 500 or more points wins. Gameplay: Dealer deals 13 cards to each player and 5 to the nest (first card to dealer's left; after each of the first five rounds one card to the nest). Players bid in multiples of 5, dealer first, clockwise; each bid must be higher than the last, and a player who passes cannot bid again. When three have passed, the fourth is the high bidder. The bidder picks up the nest without showing it (18 cards), discards any five face-down to form a new nest, names trump (a color), and calls for a partner by naming a card not in hand; whoever holds that card is the bidder's partner for the hand (secret until the card is played), and the other two form the opposing team (if the bidder holds the called card, the bidder...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Bid when you have good trump and point cards; avoid overbidding. Call a card you do not hold to find a partner. Intermediate: Track which point cards have been played; infer partner when the called card appears. Lead trump to pull opponents' trumps when your side has length. Advanced: Optimize the call to maximize chance your partner has the card; coordinate with partner through carding when identity is revealed. - Tip: If you hold the called card, do not reveal it until it helps your side. - Tip: Winning the last trick gives your team the nest points (20).",
