    "rules": "Objective: Capture cards from the layout. First player/team to reach target score (usually 11 or 21 points) wins. Gameplay: Captured cards kept face up in single pile. New captures on top. Capturing card must be on top. You can sort captured cards but opponents don't see the order. Partners share a pile in 4-player game. Layout: 4 initial cards, can be captured or added to. Builds: Single build (sum=value) or Augmented build (multiple sets with same value). Build owner must capture it eventually. Turn: Must play exactly one card from hand. Actions (in any order, before/after playing): 1) Capture (chow) - play card to capture matching card(s) or builds, 2) Create single build, 3) Change build value, 4) Create/add to augmented build, 5) Discard to layout. Can only do one of actions 1/3/5 per turn (involve playing card). Can do multiple of actions 2/4 without playing card. Cannot capture...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Capture cards when you can. Build when you have the capturing card. Be careful about capturing early - your pile becomes available to opponents. Intermediate: Tactics center on builds, especially high-value builds. If you have a monopoly on aces (value 14), build 14s that opponents cannot touch. Time your captures carefully - early capture gives opponents access to your top card. Advanced: Complex build strategies: create rival piles (e.g., building 14s vs opponent's 12s). High-value builds are powerful because they're easy to augment. Don't release Q, K, A early if it gives opponent monopoly. Use drifting to signal partner. Consider that capturing a 10 or 2 gives opponents access to valuable cards. - Tip: High-value builds (13s, 14s) are most powerful - Tip: Capturing early can be dangerous - your pile helps opponents - Tip: If opponent has 13-build, all your cards are at risk...",
