    "overview": "Cassino (also spelled Casino) is a classic fishing-capture card game originating in Italy and popular throughout the world. Players capture cards from a central tableau by matching rank (pairing) or arithmetic sum (combining). Special prizes exist for the Ten of Diamonds ('Big Cassino', worth 2 points) and Two of Spades ('Little Cassino', worth 1 point). The first player or team to reach 11 (or 21 in some variants) points wins. Origin: Italy / Europe (18th century) Players: 2-4 Deck: 52 cards Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-40 minutes",
    "history": "Cassino is one of the oldest fishing-type games in European card culture, with Italian origins. It was described in English-language card game books as early as the late 18th century. - 1700s: First documented in Italian and French sources as a fishing card game. - 1800s: Spread through Europe; documented in English by Hoyle. - 1900s: Common in American households; many regional variants develop. - Present: Widely taught as a children's and family game; many national variants (Royal Cassino, Draw Cassino, etc.). The building mechanicâ€”combining table cards into a 'build' that can only be taken with the matching rankâ€”is Cassino's most sophisticated feature, unique among fishing games. A game taught to children as an introduction to arithmetic and card handling. Remains popular in Scandinavia, the Caribbean, and North America.",
    "setup": "Players: 2, 3, or 4 (4 often as 2v2 partnerships). Deck: Standard 52 cards. Equipment: Cards. Score sheet. Dealing: 4 cards each; 4 cards face-up on table (the 'tableau'). Rest is stock. Redeal 4 each when hands empty until stock gone.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score 11 points (or 21) before opponents. Points come from special cards, counting cards, and spades. Gameplay: On your turn, play exactly one card from your hand by: 1. Pairing: If your card matches a table card's rank, capture both. 2. Combining: If your card equals the arithmetic sum of 2+ table cards, capture them all. 3. Building: Lay your hand card with table cards, announce the intended total. Another player cannot steal the build unless they match it first. 4. Trailing: If you can't or won't capture/build, simply lay a card face-up on the table. Scoring: Point awards for special cards, most cards, most spades, and sweeps. - Face cards (J, Q, K) have no numeric value for combining; they can only pair. - Aces = 1 (or 14 in Royal Cassino variant). - A 'sweep' (clearing entire tableau) earns 1 bonus point. The sweeping card is turned face-up to track sweeps. - All table...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Always capture Big Cassino (10â™¦) and Little Cassino (2â™ ) when possibleâ€”combined they're 3 points. Never let them sit on the table unguarded. Intermediate: Build Management: Build toward a number you hold in your hand. Don't announce a build unless you're confident no opponent holds the matching capture card. Monitor the count of cards you've capturedâ€”crossing 27 cards triggers the Most Cards bonus (3 pts). Advanced: Sweep Timing: Set up a sweep (clearing the table) within 2-3 turns by building so all table cards combine into your target number. Track all four Acesâ€”each is a point. In 2-player, count the spades gone to predict whether you or your opponent will hit the 7-spade threshold. - Tip: Never trail a card that creates an easy capture opportunity for your opponent. - Tip: Keep track of cards captured throughout the hand, not just at scoring. - Tip: In partnership...",
    "variations": "- Royal Cassino: Aces count as 1 OR 14. Jacks = 11, Queens = 12, Kings = 13 for building purposes. Creates much richer building combinations. - Draw Cassino: Players draw to replenish hand to 4 after each turn, rather than waiting for the whole hand to run out. - Spade Cassino: Each spade captured earns individual points (Aâ™ =2, 2â™ =2, others=1). A much higher scoring variant."
