    "overview": "Clag (also known as Nominations) is a British card game that originated in the Royal Air Force during World War II. The name is an acronym for 'Clouds Low Aircraft Grounded'â€”reflecting when air crews played while waiting for suitable weather. It combines Oh Hell!-style exact bidding with 22 deals including 8 special deals with unique rules (No Trumps, Misere, Blind, Twos Wild, etc.). Origin: UK (Royal Air Force, WWII) Players: 4-7 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 45-90 minutes",
    "history": "Clag originated in the British Royal Air Force during World War II. According to Taylor Foss, the name CLAG is an acronym for 'Clouds Low Aircraft Grounded'â€”air crews played this game while waiting for weather conditions suitable for flying. Also known as 'Clagg' or 'Cleg'. - 1940s: Originated in RAF during WWII - Post-War: Spread through British card-playing circles - Present: Played by card game enthusiasts, particularly in UK The game evolved from Oh Hell! by adding special deals with unique rules. The 22-deal structure with 8 special deals in the middle distinguishes it from simpler exact-bidding games. Strongly associated with British military culture, particularly the RAF. The acronym name reflects the practical humor of service personnel.",
    "setup": "Players: 4-7 players. Deal and play are clockwise. Deck: Standard 52-card pack. Cards rank A-K-Q-J-10-9-8-7-6-5-4-3-2. Equipment: Standard deck, score pad for cumulative scoring. Dealing: 22 deals total. Deals 1-7: 1-7 cards each. Deals 8-15: special deals, 7 cards each. Deals 16-22: 7-1 cards each. Trump determined by turning next card after deal (except special deals).",
    "rules": "Objective: Players try to predict the exact number of tricks they will win in each deal. Over 22 deals (hand sizes 1–7, then eight special seven-card deals, then 7 down to 1), each player scores one point per trick won plus a 10-point bonus for making their bid exactly. The player with the highest cumulative score after all 22 deals wins. Gameplay: 22 deals (1-7 cards, then 8 special deals, then 7-1). Bid exact tricks. Trump by turned card (except specials: No Trump, Misere, Blind, Twos Wild, etc). Follow suit. Dealer's bid restricted so total ≠ cards dealt. Score for making bid. Scoring: Cumulative scoring over 22 deals. 1 point per trick + 10 for exact bid (normal deals). Special deals have modified scoring. - 22 deals with specific structure - Deals 1-7 and 16-22 are normal Oh Hell! deals - Deals 8-15 are special deals with unique rules - Dealer's bid restricted so total cannot...",
    "strategy": "Basic: In normal deals, bid based on your hand strength. Remember the dealer restrictionâ€”if you're dealer, you may be forced into a difficult bid. Intermediate: Special deals require different strategies: In Misere, try to lose every trick. In Blind, bid conservatively. In Twos Wild, wild cards change hand valuation significantly. Advanced: Track cumulative scoresâ€”players behind may take more risks. In Dealer Calls Trumps, dealer has significant advantage. Coordinate play to cause ties in Highest Number deal. - Tip: The dealer restriction (can't make total equal cards dealt) is crucialâ€”plan accordingly - Tip: In Misere, low cards are valuable; in normal deals, high cards are valuable - Tip: Twos Wild dramatically changes hand evaluationâ€”treat Twos as potentially any card",
