    "overview": "Clobyosh (classic Bela or Klaberjass) is considered by many experts to be the finest two-player card game in existence. It combines the Jass-style trump ranking (J-9 high) with a unique melding phase and a 'Schmeiss' (Throw-in) rule. The game is played worldwide, particularly in Jewish communities, the Netherlands, and the Balkans. It is an intense duel of hand evaluation, bluffing (in melds), and precise endgame calculation. Origin: Central Europe (Jewish Diaspora) Players: 2 Deck: 32 cards Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-30 minutes",
    "history": "Likely originated in the 17th or 18th century within Jewish communities in the Netherlands or Hungary. The name 'Klaberjass' may relate to 'Klaverjas' (Dutch for Jack of Clubs). - 1910s: Spreads to Paris, London, and New York via Jewish migration. - 1920s: Known as 'Clob' or 'Kalabrias' in London's East End and NYC. - Present: Remains a staple of competitive 2-player gaming, alongside Piquet and Gin Rummy. While Belote evolved into a 4-player game, Clobyosh remained the purist's 2-player duel. The 'Bela' variant is the national card game of Croatia. Often associated with 'Coffee House' culture. The game has a rich glossary of Yiddish and Dutch terms (e.g., 'Jas', 'Manel', 'Bela', 'Stuk').",
    "setup": "Players: Two players only. One is the dealer and one is forehand (the dealer's opponent, who speaks first in bidding). The dealer rotates after each hand; there are no partnerships and each plays for their own score toward 501. Deck: 32 cards (A-10-K-Q-J-9-8-7). Equipment: Standard pack, score pad. Dealing: Deal 6 cards to each player (3-3). Turn the next card face up (The Proposal). Bidding follows. Once a trump is settled, deal 3 more cards to each player (total 9 each). The bottom card of the deck is turned face up (for information only).",
    "rules": "Objective: Score more points than the opponent to win the deal. Usually played to 501. Gameplay: 1. **Bidding**: Dealer's opponent speaks first. Can 'Take' (accept passed card), 'Schmeiss' (offer re-deal), or 'Pass'. If both pass, second round allows naming any suit. 2. **Melding**: Before playing to the first trick, announce sequences. Only the best one counts. 3. **Play**: Follow suit. Must Trump if void. Must Over-trump if possible. 4. **Winning**: If Maker scores > Opponent, points stand. If Maker fails (BÃªte), Opponent gets ALL points. Scoring: Points are cumulative. - The 'Dix' (7 of Trumps) can be swapped for the turned trump card if the trump was accepted in the first round. - Melds are declared *before* the first trick is complete, revealing information. - You do not need to win a trick to score 'Bella', but you DO need to win a trick to score Sequences. Win condition: 501...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Don't take the bid unless you have ~60 points in hand (including potential melds). The Jack is crucial (20 pts). Intermediate: The 'Schmeiss' Bluff: Offering to throw in the hand (Schmeiss) usually implies a weak hand with trumps, but can be a bluff to get out of a mediocre deal. Opponent must decide to accept the restart or force you to play (often making the suit trump automatically). Advanced: Endgames: With 9 cards, counting is exact. You can calculate if the opponent *must* hold the Ten or Ace based on previous tricks. Squeeze them by leading a suit they must trump. - Tip: Always exchange the 7 (Dix) if you canâ€”it puts a trump in your hand and the Ace/10 might be waiting in the turn-up. - Tip: Melds reveal your hand structure. Be careful declaring a sequence if it tells the opponent you have no trumps.",
    "variations": "- Bela (Croatian): National variant. Often played to 1001. 4-player partnership version also popular. - Alsatian Clob: No 'Schmeiss' rule. Simplified bidding."
