    "overview": "Compendium game with six different hands per round: Barbu, Waterloo, Siege, Touche, Guillotine, and Dominos. The Director (dealer) chooses which hand to play; players pay penalties to the Director. The holder of the Coup card may try to overthrow the Director by achieving the hand objective; success makes them Director. Winner is the player with the most money after a number of rounds equal to the number of players. Origin: USA Players: 2-6 Deck: 32-card stripped (A-7 per suit) plus optional Coup card Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 60-90 minutes",
    "history": "Believed to have been first played by political prisoners in France over two centuries ago. Published in 1966 by Parker Brothers; the basic game is related to the traditional French game Barbu, with the Coup and Hidden Coup mechanics likely an American addition. - Late 18th / early 19th century: Game first played by political prisoners in France. - 1966: Parker Brothers published the commercial version with Coup and Hidden Coup mechanics. The game developed from an older French game played by political prisoners into the published Parker Brothers version of 1966. The Coup and Hidden Coup overthrow mechanics were added, likely as an American innovation, transforming the basic Barbu-style play into a compendium with a directorship challenge. The game retains French thematic elements (francs as currency, political-prisoner origin story). It was published in the United States and played in...",
    "setup": "Players: Two to six players; each plays for himself; partnerships not permitted. For three players, remove sevens and eights from the deck. Deck: Standard 32-card pack (eight cards per suit: Ace, 10, King, Queen, Jack, 9, 8, 7) plus one special Coup card. Suits are Spades, Hearts, Diamonds, Clubs; Ace is highest, then 10, K, Q, J, 9, 8, 7. Equipment: 32-card deck (or standard 52 with agreed stripping), optional Coup card or designated wild card; play money in francs (e.g. 10,000 per player). Dealing: Director shuffles and deals eight cards face down to each player and places one card face down in the center. If the center card is the Coup card, Director shuffles and deals again.",
    "rules": "Objective: Accumulate the most money by the end of the game. Each round has six hands; the Director chooses which hand type to play. In Director play, players pay penalties to the Director for taking forbidden cards or tricks. The Coup card holder may attempt a Coup (or Hidden Coup in Waterloo, Siege, Guillotine) to overthrow the Director and collect from others. Gameplay: After the deal, the Coup card holder announces whether they will try for a Coup. If not, they discard the Coup card and take the center card. The Director then selects one of the six hands (Barbu, Waterloo, Siege, Touche, Guillotine, Dominos) and leads. In trick-taking hands, players must follow suit if possible; highest card in the suit led wins the trick. Penalties are paid to the Director as each hand specifies. In Dominos, play is by sequences (next higher or lower in suit or same rank in another suit); first out...",
    "strategy": "Basic: As Director, avoid taking penalty cards; get rid of high penalty cards early by playing them on others' winners or discarding when you cannot follow suit. As Coup attemptor, choose a hand where your cards give you a realistic chance (e.g. Barbu with several Hearts, Siege with long Spades). Intermediate: As Director, choose the hand that maximizes your chance to collect penalties given the current deal; avoid leading penalty suits first when rules forbid it. When holding the Coup card, attempt a Coup only when your hand supports the chosen objective (e.g. enough Hearts for Barbu, long Spades for Siege). In Waterloo and Siege, consider whether opponents can block you before committing. Track which hands remain in the round so you do not announce an impossible Coup. Advanced: Use Hidden Coup in Waterloo, Siege, or Guillotine when you can take all penalty cards without revealing...",
    "variations": "- Short Game: Eliminate Barbu and Touche entirely. Each round consists of three of the four remaining hands (Waterloo, Siege, Guillotine, Dominos). As soon as three hands have been completed the Directorship passes to the next player. All other rules remain the same."
