    "history": "Country/Region of Origin: France Time Period: Late 20th century Historical Development: Named after the Courchevel ski resort in the French Alps; reportedly invented by tourists. Commonly played at the Aviation Club de France in Paris (now closed). Cultural Significance: Popular in France; spread to UK and other parts of Europe. - Late 20th century: Game invented at or named after Courchevel ski resort - Aviation Club de France: Main venue in Paris until closure - Modern Era: Played in France and UK; Hi-Low 8-or-better variety exists",
    "setup": "Players: Minimum 2, maximum 9. Optimal 4-6. No partnerships. Deck: Type: Standard 52-card deck. One deck. No jokers. Equipment: Standard 52-card deck, chips. Dealing: Five hole cards each, dealt face down. One community card dealt face up before the first betting round. After that round, dealer burns one card and deals two more face up (three-card flop). Then turn (one card) and river (one card), with a burn before each. Betting after each street.",
    "rules": "Objective: Make the best five-card hand using exactly two of your five hole cards and exactly three of the five board cards. Win the pot at showdown. Gameplay: Deal five hole cards to each player and one community card face up. First betting round. Dealer burns one, deals two more board cards (flop). Second betting round. Turn card; third betting round. River card; fourth betting round. Showdown: active players show all five hole cards; best five-card hand using exactly two hole and three board wins the pot. Scoring: Pot to best hand at showdown. - Five hole cards; one board card before first betting, then flop (two more), turn, river. - Must use exactly two hole cards and three board cards. - Standard poker hand ranking. Pot Limit or other limits as agreed. Win condition: Make the best five-card hand using exactly two hole cards and three board cards. Win the pot at showdown.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Play hands that can make strong combinations with two cards from five; the exposed first board card gives extra information before the first betting round. Intermediate: Use the single exposed card to narrow ranges; position and starting hand selection matter; avoid hands that need three specific board cards. Advanced: Equity calculations with five hole cards and one known board card; blocker effects; pot control; adjust to Pot Limit or table stakes. - Tip: Exactly two hole and three board cards; the first board card is visible before preflop betting. - Tip: More flexibility than 4-card Omaha; the early board card changes preflop strategy. - Tip: Courchevel Hi-Lo 8-or-better splits the pot between high and low.",
    "variations": "- Courchevel High: Standard version; only the best high hand wins the pot. Pot Limit or other limits. - Courchevel Hi-Low 8-or-Better: Pot splits between best high and best qualifying low (8-high or better); each hand uses exactly two hole and three board. Available in some venues."
