    "history": "Developed alongside modern Poker as a 'home game' tradition. While casino poker standardized games like Texas Hold'em for efficiency and house edge visibility, social clubs and home games leaned into 'Dealer's Choice' to level the playing field and keep the game fresh. - Early 20th Century: Variations of 'Dealer's Choice' appear in home game descriptions. - 1970s: The WSOP introduces 'Mixed Games' which are structured professional versions of dealer's choice. - Present: A staple of casual card nights globally. Has evolved from simple Draw/Stud choices to complex 'wild card' games (like Baseball or Follow the Queen) and even non-poker gambling games (like 7-27 or Acey Deucey). Symbolic of 'Kitchen Table Poker' culture - informal, social, and prioritizing fun over strict professional GTO (Game Theory Optimal) play.",
    "setup": "Players: 5-7 players (Ideal: 6). Too many players limit 'Shared Card' or 'Stud' variants; too few make 'Draw' variants less tactical. Deck: Standard 52-card pack. Two packs of different colors are recommended (one being shuffled while the other is in play). Equipment: Poker chips, dealer button, reference sheet for complex variants. Dealing: The first dealer is chosen randomly. After each variant is complete, the deal passes clockwise.",
