    "history": "Dobbm is a hyper-local tradition centered in the village of Fulpmes. It was documented in 1996 by John McLeod and Remigius Geiser. - Traditional: Played for generations in the Stubai Valley. - 1960s: Transition from Salzburger pattern cards to Doppeldeutsche (Tell) cards. - 1996: formally documented for card game historians at Gasthof Jenewein. Related to Brixental Bauerntarock and Zuger Tapp. It remains popular in Fulpmes while being virtually unknown in neighboring valleys like Wipptal. Used as a major social catalyst in the Stubaital region across all generations.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 active players. If 5 play, the dealer 'feiert' (takes a holiday) and sits out. Deck: 36 German-suited (Tell) cards. Suits: Hearts, Leaves, Acorns, Bells. Equipment: 36-card deck, score sheet. Dealing: 8 cards to each player dealt in batches of 4. Four cards go face-down to the center as the 'Dobb'. The dealer for a hand is always the 'declarer' from the previous hand.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win tricks to capture point cards and reach a target score. Points are: Ace/Sow (11), Ten (10), King (4), Ober (3), Unter (2). Total: 120 points per hand. Gameplay: 1. Bidding: Players bid 'Ordinary' or 'Solo'. In an Ordinary game, the declarer takes the 'Dobb' and discards 4 cards (points count for them). 2. Trumps: Declarer names the trump suit. 3. The Dobb: In a Solo, the Dobb points count for the dealer (who is celebrating), not the soloist. 4. Card Play: Clockwise. Must follow suit. If unable, must play a trump if possible. Scoring: Points are counted from captured ace-ten cards and contract success. - The 6 of Bells ('Weli') is just a normal card in Dobbm. - The Ace is called the 'Sow' (Sau). - Defenders can 'schieben' (smear) points on their partner's trick. Win condition: Highest cumulative score after agreed hands or local target wins.",
    "strategy": "Basic: As declarer, discard weak suits into the Dobb to create 'voids' for trumping. Intermediate: Use 'schinden' - defenders holding back high cards to win later tricks with established winners. Advanced: Recognize a 'Fuchs' (fox) - a single card in a suit that can be played to trick opponents into believing you have more of that suit. - Tip: The Heart Sow is the most powerful trump starter. - Tip: Don't lead your top trumps immediately; force defenders to use theirs on side-suit tricks.",
