    "history": "Derived from Six-Card Cribbage, which dates back to the early 17th century (invented by Sir John Suckling). The domino adaptation emerged as a portable alternative for pub play. - 17th Century: Cribbage invented in England. - 19th-20th Century: Domino variations developed as companion games in working-class pubs. - Modern Era: Documented in various domino-play manuals and rulebooks. The transition from cards to tiles required redefining 'rank' as the total pip count of a domino (ranging from 0 to 12). Commonly played in environments where tiles were more durable or culturally preferred than cards.",
    "setup": "Players: Two players. One is designated the dealer, the other the non-dealer; they exchange roles after each hand. All 28 tiles are mixed, each player draws six tiles, then each discards two face down into the crib. Deck: A standard set of 28 double-six dominoes. Equipment: Dominoes, Cribbage board (or score sheet). Dealing: All 28 tiles are face-down and shuffled. Each player draws 6 tiles. Two from each hand are discarded into a four-tile 'Crib' belonging to the dealer. One tile is turned over from the boneyard as the 'Starter'.",
