    "overview": "Nine-Card Don (also known as Big Don or Welsh Don) is a traditional British pub game for four players in fixed partnerships. It is a descendant of the 17th-century game All Fours. Unlike the Irish version, only nine cards are dealt to each player, leaving a significant portion of the deck unseen, which adds a layer of uncertainty and boneyard-tracking to the strategy. Origin: United Kingdom (specifically North West England and South Wales) Players: 4 (Partnerships) Deck: 52 cards Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-30 minutes",
    "history": "Don evolved from Dom Pedro in the 19th century. While Ireland developed the 13ndcard version, British players in Cheshire, Lancashire, and South Wales standardized the 9ndcard version. - 1800s: All Fours variations like Dom Pedro spread through the UK. - Early 20th Century: The 9-card version becomes a staple in industrial pub culture (the 'Black Country' and Welsh mining valleys). - Modern Era: Maintained by dedicated leagues such as the Bolton Don League and the Abercarn Crib & Don League. The game is uniquely characterized by the immediate scoring ('pegging') of fives and trumps during play. Used as a major social catalyst in the British regimental system and workingndclass social clubs.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in fixed partnerships (2 vs 2). Deck: Standard 52-card deck (only 36 cards are dealt). Equipment: 52-card deck, peg board. Dealing: Each player receives 9 cards (dealt in batches of 3). The remaining 16 cards are set aside and not used in the hand.",
    "rules": "Objective: Reach 121 points by capturing scoring cards and winning the 'Game' point. Gameplay: 1. The Pitch: The player to the dealer's left leads a card. The suit of this card is the trump suit. 2. Trick Play: Players must follow suit if possible. In strict All Fours tradition, some leagues allow 'playing a trump' even if you have the led suit. 3. Card Ranking: A (high) K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2. Scoring: first team to reach an agreed total (91 or 121) wins the game. - Only the 36 dealt cards are scored for the 'Game' point. - Fives are often called 'Don cards' or 'Pedros'. - Winning the 'Low' point requires tracking the lowest trump seen in play. Win condition: first team to reach an agreed total (91 or 121) wins the game.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Identify the trump suit quickly and secure your 5ndpoint cards (all 5s regardless of suit) as soon as your team wins a trick. Intermediate: Since 16 cards are missing, do not assume high trumps like the King or Queen are in play. If you hold the Jack, it might be the highest trump. Advanced: Bidding/Pitching Strategy: Pitch a suit where you hold a 5 to secure immediate points, or pitch a suit where you have a void to allow your partner to trump in. - Tip: All 5s are high-value targets. Don't waste them on tricks your partner can't win. - Tip: The 'Game' point is decided by the 10s and Aces; try to capture these even if they don't provide immediate pegging points.",
