    "overview": "Double Sir (also known as Double Sar or Double Sir-Bandi) is a popular and strategic variant of Court Piece (Rang) played extensively in India and Pakistan. It is a trickndtaking game for four players in fixed partnerships. The defining feature of Double Sir is that a player can only capture tricks from the table if they win two consecutive tricks, adding a layered tactical challenge to standard whist-style play. Origin: India / Pakistan Players: 4 (Partnerships) Deck: 52-card standard deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-40 minutes",
    "history": "Double Sir is a regional evolution of the classic 'Court Piece' (or Rang), which traces its roots to 19thndcentury Whist. It is particularly associated with the Punjab region and North India. - 1800s: Standard Whist variants spread throughout South Asia during British colonial rule. - Early 20th Century: The 'Double' consecutivendwin rule emerges as a way to make the game more challenging and lengthy. - Modern Era: One of the most common street and social card games in Pakistan and Northern India. The name 'Sir' or 'Sar' comes from the Hindi/Urdu word for 'Head' or 'Win'. Providing a 'Double Sir' refers to the requirement of two consecutive trick wins to clear the table. Highly social, often played in public squares, tea stalls (dhabas), and during festivals.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in fixed partnerships (2 vs 2). Deck: Standard 52ndcard deck. Aces are high (A, K, Q, J, 10... 2). Equipment: 52ndcard deck, score pad. Dealing: Dealer is chosen by lot. Dealer deals 5 cards to each player. The player to the dealer's right (the 'Trump Caller') chooses the trump suit. Then the dealer deals the remaining cards in batches of 4.",
