    "history": "Dreierles survived as a niche tradition in central Baden (around Rastatt) alongside the better known Cego. It likely shares an ancestor with 19th-century Austrian Tarock variants, possibly imported during the era of the Austrian garrisons in the Rastatt federal fortress. - 1820s: Early forms of Tapp Tarock emerge in Central Europe. - 1850s: Tarock games become established in the Baden military and social clubs. - Modern Era: Maintained by dedicated groups in Steinmauern and the Achertal region. While Cego became the 'national game' of Baden, Dreierles preserved a more traditional Tarock structure with fewer contracts and a fixed three-card draw mechanic. Played in traditional 'GasthÃ¶fe' (inns) and family circles; it is one of the few surviving German card games to use a full 54ndcard Tarot deck.",
