    "overview": "Durak ('Fool' in Russian) is Russia's most widely played card game. The goal is to NOT be the last player holding cardsâ€”the last player is the 'Durak' (Fool). Players attack with cards; the defender must beat each attacking card with a higher card of the same suit or any trump. If unable to defend, they take all attacked cards into their hand. A 36-card deck is used (6 through Ace). After each bout, players draw to replenish to 6 cards. The Podkidnoy (passing) variantâ€”where the attack can be redirected to the next playerâ€”is the standard form. Origin: Russia Players: 2-6 Deck: 36 cards (6 through Ace) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-40 minutes",
    "history": "One of Russia's oldest documented card games. References appear from at least the 18th century. It was the default family card game across the Soviet Union and remains the most ubiquitous Russian card game today. - 1700s-1800s: Documented in Russian card literature. - 1900s: Standard across USSR households. - Present: Most popular card game in Russia, Ukraine, and many post-Soviet states. Two main variants: Prostoy Durak (simple defense, no passing) and Podkidnoy Durak (the attacker can 'throw in' additional matching-rank cards, and the attack can be 'redirected' by defender if a matching rank appears). Podkidnoy is the dominant modern form. A Russian national treasure. The term 'Durak' (Fool) has cultural weightâ€”losing at Durak is a social embarrassment. The game is played across all age groups.",
    "setup": "Players: Two to six players. Best with four playing in partnerships (two against two, partners opposite). Can also be played by two to six as individuals, or six in two teams of three sitting alternately. The player to the dealer's left is the first defender; the attacker sits to the defender's right. Deck: 36 cards: Ranks 6-7-8-9-10-J-Q-K-A in each of 4 suits. Equipment: 36-card deck. Dealing: 6 cards each. Turn one card as trump indicator; place under stock (suit visible). Stock face-down on top.",
    "rules": "Objective: Shed all of your cards before the talon is exhausted and play ends. The game has no winner—only a loser: the last player left holding cards is the durak (fool) and loses the hand; in partnership play the team left with cards loses. Gameplay: Attack with cards; defender beats or picks up. Refill to 6 from stock. Last with cards = Durak (loser). Scoring: Elimination game â€” last with cards is Fool. - Cannot have more than 6 cards in your hand after a win. Refill back to 6 after each bout. - Once stock is empty, no more drawing. Play out remaining cards. - Last player with cards = Durak (Fool). Loser of the session. Win condition: Empty your hand after stock is depleted.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Attack with low cards (6s, 7s, 8s). Save trumps for defense or critical attacks. Never attack with a trump unless you need to. Intermediate: Card Counting: Track which trump ranks have been played. In Podkidnoy, use passing strategically to offload the attack to a player with fewer cards. Advanced: Endgame: Once the stock is empty, you know all remaining cards in the game. Calculate whether to attack aggressively to push remaining cards onto opponents or defend conservatively. - Tip: Use 6s and 7s to throw in and exhaust the defender's good high cards. - Tip: Never pick up cards willingly unless the alternative is surrendering a trump.",
    "variations": "- Prostoy Durak (Simple): No passing. Defender must simply beat all attacks or take. Simpler variant. - Perevodnoy Durak (Transfer): Defender can always pass the attack to next player if they have a matching cardâ€”even after defending a card. - Cooperative Durak: All players except the defender cooperate to attack together."
