    "overview": "Earl of Coventry is a classic English 'Stops' or matching round game, closely related to Snip Snap Snorum. Unlike many complex card games, it is defined by its rhythmic verbal component - players recite rhyming phrases as they match the ranks played by their opponents. The game is a fast-paced shedding contest where the primary goal is to reach the 'stop' card (the fourth card of a rank) to regain control of the lead. Historically popular as a Victorian-era parlor game, it remains a charming social exercise in rhythm and rank tracking. Origin: England Players: 4-8 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Beginner Duration: 10-20 minutes",
    "history": "Originating in 18th-century England, it is part of a broad family of 'rhyming games' that were common before the rise of trick-taking dominance. The name refers to the Earl of Coventry, though whether it honors a specific historical figure or simply used the name for its phonetic fit in the rhyme is debated. - 1700s: Early forms of Snip Snap Snorum documented in English clubs. - 1800s: Becomes a popular 'round game' for children and mixed social groups. - Modern: Survives primarily as a historical curiosity and children's card game. Evolution from a gambling 'stop' game to a social 'rhyme' game for all ages. Used in the 19th century as a social icebreaker, where the emphasis was as much on the spirited recitation of the rhyme as it was on the card play.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 to 8 players; best with a larger group to ensure cards are widely distributed. Deck: Standard 52-card pack. Aces are low. Equipment: 52-card deck. Dealing: The entire deck is dealt out one by one. It does not matter if some players have one card more than others.",
