    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize leading ranks of which you hold multiple cards. If you hold three of a kind, you can almost guarantee being the one to play the 'best of all the three,' but you'll need the 4th player to enable you or wait for someone else to lead the 4th. Intermediate: Leading Pairs: Leading from a pair is safe, as it allows you to maintain some control over when that rank is exhausted. Advanced: Stop Tracking: Keep track of which ranks have already had their 4th card ('the Earl') played. Once a 4th card is played, that rank is 'dead'. Use this knowledge to determine which other ranks are likely to be playable. - Tip: If you hold the 4th card of a rank (the Earl), you have 'The Stop'. Save it for when you need to regain the lead to play your own advantageous sequences. - Tip: Don't lead a single card if you hold the other three; you are giving 'The Earl' (the lead) to an opponent.",
