    "overview": "Ã‰cartÃ© (French for 'discarded') is one of the most elegant and historically significant two-player trick-taking games from France. Popularized in the early 19th century, it is famous for its unique card ranking (where the King is highest and the Ace sits between the King and Queen) and its signature 'proposal' mechanic, where players must negotiate whether to discard and draw new cards. It is a game of high tension, as refusing a proposal can lead to double penalties, making every decision a psychological contest. Origin: France Players: 2 Deck: 32-card deck (7-Ace) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-20 minutes",
    "history": "Ã‰cartÃ© evolved from the older game 'Triomphe' and emerged in its modern form during the Napoleonic era. It was the premier gambling and social game for the European aristocracy throughout the 1800s. - 1820s: Rules are standardized and publish in French gaming manuals. - 1840s: Reaches peak popularity in London and Paris clubs. - Early 20th Century: Gradually replaced by Bridge, but remains a staple of French card tradition. Influenced the development of many modern 'discard' and 'proposal' mechanics in card games. Frequently mentioned in 19th-century literature (e.g., Balzac, Thackeray) as a game of skill and wit among the high-born.",
    "setup": "Players: Écarté is played by exactly two players. The players cut to determine the dealer; the elder hand (non-dealer) sits opposite the dealer. There are no partnerships. Deck: 32ndcard French pack (A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7). Equipment: 32ndcard French pack (A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7). Dealing: 1. Five cards are dealt to each player (usually in sets of 2 and 3). 2. The 11th card is turned face-up to determine the Trump Suit.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win at least three tricks to score points. The first player to reach 5 points wins the game. Gameplay: 1. King of Trumps: If the turned-up card is a King, the dealer scores 1 point immediately. If a player holds the King of Trumps in their hand, they must declare it before the first trick to score 1 point. 2. The Proposal: The nonnddealer (Elder Hand), if unhappy with their cards, can 'propose' to discard. If the dealer agrees, both players discard any number of cards and draw replacements from the deck. This can continue until the deck is empty or a proposal is refused. 3. The Play: Standard trickndtaking. You MUST follow suit if possible and MUST win the trick (over-trump or over-play) if you are able. Scoring: Points or match rules. - Must follow suit and win the trick if possible. - Aces rank between Kings and Queens. - King of Trumps is a powerful scoring card and must...",
    "strategy": "Basic: If your hand is mediocre, propose! In Ã‰cartÃ©, the dealer has a slight advantage in the draw phase, so the Elder Hand must be aggressive in trying to improve their hand. Intermediate: The 'Jeux de RÃ¨gle': Certain hands (usually involving 3 certain winners or 2 winners and high trumps) should NEVER be proposed. These are mathematically sound hands that are likely to win at least 3 tricks. Advanced: Refusal Tactics: Refusing a proposal is a high-risk move. Only do it if you are confident you can win 3 tricks, as failure gives your opponent 2 pointsâ€”effectively handing them half the game. - Tip: Always declare the King of Trumps immediately; if you forget, you lose the point for that hand. - Tip: Trumps are extremely scarce in a 5ndcard hand; leading a high trump early to clear the field is often correct.",
