    "history": "Egyptian Ratscrew is a 20th-century American game derived from the British game Egyptian Rat Pack and the classic Slapjack. The 'Egyptian' name may be a humorous Americanism unrelated to Egypt. - Pre-1900s: Slapjack played in UK and USA. - Mid-1900s: Egyptian Rat Pack documented in UK. - Late 1900s: Egyptian Ratscrew codified in American college culture. - Present: One of the most popular casual card games among teenagers and college students. The addition of multiple 'legal slap' conditions beyond simple Jack matching (sandwiches, doubles, marriages, sequences, etc.) gives ERS far more action and house-rule flexibility than Slapjack. A staple of American teenage and college card culture. Known for the physical drama of slapping - and the painful penalty for false slaps.",
    "setup": "Players: Two or more players; typically 2 to 7. The number of players is limited only by each participant's ability to reach the central pile. Cards are dealt as evenly as possible. Deck: 52 standard cards. Equipment: Cards. Flat surface. Fast reflexes. Dealing: Deal entire deck evenly. Players do not look at their cards; hold them face-down.",
    "rules": "Objective: Collect all 52 cards by winning the central pile through valid slaps (on doubles, sandwiches, sequences, etc.) and by claiming the pile when opponents fail to counter face-card challenges. Gameplay: Players take turns playing one card face-up to the center pile. When a face card or Ace is played, the next player must play a face card within a set number of turns or forfeit their pile to the face-card player: - Ace: 4 chances - King: 3 chances - Queen: 2 chances - Jack: 1 chance If they play a face card: the challenge passes forward. Scoring: The first player to collect all 52 cards wins the game. - When a player runs out of cards, they may still slap back in. - If a player false-slaps and has fewer than 2 cards, they are eliminated. - Only designated slap conditions are valid (house rules vary widely). Win condition: Hold all 52 cards.",
