    "overview": "Eight Off is a sophisticated and highly winnable solitaire card game that served as a direct precursor to the ubiquitous FreeCell. Its defining feature is the namesake eight 'free cells' (reserves)â€”twice the number found in its more famous descendant. However, this extra mobility is balanced by a stricter building rule: cards in the tableau must be built down by the CORRECT SUIT, rather than alternating colors. This makes Eight Off a pure game of logic and strategy where almost every deal can be solved with perfect planning. Origin: Uncertain (Modern Solitaire) Players: 1 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-15 minutes",
    "history": "Eight Off rose to prominence in the mid-20th century as part of the family of solitaire games that utilize 'cells' for temporary storage. It is often credited with inspiring Paul Alfille's creation of FreeCell in 1978. - Early 20th Century: Variations of 'reserves' games circulate in gambling and social circles. - 1970s: Standardized as 'Eight Off' in computer solitaire collections. - Modern: Maintained as a standard challenge for solitaire enthusiasts who find FreeCell's alternating color rule too lenient. The game shifted from simple foundations to its present form where sequence moves are heavily dependent on empty reserve calculation. Regarded as a 'Thinking Person's' solitaire due to its extremely high winnable percentage for skilled players.",
    "setup": "Players: One player only. Eight Off is a patience (solitaire) game played by a single person; there are no opponents or partners. Deck: Single standard 52-card deck; all cards dealt face up into eight tableau columns and four reserve cells at the start. Equipment: Standard deck and a playing surface; no other equipment required. Dealing: Deal face up: six cards to each of eight tableau columns, and the remaining four cards into the first four reserve (free) cells.",
    "rules": "Objective: Move all 52 cards to the four foundation piles, organized by suit from Ace up to King. Gameplay: 1. Foundation Building: Move any available Ace to a foundation slot. Build up on it in the same suit (2, 3, 4...). 2. Tableau Building: You may move a card onto another card in the tableau if it is exactly one rank lower and of the SAME SUIT (e.g., 9 of Hearts onto 10 of Hearts). 3. Reserves: Any exposed card can be moved to an empty Free Cell. Only one card per cell. 4. Moving Sequences: You can move a correctly ordered suit-sequence if you have enough empty cells to 'shuttle' the cards. Movement is limited to (Empty Cells + 1). Scoring: Move all 52 cards to the four foundation piles, organized by suit from Ace up to King. - Only KINGS (or sequences starting with a King) can be moved into an empty tableau column. - Building is strictly SAME SUIT (unlike FreeCell which is...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize clearing out the first four occupied cells as quickly as possible to increase your mobility. Never move a card into a cell if you can keep it in the tableau. Intermediate: Empty Columns: An empty column is more valuable than a free cell because it can hold a whole sequence of cards (starting with a King). Work toward emptying a column early. Advanced: The Suit-Lock: Since building is by same suit, if you have a high card of a suit (like a Jack) buried under a low card of the SAME suit (like a 4), that column is 'locked' until the 4 is moved. Identify and solve these suit-locks immediately. - Tip: Always scan the tableau for Aces to move to foundations first. - Tip: Calculate sequence moves carefully; don't initiate a move if you won't have the cell space to complete it.",
