    "overview": "Eighty-Eight (also known as Texas 88) is an expansive, 6-player variation of the classic Texan domino game 'Texas 42'. Played with two full sets of Double-Six dominoes, the game scales up the complexity and the math, with 88 points available in every hand. Two teams of three players compete to outbid each other and reach 500 points first. It is a game of high communication, careful tracking of identical tiles, and intense partnership coordination, making it a favorite for large gatherings in its home state. Origin: United States (Texas) Players: 6 (Two teams of three) Deck: Two sets of Double-Six Dominoes (56 tiles total) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-60 minutes",
    "history": "Developed as a way to expand the 4-player 'Texas 42' into a game suitable for larger social groups. The specific rules were popularized by domino manufacturers like Puremco to encourage the use of double sets. - Late 19th Century: Texas 42 becomes the state's unofficial game. - 20th Century: Variations for 3, 5, and 6 players emerge. - Present: Texas 88 remains a specialized variant for large family and community tournaments. Evolution from a single-deck puzzle to a double-deck statistical challenge where tracking duplicate tiles is the key to success. Deeply tied to Texan social identity, often played at church socials, BBQ gatherings, and local domino halls.",
    "setup": "Players: 6 players divided into two teams of three. Players from opposite teams sit in alternating order around the table. Deck: 56 Dominoes (Two Double-Six sets mixed together). Equipment: 56 Dominoes (Two Double-Six sets mixed together). Dealing: Each player is dealt 9 tiles. 2 tiles are left face-down as 'The Kitty'.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score points by winning tricks and capturing 'count' tiles. Be the first team to reach 500 points. Gameplay: 1. Bidding: Starts left of dealer. Bids range from 60 to 88 (jumping in increments of 2). Multiples of 88 (176, 264, etc.) are used for aggressive hands. 2. The Kitty: The high bidder takes the 2 kitty tiles and discards 2 tiles of their choice (cannot discard count tiles into the kitty). 3. Play: Winner of the bid leads the first tile and names trumps (a specific suit or 'No Trumps'). 4. Identical Tiles: Since there are two sets, if two identical tiles are played in one trick, the FIRST one played wins. Scoring: Score points by winning tricks and capturing 'count' tiles. Be the first team to reach 500 points. - Must follow suit (follow the high end of the tile led). - Winning bidder reveals their trump choice AFTER exchanging with the kitty. - The kitty contains no...",
    "strategy": "Basic: In a 6-player game, information is highly fragmented. Trust your teammates to help clear suits you are weak in. Intermediate: The Duplicate Rule: Use the 'First One Wins' rule to your advantage. If you know the other set's Ace is still in play, play yours early to gain the lead on tie-breaks. Advanced: Kitty Management: Discard tiles into the kitty that clarify your 'No Trump' leads. If you can void a suit by discarding into the kitty, you can trump in earlier. - Tip: Bidding is conservative at the start; only jump above 60 if you have a strong set of doubles. - Tip: With 6 players, 'No Trump' bids are significantly riskier than in 4-player Texas 42.",
