    "overview": "Eleusis is a groundbreaking card game that functions more as a simulation of scientific discovery than a traditional trick-taking or shedding game. Invented by Robert Abbott in 1956, it is a game of inductive reasoning where the rules of play are not known at the start. One player, acting as 'God' or 'Nature', secretly writes down a mathematical or logical rule governing which cards may follow others. The remaining players must intuit this rule through trial and errorâ€”observing which of their plays are accepted as 'legal' or rejected as 'illegal'â€”effectively practicing the scientific method to empty their hands. Origin: United States Players: 4-8 Deck: Two standard 52-card decks (shuffled together) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-60 minutes",
    "history": "Eleusis was created by Robert Abbott, a mathematician and puzzle designer. It first gained international fame when it was featured in Martin Gardner's 'Mathematical Games' column in Scientific American (June 1959). - 1956: First conceived by Abbott as a 'poker-like' reasoning game. - 1959: Published in Scientific American; becomes a cult classic among mathematicians. - 1970s: Abbott releases 'The New Eleusis' with revised scoring and the 'Prophet' mechanic. - Present: Taught in science and logic courses to demonstrate the scientific method. The game evolved from a simple hidden-rule game to a complex social interaction where 'God' must balance the difficulty of the rule to maximize their own score. Often cited as the ultimate example of a game that mimics the process of scientific discovery and pattern recognition.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 to 8 players. One player is designated as 'God' (The Dealer) for the round. Deck: Two standard 52-card decks mixed together (104 cards total). Equipment: Two standard 52-card decks mixed together (104 cards total). Dealing: God deals 12 cards to each player. One card is placed face-up in the center to start the 'Main Line'.",
    "rules": "Objective: For players: Empty your hand by playing legal cards. For God: Create a rule that is neither too simple to guess immediately nor too complex to ever be discovered. Gameplay: 1. Turn: A player plays a card from their hand. 2. Validation: God announces if the card is 'Legal' or 'Illegal'. 3. Legal Play: The card is placed on the 'Main Line' (the horizontal row). 4. Illegal Play: The card is placed on a 'Sideline' (vertically beneath the last legal card), and the player must draw a penalty card. 5. Induction: Players observe the pattern of the Main Line vs. the Sidelines to guess the secret rule. Scoring: For players: Empty your hand by playing legal cards. For God: Create a rule that is neither too simple to guess immediately nor too complex to ever be discovered. - God's rule must be based ONLY on the sequence and properties of the cards on the table (not on the players'...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Testing Hypotheses: Don't just play what you think is legal. Occasionally play a card you suspect is UNLAWFUL to confirm the boundaries of the rule. Intermediate: The False Scent: If you are God, design a rule that initially looks like color alternation but is actually based on rank. This delays discovery and increases your score. Advanced: Prophecy Timing: Become the Prophet as soon as you are 90% sure. Waiting too long allows other players to dump more cards, reducing your lead. - Tip: Pay close attention to cards that were rejected. They are as informative as the ones that succeeded. - Tip: As God, keep the rule elegant. A rule like 'Red if King, otherwise even' is better than a string of ifndthen-else conditions.",
    "variations": "- Eleusis Express: Streamlined version: 12 cards each, no Prophet; players may guess the rule aloud on a valid play. No-play incorrect call costs 1 penalty card; correct call replaces hand with one fewer card. Scoring: 12 minus cards in hand, plus 6 for correct guess, plus 3 for emptying hand. - The New Eleusis: Version with Prophet: 14 cards dealt to each non-dealer; player may become Prophet after valid play to judge moves; white and black markers every tenth card; elimination after fourth marker."
