    "rules": "Objective: Collect four cards of the same suit to maximize your score (maximum 41). Gameplay: 1. Turn: On your turn, you draw one card (from the Stock or the Discard Pile) and then discard one card face-up. 2. One-Card Limit: You must always have exactly 4 cards at the end of your turn. 3. The Stop: Any player who has four cards of the SAME SUIT can declare 'Stop' (Selesai). The hand ends immediately. Scoring: Each player scores the value of cards in one suit (counted positive) minus cards of other suits (counted negative). Aces=11, 10/J/Q/K=10, 2-9=face value. Maximum possible score is 41 (A-K-Q-J of one suit). - You cannot pick up the previous player's discard and immediately discard it back. - If the Stock runs out, the discard pile is shuffled to form a new Stock. - If a player 'Stops' and their 4-card suit sum is lower than an opponent's mixed-hand sum, the stopper still wins the...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Commit early. If you are dealt two high cards of the same suit (e.g., A and Q of Clubs), discard everything else and hunt for Clubs immediately. Intermediate: The Discard Signal: Pay attention to what your opponent discards. If they drop a King of Diamonds, itâ€™s a strong sign they are NOT collecting Diamonds. You can safely build a Diamond hand knowing they likely won't take your discards. Advanced: Defensive Discarding: If you know the player to your left is collecting Spades, you should hold onto high Spades even if they don't fit your hand, to prevent them from hitting 41. - Tip: Don't wait for 41. If you have 38 or 39, it's often better to Stop before an opponent manages to fix a messy hand. - Tip: High cards (10s and Aces) are the only way to reach safe scores; dump low cards (2s and 3s) unless they are part of your core suit.",
