    "overview": "Escoba (Spanish for 'Broom') is the Spanish variant of Scopa, a fishing-capture game. The defining mechanic: a player captures ALL cards from the table by playing a card that, combined with the table cards, sums to exactly 15. This 'sweep' (escoba) earns bonus points. Card values: A=1, 2-7=face value, J=8, Q=9, K=10. Targets: Most cards, Most Coins, 7 of Coins (Siete Bello), and sweeps. Origin: Spain (via Italian Scopa) Players: 2-4 Deck: 40 cards (Spanish deck) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 15-30 minutes",
    "history": "Escoba is the Spanish adaptation of the Italian game Scopa. 'Escoba de 15' means 'Broom of 15'â€”sweeping the table clean. It is particularly popular in Argentina, Spain, and the Spanish-speaking Caribbean. - 1800s: Scopa spreads from Italy to Spain as Escoba. - Present: Standard card game in Spain and Latin America, especially Argentina. While Scopa uses a target sum of 15 in Italian regions, Escoba makes the '15 sum' the ONLY valid capture condition (where Scopa allows pairing by rank too). This simplification makes Escoba easier to learn but equally scoring-rich. A popular trattoria and cafÃ© game across Argentina, Uruguay, and Spain. Often paired with other Spanish games in home settings.",
    "setup": "Players: 2 or 4 (teams of 2). Deck: 40-card Spanish baraja (A, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, J, Q, K in 4 suits: Coins, Cups, Swords, Clubs). Values: A=1, 2-7=face, J=8, Q=9, K=10. Equipment: 40-card Spanish deck. Dealing: 3 cards each; 4 cards face-up on table. Redeal 3 when hands empty until deck done.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first player or team to score 21 or more points by capturing cards that sum to exactly 15, collecting majority points in multiple categories, and earning bonus points for sweeps. Gameplay: Players take turns playing one card to capture table cards that sum to exactly 15 with the played card. Captured cards and the playing card are taken and stored face-down. If no capture is possible, the card remains face-up on the table. Players cycle through their dealt cards, and new cards are dealt after all players have played their three cards. Play continues until the deck is exhausted. Scoring: Points from sweeps, most cards, most Coins, and Siete Bello. - Capture must sum to EXACTLY 15. - Only after a complete table clear is an Escoba scored. - Setenta (optional): Each player/team selects the highest card per suit summing to a maximum = best Setenta wins 1pt. Win condition:...",
