    "overview": "Euchre is the classic American partnership trick-taking game, played with a 24-32 card deck. Its hallmark feature is the 'Bower' system: the Jack of the called trump suit (Right Bower) and the Jack of the same-colored suit (Left Bower) are the two highest trump cards. Teams of two bid for the right to name trump and must win 3 of 5 tricks; failure results in being 'Euchred' and the opponent scoring 2 points. First team to 10 points wins. Origin: United States (Midwest, 1800s) Players: 4 (two fixed partnerships) Deck: 24 cards (9, 10, J, Q, K, A in 4 suits) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-60 minutes",
    "history": "Euchre developed in the early 19th century American Midwest. It is believed to derive from 'Jucker' or 'Juckerspiel,' a card game played by German immigrants in Pennsylvania, Ohio, and Indiana. It was the dominant American card game before Poker took over in the late 19th century. - 1820s-1840s: Euchre documented in the American Midwest. - 1850s-1880s: Most popular card game in the United States. - Late 1800s: Displaced by Poker as America's premier card game. - Present: Regional staple in the Great Lakes region (Ohio, Michigan, Indiana). Also popular in Canada and parts of UK/Australia. Euchre gave English-language card play the term 'Bower' (from German 'Bauer'=farmer/Jack) and the word 'Euchred' (deceived/defeated). The Joker was also originally introduced in American decks specifically for Euchre. A keystone of American Midwest social culture. Church halls, senior centers, and...",
    "setup": "Players: 4. Partners sit opposite. Deck: 24 cards: 9, 10, J, Q, K, A in each of 4 suits (Euchre deck). Some use 28 (add 7s and 8s) or 32 cards. Equipment: 24-card Euchre deck. Score markers or score paper. Dealing: 5 cards each in batches (2+3 or 3+2). Remaining 4 cards are the Kitty; top card turned face-up.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win at least 3 of 5 tricks with your partner; first team to 10 or 11 points wins the game. Gameplay: Players bid to make trump suit, then play tricks. The making team must win at least 3 tricks to score points. Special trump cards (bowers and aces) rank highest. Players can choose to play alone for higher scoring potential. Scoring: First to 10 points wins. - Right Bower (trump Jack) is the HIGHEST trump card. - Left Bower (matching-color Jack) is the SECOND highest trump card and IS trump suit (not its original suit). - Being Euchred = losing team scores 2 points. - Maker must win 3 of 5 tricks. Win condition: First team to 10 points.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Bower Power: Only order up if you hold the Right Bower, OR the Left Bower plus Ace of trump. Ordering up with 2 small trump and no Bower is very risky. Intermediate: Suit Control: Pay attention to the suits your partner playsâ€”if they lead a high off-suit card, they likely have a void in trump (signaling strength). Counter-order: Order up from the 3rd or 4th seat only if you can cover your partnerâ€”the 4th-seat order is the safest. Advanced: Going Alone Threshold: Go alone with Right Bower + Left Bower + Ace of trump + any other trump = near-certain sweep. Any less and the Euchre risk is high. Partner Signals: The opening lead of the highest card in a suit signals a void in that suit (asking partner to trump it). - Tip: 'Never order up the first round in seat 1 or 2 unless you have 3+ trump.' - Tip: The Left Bower is a trumpâ€”don't confuse it for off-suit when leading.",
    "variations": "- Canadian Euchre: Uses 52-card deck. Played in Ontario and Maritime Canada. - Buck Euchre: 4-6 player individual variant with no fixed partnerships. - Railroad Euchre: No passing allowed in first roundâ€”all players must order or pass in order, and dealer must pick up regardless."
