    "strategy": "Basic: Avoid winning early tricks if possible. Sluff (discard) low-to-mid range cards early to preserve high cards (Aces, 10s) for the final trick. If you must win, do so with the lowest possible card. Intermediate: The 3 Strategy: The 3 is the weakest card but carries a multiplier bonus in Cameroon variant. Winning the final trick with a 3 is more valuable than with higher cards because of the stake multiplier, though it requires precise control of previous tricks. Advanced: Suit Tracking: Memorize which high cards (particularly Aces and 10s) have been played in each suit. Position yourself to lead the final trick with a card that others cannot beat with their remaining cards. Force opponents into wasteful high-card plays early by leading their strong suits. - Tip: Never waste an Ace or 10 winning an early trick unless absolutely necessary. - Tip: If you win the 4th or 5th trick with...",
    "variations": "- Agram (Niger): Standard version with 6 cards dealt (3, then 3). Winner of 6th trick wins. - Sink-Sink (Niger): Identical to Agram except only 5 cards dealt (3, then 2). Winner of 5th trick wins. - Fapfap (Cameroon): 5 or 6 cards dealt. Includes scoring multipliers: winning final trick with a 3 = double stake; two consecutive tricks with 3's = quadruple stake. - Agram (Mali): 31-card deck (removes 6-2 of all suits, plus Ace of Spades). Unusual rank order: A-K-10-Q-9-8-J-7. 5 cards dealt (2, then 3). - Sparrow (Ghana): Related game with different deck composition and regional rule modifications."
