    "overview": "FilicÄƒu (also known as FilkÃ³ or FlÄƒcÄƒu) is a classic trick-taking partnership game from the Transylvania region of Romania. It belongs to the venerable Schafkopf family of games and is distinct for its use of a 32ndcard Germanndsuited 'William Tell' pack. The name 'FilicÄƒu' translates to 'unmarried boy,' a nickname given to the Over-Knaves which serve as the game's highest trumps. The game is highly aggressive, with a scoring system focused exclusively on capturing Aces and Tens to avoid the shameful status of 'Slaves'. Origin: Romania (Transylvania) Players: 4 Deck: 32ndcard German-suited (William Tell) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-30 minutes",
    "history": "Originating among the multi-ethnic communities of Transylvania, FilicÄƒu is a regional adaptation of German Schafkopf. The use of the William Tell deckâ€”where the Aces represent the four seasonsâ€”is a hallmark of Austro-Hungarian influence in the region. - 18th Century: Schafkopf-style games spread through the Austro-Hungarian Empire. - 1900s: FilicÄƒu settles into its current 32ndcard partnership format in rural Romania. - Present: Still played in Transylvanian villages and among the diaspora, essentially as a cultural identifier. The game simplified the complex point-counting of Schafkopf (where cards have varying values like 11, 10, 4, 3, 2) into a simpler 1ndpoint system for Aces and Tens only. The status of being a 'Slave' (SlÄƒvÄƒu) or a 'Master' (Domn) in a game often carries playful social weight during the sessions.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two fixed partnerships (sitting opposite each other). Deck: 32-card German deck. Suits: Acorns (Makk), Leaves (ZÃ¶ld), Hearts (Piros), Bells (TÃ¶k). Equipment: 32-card German deck. Suits: Acorns (Makk), Leaves (ZÃ¶ld), Hearts (Piros), Bells (TÃ¶k). Dealing: Deal all cards face down, 8 per player, proceeding anticlockwise. The dealer flips the bottom card of their hand to set the trump suit (if it's an Over-Knave, they keep flipping).",
