    "history": "Derived from the ancient Schafkopf (Sheep's Head) game of Germany, FilkÃ³ followed the migration and trade patterns of the Austro-Hungarian Empire. While it shares many rules with other regional variants like SixtydSix, its partnership structure and 'Master/Slave' social dynamic are uniquely preserved in the Hungarian AlfÃ¶ld. - 1800s: Schafkopf variants proliferate in the Great Plain regional social clubs. - 1920s: The 'Master and Servant' terminology becomes standardized in local tournaments. - Present: Frequently played in rural Hungary as a fast-paced alternative to Ulti. Over time, the game evolved from a standard point-counting game into a statusndbased competition where the 'shame' of losing is more significant than the numerical score. In many villages, it is a matter of pride to avoid being the 'Servant'. The Servant (often the dealer) is sometimes required to stand up or...",
