    "overview": "Five Card Stud Lowball is a variant of Five Card Stud where the lowest hand wins instead of the highest. Played with the same dealing structure as standard Five Card Stud (one hole card, four up cards), it uses ace-to-five lowball ranking where straights and flushes do not count against the player. The best possible hand is A-2-3-4-5 (the 'wheel'). Origin: USA Players: 2-10 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-20 minutes per hand",
    "history": "Five Card Stud Lowball evolved from Five Card Stud in the early-to-mid 20th century as poker players sought low-hand variants. It became particularly popular in California card rooms and American home games as a simpler alternative to Razz (Seven Card Stud Low). - 1860s: Five Card Stud first appears as the earliest form of stud poker. - Early 1900s: Lowball variants begin appearing in American poker circles. - Mid-20th Century: Five Card Stud Lowball standardizes with ace-to-five ranking. - 1970s-Present: Overtaken in popularity by Razz and Lowball Draw, but still played in home games and some card rooms. The game maintained the simple structure of Five Card Stud while inverting the hand rankings. The ace-to-five system (A-2-3-4-5 as best hand) became standard, with straights and flushes not counting against the playerâ€”unlike deuce-to-seven lowball where they do count. More common in...",
    "setup": "Players: 2-10 players (optimal with 4-7). Deck: Standard 52-card deck. No jokers. Equipment: Standard deck, poker chips, dealer button. Dealing: Five Card Stud format: 1 card face down (hole card), 4 cards face up. Four betting rounds.",
    "rules": "Objective: Form the lowest possible 5-card hand. Lowest hand at showdown wins the pot. Gameplay: 1. **Ante**: All players post an ante. 2. **First Deal**: Each player receives 1 card face down and 1 card face up. 3. **Bring-in**: Player with the HIGHEST upcard (Ace counting as low) posts a compulsory bring-in bet. This is unique to lowballâ€”high card acts first and must bet. 4. **Subsequent Streets**: Three more cards dealt face up, with betting after each. 5. **Betting Order**: After the first round, betting begins with the player showing the LOWEST hand (pairs count as high). 6. **Showdown**: Best low hand wins. Scoring: Ace-to-five lowball hand ranking. The wheel (A-2-3-4-5) is the nuts. - Lowest hand wins (ace-to-five ranking). - Aces are always low (best card in the deck). - Straights and flushes do NOT count against the player. - A-2-3-4-5 (Wheel) is the best possible hand. -...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Starting Hand Selection: Only play if your first two cards are both 8 or lower with no pair. If your hole card is paired with your upcard, or if you show a high card like a King or Queen, strongly consider folding. Intermediate: Board Reading: Pay close attention to your opponents' upcards. If you hold A-3 in the hole but three other Aces are showing, your hand is much weaker. Conversely, if many low cards are showing, your medium-low hand gains value. Advanced: Position Play: In later betting rounds, acting first with a strong low hand can be disadvantageous (you can't gain information). However, with a marginal hand in late position, you can make better decisions based on opponents' actions. The bring-in position (highest card showing) is at a significant disadvantage. - Tip: The Ace is your best friendâ€”it's the lowest card in the deck. - Tip: Avoid pairs at all costs. A...",
    "variations": "- Ace-to-Six Lowball: Aces are low but straights and flushes DO count against you. A-2-3-4-5 is a straight and therefore a poor hand. Best hand is A-2-3-4-6 (not a straight). - Deuce-to-Seven Lowball: Aces are high, and straights/flushes count. Best hand is 2-3-4-5-7 (not a straight). Also known as Kansas City Lowball. - Five Card Stud High-Low: Pot splits between highest and lowest hands. Often played with declaration or with an 8-or-better qualifier for low. - Five Card Stud Lowball with a Buy: After the fifth card, players may 'buy' a sixth card (face down) for an additional bet. Best 5 of 6 cards play. - Razz: Seven Card Stud Lowballâ€”same hand rankings but with seven cards dealt (2 down, 4 up, 1 down). More popular in modern poker."
