    "overview": "Five Hundred is a trick-taking game for four players in fixed partnerships, invented in the USA in 1904 and now the national card game of Australia. Players bid 6-10 tricks; the high bidder takes a 3-card kitty, discards 3 cards, declares trump, and attempts to win their bid. Features unique bower mechanics (Joker and jacks of matching suit as top trumps) and No Trump / Misere contract options. First partnership to reach 500+ points wins. Origin: USA (1904), popularized in Australia & New Zealand Players: 4 (2 partnerships) Deck: 43-card stripped deck + 1 Joker Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-60 minutes",
    "history": "Five Hundred was invented in the USA in 1904 by the U.S. Playing Card Company, copyrighted officially in 1904. It evolved from Euchre, adding a 43-card pack (stripping 2s-6s) and a Joker, plus a bidding system for tricks and the 'kitty' (widow) mechanism. - 1904: Invented and trademarked by US Playing Card Company; rules published. - 1920s-1950s: Becomes the national game of Australia; also popular in New Zealand and parts of North America. - 1960s-1980s: Peak popularity in Oceania; spreads to South Africa. - Present: Remains the national game of Australia; played competitively worldwide. The game evolved from Euchre by introducing: (1) variable hand bidding (6-10 tricks), (2) the kitty/widow mechanic, (3) No Trump and Misere contracts, (4) scoring points toward a fixed target (500 points). Five Hundred holds a unique cultural position as the official national card game of Australia....",
    "setup": "Players: Exactly 4 players in 2 fixed partnerships (seated opposite each other). Deck: 43-card deck: Remove all 2s, 3s, 4s, 5s, 6s from a standard 52-card deck, keeping 7-A in all suits. Add 1 Joker. Total: 43 cards + 1 Joker = 44 cards. Equipment: Deck, scoresheet, pencil, dealer button (optional). Dealing: Dealer shuffles and right-hand player cuts. Dealer distributes: 3 cards to each player, then 3 cards to the kitty (widow), then 4 more cards to each player. Final distribution: each player holds 10 cards; 3-card kitty placed in center.",
    "rules": "Objective: First partnership to score 500+ points wins the game. Points are earned by making bid contracts. Gameplay: 1. **Bidding Phase**: Starting with player left of dealer, each player bids the number of tricks they believe they can win (6-10) and optionally names trump or passes. Bids increase; last bid stands (highest wins). Player may bid only once. 2. **Kitty Discovery**: Winning bidder takes the 3-card kitty, examines it, and discards 3 cards face-down (kitty discards are never revealed). 3. **Trump Declaration**: High bidder announces trump suit OR 'No Trumps' (or 'Misere' / 'Open Misere' if their contract value supports it). 4. **Play Phase**: High bidder leads the first trick. Each player must follow suit if able. Trick won by highest trump or highest card of led suit. Trick winner leads next. 5. **Scoring**: After all 10 tricks, count tricks won by high bidder's...",
    "strategy": "Basic: In the kitty, seek cards that complement your hand (completing suits or adding bowers). Bid conservatively early; trust your partner's bids. Always follow suit. If high bidder, play trump cards to establish who holds what. Intermediate: **Bid Calculation**: Count sure tricks + tricks your partner likely has from their bid signals. A 6-trick bid requires only 6/10 tricks (60% win rate); 10-trick requires 10/10 (100%). **Bower Recognition**: If you hold Left Bower, remember Right Bower is still higher in trump. **No Trump Strategy**: Be cautious; without trump, your Ace is no longer highestâ€”opponents' cards block you easily. Advanced: **Bid Communication**: Your bid signals your hand strength. Conservative bids suggest weak hands; aggressive bids suggest strong trump holdings. Partners interpret bids to guide their play. **Counting Out**: Track which high cards have been played...",
    "variations": "- Three-Player Five Hundred: 5 cards dealt to each player, 5 cards to dummy. Highest bidder plays against two other players + dummy. Dummy plays according to standard rules. - Two-Player Five Hundred (Cutthroat): Each player bids alone. No partnerships. High bidder plays alone against opponent(s). 10 cards each, 4-card kitty. - Six-Player Five Hundred: 3 partnerships (3 players each, not all seated in standard order). Dealt 8-9 cards per player, 2-4 card kitty. Bidding and scoring adjusted. - Family Version (Simplified): Easier for beginners: bids limited to 6-8 tricks, no Misere contracts, simplified No Trump rules."
