    "setup": "Players: Two to four players; four typically play as two against two in partnership. Deck: Standard Western double-six dominoes, 28 tiles. Equipment: Double-six domino set; cribbage board recommended for scoring. Dealing: Tiles mixed face down; two players draw 7 tiles each, three or four players draw 5 tiles each. Remaining tiles form the boneyard and are out of play (no drawing).",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first player or team to reach exactly 61 points by making the open ends of the layout total multiples of 3 or 5 (and optionally scoring for chipping out). Gameplay: First hand starter is determined by lot (e.g. each draws a tile, highest starts); in later hands the turn to start passes left. Play is clockwise. Each turn a player adds one tile to an end of the layout, matching the inward end of the played tile to the outward end of the tile it touches; doubles are placed crosswise and count as the total of their pips when at an open end. A player who cannot play must knock and the turn passes. The hand ends when one player chips out (plays their last tile) or all players are blocked; in the four-player partnership game both members of one team must chip out. After each play the two open ends are totalled: if the total is a multiple of 5, 3 or both, the player scores...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Track which tiles have been played; the player who can count outstanding tiles has an advantage. Intermediate: Note numbers on which an opponent was unable to play to infer their hand and block scoring opportunities. Advanced: Valuable tiles that move one scoring position to another: 0-3, 1-4, 2-5, 3-6, double 3, and double 6; use them to set up or deny multiples of 3 and 5. - Tip: Doubles at an end count as the sum of their pips; double 5 and a 5 at the other end make 15 (8 points). - Tip: In partnership play both members of a team must chip out to end the hand.",
