    "history": "The game is believed to have developed in Morocco and the surrounding Maghreb region during the colonial era. It likely blended French/German domino games (Bergen) with local gambling traditions involving Backgammon dice found in local bars and cafes. - Early 20th Century: Variations of Bergen spread across North Africa. - 1940s-50s: The addition of dice and regional terminology (Oasis, Caravan Master) becomes standardized in local gambling dens. - 1990s: Documented by domino researchers like Joe Celko after interviewing players with North African heritage. - Present: Played as a social game in Mediterranean cafes. Naming the boneyard the 'Oasis' and the dealer the 'Caravan Master' adds a thematic layer of survival in the desert to a mathematically focusnd game. Represented as a 'bar game' where matchsticks or small coins are used for the scoring pot.",
    "setup": "Players: 2 to 4 players. Best with 4 (playing as individuals). Deck: Double-six domino set. 28 tiles. Equipment: One pair of 'Flower' dice (e.g., Green) and one pair of 'Scorpion' dice (e.g., Red). Dealing: Each player receives 5 or 6 tiles (depending on player count). Remaining tiles are placed face-down in the 'Oasis'.",
