    "overview": "Forty Thieves, also known as 'Napoleon at St. Helena' or 'Roosevelt at San Juan', is a notoriously difficult and strategic two-deck Solitaire game. Requiring 104 cards, the game challenges players to organize a vast tableau of 40 cards into eight separate foundations. Unlike many solitaires, building in the tableau must be done strictly by suit and in descending order. Because the stock can only be cycled through once, and only a single card is drawn at a time, players must exercise extreme caution to avoid blocking their own win path. Origin: France / UK Players: 1 Deck: Two Standard 52-card decks (104 cards total) Difficulty: Expert Duration: 15-25 minutes",
    "history": "The game gained legendary status as the favorite pastime of Napoleon Bonaparte during his exile on the island of Saint Helena. Its name refers to the 40 cards dealt into the initial tableau, resembling a gathered band of thieves guarding their treasure. - 1800s: Traditional French and British versions documented in early patience manuals. - 1815-1821: Napoleon famously plays the game during his exile. - 1900s: Becomes a staple of high difficulty solitaire literature, known for its low win rate (estimated at 10-20% even for expert players). - Present: A hallmark feature of premium digital solitaire suites. Has inspired numerous easier variations, like 'Lucas' or 'Maria', but the original 'Napoleon' rules remain the most respected. Symbolizes patience and strategic endurance in the face of overwhelming odds.",
