    "overview": "Four of a Kind is a unique pokerndstyle betting game, traditionally played as a 'dealer's choice' option in home poker games. Unlike standard poker where you build a hand through drawing or communal cards, players in this game are each assigned a unique starting rank and wait for the deck to provide the matching cards. It is a game of high tension and split pots, where victory is divided between the first person to complete their set of four and the person holding the mathematically lowest hand at that moment. Origin: USA Players: 4-8 (Best with 6-8) Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-20 minutes",
    "history": "The game was documented by William Moore of New York, whose family introduced it as a 'poker night' variation in the late 1950s. It represents a genre of 'waiting games' where betting is driven by the visible progression of one's hand toward a fixed goal. - 1950s: First played in family poker circles in New York state. - 1960s: Spread to college campuses as a social gambling game. - Present: Remains a niche but beloved 'wildcard' variant in home poker rotations. The game simplifies the complex bluffing of poker into a straightforward race, using betting rounds to allow players to 'buy in' to their luck as matches appear. Often played for small stakes (nickels and dimes), emphasizing the social aspect of 'waiting for your card' rather than calculated highndstakes bluffing.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 to 8 players. Optimal at 6-8. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Aces rank high. Equipment: Standard 52-card deck. Aces rank high. Dealing: deal and betting Each player places the agreed ante into the pot.",
    "rules": "Objective: Collect all four cards of your starting rank OR hold the lowest total hand when someone else completes their set. Gameplay: 1. First Betting: After the initial cards are dealt, the player with the highest card starts the betting. 2. The Reveal: The dealer turns cards from the undealt deck one at a time. 3. Matching: If the turned card matches a player's rank, that player takes the card. 4. Betting Rounds: Every time a match occurs, a new round of betting begins, led by the player with the most cards (or highest rank if tied). 5. Discards: If a card doesn't match anyone's held rank, it is discarded, and the next card is flipped. Scoring: Two-pot system: High pot (four-of-a-kind or best hand) and Low pot (lowest 5-card hand). Split at showdown. - Follow the legal action constraints defined for Four of a Kind. - Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Scoring summary:...",
