    "overview": "Gallinazo is a distinct domino game played in Puerto Rico and parts of Mexico, characterized by its individual play format and unique penalty scoring system. While most domino games focus on partnership play, Gallinazo forces each of the four participants to hunt for themselves. The game is named after the 'vulture' (gallinazo), perhaps referring to the way players must 'prey' on others' mistakes to avoid being the one left holding highndvalue tiles. It is culturally synonymous with social stakes, where the cumulative loser is famously tasked with buying the next round of drinks for the table. Origin: Puerto Rico / Mexico Players: 4 players (Individual) Deck: Double-Six Domino Set (28 tiles) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-40 minutes (7 deals)",
    "history": "Gallinazo is particularly associated with San Lorenzo, Puerto Rico, and Guadalajara, Mexico. It represents an informal, highndvelocity version of dominoes that prioritizes social interaction and 'secret' scoring to keep the tension high until the final round. - Mid-20th Century: Regional popularity grows in Caribbean and Mexican social clubs. - 1990s: Documented by researchers Jose Carrillo and Alfonso Garcia. - Present: Remains a popular 'tavern game' for determining social obligations. The game simplifies the branching paths of many domino variants into a single line of play, focusing the strategic depth on pip management rather than board control. The tradition of keeping scores secret until the 7th deal is a specific cultural trait of Gallinazo, designed to prevent players from 'teaming up' against the leader prematurely.",
