    "history": "Game to Lose is documented as a West Midlands three-player reverse form of Chaser (Nine Card Don family). - Traditional Don and Chaser variants were widely played in English pub culture. - Game to Lose emerged as a local reverse-scoring adaptation for three active players. - Published modern rules preserve the crib-board race-to-lose format. The main innovation is inversion of value: cards that would normally be desirable become penalties. It belongs to the local Midlands all-fours and Don orbit of pub and workplace card games.",
