    "history": "Gaps belongs to the Montana family of patience games and appears in 20th-century rule collections under names including Montana and Spaces. - Early and mid 20th century: Published as part of the Montana family of patience games. - Late 20th century onward: Became common in computer solitaire suites under names such as Gaps and Addiction. The core mechanism is stable: remove aces, fill gaps by suit and ascending rank, then redeal unsorted cards when blocked. The main variation is redeal count and whether cards are shuffled. Gaps is widely played as a quick logic-focused solitaire with visible state and low-overhead turns.",
    "setup": "Players: One player. Gaps is a patience (solitaire) game; a single player plays alone and makes all decisions about which cards to move into gaps and when to redeal. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Equipment: One shuffled deck and table space for four rows of thirteen cards. Dealing: Deal all 52 cards face up into four rows of thirteen. Remove all four aces, leaving four empty spaces.",
    "rules": "Objective: Finish with each row in same-suit order from 2 through King, leaving the four gaps immediately to the right of the Kings. Gameplay: A gap may be filled only by the card that is one rank higher and the same suit as the card immediately to its left. A gap at the far left of a row can be filled only by a 2. Gaps immediately right of a King are dead until redeal. When no legal move exists, gather all cards that are not part of correct left-to-right suit runs, redeal them into the open spaces, and continue. Standard Gaps allows two redeals, for up to three passes total. Scoring: Gaps is normally scored as solved or unsolved; some software also reports cards correctly ordered. - Only legal successor cards can enter a gap: same suit and exactly one rank higher than the card on the left. - A left-edge gap accepts only a 2. - A gap to the right of a King is dead for that pass. -...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize moves that extend correct suit runs from a 2 because locked runs survive into redeals. Intermediate: Delay moves that strand Kings in central positions; dead King-right gaps reduce mobility until the next redeal. Advanced: Before redeal, maximize preserved ordered prefixes in each row so fewer cards re-enter random distribution. - Tip: Treat each row as an independent suit runway and protect long correct prefixes. - Tip: If several moves are legal, prefer the one that unlocks a second immediate legal successor.",
    "variations": "- Addiction: Same core play but typically allows three redeals rather than two. - No-shuffle Redeal: Unsorted cards are redealt without shuffling, reducing randomness and changing solvability. - Unlimited Redeals: Redeals continue until solved; turns game into a deterministic endurance variant."
