    "rules": "Objective: Play all tiles first, or in a blocked hand have the lowest pip total on unplayed tiles. Gameplay: In the first deal, the holder of highest double leads it as the center. In later deals, the designated starting player may lead any double; if none, they pass and turn continues until someone leads a double. Each player has a personal train extending from the center. On turn, a player must play if legal. A legal play is usually to own train, but a player may instead play to another player's train only if that owner previously passed and the end has not changed since that pass. When a player passes, their train is open. If another player adds a non-double to that open train, it becomes private again. Play ends when a player empties hand or everyone is blocked. Scoring: When someone goes out, each loser loses points equal to pips left in hand and winner gains their sum. In blocked...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep your train playable to avoid opening it to opponents. Intermediate: Use opponents' open trains to unload awkward tiles while preserving your own control numbers. Advanced: Track who can likely answer each branch end; selectively close an opponent train by changing its end after they pass. - Tip: In four-player games all 28 tiles are in hands, so memory of seen numbers is highly valuable. - Tip: Plan around doubles because they preserve branch direction and can stabilize your own train timing.",
    "variations": "- Three-Player Heap Exchange: With three players, one face-down 7-tile heap may be exchanged for a player's original hand under first-turn conditions; at most one exchange per deal. - Two-Player Double Heap: With two players there are two 7-tile heaps; exchange may happen twice. If the loser exchanged twice, loss can be multiplied accordingly. - Correlativa: Mexican variant without full-hand exchange. Unplayed tiles stay in boneyard; blocked players draw one by one until playable or boneyard empty."
