    "history": "Giltspiel was documented in Tyrol as an old game by the mid-19th century and is treated as the ancestor of Perlaggen. - 1853: Mentioned in an Innsbruck Perlaggen book as an established older game. - 2015: Hubert Auer documented active modern play in Fiss, Serfaus, and Ladis. - 2024: Rules documentation updated with detailed betting, showdown, and endgame procedures. Historical variants introduced wild cards and developed into Perlaggen; base Giltspiel has no wild cards. Table talk and visible signaling are part of normal play, and bluffing is explicitly accepted.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in fixed partnerships, partners opposite. Deck: 32-card German suits (bells, acorns, hearts, leaves), rank high to low: A K O U 10 9 8 7. Equipment: Scoresheet for partnership points. Dealing: Deal five cards each clockwise in two packets (2+3 or 3+2). Turn the next card up to set trump suit.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first partnership to reach the agreed target (usually 11 or 15 points). Gameplay: Eldest hand leads. On each trick, a player who has led suit may either follow suit or play trump; if unable to follow suit they may play any card. If any trump is played, highest trump wins; otherwise highest card of led suit wins. Winner leads next. Cards remain face up in front of each player in play order. Three figures are scored each deal: Gleich (best set of equal ranks), Hanger (best suited sequence), and Spiel (team that wins at least 3 of 5 tricks). Betting on each figure can happen during play and can be conceded, seen, or raised. Play stops immediately when Spiel is decided or conceded, and unresolved Gleich/Hanger are settled by staged showdown reveals. Scoring: Each deal awards figure values for Gleich, Hanger, and Spiel, with values increased by accepted bets and modified...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Use early betting to pressure opponents into conceding figures they likely cannot improve. Intermediate: Track revealed cards and showdown obligations so you know when a bluff is impossible to sustain. Advanced: Conceding Spiel at the right time can force opponents to show first in showdown, revealing information before your team commits. - Tip: Preserve flexible trumps because you can trump even when you could have followed suit. - Tip: Do not over-signal to partner; visible communication is legal, but extra information helps opponents too.",
    "variations": "- Target Score Choice: Tables commonly agree before play to 11-point or 15-point game length. - Local Signaling Systems: Different groups use different legal visual signal conventions for suits, trumps, and figure strength. - Perlique Wildcards (Historical): Some historical Tyrolean groups added wild cards, leading to the related game Perlaggen."
