    "history": "Gin Rummy was created by New York Bridge player Elwood T. Baker around 1911, from the Mexican game Conquian and the American game Rum. - Early 1910s: Developed in New York as a two-player rummy form. - 1940s: Became a popular Hollywood and club game with bonus-heavy scoring variants. - Modern era: Remains a widely played competitive and casual two-player card game. Core draw-discard and knock structure remained stable while bonus values and deal procedures vary by table and tournament rules. Gin Rummy became one of the best-known head-to-head card games in American social and tournament play.",
    "setup": "Players: Two players only, head-to-head; no partnerships. Each player receives ten cards per hand. Dealership alternates from round to round; the first dealer is often chosen by drawing cards from the pack (lower card deals), and subsequently the winner of the previous hand deals. Deck: Standard 52-card deck, ace low in runs. Equipment: Scorepad for cumulative hand and game bonuses. Dealing: Deal 10 cards to each player one at a time. Turn the next card face up as first discard and place the rest face down as stock.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win points by ending hands with lower deadwood than opponent and reach game target first. Gameplay: On each turn draw one card from stock or top discard, then discard one card face up. If you took the top discard this turn, you cannot discard that same card immediately. Melds are sets (3-4 equal ranks) and runs (3+ same-suit consecutive cards); a card cannot count in two melds at once. Ace is always low, so A-2-3 is valid but A-K-Q is not. A player may knock after drawing if deadwood is 10 or less by discarding face down, then both hands are exposed. If knocker did not go gin, opponent may lay off unmatched cards on knocker melds. If stock reaches two cards and no one has knocked, hand is cancelled and redealt. Scoring: Hand scoring uses deadwood difference plus bonus events, and game scoring continues across hands. - First turn is special: non-dealer may take upcard; if...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Lower deadwood early and avoid holding too many high cards that can punish you when opponent knocks. Intermediate: Use discard-pick information to infer opponent meld shapes and avoid feeding obvious runs or sets. Advanced: Balance knock timing against expected undercut risk by modeling opponent deadwood range from discard behavior. - Tip: Cards taken from discard reveal information; take them mainly when immediate meld value is strong. - Tip: Watch stock depth because hands can end as cancellation when stock nears two cards.",
    "variations": "- 25-Point Bonus Tables: Some groups use 25 for gin bonus and/or undercut bonus instead of 20 and 10. - Oklahoma Gin: Initial upcard value sets knock limit and a spade upcard doubles hand score; game target is often 150. - 11-Card Non-Dealer Start: Alternative opening deals 11 to non-dealer and 10 to dealer with no upcard, then non-dealer discards first."
