    "history": "Ging is known from Francis Willughby's 17th-century game collection and is also referenced as Seven Cards. - 17th century: Recorded in Willughby's manuscript collection. - 2003: Modern scholarly edition of Willughby's game book published. - 2008: Playable reconstructed rules published for modern play. The available rules are reconstruction-oriented because no continuous living ruleset survived. The game reflects early English counter-stake gambling culture with precedence by elder hand and layout sweeps.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players clockwise, with eldest hand left of dealer and dealer as youngest hand. Deck: 28 cards made by removing aces and cards below seven from a standard 52-card deck. Equipment: A square layout area and counters (chips). Dealing: Each player contributes two counters to dress the layout, then dealer gives 7 cards to each player clockwise.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win counters from the central layout by best qualifying declaration in each deal. Gameplay: There is no trick-play phase. After stake layout and deal, players evaluate hands and declare results. If no sweep combination is declared, counters are awarded for most of a suit and second most of a suit (court cards count 10, number cards by pip). Elder hand precedence breaks ties for most and second-most positions. If the same player holds both top suit totals, they take three corner counters. Five sweep combinations override suit-point awards, ranked highest to lowest: Seven Cards (all seven cards same suit), Four Sevens, Ging (a suit totaling exactly 37 with all cards held in that suit), Gentlemen (all face cards), Bare Shoulders (no face cards). A valid sweep takes all layout counters and the layout is re-dressed for next deal. Scoring: Counters are won from a central layout...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Check sweep conditions first because any valid sweep outranks suit-point outcomes. Intermediate: When no sweep exists, calculate all four suit totals quickly and resolve elder-hand tiebreaks accurately. Advanced: Maintain precise counter accounting around redressing to avoid payout mistakes in long sessions. - Tip: Remember Ging requires exactly 37 in one suit and all cards held in that suit must be included in that total. - Tip: Do not miss rare combinations like bare shoulders while focusing on suit-point counting.",
    "variations": "- Bankrupt End: Session can end when a player loses their last counter; remaining layout is then divided as agreed. - Fixed Number of Deals: Alternative session structure plays a predetermined number of deals and splits pool at the end."
