    "overview": "Gnav is a Scandinavian elimination game from the Cuckoo family. Each round players hold one hidden piece, may stand or try to swap left, and try to avoid finishing with the lowest value. Origin: Norway and Denmark Players: 2-20 Deck: Custom 42-piece Gnav pack (two of each rank/special piece) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-40 minutes",
    "history": "Gnav is a traditional round game played in Norway and in some parts of Denmark (for example on the island of Møn) as a family game on Christmas Eve and New Year's Eve. It is closely related to the old Italian game Cuccù, the German Hexenspiel and the Swedish Byteskille; a variant known as Slabberjan is played in Zeeland in the Netherlands. - 18th Century: Becomes a popular Christmas game in Norway. - 19th Century: Special wooden pieces ('brikker') become the standard equipment to allow hidden play under a table. - Present: Still played as a family tradition during holidays. Core play stayed stable: one hidden piece, leftward swaps, and special top matadors that block or redirect swaps. It is commonly played as a family holiday game and often uses small tokens or matches as lives.",
    "setup": "Players: 2-20 players in clockwise order. Deck: 42 total pieces: two each of Gjok (cuckoo), Dragon, Katt, Hest, Hus, XII-XI-X-IX-VIII-VII-VI-V-IV-III-II-I, 0, Potte, Ugle, and Narr. Equipment: Gnav card pack or wooden pawns, plus tokens or matches used as lives and a central pool. Dealing: Dealer gives one hidden piece to each player; each player privately checks their own value.",
    "rules": "Objective: Avoid ending the round with the lowest piece and preserve your tokens while other players are eliminated. Gameplay: Play starts left of dealer and proceeds clockwise. On turn, a player either stands or tries to swap with the left neighbor. A normal neighbor must swap, but top five matadors change this. Cuckoo stops further swapping and triggers immediate reveal. Dragon and Katt refuse the swap and force the attacker to pay a token. Hest and Hus pass the attacker onward so they must try the next player instead. When play reaches dealer, dealer may exchange with the undealt stock with the same matador consequences; when a pass reaches dealer, draw replacement pieces until a non-pass value appears. At round end all reveal. Lowest value pays one token to pool; ties for lowest each pay. Scoring: At reveal, the lowest piece holder pays one token to the pool. Ties for lowest each...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Stand on strong values and matadors; swap aggressively from low numbers and bottom specials. Intermediate: Track fool knocks and pass-chain effects from Hest and Hus to anticipate where dangerous pieces move. Advanced: Use seat position: when late in order, standing on mid values can be correct if several weak pieces are likely still right of you. - Tip: Cuckoo can end the round early, so value volatility is higher before dealer acts. - Tip: Dragon and Katt penalties can be as costly as losing low-card reveal; account for both risks.",
    "variations": "- Double Fool Sit-Out: If both fools are dealt and survive swap round, both holders pay and may skip the next deal. - Slabberjan Related Set: Dutch Slabberjan uses a related 42-piece order and special actions; treat it as a separate ruleset in the same family."
