    "history": "Derived from 19th-century 'Happy Families' and 'Authors' games, which used special decks. The adaptation to standard playing cards occurred in the United States around the turn of the 20th century. - 1851: 'Authors' published in the USA, introducing the mechanic of asking for specific cards to complete sets. - 1861: 'Happy Families' published in the UK with similar rules. - 20th Century: 'Go Fish' becomes the standard name for the game when played with a regular 52-card deck. - Present: Remains a starter game for millions of children worldwide. Special-pack family games such as Authors and Happy Families were adapted to standard ranks, producing modern Go Fish rules. Used widely as an educational tool for teaching numbers, matching, and memory skills.",
    "setup": "Players: 2-6 players, typically best with 3-6. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Equipment: No equipment beyond deck; optional score sheet for multi-hand sessions. Dealing: Deal 7 cards each (for 2 players) or 5 cards each (for 3+ players). The rest form the 'Ocean' (Stock) spread out face-down.",
    "rules": "Objective: Collect the highest number of books (four cards of one rank) by successful asks and stock draws. Gameplay: 1. **The Ask**: On your turn, ask any player for a specific rank (e.g., 'Alice, do you have any 3s '). **Rule**: You must already hold at least one card of that rank. 2. **The Catch**: If they have the cards, they MUST give ALL of them to you. You get another turn. 3. **The Fish**: If they have none, they say 'Go Fish!'. You draw one card from the Stock. - **Lucky Catch**: If you draw the rank you asked for, show it to everyone and take another turn. - **Miss**: If you draw anything else, your turn ends. 4. **Books**: When you have 4 of a kind, reveal them and place them face-up in front of you. 5. **Empty Hand**: If you run out of cards, draw new ones from the stock (usually 1, sometimes 5 depending on house rules). Scoring: Players earn points by collecting books...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Always ask for ranks you actually hold (it's the rules!). Start by asking for ranks where you already have 2 or 3 cards to finish books quickly. Intermediate: Listen! If Bob asks Alice for Kings, Bob has at least one King. If Alice says 'Go Fish', she has no Kings. Remember this. Advanced: The Steal: If Bob asks Alice for Kings and she gives him one, you *know* Bob has Kings. On your turn, if you have a King, ask Bob for his Kings to steal them all before he can complete the book. - Tip: Don't sort your hand too obviously if playing with sharp-eyed opponents. - Tip: In a 2-player game, card counting is powerful. If you know your opponent doesn't have 7s (because they fished earlier), don't ask for them.",
    "variations": "- Ask Specific Card: Players ask for an exact card (for example seven of diamonds) instead of rank only. - Continue After Stock Empty: After stock is exhausted, asking continues without drawing until all books are formed. - Refill Empty Hand: When a player runs out of cards, they draw up to five cards from stock if available and continue play."
