    "history": "Go Stop developed in Korea from Japanese flower-card traditions and became the best-known Korean hwatu fishing game. - Late 19th Century: Hanafuda cards introduced to Korea. - 1950s: Plastic Hwatu cards mass-produced, replacing paper ones. - 1980s: Go-Stop rules normalized, distinct from Japanese rules (e.g., introduction of 'Jokers' and 'Bomb' rules). - Present: Played universally during Chuseok (Korean Thanksgiving) and Lunar New Year. Modern Korean tables added local bonuses and optional jokers while preserving month-matching capture play. It is widely played during Korean holidays and social gatherings, often in multi-hand sessions.",
