    "history": "Derived from the traditional game of Golf, the 'Power' variants emerged in the late 20th century. Names like 'Cambio' (Spain) and 'Cabo' suggest a global spread through traveler communities and local evolution. - Traditional Golf: Simple layout and replacement game. - 1990s-2000s: Rise of folk variants adding action powers (Cambio, Pablo, Cumbia). - 2010: Commercial versions like 'Cabo' bring the mechanics to a wider audience. - Modern: 'Dacz' remains a popular social variant in Poland. The game shifted from a purely solitaire-like optimization game (Standard Golf) into a highly interactive 'take-that' style game where an opponent's layout is no longer safe from swaps. In Poland, 'Dacz' is a common parlor game. The name is likely a phonetic adaptation of 'Dutch'.",
    "setup": "Players: Two to six players; the game is often best with two to four. Four-card Golf and its power-card variants (Dacz, Cabo, Cambio, Cactus) can in theory be played by two to around eight or more; with eight or more players, two packs are shuffled together. Commercial and folk accounts typically describe play with 2-6 players. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Equipment: Standard deck, score sheet. Dealing: Each player is dealt 4 cards face-down in a row. Before play begins, each player may peek at their two outer cards (or two inner, by agreement). The rest of the deck forms the stock; one card is turned up to start the discard pile.",
    "rules": "Objective: Achieve the lowest total score in your 4-card layout by the end of the round. Gameplay: Turn: draw from stock or top discard. Use drawn card to (a) replace one face-down layout card without looking, discarding the replaced card, or (b) if it is a Jack or Queen drawn from stock, use its power (Jack: swap any two layout cards; Queen: peek at one card) then discard it. Power cards from discard have no power. To end the round: call 'Dacz' instead of drawing; each opponent gets one more turn, then all score. Scoring: Lowest total wins. Special values for Kings and certain Face cards depending on the variant. - You cannot look at a layout card before replacing it unless a Power (Peek) was used. - Discarded Power Cards cannot have their powers used by the next player (powers only activate when drawn from stock). - Penalty for Caller: If the player who called 'Dacz' does not have...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Memorize your starting two cards. Use Peeks (Queens) to identify your hidden cards or check an opponent's low card. Only swap (Jacks) when you are reasonably sure you are trading a high card for a low one. Intermediate: Watch which cards opponents draw from the discard pileâ€”this tells you where their low cards are. If an opponent draws a Red King and places it, you know exactly where to aim your next Swap. Advanced: The 'Match' bluff: discard a card quickly to pretend it matches your hidden card. If done correctly, you can shed high cards early. Timing the 'Dacz' call is critical: do it when you have two 0s or Lows and know opponents are still peeking. - Tip: Keep your Red Kings guarded; they are the prime targets for Swaps. - Tip: Don't call Dacz unless your total is below 10-15; a 20-point hand is rarely a winner. - Tip: If you have a high card you can't get rid of, try to...",
    "variations": "- Cabo (Commercial): Uses specific rhymes: 7/8 (Know your fate - peek own), 9/10 (Know a friend - peek opponent), J/Q (Switch between). King of Diamonds is 0, others 13. - Cambio (Spanish): Often played with Jokers (-5). More aggressive swapping rules. - Cactus (Vancouver): Queens swap any card. 9/10/J peek opponent. 6/7/8 peek own. Call 'Cactus' to end."
