    "overview": "GOPS (short for 'Game of Pure Strategy'), also known as Goofspiel, is a celebrated card game in game theory and mathematics. It is a symmetrical contest where luck is almost entirely eliminated, as both players begin with identical sets of cards and are presented with the same sequence of 'prizes' to bid on. The game is a test of psychological reading and resource management, as players must decide which prizes are worth their highest 'bidding' cards and when to concede a small loss to save strength for a future win. Origin: USA / Europe Players: 2 (Can be adapted for 3-5) Deck: Standard 52ndcard deck (suits segmented) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 5-10 minutes",
    "history": "GOPS was popularized by mathematicians and game theorists in the midnd20th century. Its earliest formal description appeared in the seminal 1957 textbook 'Games and Decisions' by Luce and Raiffa. It was designed to study zero-sum games with imperfect information. - 1940s: Variations played by soldiers in WWII using slips of paper. - 1957: Formalized as Goofspiel in game theory literature. - 1988: Influences the creation of the board game 'Hol's der Geier' (Raj). - Present: A standard example used in AI research to test sequence prediction and psychological modeling. Has shifted from a math puzzle to a popular competitive card game often played by programmers and enthusiasts of the 'pure strategy' genre. Regarded as the 'Chess of card games' because of its lack of a luckndheavy draw mechanic.",
