    "history": "Originating in Qingdao, Shandong in the 1960s, Gou Ji became a cultural phenomenon in Northern China. It was developed to accommodate large groups of players with a surplus of cards. - 1960s: Developed in Qingdao. - 1980s: Spread across Shandong and Northern China. - 1990s: Televised Gou Ji tournaments become popular in China. - Present: A staple of Chinese card gaming culture, with dedicated apps and professional leagues. The game evolved from standard climbing games (like Zheng Shangyou) but added the unique 'Head-to-Head' (Duel) mechanic to allow cross-table interaction. In Shandong, Gou Ji is more than a game; it is a social event. The ability to 'Gou Ji'â€”to interrupt play with a powerful handâ€”is seen as a sign of boldness and strength.",
    "setup": "Players: 6 players. Seated alternating (Team A: 1, 3, 5; Team B: 2, 4, 6). Deck: 4 decks of 52 cards + 8 Jokers (4 Red, 4 Black). Total 216 cards. Equipment: 4 decks of 52 cards + 8 Jokers (4 Red, 4 Black). Total 216 cards. Dealing: Cards are dealt until the deck is exhausted (36 cards per player).",
