    "history": "Traditional Japanese game using Hanafuda (flower cards), which originated in the 18th century. Hachi-Hachi is considered the most representative Hanafuda fishing game. - 18th Century: Hanafuda cards introduced to Japan. - 19th Century: Fishing games with Hanafuda develop. - 20th Century: Hachi-Hachi becomes the standard Hanafuda game. - Present: Played throughout Japan and in Japanese communities worldwide. Evolved from earlier Hanafuda fishing games, adding the bidding phase and complex yaku (scoring combinations) system. Represents traditional Japanese card gaming culture. Hanafuda cards are iconic in Japan and used in various games.",
    "setup": "Players: 3-6 players. Only 3 play actively each round; others sit out after bidding. Deck: 48-card Hanafuda deck (12 suits/months, 4 cards each). Equipment: Two denominations of chips: 'Kan' and 'Mon' (1 Kan = 12 Mon typically). Dealing: Deal cards to the 3 active players. Place cards face-up in the center (the 'field').",
    "rules": "Objective: Score as close to 88 points as possible (the par score). Players pay/receive based on deviation from 88. Alternatively, form valuable yaku combinations to stop play early and claim payment. Gameplay: 1. Bidding Phase (if more than 3 players): Players bid to determine the 3 active players for the round. Non-active players sit out but may still earn from teyaku. 2. Dealing: Deal cards to the 3 active players. Place cards face-up in the center (the 'field'). 3. Playing Phase: Players take turns playing a card from hand to capture matching cards from the field. A card matches if it's from the same month/suit. After playing from hand, flip the top card from the deck. If it matches a field card, capture it. Captured cards go into the player's scoring pile. 4. Scoring: Total card points = 264 (par = 88 per player). Teyaku (Initial Hand Combinations): Declared before play for...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Focus on capturing high-value cards (Brights, Animals, Ribbons). Avoid letting opponents form yaku. Intermediate: Track which cards have been played to anticipate remaining cards. Decide whether to stop early with a yaku or continue playing for more points. Advanced: Calculate exact point differentials. Bluff during bidding to become an active player. Memorize all yaku and their values to maximize scoring opportunities. - Tip: The par score of 88 is key—aim to exceed it, not just maximize points. - Tip: Stopping early with a yaku guarantees payment, but continuing might yield more. - Tip: In the bidding phase, assess your hand's potential before committing.",
    "variations": "- Regional Yaku Variations: Regional variations in yaku values and rules. - 2-Player and 7-Player Adaptations: Adapted versions for 2 or 7 players. - Different Chip Denominations: Some play with different chip denominations or scoring systems."
