    "overview": "Handjass, also known as Sackjass or Butzer, is considered the most basic and fundamental form of Jass, the national card game of Switzerland. It is a trick-taking game where players compete to reach a target score (usually represented by strokes on a slate) while avoiding the 'null' (potato) - a penalty for failing to reach a minimum point threshold in a hand. Origin: Switzerland Players: 2-4 Deck: 36-card Swiss Jass deck (Roses, Bells, Acorns, Shields) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 15-30 minutes",
    "history": "Part of the extensive Jass family of games which have been popular in Switzerland since the late 18th century. Handjass is the 'direct' version of the game, simpler than the partnership-based Schieber. - 1790s: Earliest records of Jass games in Switzerland. - 19th Century: Jass becomes the dominant card game in the country. - Present: Remains a staple of Swiss social life and pub culture. Handjass has remained relatively stable, though the threshold to avoid a 'null' has evolved from 21 points to 26 points in modern play to increase the challenge. Jass is so culturally significant in Switzerland that it is often referred to as the 'national sport' or the 'fourth language' of the country.",
    "setup": "Players: 2, 3, or 4 players playing individually. Deck: 36-card Jass deck (6-10, Jack, Queen, King, Ace). In German-speaking areas, the suits are Roses, Bells, Acorns and Shields. In French-speaking areas, standard French suits are used. Equipment: A slate or score sheet to mark 'strokes' (points). Dealing: Deal all cards (9 per player in a 4-player game). For 2-3 players, a 'blind hand' or 'besern' mechanic may be used.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first player to reach a target number of strokes (e.g., 7 or 9) without falling below the minimum point threshold in any given hand. Gameplay: 1. Players follow suit if possible. If they cannot follow suit, they may play any card (including a trump). 2. Trump Rules: In Jass, the 'Trump Under' (Jack) and 'Trump Nell' (Nine) are the highest cards. 3. A player may 'trump in' even if they can follow suit, unless they are playing the 'Jack of Trumps' (Pur). 4. Points are counted from cards taken in tricks. Scoring: Points from cards in tricks. Total 157 points per hand. 26-100 points = 1 stroke; over 100 = 2 strokes; under 26 = null (penalty). - Must follow suit if possible. - Trump Jack (Under) and Trump 9 (Nell) are the highest trumps. - Total card points per hand = 157. - Need at least 26 points to avoid a 'null' (penalty). - 26-100 points = 1 stroke; over 100 points...",
