    "history": "Derived from the 18th-century French game 'Reversis', Hearts evolved in the US during the late 19th century. 'Have a Heart' specifically refers to the standard evasion variant often played with a central betting pot. - 1750s: Reversis becomes popular in Europe. - 1880s: Standard Hearts rules solidify in the United States. - 1990s: Hearts becomes globally recognized due to its inclusion in the Windows operating system. The game moved from a complex high-card-wins system to the modern evasion-style 'Black Lady' variant. Known for its 'cut-throat' nature where players often galy-up against whoever is winning.",
