    "overview": "Hidden Rung (or Hidden Trump) is a popular South Asian trick-taking game, part of the Court Piece (Rang) family. It is played by four players in two fixed partnerships. The game is unique for its 'hidden' trump mechanic: the trump suit is determined by a card selected from the caller's first five cards and placed face-down, remaining unknown until a player is first unable to follow suit. Origin: India / Pakistan Players: 4 (partnerships) Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-40 minutes",
    "history": "Part of the Sar-Andaz or Court Piece family of games popular in the Indian subcontinent. It share roots with Whist and Bridge but features regional batch-dealing and trump-selection traditions. - 19th Century: Variations of Rang/Court Piece are established in Northern India. - 20th Century: Hidden Rung emerges as a popular social variant, adding tension to the trump reveal. - Modern Era: Remains a staple of social life in Pakistan, Northern India, and Persian-speaking regions. Developed from the standard 'Sir' or 'Rang' games where the trump is called aloud. The hidden element was likely added to increase the depth of deduction and defensive signaling. Commonly played at family gatherings and social clubs. It is often played with a high degree of speed and theatricality.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in fixed partnerships (North-South vs. East-West). Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Aces are high (A-K-Q-J-10...2). Equipment: 52-card deck and a way to track tricks (or 'Courts'). Dealing: The dealer deals 5 cards to each player in the first batch. The player to the dealer's right (Trump Caller) selects their hidden trump. The remaining cards are then dealt in batches of 4 until all cards are distributed (13 per player).",
    "rules": "Objective: A partnership aims to win the majority (at least 7) of the 13 tricks. Special bonuses (Courts) are awarded for winning 7 consecutive tricks or 7 tricks led by the same team. Gameplay: 1. **Trump Selection**: The caller chooses a card from their first 5 and places it face-down. This suit is the 'Hidden Rung'. 2. **Leading**: The caller leads the first trick. 3. **Following**: Players must follow suit if possible. 4. **Revealing Trump**: If a player cannot follow suit, they may ask the caller to reveal the trump. The caller turns the hidden card face-up. 5. **Winning Tricks**: Before the reveal, the highest card of the suit led wins. After the reveal, the highest trump wins (or highest cards of suit led if no trumps played). Scoring: Based on number of tricks won (of 13). Winning 7 consecutive tricks or 7-0 is a 'Court'. - Trumps cannot be played to a trick until they have...",
    "strategy": "Basic: As the caller, choose a suit that is both long (many cards) and strong (high cards). If you don't reveal too early, you might catch an opponent's Ace with a low trump. Intermediate: If your partner is the caller, try to lead suits where you have strength to draw out defenders' cards before the trump is revealed. Advanced: Defenders should attempt to force the trump reveal by leading suits they suspect the caller is short in. This removes the 'ambush' potential of the hidden suit. - Tip: Don't reveal the trump on a low-value trick if you can help it. - Tip: Track which suits have been led; the caller is likely void in a suit they haven't led but refuse to reveal trump on. - Tip: Save high non-trump cards for after the trumps have been depleted.",
    "variations": "- Double Sir: Tricks are only picked up when a player wins two consecutive tricks. - Satat: A variant where the hidden trump card is not revealed to everyone, but the caller just announces if the played card trumps it."
