    "setup": "Players: 4 players playing individually (non-partnership). Deck: 36-card German-suited pack (A, K, O, U, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6). Ranking: A > K > O > U > 9 > 8 > 7 > 6 > 10 (Note the low Ten!). Equipment: 36-card German-suited pack (A, K, O, U, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6). Ranking: A > K > O > U > 9 > 8 > 7 > 6 > 10 (Note the low Ten!). Dealing: Play and dealing are ANTICLOCKWISE. Dealing varies but usually involves batches of cards to reach 9 cards per player.",
    "rules": "Objective: Avoid taking tricks with high point values. The player with the MOST points in a round loses a point (mark on the slate). Gameplay: 1. The player to the right of the dealer leads. 2. You must follow suit if possible. 3. **Trumps**: The last card dealt to the dealer determines the trump suit. 4. **The Reverse Rule**: If you cannot follow suit, you MUST play a trump if you have one. Scoring: Ace-Ten scoring. A=11, 10=10, K=4, O/Q=3, U/J=2. Total=120 points. Highest scorer in round gets a stroke. 4 strokes = lose. - Follow the legal action constraints defined for Hindersche. - Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Ace-Ten scoring (A=11, 10=10, K=4, O/Q=3, U/J=2). - Winning condition: Avoid taking tricks with high point values. Win condition: Last player without 4 strokes wins.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Void suits early to smear high-value cards (Ace and Ten) onto opponents. Intermediate: Manage your trumps carefully. If you have low trumps, use them to win 'cheap' tricks so you don't have to take expensive ones later. Advanced: The 'March': If you have extremely high cards, you might try to win every single trick, which can sometimes result in a special bonus or clearing multiple strokes. - Tip: Leading a Ten is called 'selling a Tenner' - hope that someone else is forced to buy it! - Tip: Save a low card in each suit to exit tricks when needed.",
