    "history": "Believed to be a descendant of ancient Persian card games, closely related to Court Piece (Coat Piece) played in South Asia. Its current form stabilized with standard French/English decks in the 19th-20th centuries. - 19th Century: Trick-taking games with trump calling become established in Persia. - 20th Century: Becomes the de facto national card game of Iran. - Present: Exceptionally popular across generations, played in cafes, homes, and digitally. Hokm refined the trump-calling mechanic where a single player (the Hâkem) is tasked with leading the team's strategy by choosing trump based on only their first 5 cards. An essential part of Iranian social gatherings. Known for high-speed play and unspoken communication between partners.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two fixed partnerships (partners sit opposite each other). Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Ranking: Ace (high) down to 2 (low) in all suits. Equipment: Score-keeping paper and pen. Dealing: To determine the first Hâkem, deal cards face-up one at a time anticlockwise until someone receives an Ace — they become Hâkem. For play: the player to Hâkem's right deals. First deal 5 cards to each player. Hâkem looks at 5 cards and declares trump. Then deal remaining 8 cards to each player (13 total).",
    "rules": "Objective: Win tricks as a partnership. First team to win 7 tricks in a hand scores points. First team to accumulate 7 match points wins. Gameplay: 1. HÂKEM SELECTION: Deal cards face-up anticlockwise until someone gets an Ace — they are the Hâkem (Ruler). 2. DEALING: Player to Hâkem's right deals 5 cards to each player anticlockwise. 3. TRUMP DECLARATION: Hâkem looks at 5 cards and declares the trump suit (Hokm). Hâkem must not show cards to anyone. 4. REMAINING DEAL: Dealer deals remaining 8 cards to each player (13 total per player). 5. TRICK PLAY: Hâkem leads the first trick. Play proceeds anticlockwise. Each player plays one card. 6. FOLLOWING SUIT: Must follow the suit led if possible. If void, may play any card including trump. 7. TRICK WINNER: Highest trump wins. If no trump played, highest card of the led suit wins. Winner leads next trick. 8. HAND SCORING: Team that wins 7+...",
    "strategy": "Basic: As Hâkem, choose a trump suit where you have length and high cards (especially the Ace). Lead trump early to draw out opponents' trumps. Intermediate: Watch partner's signals. Discarding a specific suit hints at void or desire for that suit. As Hâkem, balance drawing trumps vs. saving them for later tricks. Advanced: Count all cards played, especially trumps. Communicate with partner through card choice. When Hâkem, calculate exactly how many trumps opponents hold before committing to a long suit play. Aim for Kot (all 13 tricks) when hand strength allows. - Tip: Leading trumps early is a strong opening to establish dominance. - Tip: Track which Aces have been played — they decide many tricks. - Tip: If your partner leads a suit, support it by playing high if you're winning, or low to save high cards. - Tip: As Hâkem, a 5-card trump suit is usually the safest choice.",
    "variations": "- Shelem: A bidding variant where players bid on how many tricks they can win. The highest bidder declares trump and must achieve their bid. - Two-Player Hokm: Adapted for 2 players. Each player gets 13 cards. No partnerships. - Rang: A variant where the trump suit is determined by the first card played face-up from the deck, rather than by the Hâkem's choice."
