    "overview": "Hungarian Tarokk is the 42-card tarot game generally known as H?szash?v?sos or Paskievics Tarokk. Four active players bid, exchange with the talon, call the XX to choose a partner, and score both card points and announced bonuses. Origin: Hungary Players: 4 active; optional fifth dealer sits out Deck: 42-card Hungarian Tarokk pack Difficulty: Expert Duration: 45-90 minutes",
    "history": "Part of the Central European Tarot tradition, which arrived in Hungary from Austria in the 19th century. - 1830s: Tarot games appear in Budapest's coffee houses. - 1890s: Modern Hungarian Tarokk rules (with bird imagery/Illustrated) solidify. - 1990s: Revival of organized Tarokk clubs in Hungary. - Present: A prestigious strategic game with a dedicated player base. The game moved away from scoring purely on points in tricks to a more complex system of bonuses (like 'Pagat Ultimo' and 'XXI Catch'). In Hungary, Tarokk is often calling the 'game of kings' or 'aristocrats', requiring significant mental agility.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players (2 vs 2 or 3 vs 1 depending on bidding). Deck: 42 cards: 22 Trumps (labeled I to XXI + Skylark/Sküs) and 20 suit cards (King, Queen, Cavalier, Jack and 10/Ace). Equipment: 42 cards: 22 Trumps (labeled I to XXI + Skylark/Sküs) and 20 suit cards (King, Queen, Cavalier, Jack and 10/Ace). Dealing: Deal in batches of 6 cards each.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score game points through contracts and bonuses. The primary goal is usually to win the majority of points in tricks (12 or 16 points). Gameplay: 1. Must follow suit. If unable, MUST play a trump. 2. Standard trick-taking rules: highest trump wins, or highest suit card if no trumps. Scoring: The scoring system is designed for people who settle up by pushing money across the table at the end of each hand. - Follow the legal action constraints defined for Hungarian Tarokk. - Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Scoring summary: Scoring follows the game-specific point or pot rules. - Winning condition: Score game points through contracts and bonuses. Win condition: The scoring system is designed for people who settle up by pushing money across the table at the end of each hand.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Identify your partner early by observing who plays the XX. Conserve your high trumps to protect your partner's high-value cards. Intermediate: The 'Talon' swap: Use the talon to discard your 'voids' (suits you have none of) so you can trump in on those suits later. Advanced: Master the cue-bid language. Knowing that your partner has the XIX and the XXI allows you to signal aggressive play styles. - Tip: The 'Sküs' is the most powerful card as it is the only one that can 'catch' the XXI. - Tip: Keep the lowest trump (I) for the final trick when possible; opponents may force it out earlier.",
    "variations": ""
