    "overview": "Young Hunter (also known as Hunter, German: JÃ¤ger) is a specialized German trick-taking game for four players. The game revolves entirely around the Jacks (the 'Youngs'), who act as both the 'Hunters' and the 'Prey'. Scoring is achieved not by winning tricks generally, but specifically by capturing or 'hitting' Jacks within tricks. Origin: Germany (Saxony / Thuringia) Players: 4 Deck: Standard 52-card deck (or 32-card German pack) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-30 minutes",
    "history": "A regional variant of traditional German trick-taking games like Skat and Schafkopf, but simplified to focus on specific cards. - 19th Century: Folk variants of 'Hunting' games recorded in East Germany. - 20th Century: 'Young Hunter' standardized in game rule collections. - Present: A niche regional game, often played in social clubs. The game's innovation is the 'Hits and Catches' system, where players must declare their targets before the round starts. Reflects the common 'Jacks-as-Special' motif found in Skat and other German-suited card games.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 individual players. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Ranking: A (High) to 2 (Low). Equipment: Standard 52-card deck. Ranking: A (High) to 2 (Low). Dealing: Deal all 52 cards (13 per player).",
    "rules": "Objective: Collect the most 'Jacks' (Youngs) by capturing them in tricks. Gameplay: 1. Standard trick-taking rules: must follow suit. 2. **The Catch**: If you play a higher card of the suit led and an opponent plays a Jack on that trick, you 'catch' it for 2 points. 3. **The Hit**: If you lead a Jack and no one can beat it with a higher card of the same suit, you 'hit' it for 2 points. Scoring: Scoring in Hunter is resolved as follows: Scoring follows the game-specific point or pot rules. - Follow the legal action constraints defined for Hunter. - Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Scoring summary: Scoring follows the game-specific point or pot rules. - Winning condition: Collect the most 'Jacks' (Youngs) by capturing them in tricks. Win condition: Win condition for Hunter: Meet the game winning condition.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Save your Aces and Kings to specifically use on tricks where you suspect a Jack will be played. If you have a Jack, try to lead it when you've already depleted that suit in your opponents' hands. Intermediate: Bidding Strategy: Only declare a 'Hit' if you have the Jack and the Ace of the same suit. The Ace ensures you can stop anyone from overplaying your lead. Advanced: Tracking 'Youngs': Keep track of exactly which Jacks have been played. If three are gone, the final Jack becomes the primary target for all players. - Tip: If you are leading and have no Jacks, lead a suit where you have high cards to flush out an opponent's Jack. - Tip: In the middle and end-game, avoid leading low cards, as it gives neighbors a free chance to dump their Jacks safely.",
