    "history": "Derived from the older game 'Marjapussi'. It includes influences from Russian games like 'Tysiacha' (1000). The current 'Huutopussi' name and rules stabilized in the mid-20th century. - 19th Century: Marjapussi is the dominant rural game in Finland. - 1920s: Bidding elements are added, creating the 'Huuto' (Bidding) variant. - Present: Highly popular in Southern Finland, particularly among university students. - Tournament Play: National championships are held annually. The game moved from a simple point-accumulation game to a contract-based game where failing a bid results in heavy penalties. Regarded as a game of high strategic depth, often associated with mathematical skill and deduction.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two partnerships. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Ranking: Ace (11), 10 (10), King (4), Queen (3), Jack (2), 9-2 (0). Note: 10 is higher than King. Equipment: Standard deck, score sheet. Dealing: Deal all 52 cardsâ€”13 per player.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first partnership to reach 500 points. Points are earned by winning tricks and declaring marriages. Gameplay: 1. Must follow suit. Must overplay (Ylimenopakko): if you can play a higher card than the current winner of the trick, you MUST. 2. Trump: There is no trump until a player who won the previous trick leads a King or Queen and declares a 'Marriage' of that suit. 3. Marriage Values: Spades (40), Clubs (60), Diamonds (80), Hearts (100). Declaring a marriage sets that suit as Trump. Scoring: Card points: Ace (11), 10 (10), King (4), Queen (3), Jack (2). Last trick +20. Marriage values: Spades 40, Clubs 60, Diamonds 80, Hearts 100. First partnership to 500 points wins. - Must follow suit - Must overplay (play higher if able) - Marriage declares trump: Spades 40, Clubs 60, Diamonds 80, Hearts 100 - First to 500 points wins Win condition: First partnership to 500...",
