    "overview": "Hwatu (í™”íˆ¬ - Flower Cards) refers to the 48-card Korean deck and the various 'fishing' games played with it, most notably Go-Stop (ê³ ìŠ¤í†±). It is a game of high tension and strategic risk-taking where players capture floral cards representing the 12 months to form specific combinations (Jokbo). The game is famous for the 'Go' or 'Stop' decision, where a winning player can choose to risk their points to double them or stop immediately to secure victory. Origin: Korea / Japan Players: 2-3 Deck: 48 Hwatu cards Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-20 minutes",
    "history": "Derived from the Japanese Hanafuda deck introduced to Korea in the late 19th century. Koreans adapted the rules, significantly increasing the complexity and adding the signature 'Go/Stop' mechanic. - 1890s: Hanafuda cards enter Korea through trade. - 1950s: Modern Go-Stop rules begin to differentiate from Japanese Koi-Koi. - 1980s: Becomes the de facto national social card game of South Korea. - Present: A ubiquitous cultural phenomenon featured in films (e.g., Tazza) and mobile apps. The addition of 'Jokers' (bonus cards) and the 'Bomb' and 'Shake' mechanics unique to Korea made the game faster and more gambling-oriented than its Japanese ancestors. An essential element of Korean holidays (Seollal and Chuseok), often played for small stakes among family.",
    "setup": "Players: Usually 3 players (Go-Stop) or 2 players (Mat-go). Deck: 48 cards (12 months x 4 cards each) + optional 2-5 bonus/joker cards. Equipment: 48 cards (12 months x 4 cards each) + optional 2-5 bonus/joker cards. Dealing: For 3 players: 7 cards each, 6 on the table. For 2 players: 10 cards each, 8 on the table.",
    "rules": "Objective: Capture cards to reach a target score (3 points for 3 players, 7 for 2 players) and then decide whether to continue ('Go') or end ('Stop'). Gameplay: 1. Play a card from hand to match a card of the same month on the table. 2. Draw a card from the deck to match another card on the table. 3. Captured cards are placed in categories: Brights (Kwang), Animals (Yul), Ribbons (Tti), or Junk (Pi). 4. **Go or Stop**: Once you hit the target score, if you say 'Go', you continue. Each subsequent 'Go' increases your point multiplier but risks 'Go-bak' (losing everything if another player scores). Scoring: Before beginning the game, the players should agree a target score at which the play of a hand can be stopped. - Follow the legal action constraints defined for Hwatu (Go-Stop). - Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Scoring summary: Scoring follows the game-specific point...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize capturing 'Junk' (Pi) cards early. While small, getting 10 Junk cards is the easiest way to reach your first point. Watch for opponents trying to get 'Godori'. Intermediate: The 'Shake': If you have 3 of the same month in hand, 'shake' them (show them) to double your score if you win. Advanced: Risk Math: Only call 'Go' if you have a clear path to your next point (e.g., you only need one more ribbon) AND you know your opponents are far from their target score. - Tip: The 'Kwang' (Bright) cards are high reward but hard to complete. - Tip: Try to 'Sswe-rip' (Sweep the table) to gain Junk cards from your opponents.",
