    "overview": "Hyakunin Isshu (ç™¾äººä¸€é¦– - 'One Hundred People, One Poem Each') is a traditional Japanese card game based on a classical anthology of waka poems. It is played as a competitive game called 'Karuta'. Players must match a poem being read aloud with the correct 'grabbing' card spread out on the floor. It is a game of extreme speed, memory, and auditory processing. Origin: Japan Players: 2 (Competitive) or Groups Deck: 200 cards (100 'Yomi-fuda' or Reading cards, 100 'Torifuda' or Grabbing cards) Difficulty: Expert Duration: 45-90 minutes",
    "history": "The anthology was compiled by Fujiwara no Teika in the 13th century. The game form, 'Kyogu Karuta', developed during the Edo period. - 1235: Poetry anthology compiled. - 16th Century: Portuguese 'Carta' (cards) arrive and merge with Japanese shell-matching traditions. - 1904: Official competitive Karuta rules established. - Present: A massive cultural event in Japan, with national tournaments held at the Omi Jingu shrine. Originally a literary game for nobility, it evolved into a highly athletic sport requiring split-second physical reactions. An essential New Year's tradition and a subject of many popular manga and anime (e.g., Chihayafuru).",
    "setup": "Players: Typically 1 vs 1 in competitive play. Deck: 100 poetry cards. In a match, only 50 are used (25 for each player). Equipment: Uta-garuta deck (200 cards: 100 Yomifuda reading cards, 100 Torifuda grabbing cards), playing mat or flat surface, reader (yomite) to read the poems. Dealing: Shuffle the 100 Torifuda (grabbing cards). Each player places 25 cards in their territory (face-up). The remaining 50 Torifuda are not used in that round. The Yomifuda (reading deck) is kept separate for the reader.",
    "rules": "Objective: Empty your territory of cards before your opponent. Gameplay: 1. A reader ('Yomite') reads the first half (upper phrase) of a poem. 2. Both players attempt to touch the grabbing card containing the second half (lower phrase). 3. If you grab a card in your own territory, it is removed. 4. If you grab a card in your opponent's territory, you send one card from your territory to theirs. 5. **Otetsuki (Fault)**: Touching the wrong card results in a penalty (opponent sends a card). Scoring: Empty your territory of cards before your opponent. - Reader recites the first phrase of a poem; first to touch the matching Torifuda wins that card. Correct touch in your territory removes the card; correct touch in opponent territory lets you send one card from your territory to theirs. Wrong touch (otetsuki) gives the opponent a card. First to empty their territory wins. Win condition:...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Memorize the first few syllables ('Kimari-ji') of each poem. Many poems can be identified after just 1, 2, or 3 syllables. Intermediate: Territory management: Group poems with similar starting sounds together so your hand is already in the right area when the reader begins. Advanced: Defensive posture: Position cards you are less confident in at the edges of your territory to make them harder for the opponent to steal. - Tip: Speed is secondary to accuracy; a 'fault' is more costly than being slightly slower. - Tip: Listen for the specific pitch and rhythm of the reader, as it can precede the actual words.",
    "variations": "- Kyōgi Karuta (Competitive): Official competitive rules: 50 Torifuda in play (25 per player), reader reads from full Yomifuda set, strict otetsuki penalty, national rules (e.g. All Japan Karuta Association). - Casual Uta-garuta: Recreational play: may use fewer cards, relaxed reading speed, or team play with multiple players. - Genpei War (Team): Team variant: two teams face each other; reader reads; first correct touch from either team wins the card for that team."
