    "overview": "Idiots (better known as Shithead, Palace, or Karma) is a widespread shedding-type card game. The goal is to avoid being the last player with cards, as that person is declared the 'Idiot' (Shithead). It features a unique three-layered hand structure and several 'magic' cards that can reset, skip, or burn the discard pile. Origin: Scandinavia / Global Players: 2-6 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Beginner Duration: 15-30 minutes",
    "history": "Likely originated in Scandinavia in the mid-20th century spread by backpackers and students. It gained popularity globally in the 1970s and 80s. - 1970s: Becomes a staple in youth hostels and university dorms. - 1990s: Rules reach North America as 'Palace'. - Present: One of the most common social card games played with a standard deck. The game evolved from simpler 'beat-the-card' games, adding the face-down 'blind' cards to create a high-tension endgame. Known for having many names, most of which are pejorative towards the loser.",
    "setup": "Players: 2 to 6 players. Deck: One deck for up to 4 players, two decks for 5+. Equipment: One deck for up to 4 players, two decks for 5+. Dealing: 1. Deal 3 cards face-down to each player (Blind cards). 2. Deal 3 cards face-up on top of them (Table cards). 3. Deal 3 cards to each player's hand. 4. Players can swap hand cards with their face-up Table cards before play begins.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first to get rid of all cards (Hand -> Table -> Blind). Gameplay: 1. Starting: Player with the lowest card (usually 3) begins. 2. Play: You must play a card equal to or HIGHER than the top card of the discard pile. 3. **Picking Up**: If you cannot play, you must pick up the ENTIRE discard pile into your hand. 4. **Magic Cards**: - **2s**: Reset the pile (can be played on any card). - **7s**: Next player must play a card equal to or LOWER than 7. - **8s**: Skip the next player. - **10s**: Burn the pile (it is removed from the game, and you play again). - **Four-of-a-kind**: Playing 4 of the same rank also burns the pile. Scoring: First player to shed all cards wins. Last player holding cards loses and is declared the Idiot/Shithead. - Follow the legal action constraints defined for Idiots. - Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Scoring summary: First player...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Swap high-value cards (Aces, 2s, 10s) to your face-up Table cards. They are your lifeline for the endgame. Avoid picking up a large pile early on. Intermediate: If you are forced to pick up a pile, try to do it when it contains many cards of the same rank, allowing you to play them all at once later. Advanced: Card Counting: Keep track of the 2s and 10s. If your opponent is on their blind cards and you know all the 10s are gone, you can block them with a high King or Ace safely. - Tip: Save a 10 or 2 for your very last card if possible. - Tip: Don't waste a 2 on a low card; use it to get out of a tight spot against an Ace or King.",
    "variations": "- Jokers: Two Jokers are added as special cards that reverse the direction of play and allow up to six players. In some variants a Joker may be played at any time; when played, the waste pile is given to another player (opponent in two-player; chooser in multiplayer). - Stacking: When rearranging before play, players may stack cards of the same rank on top of each other on the upcard piles and replenish the hand from stock; this may be done more than once if more cards can be stacked. - Tens Restricted: Tens may not be played on face cards; a player who plays a 10 may take another turn immediately. - Transparency: In one variant 8s are transparent and play is based on the card below; in others the 3 has this role instead."
