    "overview": "Jjak-mat-chu-gi (짝맞추기 - 'Making Pairs') is a traditional Korean domino game played with Gol-pae (bone tiles). Unlike Western dominoes where players match end-to-end, this is a 'pair-making' game where the objective is to form specific combinations (Civil and Military pairs). It is a draw-and-discard game similar in structure to Rummy but played with tiles. Origin: Korea Players: 2-4 Deck: 32 Gol-pae tiles Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-20 minutes",
    "history": "Dates back centuries in Korea, adapted from Chinese domino sets. It was first documented in Western literature by Stewart Culin in 1895 in his book 'Korean Games'. - 1800s: Traditional social game for Korean scholars and elders. - 1895: Documented by Stewart Culin. - Present: Played mostly by enthusiasts and elders; a precursor to modern Korean card games like Go-Stop. The game's suit system (Civil vs. Military) reflects the historical social structure of the Joseon dynasty. Used as both a social pastime and a tool for gambling (Seotda-style).",
    "setup": "Players: 2 to 4 players. Deck: 32 tiles: 11 'Civil' pairs (matching) and 10 'Military' tiles (unique, paired by total spots). Equipment: 32 tiles: 11 'Civil' pairs (matching) and 10 'Military' tiles (unique, paired by total spots). Dealing: First player draws 6 tiles, others 5 each. Remaining tiles form a face-down reserve. First player is chosen by draw (tile 2-5 or highest spots plays first).",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first to form three complete pairs in your hand. Gameplay: 1. Players draw a tile from the reserve and discard one into the center. 2. A player can 'chow' (claim) an opponent's discard ONLY if it completes a pair. 3. Pairs are laid face-down until the hand is won. Scoring: Be the first to form three complete pairs in your hand. - Each turn: either claim the previous player's discard to complete a pair, or draw one from the reserve. - After acquiring a tile, lay down any completed pairs face down, then discard one tile face up. - First to complete three pairs wins. With 2 or 3 players, 6-6 cannot be the third pair; with 4 players it can. Win condition: Be the first to form three complete pairs in your hand.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize Civil pairs early as they are easier to recognize. Keep the Supreme pair (tiles 1-2 and 2-4) if you find them—they are rare and valuable. Intermediate: Efficiency: If you have a Military tile like 6-1, keep an eye out for its counterparts 5-2 or 4-3. Knowing the pip totals is essential for speed. Advanced: Discard Management: Watch what tiles others are discarding. If both 1-1 tiles have been discarded, any remaining single 1-1 is a dead tile and should be discarded immediately. - Tip: Memorize the Military pairs (7s, 8s, 9s) to quickly identify matches. - Tip: The Supreme pair (4-2 and 2-1) is the only pair that is neither identical nor shares a total.",
    "variations": "- Win only by reserve: A player's third (winning) pair can only be made by matching a tile drawn from the reserve; winning by taking a discard is not allowed. - Row draw: Shuffled tiles are placed in a row; tiles are drawn from one end of the row instead of a reserve pile. - Alternative military pairing: Some sources use a different grouping for Military pairs: e.g. 2-6 with 3-6, 2-5 with 3-5, 2-4 with 3-4, 1-4 with 2-3, 1-2 with 4-5."
