    "overview": "Juse is a unique and high-intensity 'competitive patience' game from Sri Lanka. Unlike traditional solitaire, several players compete in real-time to build piles in the center and on each other's discard piles. It is a game of rapid pattern recognition where failure to play a 'forced' card results in a penalty (Juse!), making it a chaotic and energetic social experience. Origin: Sri Lanka Players: 2-4 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-20 minutes",
    "history": "Developed in Sri Lanka as a local adaptation of games like 'Crapette' or 'Racing Demon'. The name 'Juse' (possibly a variant of the name 'Joseph' or a local exclamation) refers to the act of calling out a player's mistake. - Mid-20th Century: Becomes a popular social game in Sri Lankan homes and schools. - 1990s: Rules are documented and standardized for club play. - Present: A beloved traditional game, specifically known for the 'Juse!' penalty call. The game evolved to be cyclic (Ace follows King), which prevents the game from 'stalling' like many other solitaire variants. In Sri Lankan culture, Juse is a game of loud exclamations and sharp focus; calling 'Juse' on a friend is the highlight of the social interaction.",
    "setup": "Players: Any number, but odd numbers (3, 5, 7) are traditional. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Ranked cyclically: ...-A-2-3...-K-A-... Equipment: Standard 52-card deck. Ranked cyclically: ...-A-2-3...-K-A-... Dealing: Deal cards face-down equally until 1 card remains. That last card is placed in the center to start the first foundation pile.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first to empty both your draw pile and your discard pile. Gameplay: 1. Each player has a face-down draw pile and a face-up discard pile. 2. In the center, four foundation piles (one for each suit) are built UPWARDS in suit. 3. **Turn Action**: You must play cards from your draw/discard pile to the center OR onto an opponent's discard pile (if one rank higher, regardless of suit). 4. **Forced Play**: If a card CAN be played to a center foundation, it MUST be played before any other move. 5. **Juse!**: If a player misses a forced play, any other player can shout 'JUSE!'. The offender must take cards from the bottom of every other player's pile. Scoring: No points; the first player to empty both their draw pile and discard pile wins the game. - Follow the legal action constraints defined for Juse. - Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Scoring summary:...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep a very close eye on the center foundations. The moment a rank appears that you have on your discard pile, play it immediately. Speed is everything. Intermediate: Offensive Filling: Use every opportunity to dump your cards onto your opponents' discard piles. This 'buries' their useful cards and gets you closer to winning. Advanced: Trap Playing: If you have a card that could go to the center, but playing it would help your opponent more than you (e.g., they have the next card in sequence), try to delayâ€”but be careful not to get 'Juse'd'! - Tip: Always check the center foundations before looking at your own draw pile. - Tip: If you are about to play a card on an opponent, check if it could go to the center first. Foundation play is always priority.",
