    "history": "Originating in the Ukrainian-Canadian communities of Saskatchewan in the early 20th century. Its name and mechanics are likely related to European games like 'Skat' or 'Euchre' brought by Central European immigrants. - 1910s: Emerges in rural Saskatchewan. - 1950s: Becomes a standard social game in Western Canada. - 1980s: Standardized rules appear in Canadian card game manuals. - Present: One of Canada's most distinct regional card games. The game's signature -3/+5 scoring evolved to reward players who can control the specific tricks they win or lose, rather than just winning 'as many as possible'. A central part of community life in the Prairies; annual tournaments are still held in small towns across Saskatchewan.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in fixed partnerships. Deck: 32 cards: 7, 8, 9, 10, J, Q, K, A in all suits, BUT the 7 of Hearts is replaced by the 5 of Hearts (+5), and the 7 of Spades is replaced by the 3 of Spades (-3). Equipment: 32 cards: 7, 8, 9, 10, J, Q, K, A in all suits, BUT the 7 of Hearts is replaced by the 5 of Hearts (+5), and the 7 of Spades is replaced by the 3 of Spades (-3). Dealing: Each player receives 8 cards. Dealing is clockwise.",
