    "history": "Developed as a social variant of Skat and Schafkopf in Northern Germany. It is specifically identified with the pub culture of Hamburg and the surrounding regions. - 19th Century: Ramsch-style games grow out of a frustration with traditional bidding. - Early 20th Century: Kalter Schlag standardizes in the Hamburg region. - Present: A niche but dedicated community keeps the game alive in North German card clubs. The game's scoring (points are bad) reversed the traditional goal of trick-taking, leading to a much more cautious and defensive playing style. Commonly played for small stakes where the local loser is the 'Cake' or pays for the round of drinks.",
