    "rules": "Objective: Avoid taking tricks containing high-value cards. The first player to accumulate 2,000 points loses the entire session. Gameplay: 1. Must follow suit. If unable, must play a trump (Jack) if held. 2. Highest Jack wins; if no Jack, highest card of led suit wins. 3. **Kontra/Re**: Before playing the first card, players can 'double' the value of the round points. Scoring: Points are bad in this avoidance game. Card values: Ace 11, Ten 10, King 4, Queen 3, Jack 2, others 0. - Ace: 11 points - Ten: 10 points - King: 4 points - Queen: 3 points - Jack (Trump): 2 points - 7, 8, 9: 0 points - Jacks are permanent trumps. Ranking: Clubs (High) > Spades > Hearts > Diamonds (Low). - Must follow suit; if unable, must play a trump Jack if held - Only the player who takes the MOST points in a round receives those points as penalty - Durchmarsch (winning all 8 tricks): Scorer gets 0, others...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Dump your Aces and Tens on tricks that OTHER players win. The goal is to stay 'under' the point threshold of the biggest loser of the round. Intermediate: Duck Tricks: Even if you could win a trick, sometimes it's better to play a low 7 or 8 to ensure you aren't forced to lead the next trick (which is always dangerous). Advanced: The Durchmarsch Gambit: If you hold 3+ Jacks and several Aces, you might try to win every single trick. This is high risk; if you miss even one trick, you will likely end up with the most points and take the penalty. - Tip: Jacks are only 2 points but they 'pull' other cards. Use them to force opponents to play their Aces. - Tip: The 'Kalter Schlag' (surprise loss) usually happens in the 6th or 7th trick when a player is 'squeezed' into winning a 20+ point trick.",
    "variations": "- Augenramsch: A very similar variant with slightly different Jack rankings. - Schieberamsch: A version where the 'Skats' (extra cards) are passed between players. - Hamburg Standard: The specific 2000-point limit variant."
