    "rules": "Objective: Be the first to 'Go Out' by melding all cards. The player with the lowest score after 7 rounds is the champion. Gameplay: 1. **Drawing**: Each turn, draw either the top card of the stock or the top discard. 2. **Melding**: Form sets (3-4 cards same rank) and runs (3+ consecutive same suit). 3. **Initial Meld**: Must meet round-specific point requirements. 4. **Discarding**: End turn by discarding one card. Scoring: Winner of a round scores 0. Losers score penalty points for cards remaining in hand. Game played over 7 rounds. - 7 rounds with different initial meld requirements each round - Sets: 3-4 cards of same rank (different suits preferred) - Runs: 3+ consecutive cards of same suit - Jokers are wild cards. Can represent any card - WARNING: Holding a Joker when someone else goes out is a 50-point penalty - Once you have made your first meld, you can add your cards to ANY...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Priority: Get rid of Jokers! Because they are 50 points, it is almost never worth holding onto a Joker to complete a long run. Play it as soon as you have a valid set. Intermediate: The 'Red Ace' Strategy: If you suspect another player is about to go out, try to hoard Red Aces. They only count as 1 point, allowing you to 'lose' the round with minimal damage. Advanced: Defensive Discarding: Pay attention to what cards are being picked up from the discard pile. If you know an opponent needs a 7 of Hearts to go out, keep that 7 in your hand even if it doesn't help your own meld. - Tip: Black Aces are high value (15) but also help hit opening requirements if your house uses themâ€”check your point values before playing. - Tip: In South African play, 'Going Out' is the only priority. Don't worry about 'perfect' melds.",
    "variations": "- SA Contract: A version that uses the 9-round contract system but with SA point values. - Kummiki: A local variant with alternate Joker exchange rules. - 7-Card Kaluki: A faster 'sprint' version."
