    "history": "Originating in Southern China (Cantonese culture), K'ap Shap is one of the most popular social domino games. It occupies a similar cultural space to Gin Rummy in the West. - 1800s: Widespread in Guangdong and Fujian. - 1900s: Follows the Chinese diaspora to San Francisco, Malaysia, and Hong Kong. - Present: A staple in community centers and family gatherings. The game's rules for 'Supreme' tiles (4-2 counting as 3 instead of 6) developed to maintain the 'Tens' theme. Commonly played during New Year celebrations for small-stakes betting.",
    "setup": "Players: 2-20 players (larger groups use more sets). Deck: 32 Chinese domino tiles (Civil and Military pairs). Equipment: 32 Chinese domino tiles (Civil and Military pairs). Dealing: First player gets 10 tiles, others get 9. Tiles are shuffled face-down and stacked in a wall.",
    "rules": "Objective: Form a 'Legal Hand' consisting of one identical pair and four additive pairs (totaling 10 or 20). Gameplay: 1. First player discards a tile. 2. Next players can either take the discard (if it completes their winning hand) or draw from the wall. 3. Every turn must end with a discard unless the player declares a win. Scoring: No points; first player to complete a legal hand wins and collects the ante pot. - Winning hand: one identical pair (eye) plus four pairs summing to 10 or 20 pips; the 4-2 tile counts as 3 pips. - First player may discard without drawing; others may take a face-up tile from the table or draw from the wall, then discard one. - Game begins with each player putting an equal ante into the pot; winner collects the pot. Win condition: First player to complete a legal hand (one identical pair plus four pairs summing to 10 or 20 pips, the 4-2 tile counting as 3)...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize forming the 'Eye' (identical pair) early, as it's the hardest part of the hand to complete. Keep tiles that are versatile for 'Tens' (like 5s and 6s). Intermediate: Watch for 'Dead Tiles': If three double-six tiles have already been discarded by others, your remaining double-six can never form an Eye and should be used in a total-sum pair instead. Advanced: The 'Wait Rule': If you draw a tile that *could* complete a 10-sum, but it's a Military tile with no matching partner left in the deck, discard it immediately to avoid 'clogging' your hand. - Tip: The four-two tile is the most flexible because its value change (from 6 to 3) allows for unexpected 10-sum completions. - Tip: Always verify the number of sets in play; with 4 sets, the probability of finding an identical pair increases significantly.",
    "variations": "- K'ap Shap (two-player): Two-player variant: single set of 32 tiles, wall four high and eight wide. First player takes 8 tiles, second takes 7. Winning hand: one identical pair (eye) plus three pairs summing to 10 or 20 (not four pairs)."
