    "history": "Brought to Greenland by Danish settlers and Arctic explorers, the game was adapted from European Crazy Eights (Olsen or Mau-Mau) to fit local social dynamics. The name 'Kapaga' is a localized phonetic name for a similar regional variant. - Early 20th Century: Shedding games spread via trade outposts in Greenland. - 1950s: Becomes a distinct variant with standardized Greenlandic house rules. - 2000s: Documented in Arctic travelogues (e.g., 'A Viking Voyage' by Hodding Carter). - Present: One of the most common family games in modern Greenland. The game simplified some of the more complex European 'Mau-Mau' rules to emphasize speed and social interaction over complex scoring. Commonly played alongside coffee and storytelling; the game's fast pace reflects the social warmth of indoor life during the Arctic winter.",
    "setup": "Players: 3 to 4 is optimal. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. No Jokers. Equipment: Standard 52-card deck. No Jokers. Dealing: Each player receives 7 cards. One card is placed face-up to start the foundations pile.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first player to discard all cards from your hand. Gameplay: 1. Players must play a card that matches either the suit or the rank of the top card on the foundation pile. 2. If a player cannot match, they must draw from the stock until they can play (or draw exactly once, depending on house variant). Scoring: Be the first player to discard all cards from your hand. - Play a card matching the suit or rank of the top card on the foundation pile. - If you cannot play, draw from the stock until you can (or once per turn in some variants). - An 8 may be played on anything and lets you choose the new suit. - An Ace skips the next player. - Call when you have one card left (e.g. Kapaga); failure may incur a draw penalty. Win condition: Be the first player to discard all cards from your hand.",
