    "overview": "KarnÃ¶ffel is arguably the oldest identifiable European card game still played today, with records dating back to 1426. It is a highly unusual trick-taking game where players are allowed to communicate freely with their partners. The game is famous for its chaotic 'Elected Suit' ranking, featuring cards with evocative names like the Joker-like 'KarnÃ¶ffel' (Unter), the 'Pope' (6), and the 'Devil' (7). Origin: Germany (Bavaria) Players: 2-8 (4 optimal) Deck: 32 cards (Standard 32, German suits); 48-card variant also played historically. Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-40 minutes",
    "history": "First mentions appear in a municipal ordinance in NÃ¶rdlingen in 1426. The name likely refers to 'testicular swelling' or 'hernia', used as a slang term for a coarse or powerful card. - 1426: Earliest written record of the game. - 1500s: Becomes a symbol of social upheaval; the 'Devil' card (7) was seen as scandalous for being able to beat high cards. - 1800s: Becomes a traditional folk game in rural Swiss and German regions (as KnÃ¼ffeln). - Present: Played by enthusiasts and historical reconstructionists. KarnÃ¶ffel predates the concept of a 'Trump' suit as we know it today; its 'Elected Suit' has only partial trumping powers, representing a transitional phase in card game history. The game was famously criticized by medieval theologians for its 'lawless' nature and for allowing low-born cards (like the 6 or 7) to defeat Kings and Emperors.",
    "setup": "Players: Typically 4 players in two partnerships. Deck: 32 cards (Standard 32, German suits: Acorns, Leaves, Hearts, Bells). French equivalent: remove 2s through 6s. Equipment: 32 cards (Standard 32, German suits: Acorns, Leaves, Hearts, Bells). French equivalent: remove 2s through 6s. Dealing: Deal 5 cards to each player, one at a time. The first card of each player's hand is dealt face up; the lowest of these face-up cards determines the chosen (trump) suit. If two tie, the first dealt decides. Then players pick up their cards.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win at least three out of the five tricks in a hand to score points for your team. Gameplay: 1. Standard trick-taking. NO OBLIGATION to follow suit (this is a key medieval rule). 2. Players can talk to their partners, showing cards or suggesting plays. 3. The leader of a partnership (the Director) often makes the final decision on signals. Scoring: Win at least three out of the five tricks in a hand to score points for your team. - No requirement to follow suit; highest card of led suit or highest trump wins the trick. - Lowest of the four face-up cards (one per player) determines the chosen (trump) suit for the deal. - First team to win three tricks wins the deal. Partners sit opposite; communication between partners is allowed. - Devil (7 of chosen suit) may not be played to the first trick; it beats all only when led. Win condition: Win at least three out of the five...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Talk! Communication is legal. Coordinate with your partner to ensure you don't 'waste' a KarnÃ¶ffel (Unter) on a trick your partner can win with a 4 or 5. Intermediate: The 'Devil' Lead: Use the Seven of the elected suit to Lead a trick you MUST win. It is almost unbeatable on the lead, but garbage if held back. Advanced: Managing the 'King-beaters': Since the 4 and 5 of trumps can only beat Kings (and lower), use them specifically when you know an opponent is holding a high card but no Master trumps. - Tip: Because you don't have to follow suit, you can 'hide' your master cards until the very last trick to secure the win. - Tip: The Seven (Devil) cannot be led on the first trick in many house rules; check before playing.",
