    "setup": "Players: Two to four players. The game is said to be good for four players. A standard 52-card pack is used; the first dealer is chosen at random and the turn to deal passes clockwise after each hand. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Aces are low (sequencing on Kings is King-Queen-Jack...2). No, wait. Standard Kings Corners Ranking: K-Q-J-10-9-8-7-6-5-4-3-2-A. Equipment: Standard 52-card deck. Aces are low (sequencing on Kings is King-Queen-Jack...2). No, wait. Standard Kings Corners Ranking: K-Q-J-10-9-8-7-6-5-4-3-2-A. Dealing: Each player is dealt 7 cards. Four cards are placed face-up in a cross (the 'T' or '+' shape). The rest form the draw pile.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first player to play all cards from your hand onto the table piles. Gameplay: 1. Players take turns. Each turn begins with drawing 1 card from the stock. 2. You can play any card from your hand onto a pile if it is one rank lower and a different color (e.g., a Red 7 onto a Black 8). 3. You can move an entire pile onto another pile if the bottom card of the first fits on the top card of the second. 4. **Kings**: If you have a King, it *must* be played into one of the empty 'Corner' slots (diagonals of the cross). Once there, it forms a new foundation pile. 5. Empty Slots: If one of the original 4 foundation slots becomes empty, you can place any card (or sequence) there. Scoring: First player to empty hand wins. Others score penalty points for cards remaining. - Build sequences in descending order - Alternate colors when building - Kings must go to corner slots - Can...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize emptying the 4 main slots by moving sequences. Every empty slot is a massive opportunity to play cards that don't fit elsewhere. Intermediate: The 'King Save': If you already have cards that fit on a King, wait for an opponent to play their King into a corner first so you don't have to waste your turn filling a corner slot. Advanced: Sequence Consolidation: Always look for ways to merge existing table piles. This frees up the 4 main slots, which can then be filled with any card, potentially dumping a large part of your hand at once. - Tip: Don't build too deep on the 4 main piles if you have a King; you want the Corners to be most accessible. - Tip: Watch your opponents' colors. If they are primarily drawing Red cards, try to play Black cards to the board to entice them to 'tack' their cards onto yours.",
