    "overview": "KlÃ¡ber (also known as FelsÅ‘s or Tartli) is the Hungarian variant of the trick-taking game Klaberjass, particularly popular among ethnic Hungarians in Hungary and neighboring regions. Unlike the international two-player version, KlÃ¡ber is primarily played by four people in fixed partnerships. It features a unique Hungarian deck with seasons-based Ace imagery and William Tell characters on the knaves (Over and Under). Origin: Hungary Players: 4 (Partnerships) Deck: 32-card Hungarian/German deck (Magyar KÃ¡rtya) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-45 minutes",
    "history": "Rooted in the 19th-century Central European 'Jass' family. It arrived in Hungary via the Austro-Hungarian empire and was adapted to the 32-card Magyar KÃ¡rtya (Hungarian cards). - Late 1800s: Jass-style games spread in Hungarian coffee houses. - 1920s: The name 'KlÃ¡ber' becomes the standard for the 4-player Hungarian variant. - 1990s: Competitive KlÃ¡ber tournaments gain a revival in Budapest. - Present: A beloved traditional game, often played in families and social clubs. Transitioned from a two-player 'Jewish' Clob to a four-player partnership game, requiring much more defensive coordination between teammates. The game uses the 'Tell' deck cards (Acorns, Leaves, Hearts, Gourds), adding a strong regional cultural flavor to the gameplay.",
