    "overview": "Klaberjass (also known as Clob, Bella, or Bela) is a quintessential two-player trick-taking game that has enjoyed immense popularity for over a century. Known for its complex ranking of trumps (where the Jack and Nine are the most powerful) and the 'Bella' bonus (King and Queen of trumps), it is a game of skill, nerve, and precise point-counting. It belongs to the Jass family of games. Origin: Central / Eastern Europe (Jewish communities) Players: 2 Deck: 32 cards (7-Ace) Difficulty: Expert Duration: 20-30 minutes",
    "history": "Rooted in the Low Countries (Netherlands/Belgium) and popularized by Jewish communities in Central Europe. The name likely stems from 'Klaverjas' (Dutch for Jack of Clubs). - 1800s: Standardizes in Western Europe. - 1920s-1940s: Becomes the most popular two-player game in social clubs across Europe and North America. - Present: Retains a cult following, particularly in Hungary, South Africa, and the United States (as Clob). The game's unique trump ranking (Jack=20, Nine=14) evolved to create a dynamic where the 'Jass' (Jack) and 'Nell' (Nine) are the only truly reliable cards. Famously played in the tea shops of Budapest and the delis of New York, it is often seen as a 'scholar's game' due to its depth.",
    "setup": "Players: Two players. The game is played head-to-head; each player receives six cards initially, then after trump is determined each receives three more for a total of nine cards per hand. Deck: 32 cards (7, 8, 9, 10, J, Q, K, A). Equipment: 32 cards (7, 8, 9, 10, J, Q, K, A). Dealing: Each player is dealt 6 cards (3 then 3). One card is turned up to start trump determination. After trump is set, another 3 cards are dealt to each player (total 9 per hand).",
    "rules": "Objective: Score 500 points by winning tricks containing point cards and declaring melds. Gameplay: 1. Must follow suit. If unable, must trump if possible. 2. **Bella**: Playing the King and Queen of trumps scores 20 points (must be announced). 3. **Meld**: Sequences of 3 (20 pts) or 4 (50 pts) in the same suit score. Only the highest meld on either side scores for the team. 4. **Last Trick**: 10 points. Scoring: Points from card values, Bella (20), melds (20-50), and last trick (10). First to 500 wins. - Trump ranking: Jack (20), Nine (14), Ace (11), Ten (10), King (4), Queen (3), Eight (0), Seven (0) - Plain suit ranking: Ace (11), Ten (10), King (4), Queen (3), Jack (2), Nine (0), Eight (0), Seven (0) - Must follow suit, must trump if cannot follow - Bella bonus for King-Queen of trumps Win condition: First player to reach 500 points",
    "strategy": "Basic: If you hold the Jass (Jack) of trumps, you should almost always 'Take' the bid. 20 points is a massive head start in a 100+ point round. Intermediate: The 'Ten' Lead: In plain suits, the 10 is very vulnerable to being captured by an Ace. Only lead a 10 if you have several of that suit or want to draw out the Ace early. Advanced: Bidding with the Nell: If you hold the Nine of trumps and several other high plain cards, you can bid even without the Jack, provided you can force the Jack to be played on a trick you don't care about. - Tip: Always announce 'Bella' when playing your King or Queen of trumps! Forgetting the call loses the 20-point bonus. - Tip: The 7 of Trumps (The 'Seven of Diamonds' in many traditions) can be swapped for the face-up card if it was the trump-setter.",
    "variations": "- Clob: Popular American English name for the game. - Klaber: Hungarian variant for 4 players. - Kalooki-style Klab: A variant with Joker wildcards."
