    "history": "Part of the 'Slapjack' family of games found across Europe. It gained a distinct identity in German student circles and rural social gatherings ('Kneipen') during the 20th century. - Early 1900s: Traditional reaction games appear in Bavarian social clubs. - 1950s-1970s: Becomes a popular youth party game under various names like 'Kloppen' or 'Hau-Zu'. - Present: A staple of German house parties and social drinking games. The game evolved from simple 'first-to-spot' matching into a rhythmic game where players chant numbers or categories while revealing cards. In Germany, 'Kartenkloppen' is associated with high social volume and physical interaction; it is a game of reflexes rather than deep logic.",
    "setup": "Players: Best with 4 to 6 players. Deck: Usually a 32-card German deck (7 to Ace) or a standard 52-card deck. Equipment: Usually a 32-card German deck (7 to Ace) or a standard 52-card deck. Dealing: All cards are dealt out evenly to the players face-down. Players may not look at their cards.",
    "rules": "Objective: Avoid being the last player to slap the pile (or be the first, depending on variant) and capture all cards to win. Gameplay: 1. Players take turns flipping one card face-up onto a central pile in a rhythmic fashion. 2. **The Chant**: As each card is played, the current player says a rank in ascending order: 'Seven', 'Eight', 'Nine', 'Ten', 'Jack', 'Queen', 'King', 'Ace', then repeats. 3. **The Kloepper (Knock)**: If the card revealed matches the rank being spoken, all players must immediately slap their hand onto the central pile. 4. **Penalty**: The last player to slap takes the entire pile of cards and adds it to their personal stack. 5. **Fake-Outs**: If a player slaps the pile when no match has occurred, they take the pile as a penalty. Scoring: First player to empty their hand wins. Last player with cards loses. - Players chant ranks in ascending order - Slap when card...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Maintain the rhythm. If you lose the beat of the chant, you will be late to react when the match occurs. Focus on the dealer's hand movement. Intermediate: Peripheral Vision: Don't stare at the center. Watch the player whose turn it is next. If you see them flinching to slap, you should slap too. Advanced: Rhythmic Deception: Some high-level variants allow the current player to pause slightly or speed up their flip to bait opponents into a 'fake' slap (foul). - Tip: Keep your slapping hand close to the table edge, not in your lap. - Tip: In drinking variants, the person who takes the pile also 'takes a sip'. - Tip: Pay attention when the chant resets from Ace back to Seven; this is the most common moment for reaction errors.",
    "variations": "- Kloppen (Bavarian): Uses German-suited cards with different chants (Unter, Ober, Sau). - Slapjack: The international version where only Jacks are slapped. - Stress: A two-player high-speed variant."
