    "overview": "Knorri (also known as HÃ¶rri) is a traditional Finnish card game for three to eight players. It belongs to the 'beating' or 'avoidance' family of games (like Skitgubbe). The game is uniquely divided into two phases: a 'Collection Phase' where cards are picked up from the table based on rank-matching, and a 'Trick Phase' where players must play the cards they collected to avoid being the last person with cards in hand. Origin: Finland (Swedish Influenced) Players: 3-8 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-40 minutes",
    "history": "Originates in Finland, likely derived from the Swedish game 'Mas' or 'Skitgubbe'. The name 'Knorri' is localized Finnish slang for the rhythmic flipping of cards. - Late 19th Century: Beating games gain popularity in the Nordic maritime trade routes. - 1920s: Knorri becomes a distinct Finnish variant with the 'Diamonds-as-Trump' rule. - Present: Played in rural Finland and among traditional card game enthusiasts. Knorri preserved the 'Complete Information' aspect of early Nordic beating games, where all cards are visible during the first phase. Commonly played as a social game where 'losing' is the main concern, rather than scoring high points.",
    "setup": "Players: Best with 4-5 players. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Diamonds are **Trumps**. Clubs are **Hard/Anti-Trump** (Anti-Truff). Equipment: Standard 52-card deck. Diamonds are **Trumps**. Clubs are **Hard/Anti-Trump** (Anti-Truff). Dealing: No cards are dealt to players. 9 cards are placed face-up on the table to start. The rest form a draw pile.",
    "rules": "Objective: Avoid being the last player left with cards in your hand at the end of the second phase. Gameplay: Knorri (HÃ¶rri) proceeds through deal, legal play actions, and end-of-round resolution. Play proceeds through legal actions until round resolution. Scoring: First Phase The player to the left of the dealer begins after which players take turns in clockwise order. - Follow the legal action constraints defined for Knorri (HÃ¶rri). - Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Scoring summary: Scoring follows the game-specific point or pot rules. - Winning condition: Avoid being the last player left with cards in your hand at the end of the second phase. Win condition: First Phase The player to the left of the dealer begins after which players take turns in clockwise order.",
    "strategy": "Basic: In Phase 1, try to avoid cards that have many smaller cards of the same suit on the table. You want a small, manageable hand for the trick phase. Intermediate: The 'Club Shield': Since Clubs cannot be trumped by Diamonds, they are the safest cards to lead if you want to ensure a trick follows standard suit-ranking without interference. Advanced: Phase 1 Memory: Since you see every card entering the table/hands, remember who picked up the high Diamonds. Use your Clubs to neutralize their trump advantage during the second phase. - Tip: In the first phase, if you must take a card, try to take one that completes a sequence (e.g., if you take a 10 and an 8 is on the table). - Tip: Save your Diamonds (Trumps) for the very end of the trick phase to secure your 'exit' from the game.",
    "variations": "- Soft clubs: Clubs can be beaten by any spade, heart or diamond; diamonds trump all, hearts and spades trump clubs, clubs beat only lower clubs. - Scoring: Optional scoring: +1 per player still in when you go out; loser gets negative points so total is zero (e.g. 4-player: +3, +2, +1, -6). - Last Card: Player with one card must announce \"last card\" before next player plays; forgetting forces them to take bottom card of trick on next turn instead of going out."
