    "overview": "Koi-Koi is the most popular card game played with traditional Japanese 'Hanafuda' (Flower Cards). Played between two people, it is a fishing (capture) game where players aim to form specific combinations of cards called 'Yaku'. The name 'Koi-Koi' refers to the strategic choice a player makes after forming a Yaku: either to end the round and take their points, or to call 'Koi-Koi' (Come On!) to continue play in hopes of a higher score at the risk of losing it all. Origin: Japan Players: 2 Deck: 48-card Hanafuda deck (12 suits of 4 cards, each suit representing a month) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-20 minutes",
    "history": "Originates from 18th-century Japan. Hanafuda cards were developed as a way to circumvent government bans on Western-style gambling cards, using floral imagery instead of numbers. - 1889: Nintendo is founded specifically to produce Hanafuda cards. - Early 20th Century: Koi-Koi emerges as the competitive standard for two-player Hanafuda. - 1980s-Present: Featured prominently in Japanese pop culture (e.g., the film 'Summer Wars'). - Modern: Standardized as a competitive mind sport with national rankings in Japan. The game's signature 'Koi-Koi' doubling mechanic was a later addition designed to increase the psychological pressure and high-stakes nature of the game. Koi-Koi is deeply tied to Japanese aesthetics, with yaku names like 'Tsukimi-zake' (Moon Viewing Sake) and 'Hanami-zake' (Flower Viewing Sake).",
    "setup": "Players: 2 players (called the 'Oya'/Dealer and 'Ko'/Child). Deck: 48 cards. Suits by month: January (Pine), February (Plum), March (Cherry)... to December (Paulownia). Each suit has 4 cards of varying value (Bright, Animal, Ribbon, Chaff). Equipment: 48 cards. Suits by month: January (Pine), February (Plum), March (Cherry)... to December (Paulownia). Each suit has 4 cards of varying value (Bright, Animal, Ribbon, Chaff). Dealing: Each player is dealt 8 cards face-down; 8 cards are placed face-up in the 'field' (ba). The rest form the draw pile.",
    "rules": "Objective: Form 'Yaku' (point combinations) and outscore your opponent over 6 or 12 rounds. Gameplay: 1. **Match**: Play a card from hand to match a card of the same suit in the field. If matched, take both. If no match, discard to the field. 2. **Draw**: Draw the top card of the deck and match it with the field. Take any matches. 3. **Forming Yaku**: If your captured cards form a combination (e.g., 3 Ribbons, or 'Ino-Shika-Cho'), you must decide: **Stop** (Score points) or **Koi-Koi** (Continue). Scoring: Form 'Yaku' (point combinations) and outscore your opponent over 6 or 12 rounds. - Match by month (suit): play a card from hand to capture a same-month card from the table, or discard to table if no match. - After play, draw one from stock; if it matches a table card capture both, else add to table. - On forming a yaku, choose to stop and score or call Koi-Koi to continue; opponent...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Priority one: The **Sake Cup** (Kiku-ni-Sakazuki). It is the most powerful card in the deck as it allows you to form the Moon-Viewing and Flower-Viewing yaku with only two cards. Intermediate: Denial: Watch your opponent's captured pile. If they have two 'Blue Ribbons', your primary goal is to capture the third Blue Ribbon to prevent them from scoring 5 points, even if it doesn't help your own Yaku. Advanced: When to call 'Koi-Koi': Only call it if you have a clear path to adding another 1 or 2 points (e.g., you need one more Ribbon) AND you know your opponent is missing the key cards for their own Yaku. - Tip: The Paulownia (December) suit is mostly 'Chaff' (1 pt cards but useful for the 10-chaff yaku). Don't ignore it. - Tip: If you have four of the same suit in your hand at the start, it is an automatic 6-point yaku (Teshi).",
    "variations": "- 6 rounds: Play 6 hands instead of 12; players may agree before the game. - Koi-Koi once per hand: Player may call Koi-Koi only once per hand (not on every yaku-forming turn). - Tsukimi-zake and Hanami-zake optional: Some rulesets omit the Moon Viewing and Flower Viewing yaku. - Ino-Shika-Cho Yaku: The boar, deer, and butterfly scoring combination in Koi-Koi; kept as Koi-Koi rules content rather than a separate game."
