    "history": "Part of the broader European family of 'Solitaire-style' shedding games. It gained popularity in the Netherlands in the mid-20th century as a social game between neighbors. - 1950s: First variants emerge in Dutch social clubs. - 1980s: Standardized rules appear in Dutch family game anthologies. - 2010s: Gains a second life as a popular app-based game in Norway (Kanin). - Present: A staple children's and family game in the Benelux region. The game evolved to allow for the 'Switch' rule, where players can break existing sequences to create new ones, adding a light mathematical challenge. The name 'Konijnen' likely refers to the speed of the gameâ€”players must 'hop' their turns quickly or lose their opportunity to play.",
    "setup": "Players: 2 to 5 players; 3 to 4 is optimal. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Aces are low (1). Equipment: Standard 52-card deck. Aces are low (1). Dealing: Each player is dealt 7 to 10 cards. The remainder forms the draw pile.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first player to discard all your cards by placing them into numerical sequences on the table. Gameplay: 1. Players take turns clockwise. 2. On your turn, you must play as many cards as possible into existing sequences or start new ones. 3. **Starting Sequences**: A new sequence must start with an Ace (1) or a Two (2). 4. **Building**: You can add a card to any sequence if it is exactly one rank higher than the current top card (e.g., a 7 on a 6). 5. **Shifting (Tactical Maneuver)**: Some house rules allow you to 'break' a long sequence (e.g., 1-2-3-4-5) to start a new branch if you have the cards to support both (e.g., moving the 4-5 to a different pile). 6. **Drawing**: If you cannot play at least one card, you must draw one from the stock and your turn ends. Scoring: Be the first player to discard all your cards by placing them into numerical sequences on the table....",
    "strategy": "Basic: Don't sit on your 2s. Playing a 2 starts a new pile, giving you more 'landing spots' for your other cards. Save your high cards (Kings) for later, as they can only be played on a complete sequence. Intermediate: Hand Thinning: If you have many cards of different suits but similar ranks (e.g., three 7s), wait until a 6 is played to dump all of them onto various piles at once. Advanced: Stalling: If you see your opponent has only 1 or 2 cards left, and you are in the lead, avoid starting a new sequence with an Ace. Force them to draw from the stock until they happen to have a card that fits an existing pile. - Tip: Watch the table carefully! Sequences move fast and a pile that was a 3 might be a 10 by the time it's your turn again. - Tip: If playing with Jokers, use them as wildcards for 2s or Aces to get the game moving.",
    "variations": "- Team Kanin: Played in teams; adds cooperation as partners work together to empty hands. - Kanin with Jokers: Jokers included as wildcards; may represent any card in a sequence (e.g. Ace or 2 to start, or any rank to build). - Basic (no shifting): No tactical shifting; players may only add to existing sequences or start new ones with A or 2, without rearranging table piles."
