    "history": "Dates to the mid-18th century. Kille cards (derived from Cuckoo cards) spread throughout the Nordic countries. Krypkille developed as a way to play with the specialized deck without the high-stakes gambling of 'Enkelt' or 'Dubbelt' Kille. - 1750s: Kille/Cambio games arrive in Sweden. - 1800s: The unique Swedish 42-card deck is standardized. - 1920s: 'Krypkille' is formally documented as a popular parlor game. - Present: A cherished traditional game, still played using specialty decks by companies like Ã–berg. The game evolved to maximize the use of the deck's symmetrical nature (two of every card) and the complex hierarchy of the character cards. Kille is a distinctively Swedish cultural asset; the cards feature archaic illustrations of masks, flowers, and the Cuckoo (Kuku).",
    "setup": "Players: Optimal with 4 to 6 players. Deck: 42 cards. Ranks: Kuku (Cuckoo), Hus (Home), Katt (Cat), Cavall (Horse), WÃ¤rd (Host), Fleur, Krans, Blomma, and Numbers 1-12. Harlekin (Joker) is the absolute highest or lowest depending on the rule phase. Equipment: 42 cards. Ranks: Kuku (Cuckoo), Hus (Home), Katt (Cat), Cavall (Horse), WÃ¤rd (Host), Fleur, Krans, Blomma, and Numbers 1-12. Harlekin (Joker) is the absolute highest or lowest depending on the rule phase. Dealing: Each player is dealt one card. The round involves exchanging cards to avoid the lowest rank.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score as few points as possible by avoiding capturing tricks or holding 'lowest' cards. Gameplay: 1. Standard trick-taking. Must follow suit (if suits are used) or rank. 2. In Krypkille, the player with the *HIGHEST* card wins the trick (and thus 'penalty' cards). 3. **The Harlekin**: Changes value. If led, it is the highest. If played later, it is the lowest. Scoring: Score as few points as possible by avoiding tricks and penalty cards. - Two of each card in the 42-card deck - Harlekin is highest when led, lowest when played second - Hus (Home) blocks exchanges - 'Home stays' - Kuku (Cuckoo) penalty when received - Avoid winning tricks to avoid penalty points Win condition: Lowest score when a player reaches the point cap",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep middle-range cards (ranks 5-8). These are rarely high enough to win a trick and rarely low enough to lose an exchange round. Intermediate: The 'Hus' Block: If you have a 'Hus' (Rank 11), use it to block exchanges from your right-hand neighbor. This forces them to keep their potentially bad card. Advanced: Harlekin Timing: If you are the leader, play the Harlekin to win the trick immediately if you suspect the trick contains zero penalty points. If you play it second, ensure it's on a trick you *don't* want to win, as it will be the lowest card. - Tip: Pay attention to the sounds! 'Kuku', 'Mjau' (Cat), and 'Home stays' tell you exactly which high/blocking cards are in play. - Tip: If you receive the 'Katt' (Cat), try to pass it on immediately; holding it until the end usually leads to a 'Mjau' penalty.",
