    "history": "Originates in 18th-century Russia, predating the modern standardized 'Podkidnoy Durak'. It belongs to the 'Nakladka' branch of games which emphasizes the volume of cards in the play pile. - 1700s: Early 'Nakladka' variants appear in Russian taverns. - 1800s: 'Kryt'-Navalivat' becomes a popular informal street game. - Present: Occasionally played as a nostalgic or simpler alternative to modern Durak. Unlike modern Durak where the defender wants to clear the table, in Kryt'-Navalivat', the goal is to force the opponent into a massive 'pickup' by making the pile too difficult to beat. The game's name itself describes the rhythmic physical action of slapping cards down quickly on top of each other.",
    "setup": "Players: 2 players is the standard competitive format. Deck: 36 cards. Rank: A (High) > K > Q > J > 10 > 9 > 8 > 7 > 6 (Low). Equipment: 36 cards. Rank: A (High) > K > Q > J > 10 > 9 > 8 > 7 > 6 (Low). Dealing: Each player is dealt 3 cards. The next card is turned up to set the Trump suit. The rest form a draw stock.",
