    "history": "Dates to the 19th century as a simplified variant of the French Jass and Belote families. The name 'Couillon' is Walloon French slang; in Flemish regions, it is often called 'Kwajongen' or 'Troeven'. - 1890s: Couillon variants become standard in Belgian cafes. - 1940s: 'Kujong' emerges as a localized phonetic name in some Eastern Belgian and border regions. - Present: A very common 'Kroegspel' (pub game) and family game in the Benelux region. The game evolved to use a very small deck (24 cards) to ensure high-rank clashes in every hand. In Belgium, the loser is traditionally 'traktatie' (must buy the next round of drinks).",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two fixed partnerships (A+C vs B+D). Deck: 24 cards: A, K, Q, J, 10, 9 in four suits. Ace is highest (4 pts), King (3), Queen (2), Jack (1). Equipment: 24 cards: A, K, Q, J, 10, 9 in four suits. Ace is highest (4 pts), King (3), Queen (2), Jack (1). Dealing: Each player is dealt 3 cards initially. The next card is turned up for the Trump suit. After the suit is accepted or rejected, each player gets 3 more cards.",
