    "history": "Derived from the ancient 'Warlords' or 'Dai Hin Min' families of games, it was adapted in the late 19th/early 20th century to reflect the Swedish Estate system (StÃ¥ndsriksdagen). - 1900s: Hierarchy games gain popularity in Swedish schools. - 1970s: The name 'Kung och Adel' becomes standardized as a variant of 'Skitgubbe' style social hierarchy. - Present: A very common household and party game in Sweden. The game evolved to include specific 'Feudal taxes' where the King can demand specific cards from the Peasant, ensuring a cycle of power. Used as a humorous metaphor for social mobility and class struggle in Swedish student circles.",
    "setup": "Players: Best with 4 to 6 players. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. 2s are usually 'clearing' cards or wildcards. Equipment: Standard 52-card deck. 2s are usually 'clearing' cards or wildcards. Dealing: The whole deck is dealt out evenly among players.",
