    "history": "Developed by Hassidic and Orthodox Jews in Galicia to bypass the use of 'standard' cards, which were seen as potentially idolatrous or too secular. Hanukkah was the only time card-playing was culturally permitted in many strict communities. - Late 1700s: First handmade Kvitlech decks appear. - 1800s: The game becomes a central secular tradition for the third night of Hanukkah. - 1910s: Piatnik (Vienna) begins commercial production of Kvitlech decks. - Present: Still played in many Jewish households, often using the game as a charitable fundraiser for 'Hanukkah Gelt'. The point values were adjusted over time; originally based on games like 'Pochen', it stabilized as a 21-clone with unique 'framed' card bonuses. Known colloquially as 'Klein Shas' (Small Talmud) because the wooden boxes for the cards resembled Talmud volumes.",
    "setup": "Players: A Banker is chosen; other players are bettors. Deck: Standard 24-card deck contains two sets of numbers 1-12. No suits, Kings, or Queens. Equipment: Standard 24-card deck contains two sets of numbers 1-12. No suits, Kings, or Queens. Dealing: The Banker deals one card face-down to each player and one to themselves.",
    "rules": "Objective: Get a card total of exactly 21, or as close as possible without exceeding it (busting). Gameplay: 1. Players bet against the house. 2. Players can 'Hit' (draw more cards) one by one. 3. If a player reaches 21, they win immediately. 4. If they exceed 21, they are out. 5. The Banker draws last and must beat all 'Open' hands to win their bets. Scoring: Closest to 21 without exceeding wins. 21 is automatic winner. - 24-card deck: two sets of numbers 1-12 - No standard suits or court cards - 12 can count as 12 or 9 (player's choice) - 1 (Ace) always counts as 1 (never 11) - Framed 2s and 11s together count as automatic 21 - Banker plays last and must beat all remaining hands Win condition: Closest to 21 without busting",
