    "overview": "L'Hombre (Spanish for 'The Man') is a historical three-player card game that was the most popular and prestigious card game in Europe for over two centuries (c. 1650-1850). Played with a 40-card Spanish-suited (or standard) deck, it introduced the revolutionary concept of bidding to determine the trump suit and the 'declarer' (The Hombre) who plays alone against the other two. It is the direct ancestor of games like Ombre, Quadrille, and Skat. Origin: Spain Players: 3 Deck: 40-card deck (Standard minus 8, 9, 10) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-45 minutes",
    "history": "Developed in Spain in the early 17th century. It was a courtly game, widely mentioned in classical literature (e.g., Cervantes, Pope). - 1610s: Early documented rules in Spain. - 1660s: Becomes the sensation of the English and French royal courts. - 1750s: Peak popularity across Europe as 'The game of the nobility'. - 1850s: Replaced by Whist and later Bridge, though it survives in Denmark and Spain today. The game's 'Matadors' system (permanent trumps) inspired the higher-rank systems in later games like Spades and Belote. Famously described in Alexander Pope's poem 'The Rape of the Lock'. In Denmark, it is still played competitively by the 'L'Hombre-Klub'.",
