    "setup": "Players: Optimal for 3 or 4 players. Can be played with 2 with modifications. Deck: 32 cards (standard 52 minus 2-6). Use a Piquet or Skat deck, or remove cards 2-6 from a standard deck. No trumps. Equipment: Standard deck and score sheet. Dealing: All 32 cards are dealt out equally. For 4 players: 8 cards each. For 3 players: 10 cards each with 2 discarded (or dealt 10-11-11).",
    "rules": "Objective: Score the fewest points. Avoid capturing the Queen of Spades (50 points) and minimize capture of other point cards. Gameplay: Standard trick-taking: 1. Must follow suit if possible. 2. If void in led suit, may play any card. 3. Highest card of the led suit wins the trick. 4. There are no trumps. 5. Winner leads to the next trick. Scoring: Penalty points for capturing cards. Lower score is better. - Queen of Spades = 50 points penalty. - Ace = 11 points, Ten = 10 points, King = 4 points, Queen = 3 points, Jack = 2 points. - 7, 8, 9 = 0 points. - Must follow suit if possible. - The Queen of Spades can be played at any time once a player is void in the led suit, even if they hold other spades (the 'Laus' rule). - Game ends when a player reaches 100 or 150 points (agreed beforehand). Lowest score wins. Win condition: Lowest total score when any player reaches 100 or 150 points.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Dump your high-value Spades! If you have the Queen of Spades, you MUST try to discard it when someone else leads a high Spade (Ace or King). If you don't have the Queen, avoid leading Spades until you know who has it. Intermediate: Suit Exhaustion: Choose one suit where you have very few cards (e.g., only one Heart). Void that suit early by playing your card when you can. Once you are void, you have a safe 'dumping ground' for the Queen of Spades or high Tens when those suits are led. Advanced: The Ace Shield: If you hold a high Ace, don't lead that suit early. Save it for the end of the game when others are more likely to have discarded their low cards, forcing you to win multiple high-point cards at once. Track which point cards have been playedâ€”if the Queen hasn't appeared yet and you're holding the King of Spades, be very careful about leading Spades. - Tip: Counting the...",
    "variations": "- Hearts: The standard international version, typically played with full 52-card deck and all Hearts as penalty cards plus the Queen of Spades. - Black Maria: British variant with three penalty cards: Queen of Spades, Ace of Spades, and Ten of Diamonds. - Schwarzer Peter: Related children's avoidance game where the loser is the one stuck with the 'Black Peter' card. - Eff-Eff: German variant with different card point weights and additional penalty rules."
