    "history": "Developed in post-WWII Italy as a variation of 'Manipulation Rummy'. Its rules were popularized in Mediterranean card clubs during the 1960s. - 1950s: Rules for 'Rummy a Terre' involve basic manipulation. - 1970s: The name 'Machiavelli' becomes standard in Italian gaming circles. - 1980s: The game's mechanics are used as the basis for the global hit board game Rummikub. - Present: One of the most common family card games in Italy and widely played on digital platforms. The game was traditionally played with Jokers, but modern tournament rules often omit them to increase the challenge of forming natural sequences. Seen as a 'smart' game, played in cafes and university lounges.",
