    "history": "Developed in the mid-19th century in the Yangtze River delta, likely from earlier card games like Madiao and Khanhoo. - 1850s: Earliest versions appear in Ningbo. - 1920: Abercrombie & Fitch brings Mah Jong to the USA, starting a global craze. - 1949: Forbidden in mainland China during the Cultural Revolution. - 1985: Re-introduced and legalized in China; remains a cultural cornerstone in Hong Kong and Taiwan. The game branched into dozens of regional variants (Hong Kong, Japanese Riichi, American, Taiwanese), each with different scoring and special hands. Used as a social lubricant and a test of character in Chinese business and family life.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players. Each represents a Wind (East, South, West, North). Deck: 3 Suits (Circles, Bamboo, Characters) numbered 1-9 (4 of each). Winds (4 of each). Dragons (Red, Green, White - 4 of each). Plus 8 Flowers/Seasons. Equipment: 3 Suits (Circles, Bamboo, Characters) numbered 1-9 (4 of each). Winds (4 of each). Dragons (Red, Green, White - 4 of each). Plus 8 Flowers/Seasons. Dealing: Each player receives 13 cards. East (dealer) starts with 14.",
    "rules": "Objective: Form a valid 14-card hand consisting of four sets of three and one pair. Gameplay: 1. Draw a card from the deck or 'steal' the previous discard. 2. Stealing: You can call 'Chow' (only from the player to your left), 'Pung' or 'Kong' (from anyone). 3. If you complete your 14th card, you call 'Mah Jong'. Scoring: Points or match rules. - Each player has 13 tiles; East (dealer) has 14 to start. Win by completing 14 tiles in four melds and one pair. - Draw one tile from the wall or claim a discard to meld (Chow from left neighbor only; Pung or Kong from any). Then discard one tile. - Melds: Pung (three identical), Kong (four identical), Chow (three same suit in sequence). Declare Mah Jong when the 14th tile completes the hand. Win condition: Win by game rules.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep your options open. Don't commit to a 'Pung' early if it ruins a potential 'Chow' sequence. A hand made mostly of Chows is easier to complete but scores fewer points. Intermediate: Efficiency (Shanten): Calculate how many cards you are away from a complete hand. If you are 'Yi-Shanten' (1 card away), focus entirely on speed rather than high points. Advanced: Defensive Discarding: If an opponent has revealed three Pungs of the same suit, never discard a card of that suit. You risk 'giving' them the winning card (Feeding the Dragon). - Tip: Wait for 'Two-Sided' sequences (e.g., 4-5) rather than 'Middle' sequences (4-6). - Tip: Pay attention to 'Wall' counting. If three copies of a card are already showing, don't wait for the 4th.",
    "variations": "- Hong Kong (Old Hong Kong): Uses standard 144 tiles, simple scoring with a relatively small set of scoring hands. Common introductory variant. - Japanese Riichi: Japanese variant with riichi (ready) declaration, furiten rule, and different scoring including yakuman hands. - American Mah Jongg: USA variant with different tile sets, card-based hands that change annually, and distinct scoring."
