    "history": "Likely brought to the Indonesian archipelago by Portuguese or Dutch traders, the game evolved into its modern 'Red focus' in the 20th century. - 1900s: Fishing games are popular across the Dutch East Indies. - 1970s: Main Merah becomes a staple of social gatherings in Java and Sumatra. - Present: Remains a common pastime, especially in rural areas where it's played for small wagers or bragging rights. The point system was simplified to focus on red cards to make scoring faster and more visually intuitive. The name reflects the Indonesian obsession with the number 10 and the color red (symbolizing luck and bravery).",
    "setup": "Players: 2 players (10 cards each) or 3 players (7 cards each). Deck: Standard 52 cards. Equipment: Standard 52 cards. Dealing: 2 players: 10 cards each; 3 players: 7 cards each.",
    "rules": "Objective: Capture as many red cards as possible, specifically those with high point values. Gameplay: 1. Play a card from your hand. 2. Draw a card from the deck and play it to the table. 3. **Capturing**: You capture a card if your card and the table card SUM TO 10 (e.g., 4 captures 6). 4. **Face Cards**: 10, J, Q, K are captured by an IDENTICAL rank (e.g., King captures King). 5. If your card doesn't capture anything, it stays on the table. Scoring: Points or match rules. - Capture by sum to 10 (e.g. 4 captures 6) or by matching rank for 10/J/Q/K. - Only red cards (Hearts, Diamonds) score points; black cards are for capturing. - Each turn: play one card from hand, then draw one from stock and play it to the table. Win condition: Player with the most points from captured red cards at end of game wins.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize the Red Ace! It is the single most valuable card (20 points). Since it can only be captured by a 9, never play your Red 9 unless you are sure you can snatch the Red Ace or if the Ace is already gone. Intermediate: The 'Setup' Discard: If you have a Black 4 and a Red 4, play the Black 4 to the table if there is a 6 you want. If you don't capture it immediately, you might lure your opponent into playing an 8, which you can then take with a different card. Advanced: Table Clearing (Clean): In many Indonesian house rules, clearing the table entirely (taking the last cards) gives a huge point bonus. Manage your 10-sum pairs to ensure you are the last one with a 'killing' move. - Tip: Empty the high cards first. Kings and Queens are easy to track because they only match themselves. - Tip: Don't waste red cards to capture black cards unless you absolutely need to clean the...",
