    "history": "Played on the coast of the Italian province of Marche. The name 'Mambassa' is of uncertain origin but the game is essentially a four-card version of Petrangola. - Traditional: Played in coastal Marche region - Related: Derived from Petrangola, which is related to Schwimmen/31 - Present: Remains a regional favorite in central Italy Developed as a four-card variant of the three-card Petrangola, increasing the maximum hand value from 31 to 41. A social game played in cafes and family gatherings in the Marche region, often for small stakes.",
    "setup": "Players: 3-8 players (optimal with 5-6). Each player starts with 4 tokens (vite/lives). Deck: 40-card Italian-suited Piacentine deck. Rank: Ace (11), King (10), Horse (10), Jack (10), 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2 (face value). No 8s, 9s, or 10s. Equipment: 40-card Italian Piacentine deck; tokens (four per player, one agreed value). Dealing: 4 cards face down to each player. Dealer looks at next 4 cards and either places them face up as the 'monte' or takes them as a hand (which counts as a knock).",
    "rules": "Objective: Have the highest total value in one suit (maximum 41) or collect a 'mambassa' (four equal cards or four-card sequence). Avoid having the lowest hand when someone knocks. Gameplay: Player to dealer's right begins; play passes anticlockwise. Each player may exchange 0, 1, 2, 3 or all 4 hand cards for an equal number from the face-up monte; if they did not exchange all 4 they may optionally discard one card face down to the bottom of the stock and draw the top card. Exchanging all 4 or exchanging none and not drawing counts as a knock; after a knock each other player has one more turn, then hands are exposed and compared. Scoring: Points or match rules. - Each turn: exchange 0–4 cards with the monte, then optionally discard one to bottom of stock and draw one from top. Exchanging all 4 or exchanging 0 and not drawing counts as a knock. After a knock, each other player has one...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Focus on building a strong hand in one suit. The maximum is 41 (Ace + King + Horse + Jack of the same suit). Don't hold onto weak cards hoping they'll improve. Intermediate: Watch what cards other players take from the monte - this reveals what suits they're building. If you see someone take a Coin card, they likely have other Coin cards in hand. Advanced: The dealer has a significant advantage by seeing the monte first. As dealer, consider taking the monte as your hand if it's strong - this immediately knocks and limits other players to one exchange. Timing your knock is crucial - knock early with a good hand to prevent opponents from improving. - Tip: Four equal cards (especially low ones like 2-2-2-2) are very strong hands - Tip: Sequences are harder to complete but beat three-of-a-kind hands - Tip: Pay attention to which cards have been exchanged - they're out of play - Tip:...",
