    "history": "Manni is the national card game of Iceland, with numerous local variations across the country. The name refers to the spare hand (Manni) that can be used to improve a player's hand. - Traditional: Played throughout Iceland for generations - Regional: HornafjÃ¶rÃ°ur hosts the World Championship during the summer lobster festival (since 1997, ~300 participants) - Documentation: Rules recorded in 'SpilabÃ³k AB' by Ãž. GuÃ°mundsson (1989, Reykjavik) - Present: Remains extremely popular in Iceland with many local variants Multiple regional variations exist, with differences in dealing patterns, trump rotation, and scoring. The HornafjÃ¶rÃ°ur version keeps the 2s in the pack and deals differently. A cornerstone of Icelandic social life, played in homes, pubs, and at festivals. The World Championship in HornafjÃ¶rÃ°ur is a significant cultural event.",
    "setup": "Players: 3 players. Each plays individually in some contracts; in others, the declarer plays against the other two. Deck: 52 cards (Trjámann). Ranking: Ace (high), King, Queen, Jack, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2 (low). In Nullo contract Ace may be low in some variants. Equipment: 52 cards (Trjámann). Ranking: Ace (high), King, Queen, Jack, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2 (low). In Nullo contract Ace may be low in some variants. Dealing: Basic version: Deal 16 cards to each of three players in packets of four, with four cards left for the Manni (spare hand). HornafjÃ¶rÃ°ur version: Keep the 2s, different dealing pattern.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score the highest net positive points after 12 deals by making or breaking contract targets. Gameplay: Standard trick-taking rules apply. Must follow suit if possible. If void, may play any card. Trump suit wins over plain suits. Highest card of led suit wins if no trumps. Scoring: Points or match rules. - Must follow suit if possible; if void may play any card. - Trick won by highest trump if any, else highest card of led suit. Winner leads next trick. - Declarer (left of dealer) chooses contract: Grand, Spades, Other, or Nullo. Each player must play each contract once over 12 deals. - Declarer may discard up to 4 cards and draw from the Manni before play. Win condition: Win by game rules.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Use the Manni exchange wisely - discard weak cards and hope to draw trumps or high cards. In Nullo, get rid of high cards early by playing them when you can't win the trick. Intermediate: Card counting is essential. Track which high cards have been played, especially in Nullo where you want to avoid taking tricks. In Grand and suit contracts, estimate how many tricks you can realistically win. Advanced: Contract selection strategy: Choose Grand when you have balanced high cards across suits. Choose a suit contract when you have length and strength in that suit. Choose Nullo when you have low cards and short suits. The exchange decision: Sometimes keeping a moderately strong hand is better than risking the Manni. - Tip: In Nullo, leading low cards in long suits often forces opponents to win tricks they don't want - Tip: Remember that in the 3-player game, there are only 16 tricks...",
