    "setup": "Players: 4 players in two fixed partnerships (partners sit opposite). Deck: 40 cards. Ranking in all suits: 3 (Highest), 2, Ace, King, Knight (Cavallo), Jack (Fante), 7, 6, 5, 4 (Lowest). Equipment: Italian deck (or 52-card deck with 8s, 9s, 10s removed). Score sheet. Dealing: Dealer deals 10 cards to each player. The Trump suit is determined by turning the last card dealt to the dealer (or by dealer declaration in some variants).",
    "rules": "Objective: Score game points by winning card points in tricks. First team to 41 Game Points wins. Gameplay: 1. **Lead**: Player to dealer's right leads. 2. **Follow Suit**: Players MUST follow suit. 3. **Trump**: If void in led suit, you may play a Trump (rank rules apply) or discard. You are not forced to trump if you can discard (unlike some games). 4. **Winning**: Highest Trump wins. If no trump, highest card of led suit wins. 5. **Signals**: Players observe strict signaling rules when playing cards (e.g., knocking on the table). Scoring: Points are tallied cumulatively. - The 3 is higher than the 2, which is higher than the Ace. - You do not have to head the trick if you follow suit. - Trumping is optional if void in led suit (you can undercut/discard). Win condition: First to 41 points.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Remember that the Ace is the most valuable card (1 point) but it is vulnerable to the 2 and 3. Don't play an Ace unless you know the 2 and 3 are gone or your partner sits behind you to protect it. Intermediate: The 'Busso': Use the Knock signal only when you command the suit (e.g., you hold 3-2-K). Your partner is obligated (strategically) to return that suit to you. Advanced: Counting the 11/3s: There are 10.66 points in the deck. You must count exactly how many 'pieces' (Figures) have been played to know if the remaining cards are worth fighting for. - Tip: In Trumps, lead the 3 or 2 early to draw out the opponents' trumps. - Tip: Saving the Ace of Trumps for the last trick guarantees 2 points (1 for Ace + 1 for Final Trick).",
    "variations": "- Beccaccino Proper: Usually played to 31 points instead of 41. Often allows 'Busso' only. - Tressette a Donne: Variant where declarations (Napola) are more elaborate, including sets of 3s, 2s, or Aces."
