    "history": "Dates to the mid-18th century. Popular legend attributes its refinement to Madame de Pompadour, the chief mistress of Louis XV, during her time at Versailles. - 1760s: First descriptions of 'La Marquise' and related piquet patiences appear in French court journals. - 1800s: Included in the 'Livre des Patiences' as a foundational solo game. - 1900s: Migrates to England and the USA as part of the 'French Solitaire' collection. - Present: Remains a favorite among traditionalists who prefer 32-card sets over 52-card decks. The game was designed to be played on a small card table (guÃ©ridon) and emphasizes 'aristocratic' grace in card movement. Symbolizes the transition of card games from social gambles to private, meditative puzzles.",
    "setup": "Players: One player only. Marquise is a solitaire (patience) game for a single player; the player works against the layout, building four foundation piles by suit and managing the tableau and stock. Deck: 32 cards (Hearts, Diamonds, Clubs, Spades). Equipment: 32 cards (Hearts, Diamonds, Clubs, Spades). Dealing: Deal according to solitaire layout; reserve and tableau as per rules.",
