    "overview": "Marriage is a three-pack Rummy game popular in Nepal (especially Kathmandu valley) and Bhutan, and by the Nepali diaspora. Played with 156 cards (three standard 52-card decks), each player receives 21 cards. Wild cards (tiplu, poplu, jhiplu) are determined during play. Players form sets of three: tunnela (three identical cards), pure sequence (three consecutive same suit), or trial (three same rank, different suits); the first to complete three pure sets chooses the tiplu (main wild). The first to arrange all cards into valid sets and discard wins the hand; scoring uses maal points and payments. Origin: Nepal Players: 2-5 Deck: 156 cards (three standard 52-card packs; no printed jokers) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-90 minutes",
    "history": "Marriage is a three-pack Rummy game popular in Nepal, especially in the Kathmandu valley, and also played in Bhutan. It is widely played during the two main Hindu festivals Dashain (Dushera) and Tihar (Deewali) and by enthusiasts throughout the year. - Traditional: Played in Nepal and Bhutan for generations - Festivals: Central to Dashain and Tihar celebrations - Present: Popular in Nepal, Bhutan, and Nepali diaspora; multiple apps and online implementations Evolved from Rummy to use three decks and a dynamic wild-card system (tiplu, poplu, jhiplu) unique to this regional variant. Commonly played during Dashain and Tihar as a social tradition; national card game of Nepal.",
    "setup": "Players: 2 to 5 players. Each plays individually. Deck: Three standard 52-card packs shuffled together: 156 cards in all. No printed jokers; wild cards are created each deal (tiplu, poplu, jhiplu). Equipment: Three standard 52-card packs shuffled together: 156 cards in all. No printed jokers; wild cards are created each deal (tiplu, poplu, jhiplu). Dealing: Dealer gives 21 cards to each player. The next card is turned face up to start the discard pile; the rest form the face-down stock. Deal and play are clockwise in the most common rules; some regional rules use anticlockwise.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first to complete three pure sets (tunnelas or pure sequences) to choose or see the tiplu, then arrange all cards into valid three-card sets (and optionally seven or eight dublees) and discard the last card to end the hand. Score the most points from maal and tunnelas. Gameplay: Each turn: take one card (top of stock or the face-up discard) then discard one card. Only the latest discard may be taken. The first player to lay down three tunnelas or pure sequences chooses a card from the stock as the tiplu (face down under the stock); that rank and the cards immediately above and below it in the same suit become wild (poplu, tiplu, jhiplu). After that, use wild cards to form dirty sets. End by laying all cards in valid sets and discarding one, or by laying eight dublees. Scoring: Points from maal (tiplu, poplu, jhiplu in hand) and tunnelas exposed at start. Winner...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize forming three pure sets (tunnelas or pure sequences) so you can choose or see the tiplu. Avoid discarding cards that complete opponents' sets. Intermediate: Track which cards have been discarded and estimate the tiplu from the first player who lays down. Use the three-deck composition to plan trials and sequences. Advanced: Balance going out quickly versus collecting maal (tiplu, poplu, jhiplu) for points. A marriage (one jhiplu, one tiplu, one poplu) scores 10 points. - Tip: Expose a tunnela at the start if dealt one for potential points - Tip: If you have seen the tiplu, you cannot take a joker from the discard - Tip: Dublee players may only take from discard if it completes the eighth dublee",
    "variations": "- Direction of play: Some play anticlockwise with first card to dealer's right; many published rules and apps use clockwise. - Dhoos: Penalty (e.g. 15 points) for cheating or throwing maal to a player who has seen the tiplu; penalty cancelled if the offender wins the next hand. - Better: After rounds complete, play with double points; dublee can trigger better. - Open: Player may see the tiplu without laying three pure sets but may only use wilds after showing three pure sets."
