    "strategy": "Basic: Take exactly one trick. Once you have captured one trick containing a low point card (like a King or a 3), stop winning. Leading small cards (4s and 5s) for the rest of the game is the safest way to stay in the 'middle' bracket. Intermediate: Dump your Aces. Because an Ace is worth 3 points, it is a dangerous liability. Try to play your Aces early into tricks led by others, especially if you cannot win the trick. Advanced: The Outlier Trap: If you see a player has 0 points and it's near the end, lead a suit they *must* follow to force them to win a trick. This protects you from being the 'low' loser and forces them into the game. - Tip: Last Trick: Avoid winning the last trick at all costs, as it carries a 3-point penalty (plus whatever cards are in it). - Tip: Suit Balance: If you have a long suit of 4-5-6, keep it for the end of the game to ensure you don't take unwanted tricks.",
    "variations": "- Rovescino: The parent game where ONLY the high score loses. - Tressette a Perdere: The high-speed 'Loser Wins' variant."
