    "overview": "Mattis (also known as Skitgubbe in Sweden) is a unique two-phase card game popular in Scandinavia. The first phase involves collecting cards by winning tricks, while the second phase reverses the objective - players try to get rid of their collected cards by beating opponents' cards. The last player holding cards loses. It is a game of strategic timing and card management. Origin: Scandinavia (Finland/Sweden) Players: 3 Deck: 52-card standard Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-45 minutes",
    "history": "The game originated in Finland as Myllymatti (mentioned in 19th century newspaper articles) and spread to Sweden where it became known as Skitgubbe. The Norwegian variant is called Mattis. - 19th Century: Myllymatti mentioned in Finnish newspapers as a popular game - Early 20th Century: Spreads to Sweden as Skitgubbe - Mid 20th Century: Norwegian variant Mattis develops with slight rule differences - Present: Played throughout Scandinavia and by Scandinavian communities abroad The game's unique two-phase structure (collecting then shedding) likely evolved from combining trick-taking with beating game mechanics. A popular social game in Scandinavia, often played in informal settings and family gatherings. The name 'Skitgubbe' roughly translates to 'Old Man' in Swedish.",
    "setup": "Players: 3 players (standard). Can be adapted for 2 or 4 with modifications. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Ranking: A (high), K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2 (low). Equipment: Standard deck, score sheet optional. Dealing: Three cards each; remainder forms face-down stock. With three players one card remains in the stock or is set aside.",
    "rules": "Objective: Avoid being the last player holding cards after both phases. Gameplay: Phase one: all players play one card to each trick (from hand or stock). Suits do not matter; highest rank wins. Ties are broken by tied players playing again until one wins. After playing, each player draws from the stock to keep three cards until stock is empty. When a player has no card to play, phase one ends; players combine hand and won cards for phase two. Phase two: each turn beat the previous card or sequence, or pick up the lowest (earliest) card or sequence in the trick. Last player with cards loses. Scoring: Usually played without scoring - the loser is simply identified. In some variants, players play multiple rounds and track losses. - Phase One: Standard trick-taking, winner collects cards - Phase Two: Beat the highest card showing or pick it up - Suit ranking in Phase Two: Spades > Hearts...",
    "strategy": "Basic: In Phase One, try to win tricks to collect high cards for Phase Two, but don't collect too many cards. In Phase Two, save your highest cards for when you really need them. Intermediate: In Phase One, control when you win tricks - winning early tricks gives you control but also more cards to shed later. Try to collect cards in strong suits (Spades and Hearts). Advanced: Card counting is crucial. Track which high cards have been played in Phase One to predict opponents' holdings in Phase Two. In Phase Two, force opponents to use their high cards early by playing medium cards that are difficult to beat without using premium cards. - Tip: Don't win too many tricks in Phase One - each card won is a card you must shed in Phase Two - Tip: Save Aces and Kings of Spades for critical moments in Phase Two - Tip: Try to maintain cards in multiple suits to have flexibility in Phase Two -...",
