    "overview": "Michigan Rummy (also known as Newmarket, Boodle, or Stops) is a classic American family game that combines elements of sequence-based shedding with a wagering layout. Players compete to be the first to play all their cards (Shedding) while simultaneously trying to play specific 'Money Cards' (Ace of Hearts, King of Clubs, etc.) that correspond to pots on the game board. It is one of the most famous 'three-games-in-one' style tabletop classics. Origin: USA / UK Players: 3-8 Deck: Standard 52-card deck + special layout board Difficulty: Beginner Duration: 20-40 minutes",
    "history": "Derived from the ancient game of 'Pope Joan', simplified into 'Newmarket' in the UK and then branded as 'Michigan Rummy' by various US publishers (including Milton Bradley and Pressman) in the 20th century. - 1800s: 'Stops' and 'Newmarket' are popular in Victorian England. - 1930s: US companies begin producing colorful 'Michigan Rummy' boards. - 1960s: Becomes a staple of the American family game closet alongside Monopoly. - Present: Still produced in various 'retro' and modern editions. The addition of a 'Poker' round at the start of some versions evolved it into a more complex gambling hybrid. Symbolizes the 'Golden Age' of American cardboard gaming.",
    "setup": "Players: Three to eight players. The game is suitable for about 3 to 8 players; all cards are dealt out one at a time so hand sizes vary slightly (some players may have one card more than others). One spare hand (dummy or window) is dealt and left face down; it is not used during play but creates stop cards when the next higher card in sequence lies in that hand. Deck: Standard 52 cards. Equipment: Staking board or cloth with four layout positions; chips or counters. Alternatively, four cards from a second deck placed on the table for stakes. Dealing: Each player receives a hand. An EXTRA 'Dummy' hand is dealt (the Window or Widow).",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the most chips by playing 'Money Cards' and being the first to empty your hand. Gameplay: Before play, the dealer places two chips on each boodle card (Ace of Hearts, King of Clubs, Queen of Diamonds, Jack of Spades) and each other player places one chip on each. All cards are dealt clockwise one at a time to each player and one spare hand (dummy), which stays face down. The player to the left of the dealer begins by playing any suit but must play the lowest card they hold in that suit. Whoever holds the next higher card in the same suit must play it; play continues in suit sequence until the ace is reached or no one can follow (a stop card). The last player to play starts the next sequence with their lowest card in any suit. Playing a boodle card collects all chips on that card. When a player runs out of cards, play ends; each other player pays one chip per card left in...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep track of the 'Stops'. If you know a card is in the dummy hand (because the sequence ended early), you can lead into that suit safely, knowing you will likely hold the last playable card and get to start the next sequence. Intermediate: Suit Control: If you have a 'Money Card' (e.g., Ace of Hearts), try to maneuver the game into Hearts. If the current sequence is Spades, wait for a Stop, then lead your lowest Heart to move toward your payout card. Advanced: The Dealer's Gamble: As the dealer, you can choose to swap your hand for the 'Dummy' hand. Only do this if your current hand is poor and has zero Money Cards. The Dummy hand is a total unknown but holds the potential for better payouts. - Tip: Don't lead your Money Cards immediately. If you lead an Ace of Hearts, you might get the payout, but you also end that suit's sequence instantly. - Tip: Watch what other players...",
    "variations": "- Free placement of chips: Players put a fixed number of chips (e.g. 6) on the layout at the start of the hand but may distribute them as they like among the boodle cards. - Dealer exchanges or auctions the spare hand: After the deal, the dealer may exchange their hand for the face-down spare hand without looking at it first; or may auction the spare hand to the highest bidder, who discards their hand and pays the dealer. - Compulsory change of suit: When starting a new sequence after a stop or ace, the leader must if possible play a card of a different suit from the one just played."
