    "overview": "Racing card game for 2–6 players. Each hand is a race to 1000 km (or 700 km in 2–3 player with optional extension). Players play distance cards to advance; opponents play hazards (Accident, Out of Gas, Flat Tire, Stop, Speed Limit) which are countered by remedies and prevented by safeties. First to reach the target distance wins the hand. Game ends when one side reaches 5000 cumulative points. Origin: France Players: 2-6 Deck: Mille Bornes deck (special) Difficulty: Beginner Duration: 30-60 minutes",
    "history": "Created in 1954 by Edmond Dujardin in France as 1000 Bornes; derived from the American game Touring (1906, William Janson Roche). Name refers to the approximate length of Route Nationale 7 from Paris to the Italian border. Parker Brothers licensed and published it in the US from 1962; at one point it outsold Monopoly. - 1954: Dujardin creates 1000 Bornes in Arcachon, France. - 1960: Revised edition with updated artwork; bilingual cards. - 1962: Parker Brothers begins US distribution. - 1982: Parker Brothers revision with simplified card art. - Present: Published by Asmodee (US), Winning Moves, others; 26 known publishers worldwide. Coup-fourré (playing a safety in response to the corresponding hazard for a 300-point bonus) was added compared to Touring. Spanish version Mil Hitos was popular in 1970s Spain; Dutch predecessor Stap op (1939) used cycling theme with same mechanics. Listed...",
    "setup": "Players: 2, 3, 4, or 6 players. Four or six players form teams of two sharing a tableau. Deck: Dedicated Mille Bornes deck of 106 cards: 18 hazards, 38 remedies, 4 safeties, 46 distance cards (25, 50, 75, 100, 200 km). Equipment: Mille Bornes card game (commercial deck). Dealing: Shuffle and deal six cards to each player. Remainder forms the draw pile; a discard pile forms next to it. Each turn: draw one card from stock, then play one card to tableau or discard one. Hand size stays at six after play.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win a hand by being first to play enough distance cards to reach the target (1000 km in 4- or 6-player team play; 700 km in 2- or 3-player with option to extend to 1000). Win the game by being first to reach 5000 cumulative points. Gameplay: On your turn draw one card, then play one card or discard. Play Roll to drive; play distance cards (25–200 km) when Roll is showing; max two 200 km per hand. Play hazards on opponents; play remedies on your battle/speed area; play safeties for immunity and optional coup-fourré. Discard when no legal play. Hand ends when someone reaches target distance or all cards are played. Scoring: Points per hand: 1 per km traveled; 100 per safety; 300 bonus for all four safeties; 300 per coup-fourré; 400 for completing the trip; 300 delayed action (trip after stock exhausted); 300 safe trip (no 200 km cards); 200 extension bonus; 500 shutout...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Play distance when you can; save remedies for when you are hit; use hazards to slow the leader. Intermediate: Time hazards (Stop, Speed Limit) when the opponent has just played Roll; hold one safety for coup-fourré if you can afford the hand space. Advanced: In teams, coordinate so one partner holds safeties for coup-fourré while the other pushes distance; in 2–3 player consider calling Extension only when you can reach 1000 before opponents. - Tip: Playing a safety gives an extra draw and another play; chain safeties when you have several. - Tip: Speed Limit limits the opponent to 25 and 50 km only until they play End of Limit. - Tip: Shutout (500 pts) requires completing the trip before the opponent has played any distance card.",
    "variations": "- Extension (2–3 player): In 2- or 3-player play the target is 700 km. The first player to reach 700 km may either claim victory and end the hand, or call for an Extension that raises the target to 1000 km. First to 1000 km then wins the hand and scores a 200-point Extension bonus."
