    "history": "Mittlere Jass is a Swiss Jass variant, part of the larger Jass family that originated in the Netherlands and became extremely popular in Switzerland. The game was documented by Nick Wedd in English-language card game literature. - Traditional: Developed within Swiss Jass tradition - Modern: Documented and shared through Jass-playing communities - Present: Played by Jass enthusiasts, particularly those seeking three-player options The game is the three-player ancestor of Molotow Jass (for four players). The unique middle-score objective distinguishes it from other Jass games. Jass is the national card game of Switzerland, played in pubs, clubs, and homes throughout the country. Mittlere offers a unique three-player option in a game family often optimized for four.",
    "setup": "Players: 3 players, each playing for themselves. Deck: 36-card Jass deck (6 through Ace in four suits: Acorns, Shields, Flowers, Bells). Can use standard deck with 5s and below removed. Equipment: 36-card Jass deck or modified standard deck, scoring slate (for sticks and potatoes). Dealing: All 36 cards dealt out, 12 to each player, in packets of 3. Deal and play are anticlockwise.",
    "rules": "Objective: Avoid having the middle score. Score points by winning tricks, but avoid being caught in the middle. Gameplay: Player to dealer's right leads to first trick. Must follow suit if possible. If unable to follow, may play any card - if no trump is established, this card's suit becomes trumps. Once trump is established, must follow suit or trump if void. Scoring: Scoring uses sticks (positive) and potatoes (negative). Check scoring conditions in priority order and apply only the first that applies. - First void play determines trumps - Avoid the middle score - Total 157 points in deck - Sticks are good, potatoes are bad - 12 hands per game Win condition: After 12 hands, player with most sticks (or fewest potatoes) wins.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Try to win either many points (79-99 is safe) or very few points (under 29 is safe). Avoid the middle range. Ensure you win at least one trick. Intermediate: Control when trumps are established by choosing when to void yourself. Win early tricks to control the lead. Preserve 6s (guaranteed exits) and Aces. Advanced: Card counting is essential. Track suit distribution to predict when trumps will be established. Coordinate (without explicit communication) to force opponents into the middle. Calculate risk ranges based on cards played. - Tip: 79-99 points guarantees a stick (others have less) - Tip: Under 29 points guarantees a stick (someone has 100+ or no tricks) - Tip: Win at least one trick or you get 2 potatoes - Tip: 6s are valuable as guaranteed exits - Tip: Leading short suits increases chance of establishing favorable trumps",
