    "history": "Money is played in several Arab countries. The name refers to the picture cards which serve as currency in the game. It shares mechanics with children's games like Beggar My Neighbour and Snap, but adds the sophisticated buying mechanic. - Traditional: Played for generations in Jordan, Syria, and Egypt - Modern: Documented and shared through card game collections - Present: Still played in the Middle East and by Arab communities abroad The game has remained relatively stable, with minor variations in the number of Jokers used and specific buying rules. A social game played in Middle Eastern cafes and homes. The buying mechanic reflects real-world economic concepts in a simple, accessible format.",
    "setup": "Players: Two players. The game is normally played with a standard 52-card pack; some variants add two Jokers. Before dealing, all picture cards (Kings, Queens, Jacks) are removed. Each player receives two Kings, two Queens, and two Jacks as their starting money, stored face up in a money area or bank. The remaining 40 numeral cards (Aces count as 1s) are shuffled and dealt equally, 20 cards each, as face-down stacks that players must not look at or reorder. Deck: Standard 52-card pack. Optional: 2 Jokers can be added. Suits are irrelevant. Equipment: Standard deck, space for 'money area' (bank) and 'floor' (play pile). Dealing: Before dealing, all picture cards (Kings, Queens, Jacks) are removed and each player receives 2 Kings, 2 Queens, and 2 Jacks as their starting 'money'. The remaining 40 numeral cards are shuffled and dealt equally (20 each) as face-down stacks.",
