    "overview": "Mus is a popular Spanish game of Basque origin, played by four players in partnerships. It is unusual among vying games in that it is played without money and with partnerships. The game involves drawing cards and betting on who has the best hand in four categories: Grande (high), Chica (low), Pares (pairs), and Juego (game). It features extensive bluffing and a complex system of signals. Origin: Spain (Basque region) Players: 4 Deck: 40-card Spanish deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-60 minutes",
    "history": "Mus originated in the Basque region of Spain. The name may derive from the Basque word 'musu' meaning 'kiss' or from the Spanish 'mus' meaning 'muzzle'. It became one of the most popular card games in Spain. - Traditional: Originated in Basque region - 18th-19th Century: Spread throughout Spain - 20th Century: Became one of Spain's most popular card games - Present: Widely played in Spain and by Spanish communities worldwide The game has developed an extensive system of signals (signos) that partners use to communicate their hands. Many variants exist with different signal systems. A deeply cultural game in Spain, particularly in the Basque Country, Navarre, and La Rioja. Played in social clubs, bars, and family gatherings. The bluffing and signaling aspects make it a game of psychological skill.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players in fixed partnerships, partners sitting opposite each other. Deck: 40-card Spanish pack. Suits: Swords, Batons, Cups, Coins. Cards: Rey (King), Caballo (Horse/Queen), Sota (Jack), 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, Ace. Note: 3s are equivalent to Kings, 2s are equivalent to Aces. Equipment: 40-card Spanish deck, small stones or counters (piedras) for scoring. Dealing: 4 cards to each player, one at a time, anticlockwise. The player to dealer's right is the 'mano' (first to speak in betting).",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first team to reach 40 points (winning a game). Win two games to win the match. Gameplay: Deal 4 cards each. Optional Mus phase: all four may agree to discard one to four cards and receive replacements. Four betting rounds follow in order (Grande, Chica, Pares, Juego); mano may pass or bet; opponents may fold, see, or raise. Órdago stakes the whole game on one category. Showdown: compare hands in each category; first team to 40 points wins the game. Scoring: Points scored for winning each category. Special combinations score extra. First to 40 points wins the game. - Four categories: Grande, Chica, Pares, Juego - Mus allows card exchange before play - Betting on each category in order - Partners use secret signals - First to 40 points wins the game Win condition: First team to 40 points wins the game. Win two games to win the match.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Learn the signals to communicate with your partner. Understand the four categories and which ones your hand is strong in. Bluff occasionally to make opponents fold. Intermediate: Time your betting to maximize value. Recognize when opponent betting indicates strength or bluffing. Use Mus wisely to improve your hand. Advanced: Master the complex signal system. Read opponents' signals (if you can detect them). Calculate odds for Juego category. Expert bluffing and folding strategy. - Tip: The signal system is crucial - learn it thoroughly - Tip: Do not always bet with the best hand - sometimes trap opponents - Tip: Mus can dramatically improve your hand but gives information to opponents - Tip: Ordago is risky but can end the game quickly - Tip: Watch for opponents' accidental signals (tells)",
