    "history": "Napoleon is a classic English pub game first documented in H.E. Heather's October 1875 edition of The Bazaar. It quickly became England's national five-card game, displacing Loo. Early authors described it as 'spirited and interesting', requiring 'considerable judgement and skill'. The expression 'going Nap' (meaning to risk all on one attempt) derives from this game. - 1875: Earliest rules published by H.E. Heather in The Bazaar - 1885: American Hoyle incorrectly claims French origin; more likely European roots with north German Fipsen as a relative - 1891: Bids of Wellington, BlÃ¼cher and MisÃ¨re added; variants like Peep Nap, Sir Garnet, and Jam emerge - 1982: Andrew Pennycook notes Nap was 'widely played in pubs' - Present: Still played in southern England, Strathclyde (Scotland), and other parts of Britain The game evolved from simple five-card bidding to include advanced bids...",
    "setup": "Players: 3-8 players; 4-5 is optimal. No permanent partnershipsâ€”high bidder plays against all others. Deck: Standard 52-card pack. Cards rank A-K-Q-J-10-9-8-7-6-5-4-3-2. Often reduced: 3 players use 20 cards (A-K-Q-J-10), 4 players use 28 (A-8), 5 players use 32 (A-7). Equipment: Standard deck, poker chips or counters for stakes. Dealing: Five cards to each playerâ€”either 3 then 2, or 2 then 3. Cards are shuffled only at start and after successful Nap/Wellington/BlÃ¼cher bids; otherwise just gathered and cut.",
