    "setup": "Players: 3-8 players; 4-5 is optimal. No permanent partnerships. Deck: Standard 52-card pack. Cards rank A-K-Q-J-10-9-8-7-6-5-4-3-2. Often reduced based on player count. Equipment: Standard deck, poker chips or counters for stakes. Dealing: Five cards to each playerâ€”either 3 then 2, or 2 then 3. Cards shuffled only at start and after successful Nap/Wellington/BlÃ¼cher.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win at least the number of tricks bid. The bidder plays against all other players who form a temporary team. Gameplay: 1. Bidding: Each player has one chance to bid, starting left of dealer. Must pass or bid higher than previous. Bids in ascending order: Three (3 tricks), Four (4 tricks), Nap (5 tricks), Wellington (5 tricks, higher stakesâ€”can only bid after Nap), BlÃ¼cher (5 tricks, highest stakesâ€”can only bid after Wellington). 2. High bidder leads first trick; suit of this card becomes trumps. 3. Players must follow suit if possible. 4. Highest trump wins trick; if no trumps, highest card of led suit wins. 5. Winner of trick leads next. Scoring: Stake-based payment system. Bidder wins stakes from all opponents if successful; pays all opponents if unsuccessful. - Five cards each; high bidder names trumps by leading - Must follow suit if possible - One bidding round...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Be aggressive with your bids if you have a high trump (Ace or King) and at least one other card of the same suit. Since you only have 5 cards, a single strong suit can carry the whole hand. If you aren't the bidder, your only goal is to 'break' the bid by taking just one trick. Intermediate: The 'Overbid' Defense: If you have a solid hand (e.g., King and Queen of a suit) and someone bids 3, you should consider bidding 4 just to prevent them from taking the pot. Even if you fail, you prevent them from certain victory and force them to play defensively next round. Advanced: Counting the BlÃ¼cher: Only bid Wellington or BlÃ¼cher if you hold the Ace and King of your lead suit. If someone else holds the Ace, they will almost certainly 'break' you on the first trick. Wellington is a psychological tool to scare away weaker 'Nap' bidders. - Tip: If you are a defender, always lead...",
