    "releaseStatus": "WorkInProgress",
    "overview": "Omaha is a community-card poker variant where each player receives four hole cards and must use exactly two of them with exactly three of the five board cards to make their best five-card hand. The constraint makes hand-reading and drawing significantly different from Texas Hold'em. Origin: USA Players: 2-10 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-60 minutes",
    "history": "Omaha emerged in the 1980s, likely in casino card rooms, and rapidly became a staple of both live and online poker. It derives from Texas Hold'em but adds the mandatory two-from-hand, three-from-board rule. - 1980s: First documented in American casino circles. - 1990s: Spread to European poker rooms and tournaments. - 2000s: Pot Limit Omaha (PLO) becomes the dominant variant online. - Present: Second only to Hold'em in tournament and cash-game popularity. Omaha High-Low 8-or-Better (Omaha/8) split the pot between best high and best qualifying low hand, adding strategic depth. Known as 'the game of the nuts' because hand values run closer; draws and redraws are frequent.",
    "setup": "Players: 2-10 players (typically 6-9 at a full table). Deck: Standard 52-card deck. No jokers. Equipment: Standard deck, poker chips, dealer button. Dealing: Each player receives four hole cards face-down. Five community cards are dealt in stages: flop (3), turn (1), river (1).",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the pot by having the best five-card hand at showdown, using exactly two hole cards and exactly three board cards. Gameplay: 1. Blinds posted (small and big blind). 2. Each player receives four hole cards. 3. First betting round (pre-flop). 4. Dealer burns one card and deals three community cards (flop). 5. Second betting round. 6. Burn and deal one card (turn). 7. Third betting round. 8. Burn and deal one card (river). 9. Final betting round. 10. Showdown: each active player must use exactly two hole cards and exactly three board cards to make their best hand. Scoring: Pot-split poker. No points; chips go to the winner(s). In Omaha/8, the pot is split between high and low. - Exactly two hole cards and exactly three board cards must be usedâ€”no more, no fewer. - Standard poker hand rankings apply. - Pot Limit Omaha (PLO): maximum raise equals current pot size. -...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Play hands with coordinated cardsâ€”same suits and close ranks for straights and flushes. Four random high cards are weaker than 9-10-J-Q double-suited. Intermediate: Blockers matter: if you hold two Aces, opponents are less likely to have nut flush draws. Fold marginal hands pre-flop; Omaha punishes loose play. Advanced: Equity runs close; avoid overvaluing top set on a coordinated board. Nut draws (e.g. nut flush + straight draw) have strong implied odds. - Tip: Position is crucialâ€”late position lets you see more action before committing. - Tip: In Omaha/8, scooping (winning both halves) is the key to profit; avoid playing hands that can only win one way.",
    "variations": "- Omaha High: Standard pot-limit or fixed-limit; only the best high hand wins. - Omaha Hi-Low 8-or-Better: Pot splits between best high and best qualifying low (8-high or better). Commonly fixed-limit. - Five Card Omaha (Big O): Each player receives five hole cards; must still use exactly two with three board cards. - Six Card Omaha: Six hole cards; must use exactly two with three board cards. - Courchevel: One board card is dealt face-up before the first betting round; popular in France."
