    "history": "Played in parts of the Eastern USA (North Carolina, Tennessee). Often home-made with a second deck cut and glued to form the board. Jax published a commercial version in 1982 under the name Sequence. - Pre-1982: Folk game with home-made boards in Eastern USA. - 1982: Jax publishes Sequence as a commercial board game. - 2003: Quinto (Quinto Game Company) releases variant with scoring system. - Present: Sequence remains popular; One-Eyed Jack name used for the traditional card-driven version. From folk game with cut-and-glue boards to commercial Sequence. Double Series (Jouets Bo-Jeux) and Quinto offer alternative formats. Regional Eastern US favourite. The one-eyed vs two-eyed jack distinction (remove vs wild) is the game's defining mechanic.",
