    "history": "Likely a relative of the game '99'. The '100' threshold has been used in various gambling and parlor games for centuries, notably the German game 'Hundert'. - 19th Century: Simple adding games recorded in children's game books. - 20th Century: Becomes a popular group game using standard decks. - Present: Commonly taught as a math-based parlor game. The game evolved by adding specialized values to card ranks (like 10s being reset cards) to prevent the game from ending too quickly. Often used in education to teach rapid addition and subtraction.",
