    "history": "Paco evolved from Conquian in Mexico. Conquian was first documented in Mexico City in 1857 and spread to North America; Paco emerged as its popular gambling variant, played mostly by the older generation. - 1857: Conquian documented in Mexico City. - Late 1800s: Paco developed as gambling variant with pay-card scoring. - Present: Remains popular in Mexico, especially with older players. Paco added pay-card economics and uses five Spanish 40-card packs. Unlike Conquian, sets must be identical cards (same rank and suit), not just same rank. Strong association with Mexican home gambling. The large deck and pay-card system are reminiscent of Panguingue.",
    "setup": "Players: 2â€“12 players. Deal and play are anticlockwise. Deck: 200 cards from five identical Spanish 40-card packs (suits: swords, clubs, cups, coins; ranks 1â€“7, 10â€“12). Equipment: Five Spanish 40-card packs, chips or money for stakes. Dealing: Ten cards dealt to each player, one at a time. Remainder forms a face-down stock.",
