    "history": "Developed in Japan during the 1950s and 1960s, it predates the 'American' Crazy Eights variant that often shares its name. - 1950s: Game actively played in Japanese social and student circles. - 1970s: American Page One (Crazy Eights variant) gains popularity. - Present: Remains a staple of Japanese household and family card gaming culture. The game evolved from trick-taking games into a unique shedding format requiring players to follow suit or suffer inflation. The 'Page One' call is a ritualized declaration indicating the player has reached the final page of their hand.",
    "setup": "Players: Typically four players sit in a circle; play and dealing proceed in a clockwise direction. Deck: Standard 52-card pack with one added Joker. Rank: Joker > Ace > King > Queen > Jack > 10 > 9 > 8 > 7 > 6 > 5 > 4 > 3 > 2. Equipment: One standard pack of cards, one Joker, and a playing surface for the central trick pile. Dealing: The dealer distributes four cards to each player one at a time. The remaining cards form the face-down stock.",
