    "history": "Pai Gow (牌九) dates back to the Song Dynasty and is the ancestor of many modern partition games including Pai Gow Poker. - 960–1279: Origins in Song Dynasty China. - 19th Century: Widespread in Cantonese gambling houses. - 1980s: Adapted into Pai Gow Poker in California. - Present: A core high-stakes game in Asian and global casino markets. The game transitioned from traditional bone tiles to acrylic casino tiles, with standardized 'House Way' rules added for modern dealer play. Regarded as a game of high prestige; skilled players can identify tiles by feeling the indentations on the tile faces.",
    "setup": "Players: Up to eight players sit around a table; the role of banker rotates among players counter-clockwise. Deck: A unique set of thirty-two tiles where eleven specific tiles are duplicated to form matched pairs. Equipment: 32 Chinese dominoes, a 'Chung' marker for the banker, and three dice to determine the deal order. Dealing: Tiles are stacked in a 4x8 woodpile; dice are rolled to select which player receives the first stack.",
    "rules": "Objective: Arrange four tiles into two pairs that both beat the banker's corresponding pairs according to traditional tile rankings. Gameplay: 1. Woodpile: Tiles are shuffled and stacked in a woodpile. 2. Deal: Players receive four tiles based on a dice roll. 3. Partition: Divide tiles into a high hand and a low hand. 4. Ranking: High hand must rank equal to or higher than the low hand. 5. Showdown: Banker compares pairs with each player. 6. Settlement: Win both pairs to win; lose both to lose; split results in a push. Scoring: Win both pair comparisons to win the bet; split outcomes result in a push where the bet is returned. - The high hand must always be equal to or higher in rank than the low hand partition. - To win the wager, both player pairs must outrank the banker's pairs; one win and one loss is a push. - Ties on a specific pair comparison (copy hands) are won by the banker....",
