    "overview": "Papillon (Butterfly) is a classic French fishing-type card game, conceptually similar to Scopa but with its own distinct bonus system and 'Talon' (stock) mechanics. Players use cards from their hand to capture cards from the table, either by matching ranks or sums. It is beloved for its evocative bonus names ('Grasshopper', 'Little Beetle') and its emphasis on being the first to 'fly away' by emptying one's hand. It is primarily a game of observation and tactical capturing. Origin: France Players: 3-4 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Beginner Duration: 20-30 minutes",
    "history": "Dates back to the 18th century as a social salon game in France. It shares DNA with Italian fishing games like Scopa and the Middle Eastern game of Basra. - 1760s: Earliest mentions in French 'Academie des Jeux'. - 1850s: Rules for 'The Grasshopper' sweep are formalized. - 1920s: Remains popular in regional French cafes before being modernised. - Present: A niche historical game often played at heritage festivals. The scoring shifted from purely trick-based to a weighted 'Jeton' (token) system to allow for gambling-style play. The name 'Papillon' refers to the way a player 'flutters' through their hand to reveal a winning trick.",
    "setup": "Players: 3 is the traditional count. Deck: 52 cards. Each player receives 3 cards initially; 7 more face-up to the tableau (3 players) or 4 (4 players); remainder as talon. After each round when hands are empty, deal 3 more each from talon. Equipment: Counters (jetons, fiches) for scoring. Dealing: Deal 3 cards each to hand (first hand to dealer's left), then 7 face-up to the tableau (4 in four-hand). Stack the remainder face down as talon. When all hands are empty, deal 3 more each from talon.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score points by capturing cards and clearing the table; be the first to empty your hand after the talon is exhausted (papillon) to sweep the pool. Gameplay: Players in turn play one card to capture: a table card of the same rank, or a set of table cards whose total equals the played card, or three of a rank from hand to capture the fourth from the table. Captured cards form a trick placed face down. If unable to capture (e.g. after a sauterelle sweep), the player trails all hand cards face up to the tableau. When all hands are empty, the dealer gives 3 more cards each from the talon. When the talon is exhausted, the first to empty their hand by taking a trick wins the deal (papillon) and sweeps the pool. Scoring: Score points by capturing cards and clearing the table. - Capture by same rank, by sum (played card equals total of table cards), or three-of-a-rank to take the...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep track of the numbers. If you have an 8, and there's a 5 and a 3 on the table, take them immediately to score more cards. Don't trail high cards (Aces/Kings) unless you have no other choice, as they are likely to be captured by opponents in the next turn. Intermediate: The 'Sauterelle' Denial: If you see the table is down to just a few cards that could be easily summed up (like a 2, 4, 1), and it's almost your opponent's turn, try to capture just ONE of those cards, even if it's less efficient. This 'breaks the chain' and prevents them from getting the Grasshopper bonus. Advanced: Stamina Management: In Papillon, being the first to empty your hand confers a bonus. If you have cards that can capture combinations, use them late. Early in the hand, focus on single-card matches (like Jack for Jack) to keep your combination-options open for when the table becomes more crowded. -...",
    "variations": "- Four-hand Papillon: Four players; dealer lays only 4 upcards on the tableau instead of 7. Otherwise the same as three-player. - Modern rules: Simplified scoring: each player antes 1 or 2 jetons to the pot; each card trailed (except Aces) pays 1 jeton to the pot; each Ace trailed is paid 1 jeton by each opponent to the player; Papillon wins and sweeps the pot, or most cards wins if no-one achieves Papillon."
