    "history": "Okey derives from 19th-century Levant and Central Asian tile games, sharing ancestry with Rummikub and Mahjong. It stabilised in Ottoman Turkey. - 1900s: Traditional Okey rules crystallise in Ottoman social circles. - 1930s: Custom wooden tiles become a luxury standard. - 1970s: Plastic sets make the game ubiquitous in Turkish households. - Present: National pastime of Turkey; played in kahvehanes and online by millions. The indicator tile and false-joker system distinguish Turkish Okey from other Rummy-tile games. A fixture of Turkish cafÃ© culture, often played alongside tea and backgammon.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players (2-3 possible). Each uses a rack (Istaka). Deck: 106 tiles: numbers 1-13 in red, yellow, green, black (8 per number); 2 unmarked false jokers. One 6-sided die. Equipment: 106 tiles, racks, one die. Dealing: Tiles mixed face-down, stacked in 21 piles of 5. Dealer throws die twice to select indicator tile. Player to dealer's right gets 15 tiles, others 14. Play proceeds anticlockwise.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first to form a winning hand of 14 tilesâ€”all in valid sets and runsâ€”and expose it after your discard. Gameplay: 1. Indicator tile determines joker: the tile of same color and one number higher (e.g. Green 10 face-up â†’ Green 11s are jokers). False jokers represent the joker tile only. 2. Player to dealer's right discards first. 3. Each turn: draw one tile (from centre or previous player's discard) and discard one. 4. No melding until the end; first to complete a valid hand exposes it and wins. 5. Winning hand: 14 tiles in sets (3-4 same number, different colors) and/or runs (3+ consecutive same color; 1 may follow 13). Alternative: seven identical pairs. Scoring: Each player starts with 20 points. Losing points when opponents win: ordinary win -2, seven pairs or joker-discard win -4, gÃ¶sterme -1. - Joker = same color, one number higher than indicator. False...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritise runs over groups; runs extend both ends. Don't hoard the indicator tile unless it completes a set. Intermediate: Sometimes take from the bank instead of an opponent's discard to hide your hand. Advanced: Never discard the joker unless forced. Winning by discarding the joker doubles point loss. Track discardsâ€”if three 10s of a color are gone, that 10 is useless for a group. - Tip: The false joker always equals the current round's joker tile. - Tip: Watch discard piles you're allowed to see (yours and to your left).",
    "variations": "- Okey 101: Players receive 21-22 tiles; melds are laid on table. First meld must total at least 101 points. - Partnership Okey: Players opposite are partners; team scoring. - Color Okey: Win with one-color hand doubles score (4 or 8 points lost)."
