    "history": "Country/Region of Origin: Europe. Pas Seul is a Klondike-style patience game; the name is French for \"solo step\" or a dance for one person. One pass through the deck distinguishes it from standard Klondike. - Origins: Klondike variant with single-pass rule - Modern: Played as solitaire in print and digital implementations",
    "setup": "Players: One player. Pas Seul is a single-player patience (solitaire) game. Deck: Type: Standard 52-card deck. One deck. No jokers. Equipment: Standard 52-card deck and table space for stock, waste, six tableau piles, and four foundations. Dealing: Layout: Place four foundation spaces (upper right). Six tableau piles (main stacks) below. Stock (closed deck) upper left; waste pile next to it. Deal one card face-up to each of the six tableau piles to start (or per variant). Remaining cards form the stock. Only one pass through the stock is allowed.",
    "rules": "Objective: Build all four foundations from Ace to King in suit. The game is won when all 52 cards are on the foundations. Gameplay: 1. Turn a card from the stock onto the waste; only the topmost card of the waste may be played. 2. The top waste card may be moved to a foundation (build up in suit from Ace to King) or to a tableau pile. 3. On the tableau, build down in alternating colors; any card may be placed on an empty tableau pile. 4. You may move one or more cards from one tableau pile to another when the build rule holds. 5. Only one pass through the stock is allowed; there is no redeal. Scoring: Points or match rules. - Foundations: build ascending in the same suit from Ace to King. - Tableau: build descending in alternating colors; only the top card of a pile is movable. - Only the top card of the waste pile may be played to foundation or tableau. - One pass through the deck...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Expose high cards and Aces early; build foundations as soon as possible; avoid blocking tableau piles with low cards. Intermediate: Prefer moving from waste to foundation when legal; move tableau cards to free spaces and unblock lower cards; plan the single pass through the stock. Advanced: Order of turning stock cards is critical; look ahead to which tableau columns will accept which ranks; minimize deadlock in the single pass. - Tip: Only one pass through the deck; plan before turning from stock. - Tip: Empty tableau piles accept any card; use them to reorder sequences. - Tip: Build foundations steadily; delayed Aces can block the game.",
