    "history": "Finnish game; name means a coward. Related to Shithead, which spread via backpackers. Face-up version; face-down version is Valepaska. - Pre-1990s: Played in Finland. - 1995: Rules first published online. - Present: Popular in Finland; variants include Pöytäpaska (table), Ruotsalainen (Swedish). Valepaska (face-down cards) split to separate page. Pöytäpaska adds table cards like Shithead. Crude name reflects casual, youth-oriented play.",
    "setup": "Players: 2-6 players. Best 3-5. Deck: Standard 52 cards. Rank: 2 (high), A, K, Q, J, 10...3 (low). 2s, 10s, Aces are special. Equipment: Standard deck. Remainder = stock. Dealing: Deal 2+2+1 = 5 cards each. Remainder stacked face down = stock.",
    "rules": "Objective: Get rid of all your cards. Last player holding cards is paskahousu (loser). Gameplay: 1. **Start**: Player with most 3s leads (or 4s, 5s...). 2. **Play**: Play 1+ cards of same rank ≥ pile top. Restrictions: pictures only on >6; Aces only on pictures or Aces; 2s on anything except Ace/10; 10s and Aces clear. 3. **Pick up**: Cannot play → take whole pile. 4. **Draw**: If stock not empty, may draw and try to play; if illegal, take drawn card + pile. 5. **Replenish**: After playing, draw to 5 until stock empty. 6. **Clear**: 10 on lower, Ace on picture, or four-of-a-kind on top clears pile; same player plays again. Scoring: Elimination. Last with cards loses. - 2: Play on any except Ace/10; nothing plays on 2 except 2. - 10: On lower card clears pile; on empty table stays (only 10s on 10). - Ace: On picture clears pile; on empty stays (only Aces on Ace). - Four of a kind on top...",
