    "history": "Rooted in the interaction between Eastern European trick-taking traditions and Middle Eastern social gaming. It bears strong similarities to 'Pastushok' and 'Preferans'. - 1800s: Rules for Paston coalesce in the Caucasus region. - 1920s: Becomes a standard social activity in Armenian 'Koffeehouses'. - 1990s: Post-Soviet resurgence, with games appearing in professional social circles in Yerevan. - Present: A symbols of heritage for Armenians worldwide, often passed down through generations. The game was simplified from 4rd-player variants to focus purely on the 3-player dynamic, which ensures every player is actively involved in every round. Played with a specific vocabulary: 'Paston' (Spades), 'Glitch' (Clubs), 'Dineri' (Diamonds), 'Gupa' (Hearts).",
    "setup": "Players: Three players; no partnerships. The game is played mainly in Armenia (e.g. Gyumri). Deal, bidding and play are clockwise; each player plays for themselves toward 121 points. Deck: 32 cards (remove 2s through 6s). Equipment: 32 cards (remove 2s through 6s). Dealing: Each player receives 10 cards (often dealt 2 at a time). 2 cards are placed face-down in the 'Prikup'.",
