    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize the Prikup! If you have a solid hand with 4 trumps, bid to get the extra 2 cards. Those 2 cards can fix a weak suit or give you the Ace you need to win your 6th trick. Don't be too aggressive without the Jack of your suit; the Jack is a powerful tool for 'Drawing' opponent trumps early. Intermediate: The 'Paston Grkha' (No-Trump) Risk: Only bid Grkha if you have Aces and Kings across at least three suits. Since there is no trump to save you, one long suit held by an opponent can destroy your contract. Conversely, if you are defending against a Grkha bid, try to figure out which suit the declarer discarded and lead precisely that suit. Advanced: Defensive Coordination: Like in Preferans, the two defenders should play 'Against the Declarer'. If your partner leads a suit, and you have the King while the Declarer has the Ace, play low to force the Ace out. Your goal is to...",
    "variations": "- Score when declarer surrenders: In some rules opponents do not score 2 points when the declarer surrenders; in others they do. Affects whether sacrifice bids are more attractive. - Paston grkha (best spades): Highest bid; spades are trump but with higher penalty for losing. Declarer scores only +10 for six tricks (not multiplied by 4); other scores multiplied by 4. - Redeal conditions: If bottom card is 7 of spades, cards are reshuffled and redealt. If a player holds 7-8-9-10-J-Q of spades but neither K nor A of spades, cards are shown and redealt by same dealer."
