    "overview": "Peeso is a popular Estonian social game, often described as a regional folk variant of the game '6 nimmt!'. Players simultaneous play cards into four rows on the table, following strict rules of ascending order and 'smallest difference'. If a player is forced to place the 6th card in a row, they must take the entire row as penalty points (called 'Sarved' or Horns). It is a game of risk management, psychological anticipation, and avoidant scoring. Origin: Estonia Players: 2-10 Deck: Specialized numbered deck (1-104), or standard cards modified Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 15-20 minutes",
    "history": "Derived from Central European 'Cow' and 'Bull' games, it found a unique cultural home in Estonia where it is often associated with the 'Sarved' (Horns) folk imagery. - 20th Century: Variations of horizontal-row card games spread through the Baltic states. - 1990s: Peeso is standardized alongside the popularity of German 'Amigo' style card games. - 2000s: Becomes a staple in Estonian social clubs and family gatherings. - Present: Highly recognized as a fun, 'mean' game of luck and timing. The transition from standard 52-card decks to numbered decks allowed for more players and higher tension in the 'Smallest Difference' calculation. To 'Get the Horns' in Peeso is a humorous idiom for making a tactical error that benefits everyone else while hurting yourself.",
    "setup": "Players: 4-6 is the ideal range. Deck: 104 cards numbered 1 to 104. Each card has a certain number of 'Horns' (points). Equipment: 104 cards numbered 1 to 104. Each card has a certain number of 'Horns' (points). Dealing: Each player gets 10 cards. Four cards are placed face-up in a vertical column to start the four rows.",
