    "setup": "Players: Minimum Players: 2 Maximum Players: 6 Optimal Players: 2 Partnerships: None Deck: Type: Standard 52-card deck Number of Decks: 1 Jokers: Not included Equipment: Standard 52-card deck, table or floor space. Cards are spread face down and must not overlap. Dealing: No cards are dealt to hands. All cards are thoroughly shuffled and spread face down on the playing surface in a regular pattern or irregular layout; positions are fixed for the round.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score the most points by collecting matching pairs. On your turn you turn two cards face up; if they match (same rank, suits ignored), you keep the pair and take another turn; if not, you turn them face down again and the turn passes. Gameplay: 1. Preparation: Shuffle the deck and spread all cards face down so they do not overlap. 2. Play: Players take turns. At your turn, turn face up any two cards from the layout. If they match (same denomination), take both cards and place them in front of you, and take another turn. If they do not match, turn them face down in place and the next player plays. 3. Matching: With a 52-card pack, cards match if they have the same rank (e.g. two sixes, two kings); suits are ignored. There are 26 pairs. 4. End: Play continues until every card has been taken as part of a pair. Each player scores one point per pair collected; the player with the...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Remember where you have seen each rank. When you turn a card, note its position and rank so you can match it later. Intermediate: Track which cards have been shown and turned back; prioritise turning a card when you already know the position of its pair. Advanced: Remember the full sequence of revealed cards and positions; minimise information given to opponents when you fail to match. - Tip: Fix the layout pattern in memory (e.g. row and column) to recall positions. - Tip: When you see a pair, take it immediately to get an extra turn.",
    "variations": "- Match by rank and colour: When using only two cards of each type (e.g. from a standard pack), players may agree that cards match only if they have the same denomination and the same colour (e.g. both red or both black), making matching harder. - Four of each type: Some packs have four identical cards of each type instead of two, so the first card of a new rank has three possible pairings and the start is easier; the game still gets easier as the layout shrinks."
