    "overview": "Petit Paquet (also known as 'Turkish Packets' or 'Paquets Turcs') is an 18th-century French game of pure chance and high-stakes gambling. It pits a single 'Banker' against a group of players. The game is played with several small piles (packets) of face-down cards. Players bet on specific piles, and the top cards are revealed one by one. It is a precursor to modern casino games like Baccarat and Pharaoh, known for its elegant simplicity and rapid wealth transfer. Origin: France Players: 2-10 Deck: Standard 52-card deck (or 32-card Piquet deck) Difficulty: Beginner Duration: 10-20 minutes",
    "history": "Dates back to the royal courts of France in the 1700s. It was particularly popular during the 'Turkish Fad' (Turquerie) in Europe, hence the alternative name. - 1710s: First described in French gaming manuals as a parlor gamble. - 1850s: Becomes a staple in common gambling dens across Paris and London. - 1920s: Mostly replaced in casinos by Baccarat, but remains a folk game in Rural France. - Present: Played occasionally as a historical curiosity or in high-stakes private circles. The game evolved from a 32-card format (removing low cards to increase the 'Hit' frequency) to a full 52-card format for more participants. Symbolizes the 'Golden Age' of French salon gambling, where fortunes were won and lost on the flip of a single 'Paquet'.",
    "setup": "Players: One Banker and any number of Punters (Players). Deck: Standard 52 cards. Rank: A (High) to 2 (Low). Equipment: Standard 52 cards. Rank: A (High) to 2 (Low). Dealing: The Banker splits the deck into several small face-down packets (usually 5 to 10 piles) and one 'Banker Pile' for themselves.",
