    "history": "Rooted in the 15th-century tradition of Tarot (Tarocchi), specifically the Piedmontese branch. Piedicavallo developed its own dialect and rule-variants due to its isolation in the Alpine valleys. - 1700s: Standard Tarocco Piemontese rules circulate in Turin. - 1800s: Piedicavallo valley residents adapt the deck to 62 cards (removing specific low suits). - 1970s: The 'Communal Talk' rules are documented as a unique cultural asset. - Present: Designated as a UNESCO Intangible Heritage activity in the Biella province. The game removed several low-rank 'Scartini' (cards of little value) to speed up play and focus on the power of the Trumps (Tarocchi). Played in local cafes (osterie) with a specific Piedmontese vocabulary (e.g., 'Batto' for 'I have the high card').",
