    "overview": "Plait Solitaire (also known as 'Plaits' or 'The Braid') is a double-deck solitaire game of significant complexity and visual appeal. It is distinguished by the 'Plait'â€”a central reserve of 13 cards arranged in a braid-like pattern, where only specific cards are accessible at any time. Requiring the player to manage two sets of foundations and eight tableau piles, it is a game of foresight, sequence planning, and careful reserve management. Origin: UK / Europe Players: 1 (Solo) Deck: Two standard 52-card decks (104 cards total) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 15-20 minutes",
    "history": "Dates back to the mid-19th century as a complex 'Grand Patience' played in European parlors. The 'Plait' layout was designed to provide a decorative yet difficult reserve element. - 1865: First appearance in German and British Patience manuals. - 1920s: Recorded in 'The Complete Patience' as a benchmark for high-level solo play. - 1990s: Implemented in early digital solitaire collections (e.g., BVS Solitaire). - Present: Remains a favorite for players who find Klondike too simple and FreeCell too repetitive. The game was simplified from even more complex triple-deck versions (like 'The Rope') to maintain a balance between difficulty and table space. Often described as the 'Thinking Player's Solitaire' because it rewards memorization of the plait's internal layers.",
    "setup": "Players: One player. Plait is a solo patience game played by a single person; BVS and historical sources describe it as a single-player card game with no opponents or partners. Deck: 104 cards (two standard 52-card decks). Equipment: 104 cards (two standard 52-card decks). Dealing: Deal 13 cards in a plait (open reserve). Deal 32 cards into 8 tableau piles (4 cards per pile). Place one card above as the first foundation base; the seven other cards of the same rank become foundation bases as they appear. Remainder is stock.",
    "rules": "Objective: Move all 104 cards to 8 foundation piles, building up in suit from the Base rank (wrapping K to A). Gameplay: Build tableaus down in sequence and alternating color. Only the top card of each tableau pile and the reserve (plait) is available for play on the foundations. One card or a group of cards in proper sequence may move to another tableau pile. When a tableau is empty, any card or legal sequence may fill it. When no moves are available, turn cards from the stock one at a time; cards from stock may move to tableau or foundations. No redeal. Scoring: Move all 104 cards to 8 foundation piles, building up in suit from the Base rank (wrapping K to A). - Foundations build up in same suit from base rank, wrapping King to Ace. - Tableau builds down in alternating color; only top cards and plait top are available for foundations. - One card or a legal sequence may move to...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Free the Plait! The 13 cards in the Plait are your biggest obstacle. Don't use your stock cards or tableau moves if you could instead move a card from the Plait to a foundation. Every card you remove from the Plait increases your tactical options for the rest of the game. Intermediate: Foundation Focus: Since foundations can start on any rank (like an 8), the sequence is non-standard (8-9-10-J-Q-K-A-2-3-4-5-6-7). Pay close attention to the wrap-around. Don't trap an Ace in a tableau pile unless you have already cleared the Kings for all foundations. Advanced: The Alternating Trap: While you build down in alternating colors in the tableau, remember that you must eventually build UP in suit in the foundations. If you create a long Black-Red-Black sequence in the tableau, you might be 'Burying' a Diamond and a Heart that are needed for early foundation building. Use empty tableau...",
    "variations": "- Standard: Standard Plait: 13-card plait reserve, 8 tableau piles (4 cards each), 8 foundations (one base rank revealed at start), remainder stock; build up in suit on foundations, down alternating color on tableau; no redeal. - Similar games: Braid, Cadonette and Fort are similar double-deck solitaires documented by BVS but are distinct games with their own rules. - Other variants: No other named variants for Plait Solitaire are documented in BVS or primary sources."
