    "history": "Plus-Minus Jass is a Swiss Jass variant documented alongside Mittlere and Molotow in English-language card game literature. It uses the same deal and trick-taking rules as Mittlere (3 players) or Molotow (4 players) but scores only sticks; the first to 7 sticks wins. - Traditional: Part of Swiss Jass family - Modern: Documented with Mittlere and Molotow in card game literature Derived from Mittlere and Molotow; simplifies scoring to sticks only with no potatoes. Played in Switzerland and among Jass enthusiasts; available in Michael Gasser's Jass!! program.",
    "setup": "Players: 3 or 4 players, each playing for themselves. Deck: 36-card Jass deck (Ace, King, Ober, Under, Banner, 9, 8, 7, 6 in four suits). Can use standard deck with cards below 6 removed. Equipment: 36-card Jass deck or modified standard deck; scoring tokens or slate for sticks. Dealing: All cards dealt equally: 9 each for 4 players, 12 each for 3 players, in packets of 3. Deal and play anticlockwise.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first to score 7 sticks. Score sticks by having the highest or lowest card-point total when everyone is under 100 points, or by having the only different total when others tie; if someone takes 100 or more, only the player with least points scores. Gameplay: Player to dealer's right leads. Must follow suit if possible. First time a player cannot follow suit and plays another suit, that suit becomes trumps for the hand. Once trumps are set, must follow suit or play a trump; may play a lower trump only when void in the led suit. Trick winner leads to the next trick. Card points: Ace 11, King 4, Ober 3, Under 2, Banner 10, 9/8/7/6 zero; in trump Under 20, 9 14. Last trick bonus 5; total 157 points. Scoring: Sticks only. Under 100 card points: most and least each score one stick. If someone takes 100 or more: only least points scores one stick. First to 7 sticks wins; if...",
