    "history": "Dates back to the early 19th century in France. It is named after the French Prime Minister Jules de Polignac, who was deeply unpopular at the timeâ€”hence why the most 'Penalty' card bears his name. - 1830: Named after the unpopular Polignac ministry. - 1870s: Becomes a popular game in the French military (Zouaves). - 1920s: Serves as the basis for the more complex game 'Barbu'. - Present: Still played in regional France, often as 'Le Valet Noir'. The game was simplified from earlier French games like 'L'Ambigu' to focus purely on the psychological tension of avoiding the 'Dirty Jack'. In French culture, 'faire Polignac' means to accidentally catch the bad card, often resulting in good-natured mockery.",
