    "releaseStatus": "WorkInProgress",
    "overview": "Preferans (derived from the French 'PrÃ©fÃ©rence') is the quintessential card game of the Russian intelligentsia and one of the most intellectually demanding games in existence. Played by 3 or 4 players with a 32-card deck, it is a bidding-based trick-taking game where players compete for contracts, including the dreaded 'Misere' (taking zero tricks). It is famous for its complex 'Pula' (pool) scoring system involving 'Bullets', 'Whists', and 'Mountains', making it a game of long-term strategy and cold mathematical calculation. Origin: Russia / France Players: 3 or 4 Deck: 32-card Piquet deck (7s to Aces) Difficulty: Expert Duration: 90-180 minutes",
    "history": "Developed in Russia in the mid-19th century from French games like 'L'Hombre' and 'Boston'. It became the preferred game of the Russian military, students, and nobility. - 1840s: First appearance in St. Petersburg gambling halls. - 1890s: Rules for 'Misere' and 'Whistling' are perfected across the Russian Empire. - 1910s: Tolstoy and Dostoyevsky mention Preferans in their works as a mark of a gentleman's skill. - Present: Remains a cultural institution in Russia, Ukraine, and the former Soviet states. Unlike Bridge, which moved toward partnerships, Preferans remained a 'Free-for-All', where alliances shift with every 'Whistle' (the decision to defend a contract). Commonly described as more a 'Social Philosophy' than a game, emphasizing honor and tactical discipline.",
    "setup": "Players: Typically 3 active players. If 4, the dealer sits out and gets 'Buy-in' points. Deck: 32 cards (remove 2s through 6s). Equipment: 32 cards (remove 2s through 6s). Dealing: 2 cards each, 2 to the 'Talon' (the Widow), continuing until everyone has 10 cards.",
    "rules": "Objective: Complete a predetermined amount of 'Bullet' points while keeping your 'Mountain' (penalty points) low. Gameplay: Three or four players (dealer sits out if four). Deal: 2 cards each, 2 to talon, then 2-2-2-2 each (10 cards per player). Bidding: eldest hand starts; players bid number of tricks (6–10) and trump (spades, clubs, diamonds, hearts, no trump) or misère. Highest bidder becomes declarer, takes talon, discards two cards, declares contract. Defenders choose to whist or pass; if both pass, declarer wins without play. Play: eldest hand leads; must follow suit, else must trump if possible. Trick winner leads next. Declarer must make contracted tricks; defenders who whist must make their quota. Scoring: Points or match rules. - Deal 10 cards each, 2 to talon; eldest hand leads bidding. - Bid tricks (6–10) and trump (spades, clubs, diamonds, hearts, no trump) or misère;...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Calculate your 'Safe' tricks. In Preferans, bidding is about certainty, not hope. If you have the Ace and King of a suit, that is two safe tricks. If you have 5 trumps including the Ace, you can safely bid 6. Be extremely careful with 'Misere'â€”it is high-risk, high-reward, and the defenders will play 'Crucial Card' logic to force you to take a trick. Intermediate: Whistling Discipline: Only 'Whistle' if you have 'Stop cards' (Aces or Kings). If you Whistle and fail (Get set), you lose massive points on the 'Mountain'. However, Whistling is the only way to earn 'Whist points'â€”the most valuable currency in the final score calculation. It is the art of knowing when to punish the declarer and when to let them win. Advanced: The 'Talon' Discard: The 2 cards you discard after picking up the Widow can win or lose you the game. Protect your weak suits by discarding the low cards of...",
    "variations": "- Sochi scoring: Pool, whist and dump points; declarer and defenders score or pay per contract and tricks. - Rostov (Moscow) scoring: Dump penalties for whisters halved; dump points replaced by 5 whist points per point to each opponent. - Misère and all-pass: Misère: bid to take zero tricks; ranks between 8 NT and 9 spades; played at no trumps with defenders' cards face up. All-pass (raspasovka): when no one bids, each player tries to take as few tricks as possible; no trumps."
