    "overview": "Púkk (often spelled 'Pukk') is a traditional Icelandic card game that belongs to the ancient 'Poch' family. It involves a unique betting layout (traditionally a series of dishes or a special mat) and three distinct phases of play: Dressing the Board, Vying (the 'Púkk' phase), and Shedding (the 'Play' phase). It is famous for its 'Pamfíll' (Jack of Clubs) which acts as a powerful wild card. Púkk is a game of social interaction, bluffing, and sequence-building that has been an Icelandic family staple for centuries. Origin: Iceland Players: 5-7 Deck: 40 cards (Standard 52 minus 2s, 3s, 4s) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-30 minutes",
    "history": "Derived from the 15th-century German game 'Pochen' and the French 'Poque'. It arrived in Iceland and evolved into its current form with localized terminology and the specific 'Pamfíll' mechanic. - 1400s: Pochen spread through Europe. - 1800s: Púkk becomes the definitive social game in Icelandic coastal villages. - 1950s: Modern layout mats are produced commercially in Reykjavik. - Present: Remains a core 'Hygge' game for Christmas and social gatherings in Iceland. The removal of 2s, 3s, and 4s was a deliberate change to make sequences (the final phase) faster and more high-stakes. Names for the positions: 'Ás' (Ace), 'Kongur' (King), 'Drottning' (Queen), 'Gosi' (Jack), 'Tía' (Ten), and 'Pamfíll' (Jack of Clubs).",
    "setup": "Players: 5-7 is optimal. Deck: 40 cards (Remove 2s, 3s, 4s). Equipment: 40-card pack, Púkk betting layout (mat or set of dishes/pools), chips. Dealing: Each player receives 5 cards.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the chips in the compartments by having specific cards or winning the betting phase. Gameplay: Each game has four stages: (1) Dressing the board—each player places one chip in each of the seven compartments (Ás, Kongur, Drottning, Gosi, Tía, Pamfíll, and central Púkk). (2) Collecting the ante—the turned card designates trump; holders of the Ace, King, Queen, Jack or Ten of trump collect the corresponding pool; the holder of Pamfíll (Jack of Clubs) collects that pool (and both if clubs are trump). (3) Að Púkka—in turn, each player may add a chip to the Púkk compartment to enter; Pamfíll is wild for hand ranking; hands are five of a kind, four, three, or pair (sixes beat Aces); wagering one chip at a time until two remain or no raises; showdown and highest hand wins the Púkk pot. (4) Playing out the hand—shedding: left of dealer leads; players follow with same suit one...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Don't bluff! In traditional Púkk rules, it is actually illegal to 'Púkk' without at least a pair. However, you can 'Soft-Púkk' by betting small with a medium pair to induce others to fold. Prioritize the 'Pamfíll' for the Vying phase, but remember it has no special power in the Playing phase—use it as a normal Jack of Clubs to continue a sequence. Intermediate: Bowl Farming: Pay attention to which bowls have the most chips. If the 'Ace' bowl hasn't been emptied for 5 rounds, it will be huge. If you hold an Ace, your primary goal in the Play phase should be to maneuver the sequence in that suit so that YOU get to play the Ace. This might mean keeping a 10 and Queen of that suit so you can jump in when someone plays the Jack. Advanced: Sequence Starvation: If you are the leader of a sequence, try to 'Kill' the suit as fast as possible by playing cards that gap the sequence (though...",
    "variations": "- Minimum pair of sixes: Some require that a player hold a combination no lower than a pair of 6s to púkk (enter the vying phase). - Pamfíll cannot substitute for Six: Some players do not permit Pamfíll to substitute for a Six in the vying hand ranking."
