    "history": "Austrian domino variant; described in Domino in vielen Spielarten (Fritz Beck, Vienna, 1960). Traditionally played for money. - 1960: Described in Domino in vielen Spielarten (Fritz Beck, Vienna). Block game variant of Austrian line dominoes; pool (Pulle) and elimination at 101+ define the game. Played in Austria; often played for stakes; buy-back (zurückkaufen) and broken pool (gesprengte Pulle) are standard.",
    "setup": "Players: Four players play as individuals. Each receives seven tiles from a 36-tile set (double-8 with nine doubles removed). No boneyard is used; it is a block game. Deck: Double-8 set with nine doubles removed (36 tiles). Equipment: Double-8 domino set; optional stakes for the pool. Dealing: Each player receives 7 tiles. No boneyard is used (block game). First round: holder of heaviest tile leads; thereafter the winner of the previous round leads.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the last player with a running score of 100 or less; players are eliminated at 101 or more. The last survivor collects the pool (Pulle). Gameplay: Block game: play a tile matching an open end of the layout or pass if unable. Tableau is built in a double line (matching end on the side). After each round, add pip total of unplayed tiles to each player's running score. At 101+ a player is eliminated unless they buy back (pay half stake, score set to middle of the other three). If all have 101+ (broken pool), scores reset to zero and everyone stakes again. Scoring: After each round, pip total of unplayed tiles is added to running score. Players at 101 or more are eliminated. Last player with score 100 or less wins the pool. - Four players, 7 tiles each; no boneyard. - Pip total of unplayed tiles added to running score each round. - Elimination at 101 or more; 100 is still in....",
    "strategy": "Basic: Try to play out tiles early to avoid accumulating pip count; when blocked, passing is mandatory. Intermediate: Track which ends are open and which tiles opponents might hold; manage running score to stay under 100. Advanced: Use buy-back strategically when score nears 100; in broken-pool situations, consider stake size versus position. - Tip: First round lead goes to holder of heaviest tile; round winner leads next.",
