    "history": "Dates back to the 19th century in the Russian Empire. It was originally played with the standard 36-card deck used for Durak and other traditional Russian games. - 1850s: First documented rules in Russian game manuals. - 1917: Survives the revolution as a popular 'proletariat' game due to its simplicity. - 1960s: Standardized children's version ('Drunkard') becomes a household name in the USSR. - Present: Still the first game taught to most Russian children. The '6 beats Ace' rule was likely added to simulate folk stories of the 'little guy' overcoming the powerful. The name 'P'yanitsa' (Drunkard) comes from the way the cards 'toss and turn' without player control, much like a person in an intoxicated state.",
