    "history": "Quadrille was introduced to the southern provinces of France around 1695 and shortly thereafter to Paris. It is an adaptation of the three-player Spanish game Ombre (Hombre) for four players. First English rules appeared in The Game of Quadrille; or Ombre by Four (1720), described as 'the favourite game at the French Court.' - ca. 1695: Quadrille introduced in southern France. - Early 18th century: Spread to Paris and Britain; French rules published (1724). - 1720sâ€“1820s: Dominant card game at the French court and among British nobility; notably popular with women. - 1813: Referenced in Jane Austen's Pride and Prejudice (Lady Catherine de Bourgh). - Mid-19th century: Declined in fashion, superseded by Whist and Boston. - Present: Almost forgotten; still played in West Friesland and by enthusiasts. Emerged as Ombre adapted for four players with variable partnerships (calling a King)....",
    "setup": "Players: 4 players. Each plays for herself; temporary alliances form per deal via the called King. Deck: 40 cards: A, K, Q, J, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2 in each suit (8s, 9s, 10s removed). Equipment: 40-card pack, counters or chips for stakes, score sheet. Dealing: 1. Dealer shuffles; pone cuts. 2. Deal 10 cards each in batches of 4-3-3, 3-4-3, or 3-3-4. 3. Deal and play rotate right (counterclockwise).",
    "rules": "Objective: As Hombre (declarer), win at least six of the ten tricks, either alone (Solo) or with a partner (Alliance). Opponents aim to prevent this. Gameplay: 1. **Stakes**: Each player antes to the pool (or dealer antes four). 2. **Auction**: Eldest opens; players bid or pass in ascending order: Solo, Casco (with both black Aces), Dimidiator (exchange with called King), Alliance (call a King for partner). If all pass, Forced Spadille: holder of Spadille becomes Hombre and calls a King. 3. **Trump**: Hombre names trump (or partner may in Forced Spadille). 4. **Play**: Eldest leads. Must follow suit; may play any card if void. Highest trump wins, else highest of led suit. Spadille, Manille, Basta may be held back when trumps are led (unless a higher trump is led). 5. **Settlement**: Hombre wins or loses; pool and premiums paid accordingly. Scoring: Stake-based. Hombre wins: takes pool...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Bid Alliance when you have a strong hand but need support; bid Solo when you can likely take six tricks alone. Call a King you do not hold to find a partner. Intermediate: Lead to weaken opponents' trumps; use Matadors (Spadille, Manille, Basta) to control key tricks. In Alliance, signal to partner through card play. Advanced: The privilege of holding back Matadors when trumps are led allows tactical delay. Sequence your plays to maximize trump pulls. As defender, coordinate to deny Hombre a sixth trick. - Tip: Red-suit inversion affects discard and lead choices. - Tip: In Forced Spadille, the called partner's identity is secretâ€”avoid revealing through early play. - Tip: Premiers bonus rewards taking six tricks before opponents take one.",
    "variations": "- Mediateur: Elaboration of Quadrille with Grandissimo, Nemo, Dimidiator, Casco, and favourite-suit concepts. French rules (1752). - Forced Spadille: When all pass, holder of Spadille (Ace of Spades) becomes Hombre, calls a King for partner. Hombre or partner may name trump. No vole allowed. - Solo / Vole: Solo: Hombre plays alone for six tricks. Vole: bid to take all ten tricks; requires extra stake to a separate pool. - Casco: Bid when holding both Spadille and Basta. Declarer calls a King; the called player names trump. Both play as a team."
