    "setup": "Players: 2â€“6 players (optimal 3â€“4). 2-player uses a stock. Deck: 32-card German pack (A,K,D,B,10,9,8,7) or dedicated Quartett pack (typically 8 quartets, 32 cards). Equipment: Quartett or 32-card deck. Optional: pool for stakes (Happy Families variant). Dealing: 1. 3+ players: Deal all cards evenly, one at a time, clockwise (some may have one more). 2. 2 players: 10 cards each; remaining 12 form a face-down stock.",
    "rules": "Objective: Collect the most complete quartets (sets of four matching cards) by asking other players for specific cards. Gameplay: 1. **Turn**: The player whose turn it is asks any other player for a specific card (e.g. 'Do you have the King of Hearts?'). 2. **Requirement**: The asker must hold at least one card of the same quartet. 3. **If the asked player has it**: They must give it; the asker gets another turn and may ask again. 4. **If not**: The asked player says 'Not at home' (Nicht da); the turn passes to them. (2-player: the asked player draws one card from the stock before asking.) 5. **Quartets**: When a player completes a quartet, they place it face-up (or face-down) on the table. 6. **End**: Play continues until all cards are in quartets. The player with the most quartets wins. Scoring: One point per quartet collected. Winner is the player with the most quartets when all...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Remember which cards you've asked for and who said 'not at home'â€”that player likely doesn't have cards of that quartet. Ask players who have recently received many cards from you; they may have completed quartets. Intermediate: Track which quartets are incomplete and where key cards might be. Ask for cards that will complete your quartets before opponents complete theirs. Advanced: In multi-player games, sequence your asks to maximize information. Asking a player who just said 'not at home' can sometimes yield a card they drew (2-player) or confirm distribution. - Tip: Prioritize quartets where you already hold 2â€“3 cards. - Tip: Note when a player gives you a cardâ€”they may be low on that quartet. - Tip: In 2-player, forcing the opponent to draw can delay their progress.",
    "variations": "- Happy Families: British version with 44 cards (11 families of 4). Pool; winner of first phase takes half; second phase: players ask for complete families; winner takes the rest. - Authors: American version; all cards dealt, no stock. Same core rules. Originally with educational author cards. - Go Fish: Uses a stock; when the asked player doesn't have the card, the asker says 'Go fish!' and draws. Turn passes left if the drawn card doesn't match. Ask for rank, not specific card. - Specific Card: Must ask for a specific card (e.g. 7 of diamonds) rather than a rank. Can optionally allow asking for a card you already hold (to mislead). - Target Score: Play multiple hands; one point per quartet. First to reach an agreed total (e.g. 10) wins."
