    "history": "Rook was introduced by Parker Brothers in 1906. The deck was designed for communities that avoided standard playing cards; the game became popular in Eastern Kentucky and among Mennonites in Southern Ontario, Manitoba, Pennsylvania, and Ohio. - 1906: Parker Brothers introduced the Rook deck and game; marketed to communities that avoided standard playing cards.",
    "setup": "Players: Four players in fixed partnerships, partners opposite. Deck: Rook deck: four suits (colors) 1–14 each plus Rook bird card. Common variant removes 2s, 3s, 4s for 45 cards. Equipment: Rook deck Dealing: Ten cards each, one at a time; five cards to the nest. Dealer adds one card to the nest after each of the first five rounds of the deal.",
    "rules": "Objective: As the bidding team, take at least the number of card points you bid; as the opposing team, score the points you take. First team to reach 500 or more points wins. Gameplay: Four in fixed partnerships. Deal 10 each, 5 to nest. Bid from 100 in multiples of 5 (left of dealer first); three passes leave the fourth as high bidder; if first three pass, dealer must bid 100. Bidder picks up nest, discards five face-down (no point cards), names trump. Left of bidder leads; follow color; Rook is highest trump. Highest trump wins trick, else highest of led color. Bidder's team makes bid scores their points, else subtracts bid; other team always scores its points. Game to 500. Scoring: Card points: 1=15, 14=10, 10=10, 5=5, Rook=20 (180 per deal). Bidder's team scores its points if they make the bid, else subtracts the bid; other team always scores its points. First team to 500 wins. -...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Bid when you have good trump and point cards; avoid overbidding. Intermediate: Count trumps and key cards; adjust bidding and play. Advanced: Partnership signaling and endplay; near-optimal bidding and defense. - Tip: Bidder may not put point cards in the nest. - Tip: If first three pass, dealer must bid 100.",
