    "history": "Saat-Aath is a two-player adaptation of the South Asian three-player quota game 3-2-5. - Traditional play: Develops in India as a compact head-to-head quota game. - Modern documentation: Published in English on international card-game references. - Present: Taught through online rule pages and Hindi-language video explainers. Compared with 3-2-5, Saat-Aath changes to two players and uses a tableau-plus-hand structure that creates 15 tricks from a short initial hand. The game uses the term 'hands' for tricks in Indian play culture and remains recognizable as a local skill game with bluff and memory elements.",
    "setup": "Players: Exactly 2 players; dealer alternates by deal. Deck: Start from 52 cards and remove all 2-6 plus 7 of clubs and 7 of diamonds. Trumps rank: 7 of hearts (highest), 7 of spades, then A K Q J 10 9 8 of trump suit. Non-trump suits rank A K Q J 10 9 8. Equipment: 30-card pack and optional score sheet for match play. Dealing: Dealer gives 5 hand cards to each player. Non-dealer calls trump. Dealer then deals 5 face-down table cards in a row to each player and places 1 face-up card on each stack, giving each player 15 playable cards over time.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win enough tricks relative to your role quota: dealer targets more than 7, non-dealer targets more than 8, and 7-8 exact is a tie. Gameplay: 1. **Lead**: Non-dealer leads first trick. 2. **Follow suit**: Second player must follow suit if possible from hand or currently face-up table cards. 3. **If void**: Any available card may be played; trumps can cut. 4. **Trick winner**: Higher card of same suit wins, otherwise trump wins, otherwise first played card wins when neither card is trump. 5. **Card availability**: You may play from hand or any face-up table card; when a face-up table card is played, its face-down undercard is revealed for future tricks. 6. **Continuation**: Winner of each trick leads next. 7. **End**: After 15 tricks, compare trick totals against quota thresholds. Scoring: Each deal is resolved by trick totals versus asymmetric quotas (dealer 7, non-dealer 8)....",
    "strategy": "Basic: Plan for trick timing: preserve high trumps and reveal-table opportunities for late control, not only early trick gains. Intermediate: Use leads to test whether opponent can follow suits from visible table cards, then exploit known voids with trump cuts. Advanced: Track both hidden undercards and spent trumps; winning quota margins matter in variants with next-deal card advantage. - Tip: Do not expose your only control suit too early unless it secures initiative. - Tip: When leading from a face-up table card, consider delaying the reveal until trick resolution where permitted. - Tip: Role matters: dealer and non-dealer have different thresholds, so risk appetite should differ.",
    "variations": "- No Special Sevens: 7 of hearts and 7 of spades lose permanent trump privilege and become normal low cards of their printed suits. - Winner Draw Advantage: As in some 3-2-5 circles, previous winner draws cards from loser before next deal according to margin over quota."
