    "overview": "Sap Ng Wu (十五湖) is a climbing game for three or four players using an 84-card Chinese domino pack. Players in turn beat the previous play with a higher card or combination or pass; the player who played the highest takes the cards as wu (winning cards). The first to collect 15 or more wu declares a win (sik wu) and receives chip payments from the others. Origin: China Players: 3-4 Deck: 84 Chinese domino cards (十五湖紙牌) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 15-30 minutes",
    "history": "Country/Region of Origin: China Time Period: Early 1900s Historical Development: The game evolved from traditional card games in the region Cultural Significance: Popular social and family game - Early 1900s: Game first documented - Mid 1900s: Spread to other regions - Modern Era: Continues to be played worldwide - Online Presence: Digital versions available",
    "setup": "Players: Minimum Players: 3 Maximum Players: 4 Optimal Players: 3 Partnerships: None Deck: Type: 84-card Chinese domino pack (十五湖紙牌); 21 tile types with four copies of each Jokers: Not included Equipment: 84-card Chinese domino pack (15 lakes playing-cards) Chips for payment (optional) Table space Dealing: Pack shuffled and spread face down; players take one card at a time anticlockwise from dealer until all taken. Three players: 28 cards each; four players: 21 each.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first to collect 15 or more wu (winning cards) and declare a win (食湖 sik wu). Gameplay: 1. Optional: Any player with a supreme combination (cards 2-4 and 1-2, at least one of each) may lay it face up; it counts as wu. 2. Dealer leads any card or valid combination face up. 3. In turn each player may beat the current play with a higher card or combination of the same count and suit mix, or pass. 4. When all but one pass in succession, that player takes the winning cards as wu and leads next. 5. First to have 15 or more wu may declare (sik wu); play ends and winner receives chip payments: 1 chip for 15-17 wu, 2 for 18-20, up to 5 for 27-28. A player may delay declaring to try for more wu but then must reach the next threshold to declare. Scoring: Each card or combination won counts as one wu. First to 15 or more wu may declare (sik wu). Chip payments: 1 for 15-17 wu, 2...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Save high civil and military cards to beat key leads; lay supreme early if you have it to count wu. Intermediate: Track which ranks have been played; decide when to declare at 15 wu versus playing on for higher chip payouts. Advanced: Time declaration to maximise chips while avoiding another player declaring first; use four-of-a-kind for immediate chip gain. - Tip: Delay declaring only if you can reach 18+ wu before others reach 15. - Tip: Supreme combination laid pre-play counts as wu; 8-card supreme is an instant 16 wu.",
    "variations": "- Wu Han games: Related climbing games from Hubei (Wu Han): military divided by red/black spots; player obliged to beat if able; bonus scores for particular cards among winning cards. - Si Chuan Luan Chuo (乱戳): Played in Si Chuan with 川牌; different ranking (e.g. middle three equal, lower four equal); 16+ wu to win; points and multipliers (番). Sometimes four players with one sitting out; talon of 3 cards. - Mā Huā Huā (抹花花): Northwest China (Shaanxi, Ningxia, Gansu); 48-card pack; three or four players (one sits out); special groups (happy cards, bull cards, pendulum, fish); 6+ pillars for one family, 11+ for two families."
