    "history": "Schnapsen is an Austrian two-player form of the wider Sechsundsechzig or Sixty-Six family. It is described as a tightened version of the German game Sechsundsechzig and is closely related to games such as Bauernschnapsen, Talon-Schnapsen, Tute, Tyziacha, and Snapszli. - 18th-19th century: Sixty-Six family games spread across Central Europe. - Modern era: Schnapsen becomes one of Austria’s best-known two-player card games, with organized tournaments. Schnapsen sharpened the Sixty-Six structure into a 20-card Austrian two-hander with a talon, marriage declarations, and the option to close the talon. Schnapsen is a well-known Austrian two-player game with organized tournament play and a reputation as a demanding concentration game rather than a light social filler.",
    "setup": "Players: Exactly two players sit opposite each other. Deck: Use 20 cards: Ace, Ten, King, Queen or Ober, and Jack or Unter in each suit. Card points are A=11, 10=10, K=4, Q/O=3, J/U=2. Equipment: A 20-card Schnapsen pack and a score sheet or counters for game points. Dealing: Deal five cards to each player. Turn one card face up to establish trump and place the remaining stock crosswise on it as the talon.",
    "rules": "Objective: Be the first player to reach at least 66 card points in the hand, then correctly declare going out to score game points. Gameplay: Players take tricks, drawing from the talon after each trick while it remains open. During the open phase, following suit is not required. A player holding king and queen or ober of the same suit may declare a marriage when leading one of them, scoring 20 points or 40 in trumps. When the talon is exhausted or closed, players must follow suit if possible and trump if unable to follow. A player who believes they have 66 or more points announces it; if correct they score game points based on the opponent’s points, otherwise the opponent scores. Scoring: Card points from tricks plus marriage declarations. A=11, 10=10, K=4, Q/O=3, J/U=2; marriages score 20, trump marriage 40. First player to 66 may go out. - First to 66 points may go out and score the...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Count card points carefully and protect high trumps. Do not declare going out unless you can verify at least 66 points. Intermediate: Use marriages to accelerate scoring, but time declarations so the announced cards are not immediately lost. Consider closing the talon when you have the lead, trump control, and enough winners to reach 66 under strict follow-suit play. Advanced: Track all high cards and estimate the opponent’s remaining point ceiling. Closing the talon is powerful only when your hand can force the last tricks; a failed close gives the opponent a large score. - Tip: Remember that tens are nearly as valuable as aces. - Tip: A trump marriage is a major swing toward 66. - Tip: Closing the talon converts the hand into a stricter perfect-information race.",
