    "setup": "Players: Minimum Players: 2 Maximum Players: 6 Optimal Players: 4 Partnerships: Optional Deck: Type: 32-card deck (strip 2-6 from standard pack). Cards 7-10, J, Q, K, A in four suits. One deck. Number of Decks: 1 Jokers: Not included Equipment: 32-card deck (or strip 2-6 from standard deck) Chips or tokens (e.g. 3 per player) Table space Dealing: Dealer gives 3 cards face down to each player plus one extra hand of 3 cards. Dealer chooses to keep their own 3 or the extra hand (before seeing the extra hand). The rejected 3 cards are turned face up and placed in the centre as the table pool. Dealing pattern: clockwise.",
    "rules": "Objective: Have the highest-scoring three-card hand at the end of a hand (same-suit total, max 31, or three of a kind). Avoid losing chips; be the last player with chips to win the game. Gameplay: 1. Setup: Each player receives chips (e.g. 3 each). Deal 3 cards each plus dealer's extra hand; dealer's rejected 3 form the table pool. 2. Play: Player to dealer's left acts first; turn passes clockwise. On your turn you may: (a) exchange one card with one table card, (b) exchange all three hand cards for the three table cards, or (c) pass. You may not exchange exactly two cards. 3. Special combinations: Feuer (three aces = 32) or Schnauz (31 in one suit) must be exposed immediately and the hand ends. 4. If all pass in succession, replace the three table cards with three from the talon and continue. 5. A player may close at end of turn; then each other player gets one more turn and the hand...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Improve your hand by swapping with the table; aim for 31 in one suit or three of a kind. Do not show reaction when someone takes a card you needed. Intermediate: Assume each player gets about three turns before someone closes. Form a plan and a backup (e.g. aim for three nines; if a nine is taken, switch to improving suit total). Watch which cards opponents take and discard to infer their targets. Advanced: Misdirect by staring at an unwanted card. If you must leave a high card, choose one that helps an opponent least. When swapping all three, opponents know your point total for the rest of the hand. - Tip: Do not stare at the card you need; opponents will notice - Tip: Games rarely last more than three rounds before someone closes - Tip: Deduce opponents' strategies from their exchanges",
