    "history": "Country/Region of Origin: Italy Time Period: 18th century (popular in Naples); roots in 15th-century Spanish games in Naples Historical Development: Evolved from Spanish games Primero and Scarabucion in Naples port area; one of three major national card games in Italy with Briscola and Tresette Cultural Significance: Popular social and family game in Italy; spread to Argentina, Brazil, and former Italian colonies via diaspora - Early 1900s: Game first documented - Mid 1900s: Spread to other regions - Modern Era: Continues to be played worldwide - Online Presence: Digital versions available",
    "setup": "Players: Minimum Players: 2 Maximum Players: 6 Optimal Players: 2 or 4 (partnerships) Partnerships: Optional for 4 or 6 players Deck: Type: 40-card Italian or stripped 52-card deck (no 8s, 9s, 10s). Suits: coins, cups, swords, batons (Latin) or diamonds, hearts, clubs, spades (French). Ranks: King=10, Horse/Queen=9, Jack=8, 7–2 face value, Ace=1. Number of Decks: 1 Jokers: Not included Equipment: 40-card deck Score sheet (optional) Table space Dealing: Three cards to each player, then four cards face up to the table; deal one at a time. After each round of three plays, deal three more to each player (no new table cards). Direction: counter-clockwise with 3+ players.",
    "rules": "Objective: Capture cards from the table by matching rank or by matching the sum of two or more table cards; score points for sweeps (scopa), most cards, most coins/diamonds, the seven of coins (settebello), and the best primiera; first to 11 or more points wins the game. Gameplay: 1. Deal: Each player receives three cards; four cards are dealt face up to the table; remaining cards form the stock. 2. Play: Non-dealer plays first. On each turn, play one card from hand. If it matches a single table card by value, capture that card; if it equals the sum of two or more table cards, capture that set; otherwise leave the played card on the table. When you capture all table cards (sweep), that is a scopa and scores 1 point. 3. After each player has played three cards, the dealer gives three more cards to each player (no new table cards). Repeat until all cards are played. 4. End of hand:...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Capture when you can; prefer taking coins/diamonds and high primiera cards (sevens, sixes, aces). Avoid leaving table cards that let the next player make a scopa. Intermediate: Track which coins and high cards have been played; delay or force sweeps to deny the opponent scope; when choosing between captures, take the set that improves your primiera or coin count. Advanced: Set up sweeps for yourself by leaving sums that only your hand can make; block opponent sweeps by taking key cards or playing a card that forces a non-sweep capture; in primiera, prioritise capturing aces and sixes. - Tip: The seven of coins (settebello) is worth one point and is key for primiera; try to capture it. - Tip: If you can capture with a single card or a set, you must take the single card; use this rule when it helps you. - Tip: Leaving the table empty after your capture (scopa) scores a point and...",
    "variations": "- Asso Pigliatutto: Playing an Ace captures all table cards (and scores a scopa) unless an Ace is on the table, in which case only the Ace is captured. Ace on empty table stays on table. - Scopa d'Assi: Like Asso Pigliatutto except capturing all cards with an Ace does not count as a scopa. Often played with four players (Scopone-style) with 9 or 10 cards each. - Scopa di Quindici: Capture only when the played card plus captured card(s) sum to 15 (e.g. play 4 to take 4+5+6). Popular in Argentina and Brazil. Some versions also allow equal-value capture."
