    "rules": "Objective: Capture cards worth points from the layout (floor). The game ends when one team has a lead of at least 100 points over the other (a baazi). Capture by playing a card that matches the capture value of one or more cards or houses on the table; build houses (piles that can only be captured as a unit) to protect or consolidate captures. Gameplay: Deal: First dealer random; dealer gives 4 cards to player on right and 4 face down on table. That player bids 9–13 (capture value) if possible, then must create a house of that value, pick up cards of that value, or throw the bid card as loose. Dealer then deals remainder in fours counter-clockwise (bidder has 11 cards, others 12). Play proceeds counter-clockwise. Each turn: play one card to (1) create or add to a house, (2) pick up matching cards or houses, or (3) throw a loose card. Houses are piles of 9–13 capture value; ordinary...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Remember cards left in denominations 9–K and point-carrying cards picked up. Try to leave only two items on the floor when possible. Intermediate: Ensure your side is last to pick up so you take remaining loose cards at end. Cement houses you want to keep; pick up with point cards if you fear the house will be broken or taken. Advanced: If you and partner hold all four of a denomination, build a cemented house in that value; opponents cannot break or capture it. Use house ownership and card memory to force favourable end-game. - Tip: Leave two items totalling at least 14 when possible to reduce sweep risk - Tip: Dealer can delay picking up a cemented house until last turn to keep loose cards for your team",
    "variations": "- Two-Player Game: Four hands dealt; two to players, two face down. Play 12 cards each; remaining loose cards stay. Each player picks up one face-down hand and play continues. Transition is vulnerable to sweeps. - Limited Houses: Only two houses allowed on the floor at once. Creating a third is illegal; player must throw or pick up. - Scoring variation: 10♦ worth 2 instead of 6; 4 points for majority of cards (more than 26); total still 100. Some allow breaking a house with a floor card plus hand card to cement. - 30-point Seep: Punjab version: 10♦=12, 9♠=9, 2♠=1, each ace=1; 4 for majority of cards (total 30). Sweep worth capture value of card played. Deal restarted if table has 10♦ or 9♠. Satthi (all 30 in one deal) = 60 points."
