    "history": "The game has a long history in southern China and is similar to Chinese draw-and-discard games played since the 18th century. The deck originated in the 19th century based on Xiangqi (Chinese chess) pieces. Stewart Culin noted these chess-derived cards were peculiar to Fujian and Guangdong; Karl Himly confirmed they were specific to Fujian. Chess cards are more recent than money-suited and domino cards. The cards were often used for gambling and may have appeared after the 1740 Qing ban on playing cards. - 1800s: Standardizes with Chess Characters. - Today: Popular in Vietnam (Tu Sac) and Taiwan. The point system is intricate (Generals score high). Played in tea houses and family gatherings.",
    "setup": "Players: Two or more players. Deck: 112 cards: four color suits (green, white, yellow, red), 28 cards per suit, each with the seven Xiangqi piece ranks and four copies per card; some sets include optional jokers. Equipment: 112 cards: four color suits (green, white, yellow, red), 28 cards per suit, each with the seven Xiangqi piece ranks and four copies per card; some sets include optional jokers. Dealing: Deal 20 cards to non-dealers, 21 to dealer.",
    "rules": "Objective: Go out (Hu) by melding all of your cards into valid sets (single General, pairs, Pongs, Kongs) so that your total point value is an odd number; the first player to do so wins the hand. Gameplay: Each turn a player draws one card from the stock or the top of the discard pile, may lay down melds (single General, pairs, triplets/Pongs, quads/Kongs), then discards one card. The first to go out (Hu) by melding all cards with an odd point total wins the hand. Point system uses piece values; Generals score high. Scoring: Go Out (Hu) by melding all cards. - Draw one from stock or top discard, then optionally meld, then discard one. - Melds: single General, pairs, triplets (Pongs), quads (Kongs). - Go out (Hu) when all cards are melded and total points are odd (e.g. 21). Win condition: Go Out (Hu) by melding all cards.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep pairs. Pairs become Pongs easily. Don't break up Chows unless necessary. Watch discards to know what's safe. Intermediate: The 'Sacrifice': Discarding a dangerous card early to avoid a huge penalty later. If you have 3 Generals, keep them for a Pong. Advanced: Defensive Discarding: Don't feed the next player. If they need Green Advisors, hold them. - Tip: Generals are valuable. - Tip: Odd points only to win.",
