    "history": "Introduced to the Faroe Islands by Danish merchants or sailors familiar with German Schafkopf. The name 'Sjavs' likely comes from *Schafkopf*. - 1800s: Becomes the dominant game in TÃ³rshavn. - 1950s: Rules codified by local clubs. - Present: Remains the most popular card game in the Faroes, often played in tournaments. The rubber scoring system (subtracting points) is unique to Sjavs compared to modern Schafkopf. Winning a rubber is cause for buying drinks. Being 'stuck' at 24 points is a humiliation.",
