    "strategy": "Basic: Count trumps. There are 8 cards per suit. If you have 5 trumps, bid. If you have 4, check your partner via signaling. Lead Aces of non-trump suits early to cash them in before they get trumped. Intermediate: The 'Partner' Signal: Discard high-low to show strength. If your partner leads a suit you are void in, discard a 10 to give them points (Schummeln/Smearing). Advanced: Rubber Strategy: If you are at 2 points and opponents are at 20, prevent them from winning a 'Grand' or high-value game even if it costs you a simple game loss. Defensive bidding to block their strong suit is key. - Tip: Clubs are often the best suit to name if lengths are equal. - Tip: Jacks are usually high trumps in Grand.",
    "variations": "- Suðuroy (Southern island): If pack is cut, deal 3-2-3 instead of 4-4. If 60-60, next game value increases by 2 or 4 depending on winner. Many do not award double victory if losers still on 24, or extra points for single player winning all tricks. - Three-player Sjavs: 10 cards each, 2 to table face down. Bidding same; one player makes trumps and plays alone vs the other two. Trump maker may discard one or two cards and pick up from table. Three columns on score sheet; soloist subtracts on win, opponents each subtract on loss. - Two-player Sjavs: Four face-down and four face-up cards per player in rows, plus 8 cards in hand. Face-up count as hand; when a table card is played the one under it is turned up. Bidding up to 12 possible."
