    "overview": "Skat is widely regarded as Germany's national card game and one of the best 3-player trick-taking games ever devised. It features complex bidding based on 'Game Value', a unique trump hierarchy (Jacks are highest), and three main contract types (Suit, Grand, Null). The 'Soloist' plays against two defenders. The game combines luck, skill in hand evaluation, and precise card play. Origin: Germany (Altenburg) Players: 3 (or 4 with dealer sitting out) Deck: 32-card Skat deck (A, 10, K, Q, 9, 8, 7, J - Wait, J is high. Rank: J, A, 10, K, Q, 9, 8, 7) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 5-10 minutes per hand (Session: 1-2 hours)",
    "history": "Invented in Altenburg, Thuringia, around 1810 by members of the 'Brommesche Tarok-Gesellschaft'. It combined elements of Tarock (bidding), Schafkopf (Jacks), and L'Hombre (Solitaire). - 1813: First rules written down. - 1886: First German Skat Congress. - 1927: International Skat Players Association founded. - 1998: New International Skat Order (ISKO) unifies rules. The switch from 'TournÃ©' bidding (turning up a card) to 'Reiz' bidding (numerical value) modernized the game. Skat is played competitively worldwide, with strict etiquette and complex scoring tables.",
