    "overview": "Slapjack (or 'Slaps') is a children's card game that tests speed and reflexes. Players take turns flipping cards onto a central pile. When a Jack appears, the first player to slap the pile wins all the cards in the center. The goal is to collect the entire deck. Origin: UK / USA Players: 2-8 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-20 minutes",
    "history": "Dates back to the 19th century as 'Beggar My Neighbor' variant. The physical act of slapping makes it a perennial favorite for kids. - 1900s: Included in Hoyle's under 'Children's Games'. - Present: A rite of passage for siblings. Rules like 'Double Slap' (slapping doubles like 6-6) add complexity. Often results in red hands and minor arguments over who was fastest.",
    "setup": "Players: Two to eight players; sources recommend about 4–10 for best play. Each player holds their cards as a face-down pile and takes turns flipping to a center pile. Play proceeds clockwise. Deck: One standard 52-card French deck. The pack is divided roughly equally between all players; everyone holds their cards as a face-down pile. No jokers or extra cards are used. Equipment: Standard playing cards, table. Dealing: Deal entirely to players face-down into personal piles.",
    "rules": "Objective: Collect the entire deck by winning the center pile when a Jack is played. The first player to slap the center pile when a Jack appears wins the pile and adds it to the bottom of their face-down stack. Gameplay: The pack is divided roughly equally between players; each holds cards as a face-down pile. Going clockwise, each player places the top card of their pile face up in the centre. When a Jack is played, the first player to slap the pile wins the centre pile and adds it face down to the bottom of their pile; play continues from the left of the winner. Do not peek at your card before turning it; everyone must see it at once. False slap: if you slap when the top card is not a Jack, you give your top card to the player who played that card. If you run out of cards, you may still slap the next Jack to re-enter; if you fail or false-slap, you are out. The player who has all...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep your hand hovering near the pile but not blocking the view. Watch for the 'J' index. Don't slap Queens or Kingsâ€”they look similar (face cards). Intermediate: Rhythm Disruption: Vary the speed of your flip to throw off opponents. Flip fast, then slow. Advanced: The 'Fake': Pretend to slap to induce a false slap from a jittery opponent. - Tip: Remove rings before playing! - Tip: Slapping too hard is frowned upon.",
    "variations": "- Time limit: Set a time limit; the player with the most cards when time runs out wins. - Double Slap: Slap on matching doubles (e.g. 6-6) as well as on Jacks. - Say Slapjack: Player must say 'Slapjack!' when covering the pile; if they fail, they do not get the pile. If they say it and the card is not a Jack, the other players divide the pile."
