    "history": "Slippery Sam is a variant of Red Dog (also called High Card Pool), a banking game in which players bet on having a card of the same suit and higher rank than a card dealt from the pack. In Slippery Sam, also known as Six-Spot Red Dog, the dealer turns cards face up until a six or lower appears; players bet on that spot card without having seen their own hands. The card games literature varies on how the names Red Dog, Slippery Sam and Shoot are assigned to the different versions. - 20th century: Red Dog variant documented as Slippery Sam (Six-Spot Red Dog); players bet on dealer's turned card without seeing their hands. - 1973: John Scarne's Encyclopedia of Games describes a Fort Wayne, Indiana variation with optional extra cards for a fee.",
