    "overview": "Smear (also spelled 'Schmier' or 'Schmear') is a card game played in the northern Midwest of the USA (Minnesota, Wisconsin), closely related to Pitch and High-Low-Jack. It is a trick-taking game typically played with 4 players in partnerships. The objective is to score points for High, Low, Jack, and Game (the 'Smear'), with bids usually starting at 2 or 3. The term 'Smear' also refers to the act of playing a high-point card (like a 10 or Ace) on a partner's winning trick. Origin: USA (Midwest) / Germany Players: 4 (Partnerships) Deck: Standard 52-card deck (or stripped deck A-10-K-Q-J-9? Usually full deck, but only specific trumps count). Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 20-30 minutes",
    "history": "Derived from All Fours and Pitch, with a focus on team play and aggressive point-dumping (smearing). The name comes from the German word 'schmieren' (to grease or bribe). - 1900s: A standard in German-American communities. - 1950s: Played in VFW halls and church basements. - Present: Remains a regional favorite. Often played to 21 points. A game of subtle signals and bold bids.",
    "setup": "Players: Four players in fixed partnerships, partners sitting opposite each other. Some variants use six players in two teams of three or three players without partners. Deck: Standard 52-card pack; cards in each suit rank A, K, Q, J, 10 down to 2. Some variants use a 54-card pack with one or two jokers as extra trumps, or a stripped 32-card deck (A-K-Q-J-10-9-8-7 per suit). Equipment: Standard 52-card pack; cards in each suit rank A, K, Q, J, 10 down to 2. Some variants use a 54-card pack with one or two jokers as extra trumps, or a stripped 32-card deck (A-K-Q-J-10-9-8-7 per suit). Dealing: Deal 6 cards each. Bidding starts left of dealer.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score points: High (1), Low (1), Jack (1), Game (1). Total 4 points per hand (or more in variants with Joker). Gameplay: Six cards each. One round of bidding (2 to 4); high bidder names trump and leads. Follow suit; may trump when void (in some variants may trump while holding led suit). Four points per hand: High (ace of trump), Low (deuce of trump), Jack (trick with jack of trumps), Game (side with more card points in tricks: A=4 K=3 Q=2 J=1 10=10). First team to reach target (e.g. 15 or 21) wins. Scoring: High (highest trump in play), Low (lowest trump in playâ€”goes to holder or winner? In Pitch, usually to holder. In Smear, usually to winner of trick? Check rules: In 'Smear', Low often goes to the *capturer* of the low trump, making it distinct from Pitch where it goes to the original holder. Four points per hand: High (player who plays ace of trump), Low (player who...",
    "strategy": "Basic: If you have the Ace and King of Trumps, lead the Ace to draw trumps out, then lead the King to let your partner smear a 10 or Jack. If you are defending, try to save your Low (2) for a trick you can win, or smear it on your partner's trick if you think they are winning. Intermediate: The 'Game' Count: Counting the 10s is vital. There are four 10s (40 points). Aces are 4 each (16). Kings are 3 each (12). Queens 2 (8). Jacks 1 (4). Total usually around 80 points. Winning the 'Game' point often decides the match. Advanced: Bidding against the Dealer: The dealer has the last say and can 'steal' the bid for the same amount (in some rules). Bid high enough to force them to 3 or 4. - Tip: Don't smear if an opponent might over-trump. - Tip: The Jack is worth a game point AND a scoring point.",
    "variations": "- Ten Point Smear: 10 points per hand with Jick (left bower) and two jokers; bids 4 to 10; played to 52. Four, five, or six players. - Wisconsin Six Point: 32-card deck (A-K-Q-J-10-9-8-7) plus two jokers; 6 points; low to holder of lowest trump other than joker; target usually 21."
