    "history": "Described in the 1926 US Playing-Card Company Official Rules as a variant of Wild Widow; one of the first shared-card poker games. - 1926: Described in US Playing-Card Company Official Rules as variant of Wild Widow.",
    "setup": "Players: Two to six players. Deck: Standard 52-card deck Equipment: Standard deck Dealing: Four cards face down to each player, then one card face up to the table. The table card and all cards of the same rank are wild.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the pot by having the highest five-card poker hand at showdown, using your four private cards plus the single community card (with wilds). Gameplay: After the deal, there is a betting round. Then each player in turn may discard any number of cards and receive that many replacements from the deck. A second betting round follows, then showdown. Each hand is the player's four cards plus the community card; the table card and all cards of its rank are wild. Highest hand wins. Scoring: Highest five-card hand at showdown wins the pot. Standard poker hand rankings; the community card and all cards of its rank are wild. - Four cards per player plus one face-up community card; that card and all of same rank are wild. - One betting round after the deal, then a draw round (discard and replace), then a second betting round, then showdown. - Best five-card hand using four hole cards...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Value the community card and its rank: if it pairs your hand or completes draws, you have hidden strength. Fold weak hands when the wild rank does not help you. Intermediate: Track how many of the wild rank are already visible; adjust your draw and betting based on whether opponents can make trips or better. Bluff when the board favors your perceived range. Advanced: Balance draw sizes to disguise hand strength; use position to check-raise or bet after the draw when the wild card creates ambiguous ranges. - Tip: The single community card is wild for everyone; hands run higher than in standard draw. - Tip: Some house rules limit the draw to two cards; clarify before play.",
    "variations": "- Max two cards draw: Some rules allow discarding at most two cards in the draw phase instead of any number."
