    "overview": "Spizzichino (or 'Pizzichino') is a complex two-player Italian card game played with a 40-card deck (Italian suits). It combines elements of Briscola (trick-taking) and Scopa (capture/fishing) with meld-making (similar to Bezique/Pinochle). The name likely refers to 'picking' or 'nibbling' at points in small increments. The game is highly strategic and lesser-known outside Italy. Origin: Italy (Bologna/Rome) Players: 2 Deck: 40 cards (Standard Italian: remove 8s, 9s, 10s from 52-card deck). Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 30-45 minutes",
    "history": "Dates to the 18th or 19th century. A hybrid of Tressette mechanics (ranking 3-2-A) and Scopa capture? No, usually Tressette ranking. - 1800s: Popular in Italian cafes. - 1900s: Fades slightly but maintained in Southern Italy. The melding mechanics ('Accuse') add layers of strategy. A game for serious card players who enjoy counting and melding.",
    "setup": "Players: Two players; they take turns to deal. Each player has a hand of 10 cards and two face-up packets of 5 cards on the table (only the top card of each packet is visible). Play is from hand or from the top of a packet. Deck: 40 cards (Italian or Stripped 52). Equipment: 40-card Italian pack (Latin suits). In some regions a 40-card French pack with Queen replacing Cavallo. Dealing: Dealer shuffles and creates eight packets of 5 cards face down. Opponent chooses four packets: two placed face-up on the table (top card visible only), two picked up as a 10-card hand. Dealer does the same with the other four. Each player has a 10-card hand and two face-up packets of 5 cards.",
    "rules": "Objective: Score more points than your opponent by winning tricks (card points plus last-trick bonus) and declaring buongiochi (three or four of a rank, or Napoletana 3-2-A of a suit). The game ends when a player reaches 51 or more points. Gameplay: Two players play 20 tricks. Non-dealer leads first; winner of each trick leads the next. You may play from your hand or the top of one of your two face-up packets (revealing the next card). Must follow suit when possible. Same suit: higher card wins; different suit: lead wins. Honours (3, 2, Ace) on top of a packet may be moved to hand. Declare buongiochi (three or four of same rank, or Napoletana 3-2-A of a suit) when acquired to score 3 or 4 points. After 20 tricks, count card points in tricks won plus 1 for last trick; add meld points. Game ends when a player reaches 51 points. Scoring: Ace=1, 2/3/Pictures=1/3 point. Last trick bonus. -...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Prioritize melds. A Napola is worth 3 points immediately plus card values. Save your 3s, 2s, and Aces for this purpose. Don't play them on empty tricks if you can meld them. Intermediate: The 'Pizzico': 'Nibbling' points. If you see your opponent collecting a suit for Napola, lead that suit to break their run or force them to play high. Advanced: Counting the Tick: There are 10 points and 2/3rds in the deck. Total score is 11 points (plus melds). Knowing exactly how many points have been played is vital for the endgame squeeze. - Tip: Three is the highest card. - Tip: Aces capture Pictures easily.",
    "variations": "- Look at bottom of packets: Before choosing which packets to turn up, each player may look at the bottom card of their own packets. - No buongiochi: Buongiochi are not declared or scored; game is played to 21 or 31 points instead of 51. - Tressette in Due a Metà Mazzo: Alternative variant: deal 10 each, 20 cards as stock. After each trick both players draw from stock (winner first) and show the card. Declare buongiochi before first trick or when a draw completes a new combination. Game to 21, 31, 41 or 51."
