    "overview": "Spoons (Pig) is the token-grabbing version of Pig: players pass cards to collect four of a kind, then the first successful player takes a spoon and everyone else races to take one of the remaining spoons. Origin: Likely USA (20th Century) Players: 2-13 (best 4-8) Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-20 minutes",
    "history": "A family of simple card-passing games played in a circle; players pass one card left and try to collect four of a kind. The group has no single documented inventor. Pig uses a nose-touch signal; Spoons uses one fewer spoon than players in the center. Regional variants include Bouchon (France, with corks), Donkey (Australia, sometimes called Hog), Vatra and Magarac (Bosnia and Herzegovina), and Popa Prostul (Romania). More dangerous variants use forks or knives instead of spoons. Widely played as a parlor and children's game, with many contributors to the documented rules. - 1950s: Becomes a staple at summer camps. - 1990s: Played as a drinking game in colleges. The use of 'Spoons' is arbitrary; it could be pens, coins, or anything grab-able. Known for chaotic scrambles and bruised knuckles.",
    "setup": "Players: 2 to 13 (up to 13 with one 52-card pack; more with multiple packs). Deck: Standard 52-card pack, or multiple packs for more than 12 players. Equipment: One spoon fewer than the number of players, placed in the center where any player can reach. Dealing: Four cards dealt to each player clockwise. Remainder forms the stock to dealer's right (stock version), or use four cards of one rank per player with no stock.",
    "rules": "Objective: Collect four cards of the same rank, then take a spoon. Do not be the last player without a spoon. Gameplay: Players pass one card face down to their left-hand neighbour at a time (and in the stock version the dealer draws from the stock while the player to dealer's right discards to a pile). Passing continues until someone collects four of a kind. That player takes a spoon from the center; as soon as one spoon is taken, all other players may grab a spoon. One fewer spoon than players, so one player loses the deal. Loser gets a letter (e.g. S-P-O-O-N) or is eliminated; remove one spoon per eliminated player. Scoring: Collect 4 of a kind. Don't be the last to grab a spoon. - Pass exactly one card at a time to the left; never hold more than four cards. - First to collect four of a kind takes a spoon; then everyone may grab a spoon. - Last player without a spoon loses the deal....",
    "strategy": "Basic: Keep your eyes on the spoons, not just your cards. If you see movement in the center, grab first, ask questions later. Don't hold disparate cards; focus on one rank early (e.g., if you have two 5s, discard everything else to find the other two 5s). Intermediate: The 'Sneaky' Grab: If you get 4 of a kind, quietly take a spoon without anyone noticing. Continue playing/passing cards with one hand while holding the spoon. Watch the chaos unfold as others realize slowly. Advanced: The 'Fake' Reach: Reach towards the spoons to make everyone panic-grab. Rules often penalize this (e.g., you lose a life if you touch a spoon without 4 of a kind). Use with caution. - Tip: Remove sharp objects (watches/rings). - Tip: Use durable spoons (plastic or metal).",
    "variations": "- With stock pile: Full 52-card deck; four cards each, remainder forms stock to dealer's right. Dealer draws from stock; player to dealer's right discards to a discard pile. When stock is exhausted, shuffle discard to form new stock. - Without stock pile: Use four cards of one rank per player (e.g. 16 cards for 4 players: aces, kings, queens, jacks). No stock; all players pass one card left simultaneously. First to collect four of a kind takes a spoon."
