    "history": "Spot is a rummy game from the west of Ireland, played for small stakes in pubs and private houses. Its name comes from the spot values of cards (A=1, 2–10 face value, J/Q/K=10) used to settle payments at the end of each hand. It is likely a local variant of Basic Rummy or 500 Rummy, with the distinctive rule that no player may drop more than four cards in one turn. Traditionally stakes were a penny per spot with a 5-unit ante before the introduction of the Euro. - 1950s: Played in rural pubs. - 1980s: Still common in Galway and Mayo. The 4-card drop limit is a unique anti-sandbagging rule. Often played for pennies per spot.",
