    "history": "Stýrivolt descends from the German game Karnöffel and probably evolved in the early 17th century, spreading to Scandinavia; the name stýr-wålt appears in Sweden in 1658 (Georg Stiernhielm's poem Hercules). It has been played on the Faroe Islands since the 18th century and is documented in Jens Christian Svabo's travel writings (1781–1782) and in Danish game compendia from 1774 onward. The game survived as a Faroese tradition while largely disappearing from Denmark; rules were recorded in the cultural magazine Varðin (1975) and in later English-language rule publications. - 1800s: Staple in Faroese homes. - Today: Still played, rules preserved orally. The term 'Stýrivolt' likely comes from Danish/German terms (Steuervalt? steer/rule?). Used chalk marks (scoring) on the table.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 (Partners opposite). Deck: 48 cards (tens removed). Deal 9 each; 12-card stock. Equipment: 48 cards (tens removed). Deal 9 each; 12-card stock. Dealing: Deal 9 cards to each player, 3 at a time. Twelve cards remain as stock; the dealer uses them to determine the two chosen suits.",
    "rules": "Objective: Each partnership aims to win at least five of the nine tricks. The team that wins the majority scores on a chalk-up by erasing strokes; the first side to erase all three strokes (or eliminate the opponents' crosses) scores a cross. Making vol (winning the first five tricks) or taking all nine tricks scores extra. Gameplay: The player to the left of the dealer leads to the first trick. Play is clockwise. Any card may be played; there is no requirement to follow suit or to beat the lead. Trick winner is determined by the game's ranking: permanent stikkar and chosen-suit stikkar beat other cards; chosen sevens (lambs) win the trick when led but cannot lead to the first trick. Whoever wins a trick leads to the next. A team that has taken five tricks may stop or continue; taking the first five (vol) or all nine scores bonus strokes. Scoring: Chalk Marks. Vertical line with 3...",
