    "overview": "Tahoe (also known as Wichita Hold'em) is a community card poker game similar to Texas Hold'em and Pineapple. Players receive three hole cards (sometimes two, but usually three in the Tahoe variant) and share five community cards. The key restriction is that players may use zero, one, or two of their hole cards to form a handâ€”never all three. It is often played High-Low Split. Origin: USA Players: 2-10 Deck: Standard 52-card deck Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 10-60 minutes",
    "history": "A variant of Texas Hold'em and Pineapple poker, arising as home games sought more action. - Late 20th century: home game variant of Pineapple and Texas Hold'em with three hole cards and 0/1/2 usage rule. Derived from Pineapple (3 cards, discard 1) but keeps all 3 with usage restrictions. Popular in dealer's choice home games.",
    "setup": "Players: Two or more players. Deck: Standard 52-card deck Equipment: Chips, dealer button Dealing: Deal 3 hole cards to each player. 5 community cards dealt in Flop-Turn-River stages.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the pot with the best 5-card hand. (Often High-Low split). Gameplay: 1. Deal 3 hole cards. 2. Pre-flop betting. 3. Flop (3 cards). 4. Betting. 5. Turn (1 card). 6. Betting. 7. River (1 card). 8. Betting. 9. Showdown. Restriction: Use 0, 1, or 2 hole cards. You cannot use all 3. (Unlike Omaha where you must use 2, or Pineapple where you discard one). Scoring: Standard Poker hand rankings. Often played High-Low split (8-or-better qualifier for Low). - 3 Hole Cards - Use 0, 1, or 2 hole cards (Never 3) - Standard flop betting structure Win condition: Win the pot with the best 5-card hand. (Often High-Low split).",
    "strategy": "Basic: Hand selection is similar to Omaha but weaker because you can't use 3 cards. Look for hands that interact well with the board using 2 cards. Avoid 'tri-hand' traps (hands that need all 3 hole cards to be good). Intermediate: Since you can use 0 or 1 card, you aren't forced to use 2 like in Omaha. This makes one-card flushes and straights possible if the board allows. However, holding 3 cards gives more combinations. Advanced: In High-Low split, aim for scooping. A2x is a strong start for low. Keep in mind the 'Lazy Pineapple' aspectâ€”you keep all 3 cards till the end, giving you more information about what's NOT in the deck. - Tip: Remember the restriction: You cannot play 3 hole cards. - Tip: A-2-3 is a premium starting hand in Hi-Lo. - Tip: Suited Aces are valuable for nut flushes.",
