    "setup": "Players: Three to five; five preferred. With four or five, partnerships (Solo or called partner). With three, each plays for themselves. Deck: A French-suited Tarot pack of 78 cards: 22 trumps (tarots) ranking 21 down to 1, the Fool (Fou), and 56 suit cards in four suits (King, Queen, Cavalier, Jack, then Ace and numerals 10–2). The standard game uses the full pack; a shortened 62-card version (tarot simplifié) exists for three or four players. Equipment: 78-card French Tarot pack; chips (50 points per player) optional for scoring. Dealing: Deal and play anticlockwise. Dealer rotates right after each hand. Five players: dealer gives packets of 5 three times around so each non-dealer gets 15 cards; dealer takes last 3 (18 total) then discards 3 face down. Dealer may not discard Kings, 21, or Fou; other tarots only if dealer would have no tarots left. Four players: each gets 19 cards...",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the majority of card points. In five-player: 68 points total (52 counting cards, 15 tricks, 1 for discard); tie at 34. Team with fewer than 34 pays the difference to the winners; payment may be doubled or quadrupled for Solo or low score. Gameplay: Bidding: first hand (right of dealer) may bid a Solo (2-Card, 1-Card, or Hand Solo) or pass; later player may bid a Solo with fewer cards to supersede. If no Solo, dealer calls a King (or Queen if dealer holds all Kings) for partner. Play: player to dealer's right leads; must follow suit, else play a tarot; if neither possible, any card. No obligation to overtake. Highest tarot wins the trick, else highest of led suit. Fool may be shown once and added to own team's tricks; it never wins a trick. Scoring: 68 card points in five-player (52 counting cards, 15 tricks, 1 discard); tie at 34. Team with fewer than 34 pays the...",
