    "history": "Brag emerged in 16th-century England as a three-card vying game. It became the parent of Indian Teen Patti and influenced betting structures used by later poker-family games. - 1500s: early versions of Post and Pair and Brag are documented in England. - 1700s: bragger wild-card variants appear, often using the Jack of Clubs. - 1900s: Brag remains common in British military, social club, and pub play. - Present: the game survives as a compact high-pressure gambling and social card game. The core hand rankings and half-price blind betting have stayed recognizable for centuries even as poker became globally dominant. Brag is often treated as a working-class British poker relative and appears in British gambling fiction and film culture.",
    "setup": "Players: Typically 3 to 7 players play individually around one pot. Deck: One Standard 52-card French deck with no jokers. Equipment: A standard pack plus chips, coins, or another agreed stake unit. Dealing: Each player antes. The dealer gives three face-down cards to every active player, then betting begins.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the pot by having the best three-card hand or by being the last player remaining after all others have folded. Gameplay: 1. Ante/Boot Phase All players place the agreed minimum stake (boot) into the pot This ensures there is something to play for in each hand Scoring: Win the pot by having the best three-card hand or by being the last player remaining after all others have folded. - Blind Player: Has not looked at their cards - Must bet at least the current stake (minimum) or up to twice the current stake (maximum) - Can bet less than a seen player - Seen Player: Has looked at their cards - Must bet at least twice the current stake (minimum) or up to four times the current stake (maximum) - Must bet double what a blind player bets Win condition: Win the pot by having the best three-card hand or by being the last player remaining after all others have folded. Rules How to...",
    "strategy": "Basic: For Beginners 1. Start as a Seen Player: Look at your cards to understand hand values 2. Play Strong Hands Aggressively: Bet and raise with trails, pure sequences 3. Fold Weak Hands: Don't chase with high card only 4. Learn Hand Rankings: Memorize the order of hands 5. Manage Your Bankroll: Don't bet more than you can afford to lose Intermediate: Blind Play Strategy Advantages: Lower betting requirements, psychological pressure on seen players When to Play Blind: Early in the game, with small stakes, or to confuse opponents When to Look: When stakes get high or when considering a sideshow Advanced: Bluffing Techniques Semi-Bluff: Bet aggressively with drawing hands (potential sequences) Pure Bluff: Represent strong hands with weak cards Timing: Bluff when opponents show weakness Table Image: Establish a tight image to make bluffs more credible Strategy Pressure and price Staying...",
    "variations": "- Brag with Braggers: A common wild-card variant where selected cards such as Jack of Clubs, 9 of Diamonds, and Ace of Diamonds can act as braggers."
