    "rules": "Objective: Correctly identify which of the three face-down cards is the queen (or money card). In honest play between friends, the punter wins the stake back plus the same amount again for a correct guess; in the street scam the dealer uses sleight of hand so the punter loses. Gameplay: 1. The dealer shows three cards (one queen, two others—often two black jacks). 2. The dealer throws the three cards face-down on the table and mixes them quickly. 3. The punter bets on which face-down card is the queen. 4. If correct (in honest play), the punter receives the stake back plus the same amount again; otherwise the stake is lost. In the scam, the dealer ensures the punter never wins. Scoring: Points based on cards captured or objectives completed Game ends when target reached -- - Only three cards are used: one target card (usually a queen) and two others. - The dealer mixes the cards...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Do not play for money in street settings—it is a scam; the dealer uses sleight of hand and accomplices (shills) so the punter cannot win. Intermediate: If playing honestly with friends with no real money, the only skill is visual tracking of the queen during the mix; the dealer can make this very difficult. Advanced: Sources advise: play only with no real money involved and explain the trick afterwards. In a true monte scam the mark has no chance of winning. - Tip: Do not be misled by apparent winners—they are often shills. - Tip: Play only for fun with friends and no money at stake.",
