    "history": "Tjuv (Thief), formerly known as Tattare, is a Swedish fishing game first documented in the 1922 rulebook Kortspelsregler till tidsfördriv och nöje (Wahlström & Widstrand). The name Tattare was a disparaging term for Romani and Swedish travellers; as a game name it alluded to an unjustified reputation for stealing. Ulf Schenkmanis used the name Tjuv in his 1988 book Kortspel & patienser. The game has remained a simple family and children's pastime in Sweden. - 1922: Documented as Tattare in a Swedish rulebook; 1988 Schenkmanis text uses the name Tjuv. Has remained a simple family/children's game. Common childhood game in Sweden.",
    "setup": "Players: Two or more players. Deck: 36-card deck: A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6 in each suit (no 2-5). Rank: A (high) to 6 (low). Equipment: Cards Dealing: Three cards to each player. Each player places their lowest card face up on the table (or one of equal lowest). With two players the dealer adds one card from the deck to the table; with three or more the dealer reduces the table to three cards then deals one more to each. Three cards face up on table; remainder forms the talon.",
