    "history": "Tod und Leben is a German folk game in the War family. German War-style variants including Bettelmann (Beggar Man) and Tod und Leben were documented as early as 1833 in C.F. Schenkel's Oekonomische Encyclopaedia (Berlin). The game uses a 32-card Skat pack; one reported variant was played by Günther Senst as a child in Mecklenburg. It has remained a common children's pastime in Germany, with play continuing until one player holds all cards (Life) and the other has none (Death). - 1833: German War variants including Tod und Leben documented in Schenkel's encyclopedia. - 19th century: played in Mecklenburg and elsewhere as a children's game. Evolved from the broader War family; German 32-card form fixed as a two-player game with recycled or round-based play. Common children's game in Germany; often played with German-suited cards; no strategy, purely luck-based.",
