    "overview": "Top Trumps is a comparison card game in which each player holds themed cards showing one item (e.g. a car, a dinosaur) and several numerical attributes. The starting player names an attribute; everyone reveals that attribute's value from their top card; the highest (or best) value wins the round and the winner takes all those cards. The winner then chooses the next attribute. Play continues until one player has won all the cards. The game was first published in the UK in 1978 and is popular with families and children. Origin: Europe Players: 2-6 Deck: Themed pack (typically 30, 32, 48 or 50 cards per deck) Difficulty: Intermediate Duration: 15-30 minutes",
    "history": "Country/Region of Origin: United Kingdom. Time Period: 1978. Historical Development: Top Trumps was first published by Dubreq in 1978 with eleven themed packs; Dubreq was taken over by Waddingtons in 1982, who continued until the early 1990s. Winning Moves UK acquired the rights in 1992 and relaunched the game with many new themes. Cultural Significance: Popular playground and family game, especially with children; themed packs cover vehicles, military hardware, sports, films, and wildlife. - 1978: First published by Dubreq (UK) with eleven packs - 1982: Waddingtons took over Dubreq; continued manufacturing into early 1990s - 1992: Winning Moves UK acquired rights and relaunched with diverse themes - Modern: Digital and mobile versions; international packs and licensed themes",
    "setup": "Players: Minimum Players: 2 Maximum Players: 6 Optimal Players: 2–4 Partnerships: No Deck: Type: Themed Top Trumps pack (typically 30, 32, 48 or 50 cards). Each card depicts one item (e.g. a car model, a dinosaur) and lists 5–8 attributes with numerical values (e.g. speed, length, power). Number of Decks: 1. Jokers: Not used. Equipment: One Top Trumps themed deck; table space. No score sheet required. Dealing: All cards are dealt as equally as possible to the players. Each player holds their cards in a face-down stack so that only their top card is visible to them; opponents cannot see a player's cards. The starting player is normally the player to the dealer's left.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win all the cards by repeatedly winning rounds: in each round the active player chooses an attribute, everyone reveals that attribute's value from their top card, and the player with the highest (or best) value takes all the cards played that round and chooses the next attribute. The game ends when one player has collected the entire pack. Gameplay: 1. Setup: Deal all cards as equally as possible; each player holds their cards in a face-down stack, viewing only the top card. 2. Play: The starting player (normally left of dealer) chooses one attribute and reads its value from their top card; each other player reads the same attribute from their top card. The best value (usually highest; for some attributes like weight or time, lowest is better) wins the round. 3. Winner takes all the top cards from that round and places them at the bottom of their stack, then chooses the next...",
    "strategy": "Basic: Choose attributes where your top card's value is strong relative to what you have seen. Avoid categories where your card is weak. Intermediate: Remember which attribute values have already been played or are likely on opponents' cards. When you are likely to lose a round, sometimes choosing an attribute where you might tie forces a center pile and gives you another chance to choose. Advanced: Track the distribution of values across the pack if possible; prioritise winning when you can choose so you keep the lead and control the category. - Tip: Pick categories where your visible card ranks highly - Tip: On a weak card, consider choosing an attribute that might tie to get another turn - Tip: Winning the round lets you choose the next attribute from your new top card",
    "variations": "- Three card pick: When a player has three or fewer cards left, they may look at all of them and choose in which order to play. This variant lengthens the game."
