    "history": "Trionfetti is a four-player partnership game from the rural area between Venice and Padua, played with an Italian suited deck (Trevigiane pattern). It was formally described by Alberto Fiorin at the IPCS Convention in 1997 and is the subject of a booklet by Gianluca Angi (2020). During the 1970s and 1980s several large tournaments were held; in 2009 the Club del 15 in Fossò founded the Scuola di Trionfetti, which still runs weekly meetings to teach new players. The game is a relative of Truc and Put and is preserved as a regional Venetian tradition. - 1997: Described by Alberto Fiorin at IPCS Convention; 2009: Scuola di Trionfetti founded in Fossò; 2020: Booklet by Gianluca Angi. Preserved by local clubs (Club dei 15 di FossÃ²). Regional Venetian tradition.",
    "setup": "Players: 4 (partnerships, partners opposite) Deck: 40- or 52-card Italian deck (Trevigiane). Default ranking ascending: 1 (low) to King (high); 40-card lacks 8s, 9s, 10s. Equipment: Cards, score sheet Dealing: Deal 3 cards each per hand. Same dealer deals up to 4 hands from the deck (smazzata). Cutter (to dealer's left) sees cut card and announces ranking (comanda) for the smazzata.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win at least two of three tricks per hand to score 1 point (or more if vagaresto is accepted). First team to 20 points wins the game; first to 3 games wins the match. Gameplay: Play is anticlockwise. Player to dealer's right leads. No requirement to follow suit; each trick is won by the highest card according to the current ranking (comanda). Ties go to the first played of equal cards. Optional vagaresto raises the stake for the hand. Up to 4 hands (smazzata) from the same deck; then new dealer shuffles and new cutter announces ranking. Scoring: One point per hand to the team that wins two of three tricks (or more if vagaresto accepted). Points recorded per smazzata; first team to 20 points wins the game; first to 3 games wins the match. - Cutter announces card ranking (comanda) for each smazzata - No follow suit; highest by ranking wins trick - 2 of 3 tricks wins the hand -...",
