    "history": "Tripoli (also known as Three in One) is a modern North American adaptation of the ancient European game Poch (Pochspiel), which has been played in Germany for at least 500 years. Poch is documented in 15th-century sources; Thierry Depaulis's article in The Playing-Card details its early history. Tripoli retains Poch's three-phase structure (pay cards, betting, Stops) but uses a standard 52-card deck and fixed heart-suit layout; it is sold in North America as Tripoley, Rummoli, Michigan Rummy, and Royal Rummy, and in Australia as Calliente. - 15th century: Poch played in Germany. - 20th century: Tripoli/Tripoley and Rummoli sold in North America. Adapted for North American market with game board. Popular family game.",
    "setup": "Players: Two or more players. Deck: Standard 52-card deck. Board/mat with labeled sections. Equipment: Cards, chips, Tripoli board Dealing: Deal all cards (one hand is 'dead'). Ante chips to board.",
    "rules": "Objective: Accumulate the most chips by winning stakes in the Pay Cards phase, the Poker phase, and the Michigan (Stops) phase; the player with the most chips when the game ends wins. Gameplay: Phase 1: Pay Cards â€” Heart holders collect. Phase 2: Poker â€” Pick 5 from hand, bet/fold. Phase 3: Michigan Rummy â€” Play ascending sequences by suit; first out wins Kitty + chips per card left in opponents' hands. Scoring: Chips won across all phases. Unclaimed sections roll over. - 3 distinct phases per round - Dead hand creates stops - Unclaimed chips roll to next round Win condition: Win the most chips across 3 phases.",
    "strategy": "Basic: Ante wisely. Play strong poker hands. Shed low cards in Phase 3. Intermediate: Remember what's in the dead hand (stops). Advanced: Manage stop cards to control Phase 3.",
