    "history": "Turkish poker (Türk pokeri) is a draw poker variant using a shortened French deck; information is attributed to Burçin Kerem Seyis. It follows the same deal–bet–draw–bet–showdown structure as five-card draw but with a minimum opening requirement of a pair of Kings and distinct hand rankings. - Contemporary: Turkish draw poker variant using a shortened French deck; information attributed to Burçin Kerem Seyis. Uses stripped deck (24–36 cards) and regional hand order: flush above full house, three of a kind above straight; suits break ties. Played in Turkey with Turkish hand names (Floş, Kare, Ful, Üçlü, Kent, Doper, Per, Yüksek Kart).",
    "setup": "Players: 2 to 5 players. Deck: Short deck: 24 cards (2 players) through 36 cards (5 players), made by removing low cards from a standard 52-card pack. Equipment: Cards and chips. Dealing: First dealer chosen at random; deal rotates left. Before each deal all players ante an equal amount. Cards are shuffled, cut, and dealt clockwise one at a time until each player has five cards.",
    "rules": "Objective: Win the pot by having the highest-ranked five-card hand at showdown or by being the last player left after all others fold. Gameplay: 1. Ante: All players post an equal ante. 2. Deal: Five cards each, one at a time, clockwise. 3. First betting round: Minimum to open is a pair of Kings; players with less must pass; opener may pass. Once someone opens, others may call, raise, or fold. 4. Draw: Starting left of dealer, each player may exchange 0–4 cards (discard face down, receive replacements from stock). 5. Second betting round: Opener (or next active player) may check or bet; others fold, call, or raise. 6. Showdown: Remaining players reveal hands; highest hand wins. If nobody opens, hands are thrown in, pot stays, players ante again and the next dealer deals. Scoring: Highest-ranked five-card hand at showdown wins the pot; last player remaining after all others fold wins...",
