    "history": "War is a children's comparison card game played in many parts of the world. No single inventor or date is documented; it has long been passed on as a first card game. In the United Kingdom it is often called Battle. Related regional forms include the Russian P'yanitsa (Drunkard), the German Tod und Leben (Life and Death), and Syrian War, indicating broad dissemination across Europe and beyond. The core mechanic—simultaneously revealing cards and the higher rank capturing both—is one of the simplest card-game structures and requires no strategy, only the ability to compare ranks. - 20th century: Documented as a children's comparison game in English-speaking and European sources. Universal children's first card game; regional variants (Russian Drunkard, German Tod und Leben, Syrian War) share the same flip-and-compare core. Known worldwide as a first card game for children; played in...",
